/* AQA GCSE Chemistry (8462) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Opus 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'AQA-8462', KITS: {"3":{"id":"AQA-8462","topic":"3","family":"science","lesson":{"why":"Working scientifically is not a topic you revise once and forget: it is assessed on both GCSE Chemistry papers, inside questions about atoms, acids, electrolysis, energy changes, rates, analysis and using resources. Questions drawing on the knowledge and understanding gained from the eight required practical activities count for at least 15 % of the marks for the qualification, and mathematics for at least 20 %, so the marks in this room are some of the most predictable on the paper. This room drills the exact moves examiners ask for again and again — identifying the independent, dependent and control variables, choosing apparatus with the right resolution, spotting an anomalous titre, calculating a mean and the uncertainty from the range, reading a gradient off a volume–time curve, rounding to a sensible number of significant figures, and using the specification's precise words for accuracy, precision, repeatability, reproducibility and error — all set in the eight required practicals of GCSE Chemistry so the skills are practised the way they will actually be tested.","idea":[{"h":"How chemical ideas change when new data arrives","t":"A scientific method or theory is kept only while it explains all the data; when new data from experiments or observations cannot be explained, the theory is revised or replaced. The classic chemistry example is the model of the atom: the plum pudding model, in which positive charge was spread through the atom, could not explain why a small number of alpha particles bounced back from thin gold foil, so it was replaced by the nuclear model. In the exam you may be given a historical example, or fresh experimental data, and asked to explain why the data forced the change, or to decide whether given data supports a stated theory. Point at the specific result and say what it showed that the older idea could not.","code":"WS 1.1"},{"h":"Using models to explain and predict in chemistry","t":"A model is a simplified representation of something real: a particle diagram, a dot and cross diagram, a reaction profile, a balanced equation or a rule such as 'at the cathode, hydrogen is produced if the metal is more reactive than hydrogen'. In Required practical 3 you use that rule to predict the products of electrolysing an aqueous solution, then test the prediction with gas tests. In the exam you may be asked to use a model to make a prediction or calculate a quantity, to match a feature of a model to the data it describes, or to give a limitation of the model and suggest an observation or experiment that would test it.","code":"WS 1.2"},{"h":"What science can and cannot settle, and the ethics of it","t":"Science answers only the questions that data can address, and that data may be uncertain, incomplete or simply not yet available — for example the long-term effects of very low concentrations of a substance in drinking water. Questions about whether something should be done, such as whether to add a chemical to a public water supply, also involve values, so they cannot be settled by data alone. In the exam you may be asked to explain why data on an issue is uncertain or missing, or to outline a simple ethical argument for and against a technology, giving both sides rather than a personal opinion.","code":"WS 1.3"},{"h":"Applications of chemistry and their implications","t":"Every application of chemistry — sterilising potable water with chlorine, desalinating sea water by distillation or reverse osmosis, using a catalyst to speed a reaction up — carries personal, social, economic and environmental consequences that have to be weighed against each other. In the exam you may be asked to describe and explain a named application, or to use data supplied in the question to describe and evaluate a method of tackling a problem caused by human activity, and then to reach a decision. The decision must be justified by the evidence given, not by opinion: quote the figures that make one option better.","code":"WS 1.4"},{"h":"Weighing hazard, risk and how risk is perceived","t":"Every chemical technology has hazards that must be set against its benefits, and the risk people feel is often different from the risk the data shows. Risks that are imposed rather than chosen, unfamiliar rather than familiar, or invisible rather than visible, feel larger than measured risk — which is why chlorine added to tap water worries people more than the same hazard met in household bleach. In the exam you may be asked to give an example of a hazard weighed against a benefit, or to suggest why a perception of risk differs from the measured risk in the situation described.","code":"WS 1.5"},{"h":"Peer review and reporting results","t":"Before new work is published, other scientists working in the same field check the method, the data and the conclusions. This peer review helps to detect false claims and to establish a consensus about which claims should be regarded as valid, which is why a single unchecked result is not accepted straight away. Reports of scientific developments in newspapers, on television and online are not peer reviewed and may be oversimplified, inaccurate or biased. In the exam you may be asked to explain what peer review adds, or why a media report of a chemistry finding should be treated more cautiously than the published study.","code":"WS 1.6"},{"h":"Turning an observation into a hypothesis","t":"A hypothesis is a testable statement, built on a scientific idea, that explains an observation and predicts what should happen next. Required practicals 3 and 5 are both specified as investigations involving developing a hypothesis: for example, 'the more concentrated the sodium thiosulfate solution, the faster the cross disappears, because there are more particles in the same volume so collisions are more frequent'. In the exam you are given an observation or a set of data and asked to suggest a hypothesis; link the pattern to chemistry already in the specification — collision frequency, reactivity, solubility — rather than restating the observation.","code":"WS 2.1"},{"h":"Planning an investigation and controlling the variables","t":"The independent variable is the one you change or select, the dependent variable is the one you measure for each change, and the control variables are kept the same so that any change in the dependent variable can be put down to the independent variable alone. In Required practical 5 the independent variable is the concentration of the solution, the dependent variable is the volume of gas collected in a fixed time or the time for a cross to disappear, and temperature, volume of acid and the mass and size of the solid must all be held constant. In the exam you must identify each variable in an unfamiliar context and explain why a named control variable matters.","code":"WS 2.2"},{"h":"Choosing the right technique, instrument and apparatus","t":"Apparatus must suit the measurement and give the resolution the task needs: a pipette delivers one fixed volume accurately, a burette measures any volume added and is read to 0.1 cm³ divisions, a measuring cylinder is quicker but coarser, a gas syringe measures the volume of a gas directly, a thermometer or temperature probe reads temperature change, and a balance reading to 0.01 g weighs a salt crystallised in Required practical 1. In the exam you may be asked to select or suggest apparatus for a stated purpose and to explain the choice in terms of the range or resolution required.","code":"WS 2.3"},{"h":"Working safely and manipulating apparatus correctly","t":"A safety answer must name the hazard in that specific practical and a specific control: dilute acids and alkalis are irritant, so eye protection is worn; the dilute acid heated with a Bunsen burner in Required practical 1 and the hot evaporating basin are burn hazards, so tongs and a water bath or electric heater are used; chlorine produced at the anode in Required practical 3 is toxic, so only small volumes are electrolysed in a well-ventilated room or fume cupboard. In the exam, 'be careful' earns nothing — the hazard and the measure that reduces the risk must both be named.","code":"WS 2.4"},{"h":"Sampling so the sample represents the whole","t":"When the material being tested is not uniform, the sample must represent the whole of it. Required practical 8 analyses water samples from different sources, so samples are taken from several points, in clean containers, at the same depth and on the same day, and are then combined or tested separately rather than taking one convenient scoop from the bank. In the exam you may be asked to suggest and describe a sampling technique for a stated context and to explain how it avoids bias, so that the pH and dissolved solids measured are typical of the source rather than of one spot.","code":"WS 2.5"},{"h":"Making and recording measurements properly","t":"Every instrument has a resolution — the smallest change it can detect — and a reading is recorded to the resolution of the scale, with its unit, every time. A burette graduated in 0.1 cm³ divisions is read at the bottom of the meniscus with the eye level with the scale and the titre recorded to two decimal places; a thermometer marked in 0.1 °C is read to 0.1 °C; a balance reading 0.01 g is recorded to two decimal places. In the exam you may be shown a scale or a display and asked to record the value shown and state the resolution.","code":"WS 2.6"},{"h":"Evaluating a method and improving it","t":"Evaluating a method means judging whether it is valid — whether it measures what it claims to measure with the other variables controlled — and whether enough precise measurements were taken to support the conclusion. An ink origin line in Required practical 6 makes the chromatogram invalid because the ink itself dissolves and runs; a single run with no repeats means the spread of results, and so the precision, cannot be judged at all; only two concentrations tested is too few to show a trend. In the exam, name the specific weakness and give the specific improvement.","code":"WS 2.7"},{"h":"Presenting observations and data","t":"Data is presented in the form that fits it. Results for named substances — the flame colours of lithium, sodium, potassium, calcium and copper compounds in Required practical 7, or which metal gives the largest temperature rise — are categoric, so a bar chart or a results table is used. Two continuous variables, such as the volume of gas against time in Required practical 5, go on a line graph: independent variable on the x-axis, dependent variable on the y-axis, evenly spaced labelled scales with units, points plotted accurately and a line or smooth curve of best fit. In the exam you may be asked to construct or interpret either.","code":"WS 3.1"},{"h":"Translating data between tables and graphs","t":"The same results can appear as a table of numbers or as a graph, and you must move between the two in either direction: plotting tabulated titres or temperatures onto axes, or reading a value off a curve and writing it into a table. From a volume–time graph in Required practical 5 you can read the volume of gas collected at 30 s, and from a table you can plot the curve that shows it. In the exam, questions say 'plot', 'determine from the graph' or 'use the graph to find', and every value read off must carry its unit.","code":"WS 3.2"},{"h":"The mathematics examiners expect","t":"You must use an appropriate number of significant figures, find the arithmetic mean and the range of a set of repeats, make order of magnitude calculations, change the subject of an equation, substitute values with the correct units, and determine the slope and intercept of a straight-line graph. For a curve, the gradient of a tangent gives the rate of change at that instant — in Required practical 5 the tangent to the volume–time curve at t = 0 s gives the initial rate of reaction in cm³/s. The area between a curve and the x-axis has a physical meaning and can be measured by counting squares. Practise each of these with real practical numbers.","code":"WS 3.3"},{"h":"Estimating the uncertainty in a result","t":"Whenever a measurement is made there is some uncertainty in the result, because no instrument or technique is perfect. For a set of repeat readings, the uncertainty in the mean is estimated as half the range of the readings about the mean: uncertainty = range ÷ 2, and the result is written as mean ± uncertainty. Concordant titres in Required practical 2 that lie within 0.10 cm³ of each other give a small uncertainty; widely spread temperature rises in Required practical 4 give a large one. In the exam you may be asked to calculate the mean and then use the range to estimate the uncertainty.","code":"WS 3.4"},{"h":"Patterns, trends and conclusions","t":"A conclusion goes only as far as the data allows. Describe the pattern — 'as the concentration of acid increases, the time for the reaction to finish decreases, so the rate increases' — and predict beyond the values tested only if the question asks, saying that the prediction assumes the trend continues. On a volume–time curve, a section that becomes less steep shows the rate falling and a horizontal section shows that the reaction has finished. In the exam you may be asked to recognise or describe a trend in a table or graph, to predict an untested value, or to draw a conclusion supported directly by the results given.","code":"WS 3.5"},{"h":"Testing a hypothesis against the data","t":"Data can support a hypothesis, fail to support it, or support it over only part of the range tested, and a good answer comments on how far the match goes rather than answering yes or no. Where two hypotheses are offered — for example whether an unknown compound in Required practical 7 is a calcium salt or a magnesium salt — decide which one the results fit better and quote the specific observation that separates them, and say which observations do not distinguish between them. In the exam, 'justify your answer' means referring to actual values or named observations from the question.","code":"WS 3.6"},{"h":"Accuracy, precision, repeatability, reproducibility and error","t":"An accurate measurement is close to the true value. Measurements are precise if they cluster closely together, whether or not they are close to the true value. Results are repeatable when the same investigator, using the same method and equipment, gets similar results on repeating the work, and reproducible when a different investigator, or different equipment, gets similar results. Random error makes readings vary unpredictably and is reduced by taking more measurements and reporting a mean; systematic error shifts every reading from the true value by the same amount, so averaging never removes it — the apparatus or method must be corrected. Anomalous values should be examined to find the cause and, if they are the product of a poor measurement, ignored.","code":"WS 3.7"},{"h":"Communicating the rationale, method and conclusion","t":"A full account of an investigation explains why the method was chosen, what was done, and what the results allow you to conclude, using words, diagrams, tables, graphs and equations together so that another person could follow and repeat it. In the exam this is assessed by extended-response questions worth up to six marks and marked by levels: give a coherent, logically ordered answer that names the independent, dependent and control variables, describes the method in sequence with quantities and apparatus, includes repeats, and says how the results will be processed and used to reach a conclusion.","code":"WS 3.8"},{"h":"Using the specification's own vocabulary","t":"Mark schemes reward the specification's technical terms used precisely: 'independent variable' not 'the thing I changed', 'anomalous result' not 'odd number', 'concordant titres' not 'close ones', 'precipitate' not 'cloudy bit', 'valid' not 'fair'. These terms apply across the whole specification, including the required practicals. In the exam, vague everyday language can cost a mark even when the underlying idea is right, so learn each working-scientifically word as a definition you could write out, and use it in the sentence where the mark scheme expects it.","code":"WS 4.1"},{"h":"Scientific quantities and how they are determined","t":"A scientific quantity is defined by what it measures, how it is measured and the unit it is measured in: volume of solution by pipette or burette in cm³, mass by balance in g, temperature change by thermometer in °C, time by stopwatch in s, concentration in mol/dm³ or g/dm³. Knowing how a quantity is obtained lets you judge whether a value in a question is sensible and which quantity is the right one to record. In the exam you may be asked which quantity should be measured in a described investigation, with what apparatus, and why that quantity rather than another.","code":"WS 4.2"},{"h":"SI units and chemical names","t":"Quantities are given in SI units and their prefixed forms — kg, g and mg for mass; km, m and mm for length; J and kJ for energy; s for time; cm³ and dm³ for volume — and substances are named by IUPAC rules unless a common name is more appropriate: sodium chloride rather than salt, ethanoic acid rather than vinegar, calcium hydroxide solution rather than limewater in a formal answer. In the exam a numerical answer written without its unit, or with the wrong unit, usually loses a mark even when the method is right.","code":"WS 4.3"},{"h":"Prefixes and powers of ten","t":"Prefixes scale a unit by powers of ten: tera ×10¹², giga ×10⁹, mega ×10⁶, kilo ×10³, centi ×10⁻², milli ×10⁻³, micro ×10⁻⁶ and nano ×10⁻⁹. They let you write a measurement at a sensible size — 45 mg of dissolved solids rather than 0.000045 kg, or a nanoparticle diameter in nanometres rather than metres. In the exam you may need to write a value in standard form, choose the most sensible prefixed unit for an order of magnitude, or compare two quantities by their powers of ten.","code":"WS 4.4"},{"h":"Converting units before you calculate","t":"Values must be converted into the units an equation needs before they are substituted: 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³, 1 g = 1000 mg, 1 kJ = 1000 J, and 1 minute = 60 s. A concentration in g/dm³ cannot be worked out from a volume left in cm³, and a titre of 22.5 cm³ becomes 0.0225 dm³. In the exam, leaving a volume in the wrong unit gives an answer wrong by a factor of a thousand even when the method is perfect, so convert first and write the converted value down.","code":"WS 4.5"},{"h":"Choosing the number of significant figures","t":"A calculated answer is normally given to the same number of significant figures as the least precise measurement used in the calculation, and never to more figures than the data justifies, because extra figures claim a precision that was never measured. Two lengths measured to 2 significant figures on a chromatogram give an Rf to 2 significant figures. In the exam a question may name the number of significant figures required; marks are lost for over-rounding or for copying the whole calculator display, so round only at the end of the calculation.","code":"WS 4.6"}],"examples":[{"title":"Anomalous result, mean and uncertainty in a rate experiment","setup":"In Required practical 5 a student adds dilute hydrochloric acid to sodium thiosulfate solution in a conical flask standing on a paper cross, and times how long the cross takes to disappear. Five repeats at the same concentration and temperature give 42 s, 44 s, 43 s, 58 s and 43 s. Identify the anomalous result, calculate the mean time from the readings kept, and estimate the uncertainty in that mean.","steps":["A reading of 58 s sits far outside the cluster formed by the other four, so it is anomalous and is excluded before averaging.","Mean = (42 + 44 + 43 + 43) ÷ 4 = 172 ÷ 4 = 43 s.","Range of the readings kept = 44 s − 42 s = 2 s.","Uncertainty = range ÷ 2 = 2 ÷ 2 = ±1 s, so the time is recorded as 43 s ± 1 s."],"cues":["Which of the five readings does not belong with the rest, and why?","How are the readings you kept turned into a single representative value?","How spread out are the readings you kept?","How is that spread turned into an uncertainty in the mean?"]},{"title":"Concordant titres: mean titre, uncertainty and percentage uncertainty","setup":"In Required practical 2 a student titrates 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution against dilute sulfuric acid from a burette graduated in 0.1 cm³ divisions. The rough titre is 25.90 cm³ and the next three titres are 25.10 cm³, 25.20 cm³ and 25.15 cm³. Calculate the mean titre from the concordant results, estimate the uncertainty in it from the range, and express that uncertainty as a percentage of the mean.","steps":["Only titres that agree within 0.10 cm³ of one another are concordant, so the rough result is discarded and the last three are used.","Mean titre = (25.10 + 25.20 + 25.15) ÷ 3 = 75.45 ÷ 3 = 25.15 cm³.","Range = 25.20 cm³ − 25.10 cm³ = 0.10 cm³, so uncertainty = range ÷ 2 = ±0.05 cm³.","Percentage uncertainty = (0.05 ÷ 25.15) × 100 = 0.20 % to 2 significant figures."],"cues":["Which of the four titres should be left out of the mean, and why?","How is the mean of the titres you kept calculated?","What is the spread of those titres, and what uncertainty does it give?","How is an uncertainty turned into a percentage of the result?"]},{"title":"Calculating an Rf value to a sensible number of figures","setup":"In Required practical 6 a student spots a food colouring on a pencil origin line and runs a paper chromatogram. Measured from the origin line, the solvent front has moved 9.6 cm and the centre of the single spot produced has moved 4.2 cm. Calculate the Rf value of the colouring, give it to an appropriate number of significant figures, and say what the single spot shows.","steps":["Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent, both measured from the origin line to the centre of the spot and to the solvent front.","Rf = 4.2 ÷ 9.6 = 0.4375 on the calculator display.","Both distances were measured to 2 significant figures, so the answer is rounded to 2 significant figures: Rf = 0.44, with no unit because it is a ratio of two lengths.","One spot in this solvent means the colouring behaved as a single substance here, so it could be a pure compound; a second solvent would be needed to be sure."],"cues":["What two distances does an Rf value compare, and where are they measured from?","What does the division give before any rounding?","How many figures does the data justify, and does the answer carry a unit?","What does producing only one spot tell you about the colouring?"]},{"title":"Initial rate of reaction from the gradient of a tangent","setup":"In Required practical 5 a student collects the hydrogen given off as magnesium ribbon reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid in a gas syringe, and plots the volume of gas against time. A tangent drawn to the curve at t = 0 s passes through the origin and through the point (20 s, 34 cm³). Determine the initial rate of the reaction, in cm³/s.","steps":["The rate at any instant is the gradient of the tangent drawn to the volume–time curve at that instant, so the tangent at the start gives the initial rate.","Gradient = change in volume ÷ change in time = (34 − 0) cm³ ÷ (20 − 0) s.","Initial rate = 34 ÷ 20 = 1.7 cm³/s, and this is the largest rate of the whole reaction because the acid is most concentrated at the start."],"cues":["What feature of the graph represents the rate at one moment in time?","Which two quantities are divided, and using which two points?","What is the value of the gradient, with its unit, and how does it compare with the rate later on?"]},{"title":"Dissolved solids in a water sample, in g/dm³","setup":"In Required practical 8 a student measures the dissolved solids in river water. A clean, dry evaporating basin has a mass of 42.36 g. The student evaporates 50.0 cm³ of the filtered sample to dryness in the basin using a water bath, and after cooling the basin and residue together have a mass of 42.51 g. Calculate the concentration of dissolved solids in the sample, in g/dm³.","steps":["Subtracting the two weighings leaves the residue alone: 42.51 g − 42.36 g = 0.15 g of dissolved solids.","The volume must be in dm³ before dividing: 50.0 cm³ ÷ 1000 = 0.0500 dm³.","Concentration = mass ÷ volume = 0.15 g ÷ 0.0500 dm³ = 3.0 g/dm³, to 2 significant figures as the mass of residue was known to 2 significant figures."],"cues":["How is the mass of the residue alone obtained from the two weighings?","What must be done to the volume before it can be used with an answer in g/dm³?","Which quantities are divided, and how many figures does the data justify?"]},{"title":"Identifying an unknown compound from test results","setup":"In Required practical 7 a student tests an unknown white solid. It dissolves in water to give a colourless solution. A flame test on the solid gives a lilac flame. To a fresh portion of the solution the student adds dilute nitric acid and then silver nitrate solution, and a cream precipitate forms. Identify the two ions present and name the compound.","steps":["A lilac flame colour identifies potassium ions, K⁺, as the metal ion present.","Silver nitrate with dilute nitric acid tests for halide ions, and a cream precipitate is silver bromide, so the negative ion is the bromide ion, Br⁻.","A potassium ion carries one positive charge and a bromide ion one negative charge, so they combine in a 1 : 1 ratio and the compound is potassium bromide, KBr."],"cues":["Which metal ion gives that flame colour?","What is the silver nitrate test for, and which halide gives a cream precipitate?","How do the charges on the two ions fix the formula and the name?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why is the origin line in paper chromatography drawn in pencil rather than in ink?","a":"Pencil does not dissolve in the solvent, so the line stays put; an ink line would dissolve and run up the paper with the solvent, mixing with the substances being separated."},{"q":"Three titres cluster within 0.05 cm³ of each other but every one is 0.50 cm³ larger than the true titre because the burette was misread the same way each time. Describe these results using the words precise and accurate.","a":"They are precise, because they cluster closely, but not accurate, because each is far from the true value. A systematic error like this is not removed by repeating and averaging."},{"q":"In Required practical 4, a student investigates how the mass of zinc powder added affects the temperature rise of copper sulfate solution. What are the independent and dependent variables?","a":"Independent variable: the mass of zinc powder added. Dependent variable: the maximum temperature rise of the solution."},{"q":"Why is a mean calculated from repeat readings rather than one reading being used on its own?","a":"Repeating and taking a mean reduces the effect of random error, which makes individual readings vary unpredictably, and it lets an anomalous result be spotted."}]},"room":{"facts":["GCSE Chemistry 8462 is examined by two written papers: Paper 1 covers topics 4.1 to 4.5 and Paper 2 covers topics 4.6 to 4.10. Each paper is 1 hour 45 minutes, carries 100 marks and is worth 50 % of the GCSE, and working scientifically is assessed on both.","Both papers are available at Foundation Tier, entry code 8462F, and Higher Tier, entry code 8462H. Questions drawing on the knowledge and understanding gained from the eight required practical activities count for at least 15 % of the marks for the qualification, and mathematics for at least 20 %.","The eight required practicals for GCSE Chemistry: 1 preparing a pure, dry sample of a soluble salt from an insoluble oxide or carbonate; 2 titration of a strong acid and a strong alkali (chemistry only); 3 electrolysis of aqueous solutions with inert electrodes; 4 temperature changes in reacting solutions; 5 the effect of concentration on rate by gas volume and by colour or turbidity; 6 paper chromatography and Rf values; 7 chemical tests to identify ions (chemistry only); 8 analysis and purification of water samples.","The independent variable is the one you change or select; the dependent variable is the one you measure for each change; control variables are kept the same so they cannot affect the result.","Accurate means close to the true value. Precise means the repeats cluster closely. Repeatable means the same investigator with the same equipment gets similar results on repeating. Reproducible means a different investigator, or different equipment, gets similar results.","Random error makes readings vary unpredictably and is reduced by taking more readings and calculating a mean. Systematic error shifts every reading from the true value by the same amount and is not removed by averaging.","Mean = sum of the values ÷ number of values. Range = largest value − smallest value. Uncertainty in a mean from repeats = range ÷ 2, written as mean ± uncertainty. The specification (WS 3.4) asks you to use the range of a set of measurements about the mean as a measure of uncertainty; half the range is the usual way of quoting it, so give the range itself if a question asks for it.","Resolution is the smallest change in a quantity that a measuring instrument can detect: 0.1 cm³ for a burette graduated in 0.1 cm³ divisions. Such a burette is read to the nearest half division, 0.05 cm³, which is why a titre is recorded to two decimal places, for example 23.40 cm³.","Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent, both measured from the origin line. Rf has no unit and always lies between 0 and 1.","1 dm³ = 1000 cm³, 1 g = 1000 mg and 1 kJ = 1000 J: convert to the unit the calculation needs before substituting."],"questions":[{"q":"In Required practical 5 a student adds dilute hydrochloric acid to sodium thiosulfate solution and times how long a cross drawn on paper takes to disappear, repeating this with five different concentrations of sodium thiosulfate at the same temperature. Identify the independent variable and the dependent variable in this investigation.","a":"Independent variable: the concentration of the sodium thiosulfate solution. Dependent variable: the time taken for the cross to disappear.","sol":"The independent variable is the one the student changes or selects before each run, which here is the concentration of the sodium thiosulfate solution (1 mark). The dependent variable is the one measured for each change in the independent variable, which here is the time taken for the cross to disappear from view (1 mark). Temperature and the volume of each solution are control variables.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["One of these quantities is fixed by the student before a run starts; the other is not known until the run has finished.","Decide which quantity the student deliberately sets each time, and which one the stopwatch is being used for.","Two separate labelled lines are wanted, each naming one quantity, with no description of the method."],"codes":["WS 2.2"]},{"q":"In Required practical 2 a student titrates sodium hydroxide solution against dilute hydrochloric acid. Identify the apparatus used to transfer exactly 20.0 cm³ of the sodium hydroxide solution into the conical flask, the apparatus used to add the acid a little at a time, and the unit in which both volumes are recorded.","a":"A pipette (used with a pipette filler); a burette; volumes recorded in cm³.","sol":"The fixed 20.0 cm³ portion of alkali is measured with a pipette, used with a pipette filler and allowed to drain into the conical flask (1 mark). The acid is run in from a burette, which is graduated along its length so that any volume added can be read off and the flow can be slowed to one drop near the end point (1 mark). Both volumes are recorded in cubic centimetres, cm³ (1 mark).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["One piece of glassware delivers a single fixed volume only; the other can deliver any volume and lets you read how much has gone in.","Think about which item is calibrated for one volume, and which is graduated along its length in 0.1 cm³ divisions.","Three short items are wanted: two pieces of glassware and one unit of volume."],"codes":["WS 2.3","WS 4.2","WS 4.3"]},{"q":"A burette used in Required practical 2 is graduated in 0.1 cm³ divisions. At the start of a titration the liquid level is on the 0.00 mark, and at the end point the bottom of the meniscus rests exactly on the fourth division below the 23.0 mark. Determine the final burette reading and identify the resolution of the burette.","a":"Final reading: 23.40 cm³ (23.4 cm³ is equally acceptable). Resolution: 0.1 cm³.","sol":"Four divisions of 0.1 cm³ below 23.0 cm³ is 23.0 + 0.4 = 23.4, so the final reading is 23.40 cm³, and 23.4 cm³ earns the same mark; it is read at the bottom of the meniscus with the eye level with the scale, and because a burette is read to the nearest half division, 0.05 cm³, a titre is recorded to two decimal places (1 mark). The resolution is the smallest change the scale can detect, which for divisions of 0.1 cm³ is 0.1 cm³ (1 mark).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Resolution describes the instrument itself, not this one reading: ask what the smallest change its scale can show is.","Work out the reading first, then answer a separate question about the scale itself rather than about this particular reading.","Two values are wanted, both in cm³: one taken from the scale and one describing the scale."],"codes":["WS 2.6"]},{"q":"In Required practical 2 a titre of 22.5 cm³ was recorded, and in Required practical 8 an evaporation left 0.045 g of dissolved solids. Calculate (a) the titre in dm³ and (b) the mass of dissolved solids in mg.","a":"(a) 0.0225 dm³ (b) 45 mg","sol":"(a) 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³, so 22.5 ÷ 1000 = 0.0225 dm³ (1 mark). (b) 1 g = 1000 mg, so 0.045 × 1000 = 45 mg (1 mark).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Both parts depend on what the prefixes in these units mean as powers of ten.","For each part, decide whether the new unit is bigger or smaller than the old one, then multiply or divide by the right power of ten.","Two numbers are wanted, each written with the unit the question asks for."],"codes":["WS 4.4","WS 4.5"]},{"q":"On a chromatogram from Required practical 6, the solvent front has moved 47 mm from the pencil origin line and the centre of a dye spot has moved 30 mm. Calculate the Rf value of the dye and give your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.","a":"Rf = 0.64 (2 significant figures, no unit)","sol":"Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent = 30 ÷ 47 = 0.63829… (1 mark). Both distances were measured to 2 significant figures, so the answer is given to 2 significant figures: Rf = 0.64 (1 mark). Rf is a ratio of two lengths, so it has no unit.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["An Rf value compares how far the dye has travelled with how far the solvent has travelled, both measured from the same line.","Do the division and keep the full calculator value, then decide separately how many figures the measurements themselves justify.","One rounded number is wanted, with no unit attached to it."],"codes":["WS 4.6"]},{"q":"In Required practical 4 a student measures the temperature rise when 25 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution is neutralised by 25 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid in a polystyrene cup. Five repeats give temperature rises of 6.4 °C, 6.6 °C, 6.5 °C, 9.8 °C and 6.5 °C. Identify the anomalous result and calculate the mean temperature rise, excluding it.","a":"Anomalous result: 9.8 °C. Mean temperature rise = 6.5 °C.","sol":"The rise of 9.8 °C does not cluster with the other four readings, so it is anomalous; it should be investigated and, as the product of a poor measurement, excluded before the mean is calculated (1 mark). Mean = (6.4 + 6.6 + 6.5 + 6.5) ÷ 4 (1 mark) = 26.0 ÷ 4 = 6.5 °C (1 mark).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Repeats of the same measurement should cluster together; one of these does not belong to the cluster.","Leave that reading out altogether, then add the ones that remain and divide by how many of them there are.","Two things are wanted: one reading quoted with its unit, and one calculated value with its unit."],"codes":["WS 3.3","WS 3.7"]},{"q":"Three concordant titres recorded in Required practical 2 are 24.80 cm³, 24.60 cm³ and 24.70 cm³. Calculate the mean titre and estimate the uncertainty in the mean from the range of these results.","a":"Mean titre = 24.70 cm³; uncertainty = ±0.10 cm³ (so 24.70 cm³ ± 0.10 cm³).","sol":"Mean = (24.80 + 24.60 + 24.70) ÷ 3 = 74.10 ÷ 3 = 24.70 cm³ (1 mark). Range = 24.80 − 24.60 = 0.20 cm³ (1 mark). Uncertainty = range ÷ 2 = 0.20 ÷ 2 = ±0.10 cm³, so the titre is recorded as 24.70 cm³ ± 0.10 cm³ (1 mark).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two different jobs here: one value that represents the whole set, and one number that says how spread out the set is.","After averaging, find the difference between the largest and the smallest titre, then use the rule that links that spread to the uncertainty in a mean.","Give one volume and one ± value, both in the same unit as the titres."],"codes":["WS 3.3","WS 3.4"]},{"q":"A student carried out Required practical 6 by drawing the origin line in ink, running one chromatogram for each of four food colourings, and lifting the paper out only after the solvent had reached the top edge of the paper. Evaluate this method, using the terms valid and precise.","a":"It is not valid: the ink origin line dissolves and runs with the solvent, mixing with the colourings; and once the solvent has reached the top edge the position of the solvent front cannot be identified, so Rf values cannot be measured. With only one run per colouring there are no repeats, so the precision of the results cannot be judged at all.","sol":"The origin line should be drawn in pencil, which is insoluble; an ink line dissolves in the solvent and runs up the paper, so the substances separated are mixed with the ink and the starting position is lost — the results do not measure what they claim to measure, so the method is not valid (1 mark). Allowing the solvent to reach the top edge means the solvent front can no longer be located, so the distance moved by the solvent, and therefore the Rf value, cannot be determined (1 mark). Running each colouring only once means there is a single result for each, so the spread of repeats — and hence the precision — cannot be judged, and repeatability has not been demonstrated (1 mark).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the origin line is for, and what the solvent would do to a line drawn in something it can dissolve.","Then ask whether both of the distances an Rf value needs can still be measured once the solvent has run off the edge of the paper.","Finish by commenting on how many results exist for each colouring and what that allows you to say about the spread of repeats."],"codes":["WS 2.7","WS 4.1"]},{"q":"In Required practical 5 a student plots the volume of carbon dioxide collected against time as small marble chips react with dilute hydrochloric acid. The curve rises steeply at first, becomes less steep, and is horizontal after 90 s. The curve passes through the point (30 s, 48 cm³). Describe what the shape of the curve shows about the rate of reaction, and calculate the mean rate of reaction over the first 30 s.","a":"The rate is fastest at the start and falls as the reaction goes on, becoming zero after 90 s. Mean rate over the first 30 s = 48 ÷ 30 = 1.6 cm³/s.","sol":"The steepness of the curve at any point is the rate at that moment, so a curve that is steepest at the start and becomes less steep shows that the rate of reaction is greatest at the start and decreases as the acid is used up (1 mark). The horizontal section after 90 s shows that no more gas is being produced, so the rate is zero and the reaction has finished (1 mark). Mean rate over the first 30 s = volume of gas ÷ time = 48 cm³ ÷ 30 s = 1.6 cm³/s (1 mark).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The steepness of a volume–time curve at any moment is the rate of reaction at that moment.","Compare the steepness early on with the steepness later, and then decide what a completely flat section means for the gas being given off.","For the number, divide the volume collected by the time taken over the section named, and give the unit."],"codes":["WS 3.2","WS 3.3","WS 3.5"]},{"q":"In Required practical 3 a student electrolyses aqueous copper chloride solution and aqueous sodium chloride solution using inert electrodes. Suggest a hypothesis the student could test about the product formed at the cathode, and predict the cathode product for each solution.","a":"Hypothesis: the metal is produced at the cathode only when it is less reactive than hydrogen; otherwise hydrogen is produced. Copper chloride solution: copper. Sodium chloride solution: hydrogen.","sol":"A suitable hypothesis is that at the cathode the metal will be discharged if it is less reactive than hydrogen, and hydrogen will be discharged if the metal is more reactive than hydrogen (1 mark). Copper is less reactive than hydrogen, so copper is deposited on the cathode from copper chloride solution (1 mark). Sodium is more reactive than hydrogen, so hydrogen is produced at the cathode from sodium chloride solution (1 mark).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The rule for the negative electrode in an aqueous solution compares the metal with hydrogen.","Write the hypothesis as one testable statement about which of the two is discharged, then apply that statement to each solution in turn.","Three items are wanted: one sentence, then one named product for each of the two solutions."],"codes":["WS 2.1","WS 1.2"]},{"q":"In Required practical 8 a student is asked to compare the dissolved solids in the water of a river. Describe how to collect a representative sample of the river water, and identify one hazard when that sample is purified by distillation, together with a precaution that reduces the risk.","a":"Take samples from several different points along the river, at the same depth, in clean containers, and combine them to give one representative sample. Hazard: the hot apparatus and boiling water during distillation. Precaution: wear eye protection and use tongs or a clamp, keeping hands away from the hot glassware.","sol":"Collect water from several different points along the river rather than from one convenient spot, using clean containers and sampling at the same depth and on the same day (1 mark). Combine the samples, or test each and take a mean, so that the result is typical of the river as a whole and is not biased by one unusual place (1 mark). A hazard in the distillation is the boiling water and the hot glassware or heater, which can scald or burn (1 mark). The risk is reduced by wearing eye protection, clamping the apparatus securely and handling hot items with tongs rather than by hand (1 mark).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["One scoop taken from the nearest point on the bank may not be typical of the whole river.","Describe how many samples are taken and where, and what is done with them, before anything is measured.","Then give one specific danger of the heating step and one specific action that reduces it — 'be careful' earns nothing."],"codes":["WS 2.5","WS 2.4"]},{"q":"A white solid tested in Required practical 7 dissolves in water. Student A suggests it is calcium chloride and Student B suggests it is magnesium chloride. Adding sodium hydroxide solution to the solution gives a white precipitate that does not dissolve in excess sodium hydroxide; a flame test on the solid gives an orange-red flame; and adding dilute nitric acid followed by silver nitrate solution gives a white precipitate. Determine which hypothesis the results better support, and explain your answer.","a":"Student A: the solid is calcium chloride. The orange-red flame shows calcium ions; the white precipitate with sodium hydroxide fits either hypothesis, so it does not separate them; the white precipitate with silver nitrate shows chloride ions, which both hypotheses predict.","sol":"The results better support Student A's hypothesis that the solid is calcium chloride (1 mark). The orange-red flame colour identifies calcium ions, and magnesium compounds do not give this flame colour, so this observation is what separates the two hypotheses (1 mark). The white precipitate with sodium hydroxide that does not dissolve in excess is given by both calcium and magnesium ions, so it does not distinguish between them (1 mark). The white precipitate formed with silver nitrate in the presence of dilute nitric acid shows that chloride ions are present, which both hypotheses had already predicted (1 mark).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Only one of the three tests can tell these two metal ions apart; find it before writing anything.","Go through each observation in turn and ask whether both hypotheses predict it, or only one of them.","Name the compound first, then comment on each of the three observations separately, saying what it does or does not show."],"codes":["WS 3.6","WS 3.5"]},{"q":"Chlorine is added to drinking water in the United Kingdom to sterilise it. Chlorine gas is toxic. Evaluate the use of chlorine for sterilising potable water, and suggest why some people judge this risk very differently from the measured risk.","a":"Benefit: chlorine kills microbes so the water is safe to drink and waterborne disease is prevented, and only a very small measured concentration is needed. Hazard: chlorine is toxic in larger amounts. Perception: the risk is imposed rather than chosen and the hazard is invisible, so it feels greater than the measured risk; data on the effects of very long-term, low-level exposure is incomplete, so science alone cannot settle the argument.","sol":"Benefit: sterilising with chlorine kills microbes in the water supply, making it potable and preventing waterborne disease, and the concentration used is very small (1 mark). Hazard: chlorine is toxic, so a fault that allowed a high concentration into the supply would be harmful (1 mark). Perception: the risk is imposed on everyone rather than chosen voluntarily, and the hazard is invisible and unfamiliar, so people judge it to be larger than the measured risk shows it to be (1 mark). Judgement: on the data, the benefit of preventing disease greatly outweighs the very small measured risk, although data on very long-term low-level exposure is incomplete, and whether a substance should be added to everyone's water is partly an ethical question that data alone cannot answer (1 mark).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Start from what the chlorine is actually there to do, and what would happen to the water supply without it.","Balance that against what makes chlorine hazardous, then think about how this risk reaches people compared with a risk they choose for themselves.","Four separate points are wanted: a benefit, a hazard, a reason the perception differs, and an overall judgement that also says what the data cannot settle."],"codes":["WS 1.4","WS 1.5","WS 1.3"]},{"q":"A newspaper reports that a school has discovered a catalyst that doubles the rate of the reaction between sodium thiosulfate and dilute hydrochloric acid used in Required practical 5. Explain why other scientists would not accept this claim until it had been peer reviewed, and explain what would have to happen before the accepted explanation of how catalysts work was changed.","a":"Peer review means scientists in the same field check the method, data and conclusions before publication; it helps detect false claims and establish a consensus about which claims are valid. A newspaper report is not peer reviewed, so it may be oversimplified, inaccurate or biased. The accepted explanation would change only if other investigators repeated the work, obtained the same data, and that data could not be explained by the current theory.","sol":"Peer review is the checking of a study's methods, data and conclusions by other scientists working in the same field before it is published (1 mark), and it helps to detect false claims and to establish a consensus about which claims should be regarded as valid (1 mark). Reports of scientific developments in newspapers and other popular media are not peer reviewed, so they may be oversimplified, inaccurate or biased, and a single unchecked result from one school is not evidence enough (1 mark). An accepted scientific explanation is changed only when other investigators repeat the work, obtain reproducible data, and that new data cannot be explained by the existing theory — at which point the theory is revised or replaced (1 mark).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Think about who looks at a piece of work between it being finished and it being published in a journal.","Then contrast that route into print with the route a newspaper story takes, and say what can go wrong on the second one.","The last part is about evidence, not about this one school: describe the conditions under which an established explanation gets replaced."],"codes":["WS 1.6","WS 1.1"]},{"q":"Describe how you would investigate the effect of the concentration of dilute hydrochloric acid on the rate of its reaction with small marble chips, using the volume of gas produced as the measure of rate (Required practical 5). Your answer should include the variables, the method and how the results would be used to reach a conclusion. [6 marks]","a":"Independent variable: concentration of the acid (for example 0.5, 1.0, 1.5 and 2.0 mol/dm³). Dependent variable: volume of carbon dioxide collected in a gas syringe, recorded every 10 s. Control variables: mass and size of the marble chips, volume of acid, and temperature. Repeat each concentration, discard anomalies, take means, plot volume against time for each concentration and compare the initial gradients, then plot rate against concentration to conclude.","sol":"This is a levels-marked extended response. A Level 3 (5–6 marks) answer is coherent and logically ordered and covers all three parts. Variables: the independent variable is the concentration of the hydrochloric acid, made up at several values such as 0.5, 1.0, 1.5 and 2.0 mol/dm³; the dependent variable is the volume of carbon dioxide collected; the control variables are the mass and size of the marble chips, the volume of acid used and the temperature, each kept the same because each of them would otherwise change the rate and make the comparison unfair. Method: measure a fixed mass of small marble chips into a conical flask, add a fixed volume of the first acid, immediately fit the bung and delivery tube to a gas syringe and start the stopwatch, and record the volume of gas every 10 s until no more gas is produced; repeat twice more at that concentration and then repeat the whole procedure at each of the other concentrations. Using the results: for each concentration plot volume of gas against time, draw a tangent at t = 0 s and take its gradient as the initial rate, or use the time taken to collect a fixed volume of gas; repeats allow an anomalous result to be identified and excluded and a mean to be calculated; plotting mean rate against concentration shows whether rate increases with concentration and supports the conclusion. A Level 2 (3–4 marks) answer makes relevant points with gaps, for example naming the variables and describing the method but not saying how the results are processed. A Level 1 (1–2 marks) answer is fragmentary, such as 'change the concentration and measure the gas', with no apparatus, no control variables and no analysis.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Plan the answer in three blocks before writing: the variables, then what is actually done at the bench, then what happens to the numbers afterwards.","For each control variable you choose, be ready to say why leaving it uncontrolled would change the rate, and include repeats in the method.","Write in a logical order with quantities and named apparatus, and finish with the graph you would draw and the conclusion it would let you reach."],"codes":["WS 3.8","WS 2.2","WS 3.1"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How is the uncertainty in a mean estimated from a set of repeat titres?","a":"As half the range of the titres: uncertainty = range ÷ 2."},{"q":"What does the resolution of a burette mean?","a":"The smallest change in volume its scale can detect — 0.1 cm³ for a burette graduated in 0.1 cm³ divisions."},{"q":"Which type of error is not removed by repeating a measurement and taking a mean?","a":"Systematic error, because every reading differs from the true value by the same amount."},{"q":"Write the equation used to calculate an Rf value.","a":"Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent, both measured from the origin line."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Independent, dependent and control variables","back":"Independent: the variable the investigator changes or selects. Dependent: the variable measured for each change in the independent variable. Control: variables kept the same so they cannot affect the dependent variable.","code":"WS 2.2","open":false},{"front":"Accurate, precise, repeatable, reproducible","back":"Accurate: close to the true value. Precise: the repeats cluster closely. Repeatable: the same investigator and equipment give similar results on repeating. Reproducible: a different investigator, or different equipment, gives similar results.","code":"WS 3.7","open":false},{"front":"Random error, systematic error and an anomalous result","back":"Random error: readings vary unpredictably; reduce it by repeating and taking a mean. Systematic error: every reading is out by the same amount, so averaging does not remove it. Anomalous result: one outside the cluster; investigate, then exclude it.","code":"WS 3.7","open":false},{"front":"Mean and range of a set of repeats","back":"Mean = sum of the values ÷ number of values. Range = largest value − smallest value. Both are worked out only from the results kept after any anomalous result has been excluded.","code":"WS 3.3","open":false},{"front":"Uncertainty in a mean from repeat readings","back":"WS 3.4 uses the range of repeats about the mean as the measure of uncertainty; half the range is how it is quoted: mean ± range ÷ 2, in the readings' unit. A wider spread means more uncertainty.","code":"WS 3.4","open":false},{"front":"Resolution of a measuring instrument","back":"The smallest change the instrument can detect: a burette graduated in 0.1 cm³ divisions is read to the nearest 0.05 cm³, so titres are recorded to two decimal places; a thermometer marked in 0.1 °C reads to 0.1 °C.","code":"WS 2.6","open":false},{"front":"Give one piece of apparatus used to measure the volume of gas produced in Required practical 5","back":"A gas syringe connected to the flask by a bung and delivery tube; an inverted measuring cylinder full of water in a trough; an inverted burette full of water.","code":"WS 2.3","open":true},{"front":"Hazard and risk","back":"A hazard is something that could cause harm, such as irritant dilute acid or a hot evaporating basin. Risk is the chance of that harm happening; reduce it with a named control such as eye protection or tongs.","code":"WS 2.4","open":false},{"front":"A valid method","back":"One that measures what it claims to measure: the other variables are controlled, the technique does not disturb the quantity being measured, and enough repeats are taken to judge the spread of the results.","code":"WS 2.7","open":false},{"front":"Hypothesis","back":"A testable statement, built on a scientific idea, that explains an observation and predicts what will happen — for example that hydrogen forms at the cathode when the metal is more reactive than hydrogen.","code":"WS 2.1","open":false},{"front":"Model (in science)","back":"A simplified representation — a particle diagram, a reaction profile, an equation or a rule — used to explain observations and make predictions, such as predicting the products of electrolysing an aqueous solution. Every model has limitations.","code":"WS 1.2","open":false},{"front":"Why an accepted theory is changed","back":"New data from experiments or observations cannot be explained by it, so it is revised or replaced — as when alpha-particle scattering replaced the plum pudding model with the nuclear model of the atom.","code":"WS 1.1","open":false},{"front":"Measured risk and perceived risk","back":"Measured risk comes from data; perceived risk is how large the danger feels. Imposed, unfamiliar or invisible hazards, such as chlorine added to tap water, feel riskier than chosen, familiar ones.","code":"WS 1.5","open":false},{"front":"Peer review","back":"Other scientists in the same field check a study's methods and conclusions before publication. It helps detect false claims and establish a consensus. Media reports are not peer reviewed, so may be oversimplified, inaccurate or biased.","code":"WS 1.6","open":false},{"front":"Significant figures in a calculated answer","back":"Give the answer to the same number of significant figures as the least precise measurement used. A ratio of two lengths each measured to 2 significant figures is quoted to 2 significant figures.","code":"WS 4.6","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Relationships for handling data from the chemistry required practicals","items":["Mean = (sum of the values kept) ÷ (number of values kept) — anomalous results are excluded first","Range = largest value − smallest value, in the same unit as the readings","Uncertainty in a mean from repeats = range ÷ 2, written as mean ± uncertainty (for example 24.70 cm³ ± 0.10 cm³); WS 3.4 asks for the range of a set of measurements about the mean as a measure of uncertainty, and half the range is the usual way of quoting it","Percentage uncertainty = (uncertainty ÷ mean) × 100 %","Percentage change = (change in value ÷ original value) × 100 %","Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent, both measured from the origin line; Rf has no unit","Mean rate of reaction = quantity of product formed ÷ time taken, in cm³/s or g/s","Rate at an instant = gradient of the tangent drawn to the volume–time curve at that instant = change in y ÷ change in x","Gradient of a straight-line graph = change in y ÷ change in x, taken between two points far apart on the line","Concentration of dissolved solids (g/dm³) = mass of residue (g) ÷ volume of sample (dm³)","Unit conversions used constantly: 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³; 1 g = 1000 mg; 1 kJ = 1000 J; 1 minute = 60 s","None of these relationships is printed on the paper, so all must be recalled; the only sheet supplied with both papers is the periodic table"]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: investigating the variables that affect a temperature change","items":["Question: 'A student is investigating how the mass of zinc powder added affects the temperature rise when zinc displaces copper from copper sulfate solution (Required practical 4). Describe an investigation the student could carry out, including the variables, the method and how the results would be used. [6 marks]'","Level 3 (5–6 marks): Independent variable: the mass of zinc powder added, using several values such as 0.5 g, 1.0 g, 1.5 g, 2.0 g and 2.5 g, weighed on a balance reading to 0.01 g. Dependent variable: the maximum temperature rise of the solution, found by recording the starting temperature and the highest temperature reached with a thermometer reading to 0.1 °C. Control variables: the volume and concentration of the copper sulfate solution (for example 25 cm³ of 0.5 mol/dm³ measured with a measuring cylinder), the starting temperature, and the same insulated polystyrene cup with a lid, because each of these also affects the temperature change and would otherwise make the comparison unfair. Method: place 25 cm³ of copper sulfate solution in the polystyrene cup standing in a beaker for support, record the starting temperature, add the weighed zinc powder, stir with the thermometer and record the highest temperature reached; repeat the whole run twice more for each mass. Using the results: calculate the temperature rise for each run, identify and exclude any anomalous value, calculate a mean rise for each mass and estimate the uncertainty as range ÷ 2, then plot mean temperature rise against mass of zinc. The conclusion is read from the graph: the rise increases with mass until the copper sulfate is used up, after which extra zinc gives no further rise.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): relevant points with gaps or weak structure — for example naming the independent and dependent variables and describing a workable method, but not stating which variables are controlled and why, or not explaining how the repeats, the mean and the graph lead to a conclusion.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): fragmentary — for example 'add different amounts of zinc to copper sulfate and measure the temperature', with no quantities, no named apparatus, no control variables and no analysis.","What a bottom-level answer misses: the specific quantities and apparatus that make the method repeatable by someone else; the named control variables with a reason for each; repeats, the mean and the treatment of anomalous results; and a clear statement of the graph to be plotted and the conclusion it supports."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"The working-scientifically words a chemistry mark scheme expects","items":["Power and limitations of science — data can show what happens and by how much, but cannot by itself decide what ought to be done, and it may be uncertain, incomplete or not available yet. Whether a technology should be used, who benefits and who carries the risk are ethical questions, answered by weighing both sides (WS 1.3).","Applications of science and their implications — explain the chemistry behind an everyday or industrial use, then evaluate it: weigh the personal, social, economic and environmental effects using the data given and say which decision the evidence supports, as for chlorinating drinking water or recycling a metal instead of extracting it (WS 1.4).","Resolution — the smallest change an instrument can detect (0.1 cm³ on a burette, 0.1 °C on a thermometer, 0.01 g on a balance). A burette is graduated in 0.1 cm³ divisions but read to the nearest 0.05 cm³, so titres are recorded to two decimal places. Not the same as accuracy.","Concordant titres — titres within 0.10 cm³ of one another; only these are used to calculate a mean titre, and the rough titre is discarded.","Anomalous result — a result outside the cluster of repeats. Examine it for a cause and exclude it from the mean if it came from a poor measurement.","Translating between a table and a graph — read a value off the line or curve and quote it with the unit the table uses, or take a pair of values from the table and say where that point belongs, then put the shape into words. Nothing new is measured: the same results are shown in another form (WS 3.2).","Patterns, trends and conclusions — name the pattern ('the volume of gas rises quickly at first and then levels off'), quote the figures that show it, and make the conclusion answer the question asked. Claim no more than the data covers, and keep any prediction inside the range measured (WS 3.5).","Valid — measures what it claims to measure, with the other variables controlled. 'Fair test' is worth less in an answer than a named control variable and a reason.","Categoric and continuous data — flame colours and names of metals are categoric, so use a bar chart; volume of gas against time is continuous, so use a line graph with a curve of best fit.","SI units and prefixes — g, mg, kg; cm³, dm³; J, kJ; s. Convert before substituting: 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³, 1 g = 1000 mg. Powers of ten: kilo ×10³, centi ×10⁻², milli ×10⁻³, micro ×10⁻⁶, nano ×10⁻⁹.","Relating data to a hypothesis — say how far the results are consistent with the hypothesis and where they are not, quoting the figures; when two hypotheses are offered, choose the one that explains more of the data and name the result that rules the other one out (WS 3.6).","Sampling — several samples taken from different places, so the results are representative rather than biased by one convenient spot (Required practical 8).","Scientific quantities and how they are determined — a quantity is a number with a unit that comes from a stated measurement or calculation: a concentration determined by titration from the mean titre, a mean rate determined from the gradient of a volume–time graph, an Rf determined from two distances measured from the origin line (WS 4.2)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:08:49Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Every number re-solved independently and every answer key is right (WE1 43 s ± 1 s; WE2 25.15 cm³, ±0.05 cm³, 0.20 %; WE3 4.2/9.6 = 0.4375 → Rf 0.44; WE4 34/20 = 1.7 cm³/s; WE5 0.15 g in 0.0500 dm³ = 3.0 g/dm³; Q2 23.0 + 4 × 0.1 = 23.4; Q3 0.0225 dm³ and 45 mg; Q4 30/47 = 0.638 → 0.64; Q5 26.0/4 = 6.5 °C; Q6 74.10/3 = 24.70 cm³ ± 0.10 cm³; Q8 48/30 = 1.6 cm³/s), the definitions match WS 3.7 and WS 2.2 almost word for word, RP1–RP8 are described correctly with RP2 and RP7 flagged chemistry only and RP3 and RP5 correctly called hypothesis-led, question 12's reasoning is sound (the orange-red flame is a positive identification of calcium from the specification's own list, and magnesium is simply not on that list, so 'magnesium does not give this colour' is safe — the white hydroxide precipitate insoluble in excess and the white silver halide precipitate genuinely fail to separate the two hypotheses), no worked-example data is reused in any bank question, room.facts states both papers, 1 h 45, 100 marks, 50 % each, 8462F/8462H, practicals at least 15 % and maths at least 20 %, and every power is a Unicode superscript with no caret anywhere; the only real gap is that five WS statements survive in the lesson prose alone once the 15-card cap bites."}}},"9":{"id":"AQA-8462","topic":"9","family":"science","lesson":{"why":"Both GCSE Chemistry papers test these mathematical skills alongside the chemistry content, because AQA sets maths questions throughout the papers rather than as a separate topic, at a level no lower than Key Stage 3 at Foundation Tier and no lower than Foundation Tier GCSE Mathematics at Higher Tier. Marks in calculation questions are lost far more often through avoidable slips — an equation rearranged wrongly, cm³ never converted to dm³, a missing unit, the wrong number of significant figures, a gradient read off without a triangle — than through a lack of chemical understanding. Mastering standard form for atomic sizes and the Avogadro constant, percentages for yield and atom economy, concentrations in g/dm³ and mol/dm³, moles and gas volumes at room temperature and pressure, means and ranges from repeat titres, Rf values from chromatograms, and rates from the gradients of graphs and tangents gives you the tools for every calculation the specification can set.","idea":[{"h":"Decimal form","t":"Almost every measurement you record in chemistry is a decimal: a titre of 27.30 cm³ read from a burette, a mass of 6.12 g of dried crystals from a balance, an Rf value of 0.37 from a chromatogram, or an energy change of −92 kJ/mol. You must read, write and calculate with decimals confidently, line the decimal points up when adding or subtracting masses and volumes, and keep the number of decimal places that the apparatus justifies — a burette graduated in 0.1 cm³ divisions is read to two decimal places.","code":"MS 1a"},{"h":"Standard form","t":"Chemistry uses numbers far too large or too small to write out in full, so they are written as A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10: an atom has a radius of about 0.1 nm, which is 1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m, the radius of a nucleus is less than 1/10 000 of that, about 1 × 10⁻¹⁴ m, a fine particle (PM2.5) has a diameter between 1 × 10⁻⁷ m and 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m, and (Higher tier) the Avogadro constant is 6.02 × 10²³ per mole. You must convert ordinary numbers to standard form and back, keeping careful track of whether the power of ten is positive or negative.","code":"MS 1b"},{"h":"Ratios, fractions and percentages","t":"More chemistry marks depend on this one skill than on any other. You need percentage by mass in a compound, percentage yield, percentage atom economy, the Rf value of a spot, the approximate 80 % nitrogen and 20 % oxygen of the atmosphere, the surface area to volume ratio of a solid, and (Higher tier) the whole-number mole ratio in a balanced equation. You must simplify a ratio to its lowest whole-number form or to n : 1, convert between fractions, decimals and percentages, and calculate a percentage of a quantity.","code":"MS 1c"},{"h":"Estimates","t":"Before or after a full calculation, round the numbers to one significant figure, do the simple version in your head, and check that the real answer is about that size — a concentration that comes out ten times too large usually means cm³ were never converted to dm³. The specification's work on nanoparticles asks you to compare 'nano' dimensions to typical dimensions of atoms and molecules, and an estimate is all that is wanted: a nanoparticle 100 nm across is about 500 times wider than an atom, whose diameter is about 0.2 nm.","code":"MS 1d"},{"h":"Significant figures","t":"An answer must not be given to more digits than the data used to find it justify, so results are rounded, usually to two or three significant figures in GCSE Chemistry. The specification asks for an appropriate number of significant figures, and repeats the point against the work on chromatography, where you determine Rf values from chromatograms: a concentration worked out from titres recorded to three significant figures is quoted to three, and an Rf value found from two lengths measured to the nearest millimetre is quoted to two. Remember that leading zeros are not significant, so 0.0417 to two significant figures is 0.042.","code":"MS 2a"},{"h":"Arithmetic means","t":"Repeating a measurement and taking the mean reduces the effect of random error. Add all the readings and divide by how many there are, leaving out any anomalous result first — in a titration only concordant titres, those within 0.10 cm³ of each other, are used. The specification also asks you to use the range of a set of measurements about the mean as a measure of uncertainty, so quote the range (largest value − smallest value) alongside the mean of repeat titres or repeat temperature changes.","code":"MS 2b"},{"h":"Frequency tables, bar charts and histograms","t":"Information about resources, pollutants and waste is presented in frequency tables, bar charts and histograms, and the specification asks you to extract and interpret it. Read a frequency straight from the table, identify the modal category or modal class as the one with the highest frequency, and calculate a percentage of the total. Use a bar chart, with separated bars, for discrete categories such as types of waste material, and a histogram for continuous data grouped into class intervals with no gaps between them.","code":"MS 2c"},{"h":"Order of magnitude","t":"An order of magnitude calculation gives only the rough size of an answer, as a power of ten. The specification flags this skill in three places. (Higher tier) As the pH of a solution decreases by one unit the hydrogen ion concentration increases by a factor of 10, so a fall of three pH units is a rise of 10³, a thousand times. Orders of magnitude are also used to judge the significance of data about resources, and to compare nano dimensions with the size of atoms and molecules.","code":"MS 2h"},{"h":"Mathematical symbols","t":"Chemistry uses symbols beyond the four operations: = (equals), < (less than), > (greater than), << (much less than), >> (much greater than), ∝ (directly proportional to) and ~ (approximately equal to). The specification asks you to understand and use them, and flags the skill against the Higher Tier work on moles, but they are used wherever quantities are compared: the concentration of hydrogen ions in a weak acid << that in a strong acid of the same concentration, or a mean rate of reaction ∝ concentration of acid when doubling the concentration doubles the rate.","code":"MS 3a"},{"h":"Changing the subject of an equation","t":"Rearranging is the single most examined algebra skill in chemistry. From concentration = mass of solute ÷ volume of solution come mass of solute = concentration × volume and volume = mass ÷ concentration. (Higher tier) From moles = mass ÷ Mr come mass = moles × Mr and Mr = mass ÷ moles, and from concentration in mol/dm³ = moles ÷ volume in dm³ come moles = concentration × volume and volume = moles ÷ concentration. Isolate the unknown by doing the same operation to both sides before any numbers go in.","code":"MS 3b"},{"h":"Substituting numerical values with units","t":"Put the numbers into the rearranged equation with their units attached, and make the units match before you divide or multiply: a concentration in g/dm³ or mol/dm³ needs the volume in dm³, so a volume in cm³ must be divided by 1000 first (1 dm³ = 1000 cm³). The specification flags this skill against the Higher Tier work on reacting masses in balanced equations, on concentrations of solutions in mol/dm³ and on the volumes of gases. Writing the unit beside every substituted value is the quickest way to catch a conversion you have forgotten.","code":"MS 3c"},{"h":"Translating between graphical and numeric form","t":"The same information appears sometimes as a table of numbers and sometimes as a graph, and you must move between them: read the volume of gas collected at a given time off a rate curve or out of a table, state the time at which the volume stops increasing and the reaction has finished, or interpret an instrumental result given in chart or tabular form alongside a reference set, as in flame emission spectroscopy.","code":"MS 4a"},{"h":"Linear relationships, y = mx + c","t":"A straight-line graph — the volume of gas collected against time while a reaction proceeds at a steady rate, or the falling mass of a flask as carbon dioxide escapes — is described by y = mx + c, where m is the gradient and c is the y-intercept, the value of y where the line crosses the y-axis at x = 0. Recognising this form lets you say what the gradient measures (here, a rate) and what the intercept means (here, the starting mass).","code":"MS 4b"},{"h":"Plotting two variables","t":"The independent variable, the one you changed or selected, goes on the x-axis; the dependent variable, the one you measured, goes on the y-axis. Label both axes with the quantity and its unit, choose evenly spaced scales in sensible steps that use more than half the grid, plot each point accurately, and draw a line or smooth curve of best fit. The specification names plotting the quantity of product formed or of reactant used up against time.","code":"MS 4c"},{"h":"Slope and intercept of a linear graph","t":"To determine the gradient of a straight line, pick two points that are far apart and lie on the line, draw the triangle between them, and divide the change in y by the change in x. Give the gradient the unit that the two axes give it: cm³/s for a volume of gas against time, g/s for a falling mass. The intercept is read where the line crosses the y-axis, and a negative gradient simply means y falls as x rises.","code":"MS 4d"},{"h":"Tangents to a curve","t":"A rate curve is steep at the start and flattens out, so its gradient changes: the slope of a tangent drawn to touch the curve at one point measures the rate of reaction at that moment. Draw the tangent with a ruler so that it touches the curve at the time asked for and follows the curve's direction there. (Higher tier) You then calculate the gradient of that tangent, change in quantity ÷ change in time, taking two points far apart on the tangent itself, never on the curve.","code":"MS 4e"},{"h":"Two- and three-dimensional representations","t":"Chemical structures are three-dimensional but are drawn on flat paper, so you must read and draw dot and cross diagrams, ball and stick models, two- and three-dimensional diagrams of giant structures, and displayed structural formulae. Each representation leaves something out, and you should be able to state its limitations: a ball and stick model shows gaps between particles that are not really there and does not show the correct relative sizes, while a dot and cross diagram shows which atoms the electrons come from but nothing about shape.","code":"MS 5b"},{"h":"Areas and volumes","t":"You need the area of a rectangle (length × width), the area of a triangle (½ × base × height), and for a cube of side s the surface area, 6 × s², and the volume, s³, so that the surface area to volume ratio is 6 ÷ s. This is the mathematics behind two pieces of chemistry: nanoparticles have very high surface area to volume ratios, and as the side of a cube decreases by a factor of 10 the ratio increases by a factor of 10; and breaking a reacting solid into smaller pieces raises the ratio and so the rate.","code":"MS 5c"}],"examples":[{"title":"Standard form: the radius of an atom","setup":"A sodium atom has a radius of about 0.000 000 000 19 m. Write this radius in standard form and give the unit.","steps":["Keep the significant digits and put the decimal point after the first of them: the digits 1 and 9 give 1.9, which lies between 1 and 10.","Count how many places the decimal point has to move from its place in 0.000 000 000 19 to reach 1.9: it moves 10 places to the right.","Moving the point to the right means the original number is smaller than one, so the power of ten is negative: 10⁻¹⁰.","Put the two parts together and add the unit: the radius of a sodium atom is 1.9 × 10⁻¹⁰ m."],"cues":["Which number between 1 and 10 do these digits give?","How many places must the decimal point travel?","Is the power of ten positive or negative for a number smaller than one?","How are the two parts and the unit written together?"]},{"title":"Percentage yield of a salt preparation","setup":"In a preparation of magnesium sulfate crystals the maximum theoretical mass of product is 8.50 g. The student's dried crystals have a mass of 6.12 g. Calculate the percentage yield of this reaction.","steps":["Write the equation: percentage yield = mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product × 100.","Decide which mass is which: the dried crystals, 6.12 g, are the mass actually made, and 8.50 g is the maximum theoretical mass.","Substitute the two masses: percentage yield = (6.12 ÷ 8.50) × 100.","Divide first, then multiply: 6.12 ÷ 8.50 = 0.720, and 0.720 × 100 = 72.0 %."],"cues":["Which equation turns two masses into a percentage yield?","Which of the two masses was actually obtained?","Which mass goes on the top of the fraction?","What is the final percentage, and does it need a unit?"]},{"title":"Rearranging to find the mass of solute in a solution","setup":"A solution of sodium chloride has a concentration of 40 g/dm³. Calculate the mass of sodium chloride dissolved in 250 cm³ of this solution.","steps":["Change the subject: concentration = mass of solute ÷ volume of solution, so mass of solute = concentration × volume of solution.","Convert the volume to the unit the concentration uses: 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³, so 250 ÷ 1000 = 0.250 dm³.","Substitute with the units in place: mass of solute = 40 g/dm³ × 0.250 dm³.","Multiply and give the unit: 40 × 0.250 = 10, so the mass of sodium chloride is 10 g."],"cues":["Which quantity must become the subject of the equation?","Which unit of volume does a concentration in g/dm³ require?","What goes into the rearranged equation now?","What is the mass, and what unit does it carry?"]},{"title":"(Higher tier) Rate of reaction from the gradient of a tangent","setup":"A student followed a reaction by collecting the hydrogen given off in a gas syringe and plotted the volume of hydrogen against time. A tangent drawn to the curve at 30 s passes through the points (10 s, 8 cm³) and (50 s, 56 cm³). Determine the rate of reaction at 30 s.","steps":["Find the change in volume between the two points on the tangent: 56 − 8 = 48 cm³.","Find the change in time between the same two points: 50 − 10 = 40 s.","The gradient of the tangent at 30 s is the change in volume divided by the change in time: 48 ÷ 40 = 1.2.","Give the gradient its unit and say what it measures: the rate of reaction at 30 s is 1.2 cm³/s."],"cues":["How far does the volume rise between the two points?","How much time passes between the same two points?","Which of the two changes is divided by the other?","What unit does this gradient carry?"]},{"title":"Rf value from a chromatogram","setup":"On a chromatogram the solvent front moved 7.5 cm from the origin line. The centre of one spot moved 4.8 cm from the origin line. Calculate the Rf value of this spot.","steps":["Write the equation: Rf = distance moved by the substance ÷ distance moved by the solvent, both measured from the origin line.","Match each distance to the equation: the substance moved 4.8 cm and the solvent moved 7.5 cm.","Substitute and divide: Rf = 4.8 ÷ 7.5 = 0.64.","State the answer to two significant figures, as the two lengths were measured: the Rf value is 0.64, and because it is one length divided by another it has no unit and lies between 0 and 1."],"cues":["Which two distances does an Rf value compare?","Which of the two distances is the larger one?","Which distance is divided by which?","Does an Rf value carry a unit, and what range must it lie in?"]},{"title":"Surface area to volume ratio of a reacting solid","setup":"A cube of calcium carbonate has sides 5.0 cm long. Calculate its surface area and its volume, and give the surface area to volume ratio in the form n : 1.","steps":["Find the area of one square face: 5.0 × 5.0 = 25 cm².","A cube has six identical faces, so surface area = 6 × 25 = 150 cm².","Volume of a cube = side³ = 5.0 × 5.0 × 5.0 = 125 cm³.","Divide both parts of the ratio by the volume so that the right-hand part becomes one: 150 ÷ 125 = 1.2, so the surface area to volume ratio is 1.2 : 1."],"cues":["What is the area of a single face of this cube?","How many faces does the total surface area cover?","Which formula gives the space the cube fills?","What must both parts be divided by to reach the form n : 1?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What do m and c represent in the equation y = mx + c?","a":"m is the gradient of the line; c is the y-intercept, the value of y where the line crosses the y-axis."},{"q":"How do you find the range of a set of repeat titres, and what is it used for?","a":"Range = largest value − smallest value. It is quoted about the mean as a measure of the uncertainty in the mean."},{"q":"How do you make an order of magnitude calculation?","a":"Round each number to one significant figure, do the simplified calculation, and give the result as a power of ten."},{"q":"A concentration is given in g/dm³ but the volume is measured in cm³. What must you do before substituting?","a":"Convert the volume to dm³ by dividing by 1000, because 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³."}]},"room":{"facts":["GCSE Chemistry 8462 is examined by two written papers: Paper 1 covers topics 4.1 to 4.5 and Paper 2 covers topics 4.6 to 4.10. Each paper is a written exam of 1 hour 45 minutes, carries 100 marks and is worth 50 % of the GCSE.","Both papers are available at Foundation Tier, entry code 8462F, and at Higher Tier, entry code 8462H. Mathematics is assessed on every paper and is worth at least 20 % of the marks for the qualification; Foundation Tier maths demand is no lower than Key Stage 3 and Higher Tier no lower than Foundation Tier GCSE Mathematics. A periodic table is supplied with both papers, but no equation sheet, so every equation below must be recalled.","Standard form: a number written as A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10 and n a whole number, positive for large numbers and negative for small ones. An atom has a radius of about 0.1 nm = 1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m and the radius of a nucleus is less than 1/10 000 of that, about 1 × 10⁻¹⁴ m; (HT only) the Avogadro constant is 6.02 × 10²³ per mole.","Percentage yield = mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product × 100. Percentage atom economy = Mr of the desired product from the equation ÷ sum of the Mr of all the reactants from the equation × 100.","Percentage by mass of an element in a compound = (Ar of the element × number of its atoms in the formula) ÷ Mr of the compound × 100.","Concentration of a solution = mass of solute ÷ volume of solution, in g/dm³; rearranged, mass of solute = concentration × volume, and volume = mass of solute ÷ concentration.","(HT only) Moles = mass ÷ Mr, so mass = moles × Mr. Concentration in mol/dm³ = moles of solute ÷ volume of solution in dm³, so moles = concentration × volume.","(HT only) The volume of one mole of any gas at room temperature and pressure (20 °C and 1 atmosphere) is 24 dm³, so volume of gas at RTP in dm³ = moles × 24.","Rf = distance moved by the substance ÷ distance moved by the solvent, both measured from the origin line. Rf has no unit and always lies between 0 and 1.","Mean rate of reaction = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken, or quantity of product formed ÷ time taken, in g/s or cm³/s (HT only: in mol/s). The rate at a specific time is the slope of the tangent drawn to the curve at that time.","For a straight-line graph, y = mx + c, where m is the gradient (change in y ÷ change in x) and c is the y-intercept, the value of y at x = 0.","For a cube of side s: surface area = 6 × s², volume = s³, so the surface area to volume ratio = 6 ÷ s. Area of a rectangle = length × width; area of a triangle = ½ × base × height.","(HT only) As the pH of a solution decreases by one unit, the hydrogen ion concentration increases by a factor of 10.","Unit conversions: 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³, 1 g = 1000 mg, 1 kJ = 1000 J, 1 nm = 1 × 10⁻⁹ m. Convert before substituting, never after."],"questions":[{"q":"(a) A fine particle (PM2.5) has a diameter of 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m. Give this diameter as an ordinary decimal number. (b) A student calculates a mean rate of reaction as 0.083333 cm³/s. Give this value to 2 significant figures.","a":"(a) 0.0000025 m. (b) 0.083 cm³/s.","sol":"(a) The power −6 means the decimal point moves 6 places to the left from its place in 2.5, giving 0.0000025 m. (b) Leading zeros are not significant, so the first two significant figures of 0.083333 are 8 and 3; the next digit is 3, which is less than 5, so the 3 stays as it is and the value is 0.083 cm³/s.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what the power of ten in standard form tells you about the size of the number, and about which digits in a number count as significant.","For (a) move the decimal point the number of places the index gives, in the direction that makes the number smaller; for (b) start counting significant figures at the first non-zero digit and look at the digit after the last one you keep.","Your answer gives a decimal beginning with several zeros and carrying the unit m, and a value with two significant digits in cm³/s."],"codes":["MS 1a","MS 1b","MS 2a"]},{"q":"Ammonium nitrate, NH₄NO₃, is used as a fertiliser. Its relative formula mass is 80. The relative atomic mass of nitrogen is 14. Calculate the percentage by mass of nitrogen in ammonium nitrate.","a":"35 %.","sol":"The formula NH₄NO₃ contains 2 nitrogen atoms, so the mass of nitrogen in one formula unit = 2 × 14 = 28. Percentage by mass = mass of the element ÷ Mr of the compound × 100 = 28 ÷ 80 × 100 = 0.35 × 100 = 35 %.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Recall that a percentage by mass compares the mass of one element in the formula with the relative formula mass of the whole compound.","Count how many nitrogen atoms the formula contains and multiply that number by the relative atomic mass of nitrogen before dividing by the relative formula mass and multiplying by 100.","Your answer is a percentage with no decimal places."],"codes":["MS 1c","MS 1a"]},{"q":"A student carried out a titration three times and obtained concordant titres of 24.10 cm³, 24.20 cm³ and 24.15 cm³. Calculate the mean titre and the range of the three titres.","a":"Mean = 24.15 cm³; range = 0.10 cm³.","sol":"Mean = sum of the values ÷ number of values = (24.10 + 24.20 + 24.15) ÷ 3 = 72.45 ÷ 3 = 24.15 cm³. Range = largest value − smallest value = 24.20 − 24.10 = 0.10 cm³. The range is quoted about the mean as a measure of uncertainty, so the mean titre is 24.15 ± 0.05 cm³ if half the range is used.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Recall why a titration is repeated at all, and what the spread of the repeats tells you about the mean.","Add the three titres and divide by how many there are, then subtract the smallest titre from the largest.","Your answer gives two volumes in cm³, one close to the readings themselves and one very much smaller."],"codes":["MS 2b","MS 1a"]},{"q":"The symbols used in GCSE Chemistry are =, <, <<, >>, >, ∝ and ~. (a) State which of these symbols means 'much greater than'. (b) In an investigation the mean rate of reaction was found to be directly proportional to the concentration of the acid. Complete this statement with one symbol from the list: mean rate of reaction ___ concentration of acid.","a":"(a) >> (b) ∝","sol":"(a) 'Much greater than' is written >>, a doubled version of the greater-than sign; 'much less than' is written <<. (b) Directly proportional to is written ∝, so mean rate of reaction ∝ concentration of acid: doubling the concentration doubles the mean rate.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Recall that some of the seven symbols compare the sizes of two quantities while others describe how two quantities change together.","For (a) look for the symbol that doubles an ordinary inequality sign; for (b) choose the one that is not an inequality at all.","Your answer gives two symbols, each of no more than two characters."],"codes":["MS 3a"]},{"q":"(a) Name the type of diagram in which the electrons in the outer shells of the atoms in a covalent molecule are drawn as dots and crosses. (b) Describe one limitation of using a ball and stick model to represent the giant ionic structure of sodium chloride.","a":"(a) A dot and cross diagram. (b) It shows gaps between the ions when there are none in the real structure (or: the ions are not shown at their correct relative sizes; only a tiny part of the giant lattice is shown).","sol":"(a) A dot and cross diagram represents the outer-shell electrons of each atom as dots or crosses so that it is clear which atom each electron came from; it shows the bonding but nothing about shape or size. (b) Any one limitation of a ball and stick model of sodium chloride: it shows gaps between the ions, whereas in the real lattice the ions touch; the balls are not drawn to the correct relative sizes for sodium ions and chloride ions; and it shows only a very small part of the giant structure, which continues in all directions.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Recall the names of the different ways chemists draw a three-dimensional structure on flat paper, and that every one of them leaves something out.","For (a) think about which representation shows where each bonding electron came from; for (b) picture the spacing and the relative sizes of the balls compared with the real ions.","Your answer names one type of diagram and gives one shortcoming of a model in a single sentence."],"codes":["MS 5b"]},{"q":"Iron is extracted in the blast furnace by the reaction Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂. Relative atomic masses: C = 12, O = 16, Fe = 56. (a) Calculate the percentage atom economy of this reaction for producing iron. Give your answer to 3 significant figures. (b) In one run of the process the maximum theoretical mass of iron was 250 kg, but only 212 kg of iron was obtained. Calculate the percentage yield.","a":"(a) 45.9 %. (b) 84.8 %.","sol":"(a) Percentage atom economy = Mr of the desired product from the equation ÷ sum of the Mr of all the reactants from the equation × 100. Desired product: 2Fe = 2 × 56 = 112. Reactants: Fe₂O₃ = (2 × 56) + (3 × 16) = 112 + 48 = 160, and 3CO = 3 × (12 + 16) = 3 × 28 = 84, so the sum of the reactant masses = 160 + 84 = 244. Atom economy = 112 ÷ 244 × 100 = 45.9016… = 45.9 % to 3 significant figures. (b) Percentage yield = mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product × 100 = 212 ÷ 250 × 100 = 0.848 × 100 = 84.8 %.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Recall that atom economy measures how much of the starting material ends up as the product you actually want, while percentage yield compares what was obtained with what the equation says was possible.","For (a) add the relative formula masses of every reactant in the quantities the equation shows, then divide the relative mass of the iron produced by that total before multiplying by 100; for (b) divide the mass obtained by the maximum theoretical mass and multiply by 100.","Your answer gives one percentage below 50 % to three significant figures and one percentage above 80 %."],"codes":["MS 1c","MS 2a","MS 1a"]},{"q":"A student added excess marble chips to dilute hydrochloric acid and measured the volume of carbon dioxide collected. The results were: 0 s, 0 cm³; 20 s, 32 cm³; 40 s, 52 cm³; 60 s, 64 cm³; 80 s, 70 cm³; 100 s, 70 cm³. (a) State the volume of carbon dioxide collected at 40 s. (b) Determine the time at which the reaction finished, and explain how the results show this. (c) The student plots these results as a graph. State which quantity should be plotted on the x-axis and give a reason.","a":"(a) 52 cm³. (b) 80 s, because the volume stops increasing after that (it is still 70 cm³ at 100 s). (c) Time, because it is the independent variable.","sol":"(a) Reading the pair of values from the table, the volume at 40 s is 52 cm³. (b) The volume rises to 70 cm³ at 80 s and is still 70 cm³ at 100 s, so no more gas is being produced after 80 s and the reaction has finished at 80 s. (c) Time is the independent variable, the quantity the student selected the readings at, so it goes on the x-axis; the volume of carbon dioxide collected is the dependent variable, the quantity measured, and goes on the y-axis.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Recall that a table of paired readings holds exactly the same information as the graph drawn from it, and that a reaction has finished once one reactant has been used up.","Read the volume listed against the time asked for, then look down the volume column for the first value that is not larger than the one after it, and decide which of the two quantities the student chose rather than measured.","Your answer gives a volume in cm³, a time in s with a reason drawn from the last two rows, and the name of one quantity with a reason."],"codes":["MS 4a","MS 4c"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Carbon dioxide has a relative formula mass of 44. (a) Calculate the number of moles in 22 g of carbon dioxide. (b) Calculate the volume this amount of carbon dioxide occupies at room temperature and pressure.","a":"(a) 0.50 mol. (b) 12 dm³.","sol":"(a) moles = mass ÷ Mr = 22 ÷ 44 = 0.50 mol. (b) The volume of one mole of any gas at room temperature and pressure is 24 dm³, so volume = moles × 24 = 0.50 × 24 = 12 dm³. Moles and gas volumes at room temperature and pressure are Higher tier only.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Recall the equation linking an amount in moles to a mass in grams, and that equal amounts in moles of any gases occupy the same volume under the same conditions.","Divide the mass by the relative formula mass first, then multiply the amount you obtain by the volume that one mole of any gas occupies at room temperature and pressure.","Your answer gives an amount in mol and a volume in dm³, both smaller than the numbers you started with."],"codes":["MS 3b","MS 3c","MS 1a"]},{"q":"A survey sorted 200 items of household waste by the material they were made from: paper and card 64, glass 30, metal 22, plastic 58, food waste 26. (a) Name the modal category. (b) Calculate the percentage of the items that were plastic. (c) State whether these data should be shown as a bar chart or as a histogram, and give a reason.","a":"(a) Paper and card. (b) 29 %. (c) A bar chart, because the materials are separate discrete categories rather than continuous data grouped into class intervals.","sol":"(a) The modal category is the one with the highest frequency: paper and card, with 64 items, more than any other. (b) Percentage = frequency ÷ total × 100 = 58 ÷ 200 × 100 = 0.29 × 100 = 29 %. (c) A bar chart is used because the five materials are discrete categories with no numerical order between them, so the bars are drawn separated by gaps; a histogram is used only for continuous data grouped into class intervals that run up to the start of the next class with no gaps.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Recall what makes one category in a frequency table the modal one, and what makes data continuous rather than a set of separate groups.","Compare the five frequencies, then divide the plastic frequency by the total number of items and multiply by 100, before deciding whether the five materials can be put in numerical order.","Your answer names one material, gives a percentage below a third, and names one type of chart with a reason that refers to the kind of data."],"codes":["MS 2c","MS 1c"]},{"q":"A student dissolved 24.8 g of copper sulfate in water and made the solution up to 500 cm³. (a) By rounding the mass and the volume to numbers that can be divided without a calculator, determine an estimate of the concentration of the solution in g/dm³. (b) Calculate the concentration of the solution in g/dm³.","a":"(a) About 50 g/dm³. (b) 49.6 g/dm³.","sol":"(a) Round the numbers to values that divide easily: 24.8 g is about 25 g, and 500 cm³ is 0.5 dm³, so the concentration is about 25 ÷ 0.5 = 50 g/dm³. (b) Convert the volume: 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³, so 500 ÷ 1000 = 0.500 dm³. Concentration = mass of solute ÷ volume of solution = 24.8 ÷ 0.500 = 49.6 g/dm³. The calculated value is close to the estimate, which shows that the conversion from cm³ to dm³ has not been missed.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Recall that an estimate is only there to show you the size of answer to expect, and that a concentration in g/dm³ compares a mass with a volume measured in a particular unit.","For (a) replace each number with a nearby one you can divide in your head; for (b) convert the volume from cm³ to dm³ before dividing the mass by it.","Your answer gives two concentrations in g/dm³ that differ by less than one gram per cubic decimetre."],"codes":["MS 1d","MS 3b","MS 3c"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) A solution of hydrochloric acid is diluted and its pH changes from 1 to 4. Calculate how many times smaller the hydrogen ion concentration becomes, and give your answer as an order of magnitude.","a":"1000 times smaller, an order of magnitude of 10³.","sol":"As the pH of a solution decreases by one unit the hydrogen ion concentration increases by a factor of 10, so as the pH increases by one unit the hydrogen ion concentration decreases by a factor of 10. The pH rises from 1 to 4, a change of 4 − 1 = 3 units, so the hydrogen ion concentration falls by 10 × 10 × 10 = 10³ = 1000 times. Strong and weak acids and hydrogen ion concentration are Higher tier only.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Recall the link the specification gives between a change of one pH unit and the hydrogen ion concentration of the solution.","Count how many pH units the solution has moved through, then apply that factor of ten once for each unit.","Your answer is a whole number of thousands, written also as ten raised to a whole-number power."],"codes":["MS 2h","MS 1b"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) In a titration, 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution was exactly neutralised by 23.4 cm³ of hydrochloric acid of concentration 0.100 mol/dm³. The equation is NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. The relative formula mass of NaOH is 40. Calculate the concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution in mol/dm³ and in g/dm³. Give each answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"0.0936 mol/dm³ and 3.74 g/dm³.","sol":"Moles of HCl = concentration × volume in dm³ = 0.100 × (23.4 ÷ 1000) = 0.100 × 0.0234 = 0.00234 mol. The equation shows 1 mol of NaOH reacts with 1 mol of HCl, so moles of NaOH = 0.00234 mol. Concentration of NaOH = moles ÷ volume in dm³ = 0.00234 ÷ (25.0 ÷ 1000) = 0.00234 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.0936 mol/dm³ (3 s.f.). Concentration in g/dm³ = concentration in mol/dm³ × Mr = 0.0936 × 40 = 3.744 = 3.74 g/dm³ (3 s.f.). Titration calculations using concentrations in mol/dm³ and g/dm³ are Higher tier only.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Recall that only one of the two solutions has both its volume and its concentration given, so that is the one whose amount in moles you can find first.","Convert each volume from cm³ to dm³, find the amount in moles of the acid, use the balanced equation to give the amount in moles of the alkali, then divide by the alkali's volume and finally convert from mol/dm³ to g/dm³.","Your answer gives one concentration below 0.1 mol/dm³ and one of a few grams per cubic decimetre, each to three significant figures."],"codes":["MS 3b","MS 3c","MS 1c","MS 2a"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) A student plotted the volume of hydrogen collected against time for a reaction between magnesium and dilute sulfuric acid. A tangent drawn to the curve at 60 s passes through the points (20 s, 18 cm³) and (100 s, 74 cm³). (a) Determine the rate of reaction at 60 s. (b) Explain why the gradient of the curve is smaller at 60 s than at the start of the reaction.","a":"(a) 0.70 cm³/s. (b) The acid has been partly used up, so its concentration has fallen; there are fewer acid particles in the same volume, so the frequency of collisions is lower and the rate is slower.","sol":"(a) Change in volume = 74 − 18 = 56 cm³; change in time = 100 − 20 = 80 s. Gradient of the tangent = change in volume ÷ change in time = 56 ÷ 80 = 0.70, so the rate of reaction at 60 s is 0.70 cm³/s. (b) As the reaction proceeds the sulfuric acid is used up, so the concentration of acid falls. A lower concentration means fewer acid particles in the same volume, so the frequency of collisions between reacting particles falls and the rate of reaction falls with it; the curve therefore becomes less steep, and it flattens completely when a reactant is used up. Calculating the gradient of a tangent as a measure of rate at a specific time is Higher tier only.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Recall that the steepness of a rate curve at one moment is measured by the line that just touches it there, and that rate depends on how often reacting particles meet.","Take the two points that lie on the tangent, not on the curve, and divide the change in volume by the change in time; then think about what has happened to the amount of acid left by 60 s.","Your answer gives a rate of less than 1 cm³/s and an explanation that links a falling concentration to how often particles collide."],"codes":["MS 4e","MS 4d"]},{"q":"Marble chips were added to dilute hydrochloric acid in an open flask on a balance. Carbon dioxide escaped and the mass of the flask and its contents fell. For the first 100 s the graph of mass against time was a straight line passing through (0 s, 152.40 g) and (100 s, 150.00 g). (a) Determine the gradient of the line and give its unit. (b) State the value of the y-intercept and explain what it represents. (c) The y-axis grid is 24 large squares tall. Suggest a suitable scale for the y-axis.","a":"(a) −0.0240 g/s. (b) 152.40 g, the mass of the flask and its contents at the start, when t = 0. (c) 1 large square = 0.10 g, running from 150.00 g at the bottom to 152.40 g at the top.","sol":"(a) Gradient = change in y ÷ change in x = (150.00 − 152.40) ÷ (100 − 0) = −2.40 ÷ 100 = −0.0240 g/s. The gradient is negative because the mass falls as time passes, and its size is the mean rate at which mass is lost. (b) In y = mx + c the intercept c is the value of y when x = 0, which is 152.40 g: the mass of the flask and its contents before any carbon dioxide had escaped. (c) The masses span 152.40 − 150.00 = 2.40 g and the grid is 24 large squares tall, so 2.40 ÷ 24 = 0.10 g per large square fills the grid exactly, with 150.00 g at the bottom of the axis.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Recall that a straight-line graph is summed up by y = mx + c, and that a good scale uses most of the grid in even, easy steps.","Use the two given points to find the change in y over the change in x, read the value of y where x is zero, then share the full range of the masses between the squares available.","Your answer gives a negative gradient in g/s, a mass in g with what it describes, and a scale written as an amount per large square."],"codes":["MS 4b","MS 4d","MS 4c"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Hydrogen reacts with chlorine: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. The bond energies are H–H 436 kJ/mol, Cl–Cl 242 kJ/mol and H–Cl 431 kJ/mol. Calculate the overall energy change of this reaction and state whether it is exothermic or endothermic.","a":"−184 kJ/mol; the reaction is exothermic.","sol":"Energy needed to break the bonds in the reactants = 436 (H–H) + 242 (Cl–Cl) = 678 kJ/mol. Energy released when the bonds in the products are formed = 2 × 431 (two H–Cl bonds) = 862 kJ/mol. Overall energy change = energy to break bonds − energy released making bonds = 678 − 862 = −184 kJ/mol. The energy released in forming bonds is greater than the energy needed to break them, so the reaction is exothermic and the energy change is negative. Bond energy calculations are Higher tier only.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Recall that energy must be supplied to break bonds and is released when bonds are formed, and check the balanced equation for how many bonds of each kind there are.","Add the bond energies of every bond broken in the reactants, add the bond energies of every bond made in the products, then subtract the second total from the first.","Your answer is an energy change in kJ/mol carrying a sign, plus one word describing the reaction."],"codes":["MS 1a","MS 3c","MS 1c"]},{"q":"A cube of calcium carbonate has sides 2.0 cm long. (a) Calculate its surface area and its volume, and give the surface area to volume ratio in the form n : 1. (b) The cube is cut into eight smaller cubes, each with sides 1.0 cm long. Calculate the total surface area of the eight small cubes and explain why the eight small cubes react faster with dilute hydrochloric acid than the single large cube did.","a":"(a) Surface area = 24 cm², volume = 8.0 cm³, ratio = 3 : 1. (b) Total surface area = 48 cm²; the surface area to volume ratio has risen to 6 : 1, so more particles are exposed to the acid, the frequency of collisions is greater and the rate of reaction is higher.","sol":"(a) Surface area of a cube = 6 × side² = 6 × 2.0² = 6 × 4.0 = 24 cm². Volume of a cube = side³ = 2.0³ = 8.0 cm³. Surface area to volume ratio = 24 : 8.0; dividing both parts by 8.0 gives 3 : 1. (b) Each small cube has surface area 6 × 1.0² = 6 cm², so the eight together have 8 × 6 = 48 cm². The total volume is unchanged at 8 × 1.0³ = 8.0 cm³, so the ratio is now 48 : 8.0 = 6 : 1, twice as large. A larger surface area to volume ratio means more of the calcium carbonate particles are exposed at the surface where the acid can reach them, so the frequency of collisions between the acid particles and the solid increases and the rate of reaction increases.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Recall the two formulae a cube needs, and that only particles at the surface of a solid can be hit by the acid.","Square the side and multiply by six for the surface area and cube the side for the volume, then work out one small cube's surface area and multiply by how many there are before comparing the two ratios.","Your answer gives an area in cm², a volume in cm³, two ratios whose second part is 1, and an explanation that mentions how often particles collide."],"codes":["MS 5c","MS 1c"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the equation for percentage yield?","a":"Percentage yield = mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product × 100."},{"q":"What is the equation for the Rf value of a spot on a chromatogram?","a":"Rf = distance moved by the substance ÷ distance moved by the solvent, both measured from the origin line."},{"q":"What volume does one mole of any gas occupy at room temperature and pressure?","a":"24 dm³ (Higher tier)."},{"q":"How do you determine the rate of reaction at a specific time from a curve of volume of gas against time?","a":"Draw a tangent to the curve at that time and find its gradient, change in volume ÷ change in time (Higher tier)."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Decimal form","back":"A number written out in full with a decimal point, such as a titre of 27.30 cm³ or a mass of 6.12 g. Keep only the decimal places the apparatus justifies, and line the points up when adding.","code":"MS 1a","open":false},{"front":"Standard form","back":"A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10; n is positive for large numbers and negative for small ones. An atom's radius is about 1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m; (HT only) the Avogadro constant is 6.02 × 10²³ per mole.","code":"MS 1b","open":false},{"front":"Percentage yield and percentage atom economy","back":"Percentage yield = mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product × 100. Percentage atom economy = Mr of the desired product ÷ sum of the Mr of all reactants × 100, from the balanced equation.","code":"MS 1c","open":false},{"front":"Rf value","back":"Rf = distance moved by the substance ÷ distance moved by the solvent, both measured from the origin line. It has no unit and lies between 0 and 1, and differs for the same compound in different solvents.","code":"MS 1c","open":false},{"front":"Name one two-dimensional way of representing a three-dimensional chemical structure","back":"A dot and cross diagram; a ball and stick model drawn on paper; a displayed structural formula; a two-dimensional diagram of a giant structure. Each leaves something out, such as the gaps a ball and stick model shows between particles that touch in reality.","code":"MS 5b","open":true},{"front":"Significant figures","back":"Counting starts at the first non-zero digit, so 0.0417 to 2 significant figures is 0.042. Give answers from titrations and chromatograms to the number of significant figures the measurements justify, usually two or three.","code":"MS 2a","open":false},{"front":"Mean, range and uncertainty","back":"Mean = sum of the values ÷ number of values, leaving out anomalies; in titrations use concordant titres only. Range = largest value − smallest value, quoted about the mean as a measure of uncertainty.","code":"MS 2b","open":false},{"front":"Bar charts and histograms","back":"A bar chart, with separated bars, shows discrete categories such as types of waste material. A histogram shows continuous data grouped into class intervals with no gaps. The modal class or category has the highest frequency.","code":"MS 2c","open":false},{"front":"Order of magnitude","back":"The rough size of an answer, written as a power of ten: round each number to 1 significant figure and calculate. (HT only) As pH falls by one unit the hydrogen ion concentration rises by a factor of 10.","code":"MS 2h","open":false},{"front":"The symbols =, <, <<, >>, >, ∝ and ~","back":"= equals; < less than; > greater than; << much less than; >> much greater than; ∝ directly proportional to; ~ approximately equal to. The specification flags this skill against the Higher Tier work on moles.","code":"MS 3a","open":false},{"front":"Concentration of a solution in g/dm³","back":"Concentration = mass of solute ÷ volume of solution. Rearranged: mass of solute = concentration × volume, and volume = mass of solute ÷ concentration. The volume must be in dm³, and 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³.","code":"MS 3b","open":false},{"front":"(HT only) Moles, Mr and the volume of a gas","back":"Moles = mass ÷ Mr, so mass = moles × Mr. Concentration in mol/dm³ = moles ÷ volume in dm³. One mole of any gas occupies 24 dm³ at room temperature and pressure (20 °C and 1 atmosphere).","code":"MS 3c","open":false},{"front":"y = mx + c","back":"The equation of a straight-line graph: m is the gradient, change in y ÷ change in x, and c is the y-intercept, the value of y when x = 0. A negative gradient means y falls as x rises.","code":"MS 4b","open":false},{"front":"Rate of reaction from a graph","back":"Mean rate = quantity of reactant used or product formed ÷ time taken, in g/s or cm³/s. The rate at a specific time is the slope of the tangent drawn to the curve there; (HT only) calculate that gradient.","code":"MS 4e","open":false},{"front":"Surface area, volume and their ratio for a cube","back":"For a cube of side s: surface area = 6 × s², volume = s³, so surface area to volume ratio = 6 ÷ s. Smaller pieces have a larger ratio, so they react faster; nanoparticles have very high ratios.","code":"MS 5c","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"extended","title":"Extended response: processing and presenting the results of a rate investigation (6 marks)","items":["Question: 'A student investigated how the concentration of hydrochloric acid affects the rate of its reaction with marble chips. For each of five concentrations of acid the student collected the carbon dioxide given off in a gas syringe and recorded its volume every 10 s for 100 s, repeating each concentration three times. Describe how the student should process these results and present them as a graph, and explain how the graph can then be used to compare the rate of reaction at 20 s for the different concentrations.' (6 marks)","Level 3 (5–6 marks) model answer: For each concentration the student should first calculate the mean volume of carbon dioxide collected at each time, adding the three repeat readings and dividing by three, leaving out any anomalous reading that is clearly out of line with the other two, because taking a mean reduces the effect of random error. Time is the independent variable and goes on the x-axis in seconds, and the mean volume of carbon dioxide collected is the dependent variable and goes on the y-axis in cm³; both axes are labelled with the quantity and its unit and are given evenly spaced scales in sensible steps that use more than half the grid. Each mean value is plotted accurately and a smooth curve of best fit is drawn for each concentration, with all five curves on the same pair of axes so that they can be compared. To compare the rates at 20 s, a tangent is drawn with a ruler so that it touches each curve at 20 s and follows the direction of the curve there, and the slope of that tangent is used as the measure of the rate of reaction at that moment. The steeper the tangent, the faster the reaction, so comparing how steep the five tangents are shows how increasing the concentration of the acid increases the rate of reaction.","Level 2 (3–4 marks) answer: makes relevant points but with gaps or weak structure — for example it says to take a mean of the three repeats and to plot volume against time, but does not say which quantity belongs on which axis, or how the scales and the curve of best fit should be chosen; or it says that the steeper curve is the faster reaction without drawing a tangent at 20 s at all, so it never compares the reactions at the one time the question asks about.","What a bottom-level (1–2 mark) answer misses: it may say only 'work out an average' and 'draw a graph', without naming the axes, the units, the scales or the curve of best fit, without excluding anomalous readings, and without any tangent at all — so it has no way of finding a rate at one particular time and can only say, vaguely, that stronger acid reacts faster.","Note: drawing and using the slope of a tangent is required at both tiers, but calculating the gradient of that tangent as a measure of the rate at a specific time is Higher tier only."]},{"kind":"formulae","title":"Key equations for the mathematical requirements","items":["Percentage of a quantity = part ÷ whole × 100. Percentage by mass of an element in a compound = (Ar of the element × number of its atoms in the formula) ÷ Mr of the compound × 100.","Percentage yield = mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product × 100.","Percentage atom economy = Mr of the desired product from the equation ÷ sum of the Mr of all the reactants from the equation × 100.","Concentration of a solution = mass of solute ÷ volume of solution, in g/dm³; rearranged, mass of solute = concentration × volume of solution.","(HT only) Moles = mass ÷ Mr; mass = moles × Mr. Concentration in mol/dm³ = moles of solute ÷ volume of solution in dm³; moles = concentration × volume.","(HT only) Volume of a gas at room temperature and pressure (20 °C and 1 atmosphere) = moles × 24, in dm³, since one mole of any gas occupies 24 dm³.","Rf = distance moved by the substance ÷ distance moved by the solvent, both measured from the origin line; Rf has no unit and lies between 0 and 1.","Mean rate of reaction = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken, or quantity of product formed ÷ time taken; units g/s or cm³/s, and (HT only) mol/s.","Gradient = change in y ÷ change in x; for a straight line y = mx + c, where m is the gradient and c is the y-intercept. The rate at a specific time is the gradient of the tangent drawn to the curve at that time (HT only for the calculation).","Mean = sum of the values ÷ number of values; range = largest value − smallest value, used about the mean as a measure of uncertainty.","Area of a rectangle = length × width; area of a triangle = ½ × base × height. For a cube of side s: surface area = 6 × s², volume = s³, surface area to volume ratio = 6 ÷ s.","Standard form: A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10. Unit conversions: 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³, 1 g = 1000 mg, 1 kJ = 1000 J, 1 nm = 1 × 10⁻⁹ m.","AQA supplies a periodic table with both GCSE Chemistry papers but no equation sheet, so every equation above must be recalled."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:41:58Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"The fix pass holds and the room now publishes. Re-read the kit from 9.json, not the stale copy in the judge prompt, and re-solved every calculation in Python from the data as given: worked examples 1–6 (1.9 × 10⁻¹⁰ m; 6.12/8.50 = 72.0 %; 40 g/dm³ × 0.250 dm³ = 10 g; 48/40 = 1.2 cm³/s; 4.8/7.5 = 0.64; 150 cm², 125 cm³, 1.2 : 1) and all 16 questions (2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m = 0.0000025 m and 0.083333 → 0.083; 28/80 = 35 %; mean 24.15 cm³, range 0.10 cm³; 112/244 = 45.9 % and 212/250 = 84.8 %; 22/44 = 0.50 mol → 12 dm³; 58/200 = 29 %; 24.8/0.500 = 49.6 g/dm³ against an estimate of 50; pH 1→4 = 10³ = 1000; 0.00234 mol → 0.0936 mol/dm³ → 3.74 g/dm³; 56/80 = 0.70 cm³/s; −2.40/100 = −0.0240 g/s, intercept 152.40 g, 2.40/24 = 0.10 g per large square; 678 − 862 = −184 kJ/mol exothermic; 24 cm² and 8.0 cm³ giving 3 : 1, then 48 cm² giving 6 : 1), plus the new card illustration 0.0417 → 0.042: every answer key, unit and significant-figure count is right. The three fixes verify against spec.txt: fact 3 and MS 1b now give 1 × 10⁻¹⁴ m as the nucleus radius, less than 1/10 000 of the atom's, exactly as 4.1.1.5 words it; 27.30 cm³, Rf 0.37, −92 kJ/mol and 0.0417 → 0.042 appear nowhere in the bank, so no question answer or worked-example answer now sits in the lesson or on a card; the reworded attributions are all true of the document (MS 1d quotes 4.2.4.1's 'compare ‘nano’ dimensions to typical dimensions of atoms and molecules'; MS 2a matches the chromatography bullet 'provide answers to an appropriate number of significant figures' at 4.8.1.2; MS 3c's three annotations are 4.3.2.2/4.3.2.3, 4.3.4 and 4.3.5, all HT; MS 3a's single annotation is 4.3.2.1; and MS 2h is indeed flagged in exactly three places — 4.2.4.1, 4.4.2.6 and 4.10.1.1's 'use orders of magnitude to evaluate the significance of data'); and the Level 3 model answer now draws a tangent at 20 s and compares slopes qualitatively, which 4.6.1.1 allows at both tiers, with the Level 2 descriptor adjusted to match and the tier note intact. Everything else re-checked: the eighteen ideas are section 7's list exactly (MS 1a–1d, 2a–2c, 2h, 3a–3c, 4a–4e, 5b, 5c) with nothing outside it and every code tested by at least one question; Higher tier labels are right on questions 7, 10, 11, 12 and 14 (moles and gas volumes 4.3.2.1/4.3.5, hydrogen ion concentration 4.4.2.6, titration in mol/dm³ 4.3.4/4.4.2.5, tangent gradient 4.6.1.1, bond energies 4.5.1.3) and every all-tier item, including the g/dm³ concentration at question index 9 and the linear gradient at index 13, is left untiered; the contexts (magnesium sulfate crystals, marble chips and acid, blast-furnace equation supplied in the stem, ammonium nitrate, chromatogram, waste survey, flame emission) are all 8462 content at the document's precision; 16 questions at 5/6/5 by difficulty with marks 2–5, 15 cards all inside the word limits, 4 exit questions, 14 facts carrying both papers, 1 h 45 min, 100 marks, 50 % each, 8462F/8462H and the 20 % maths weighting; no caret anywhere, every power a Unicode superscript and every formula in Unicode subscripts (NH₄NO₃, Fe₂O₃, CO₂, H₂, Cl₂, H₂O)."}}},"4.1":{"id":"AQA-8462","topic":"4.1","family":"science","lesson":{"why":"This room builds the model of the atom and the structure of the periodic table that every later chemistry topic depends on: bonding, electrolysis, quantitative chemistry and the reactions of every group are explained using atomic structure and electron arrangement. You are given a periodic table in the exam, so the marks are for using it — working out protons, neutrons, electrons, electronic structure and reactivity trends — rather than memorising positions, and for describing how the atomic model and the periodic table changed as new evidence emerged. Separate chemistry also examines the transition elements: how they differ from the Group 1 metals and the three properties (variable ion charge, coloured compounds, catalysis) that make them useful.","idea":[{"code":"4.1.1.1","h":"Atoms, elements, compounds and equations","t":"An atom is the smallest part of an element that can exist, and each element is represented by a chemical symbol, such as O for oxygen or Na for sodium — there are about 100 elements, shown in the periodic table. A compound forms when two or more elements react chemically and combine in a fixed proportion; a chemical reaction always makes one or more new substances and often involves a detectable energy change, and a compound can only be split back into its elements by another chemical reaction, never by a physical process. You must be able to name compounds from a formula or symbol equation, and write word equations and balanced symbol equations for reactions in this specification, e.g. 2Na + Cl₂ -> 2NaCl. Higher tier only: you must also be able to write balanced half equations and ionic equations where appropriate, such as Na -> Na⁺ + e⁻ and Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ -> 2Cl⁻ + Br₂."},{"code":"4.1.1.2","h":"Separating mixtures","t":"A mixture contains two or more elements or compounds that are not chemically combined, so each substance keeps its own chemical properties and can be separated by a physical process rather than a reaction — no new substance is made. You need to describe, explain and choose the correct method for a given mixture: filtration for an insoluble solid in a liquid, crystallisation for a soluble solid dissolved in solution, simple distillation to collect a solvent from a solution, fractional distillation to separate miscible liquids with different boiling points, and chromatography to separate substances dissolved in a mixture, such as dyes, using their different attraction to a moving solvent and stationary paper."},{"code":"4.1.1.3","h":"How the atomic model changed over time","t":"New experimental evidence can change or replace a scientific model. Atoms were first thought to be tiny spheres that could not be divided; the discovery of the electron led to the plum pudding model (a ball of positive charge with negative electrons embedded in it). The alpha particle scattering experiment fired alpha particles at thin gold foil: most passed straight through, but some were deflected and a very few bounced back, showing that an atom's mass and positive charge are concentrated at a charged centre — the nucleus — replacing the plum pudding model with the nuclear model. Niels Bohr then adapted the nuclear model, suggesting electrons orbit the nucleus at specific distances, and his theoretical calculations agreed with the experimental observations. Later experiments showed the nucleus's positive charge could be subdivided into particles of equal positive charge, named protons, and James Chadwick's work, about 20 years after the nucleus became accepted, provided evidence for neutrons in the nucleus. You must be able to describe why the scattering evidence changed the model, and the difference between the plum pudding and nuclear models; details of the experimental work behind the Bohr model and Chadwick's work are not required."},{"code":"4.1.1.4","h":"Charges of the particles in an atom","t":"Every atom contains protons (relative charge +1) and neutrons (charge 0) in the nucleus, with electrons (relative charge -1) arranged around it. Because the number of electrons always equals the number of protons in an atom, the positive and negative charges cancel out and the atom has no overall electrical charge. The number of protons is called the atomic (proton) number: every atom of a given element has the same number of protons, and atoms of different elements have different numbers of protons. You must be able to use this nuclear model — a small charged nucleus surrounded by electrons — to describe any given atom."},{"code":"4.1.1.5","h":"Size, mass and isotopes","t":"Atoms are extremely small, with a radius of about 0.1 nm (1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m); the radius of the nucleus is less than 1/10 000 of that of the atom (about 1 × 10⁻¹⁴ m) — yet almost all the atom's mass is concentrated there, because protons and neutrons each have a relative mass of 1 while an electron's relative mass is very small. The mass number is the sum of the protons and the neutrons in an atom. Atoms of the same element (same atomic number) can have different numbers of neutrons — these are isotopes of that element. You should be able to calculate the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in an atom or ion from its atomic number and mass number, and relate the tiny scale of atoms to objects in the physical world."},{"code":"4.1.1.6","h":"Relative atomic mass","t":"A sample of an element usually contains a mixture of its isotopes, so the relative atomic mass (Ar) is an average value that takes account of the abundance of each isotope, rather than a simple whole number. To calculate it, multiply each isotope's mass number by its percentage abundance, add the results together, and divide by 100 — for example chlorine, which is 75% chlorine-35 and 25% chlorine-37, has a relative atomic mass of (35 × 75 + 37 × 25) / 100 = 35.5. You must be able to carry out this calculation given the percentage abundances of the isotopes."},{"code":"4.1.1.7","h":"Electronic structure","t":"Electrons occupy the lowest available energy levels (the innermost available shells) first, closest to the nucleus. For the first 20 elements, shells fill up to a maximum of 2, then 8, then 8 electrons; the structure can be written as numbers, e.g. sodium is 2,8,1 (two electrons in the lowest energy level, eight in the second and one in the third), or drawn as a shell diagram. You may answer in terms of either energy levels or shells, and you should be able to work out and represent, in both forms, the electronic structure of any of the first twenty elements from its atomic number, by filling each shell in turn before starting the next."},{"code":"4.1.2.1","h":"How the periodic table is arranged","t":"The periodic table lists elements in order of atomic (proton) number, arranged so that elements with similar properties fall in the same vertical column, called a group — similar properties recur at regular intervals, which is why it is called a periodic table. Elements in the same group have the same number of electrons in their outer shell, and it is this outer-shell arrangement that gives them similar chemical properties. You should be able to explain how an element's position links to its electron arrangement and hence to its atomic number, and use its position to predict its possible reactions and probable reactivity compared with its neighbours."},{"code":"4.1.2.2","h":"How the periodic table developed","t":"Before protons, neutrons and electrons were discovered, scientists tried to classify elements by arranging them in order of atomic weight, but early tables were incomplete and a strict weight order sometimes placed elements in inappropriate groups. Mendeleev overcame some of these problems by leaving gaps for elements he believed had not yet been discovered and, in some places, changing the order based on atomic weights; when elements with the properties he had predicted were later discovered they filled those gaps, strongly supporting his table. Once isotopes were understood, scientists could explain why ordering by atomic weight had sometimes given the wrong sequence, and elements are now ordered by atomic (proton) number instead. You should be able to describe these steps in order."},{"code":"4.1.2.3","h":"Metals and non-metals","t":"Elements that react to form positive ions (by losing electrons) are metals; elements that do not form positive ions are non-metals. The majority of elements are metals, positioned to the left and towards the bottom of the periodic table, while non-metals sit towards the right and top. You should be able to explain the typical physical and chemical differences between metals and non-metals (for example metals conducting electricity and forming positive ions, non-metals often not conducting and gaining or sharing electrons instead), and link both an element's position and its reactions to the arrangement of electrons in its atoms and hence to its atomic number."},{"code":"4.1.2.4","h":"Group 0: the noble gases","t":"The noble gases of Group 0 have a stable arrangement of electrons — eight in the outer shell, except helium, which has only two — so they are unreactive and do not easily form molecules, having no tendency to gain, lose or share electrons. Going down the group, the boiling points of the noble gases increase with increasing relative atomic mass. You should be able to explain how the properties of Group 0 elements depend on their outer shell of electrons, and predict a noble gas's properties, such as its boiling point, from the trend down the group."},{"code":"4.1.2.5","h":"Group 1: the alkali metals","t":"Group 1 elements (the alkali metals) have characteristic properties because of the single electron in their outer shell, which they lose easily to form a 1+ ion: with oxygen they form a metal oxide (e.g. 4Li + O₂ -> 2Li₂O), with chlorine a metal chloride (e.g. 2Na + Cl₂ -> 2NaCl), and with water a metal hydroxide plus hydrogen gas (e.g. 2K + 2H₂O -> 2KOH + H₂), with the reactions becoming more vigorous down the group. Reactivity increases going down Group 1 because the outer electron is further from the nucleus and shielded by more inner shells, so it is held less strongly and lost more easily. You should be able to describe the reactions of the first three alkali metals with oxygen, chlorine and water, and predict properties from given trends down the group."},{"code":"4.1.2.6","h":"Group 7: the halogens","t":"Group 7 elements (the halogens) are non-metals that consist of molecules made of pairs of atoms (e.g. Cl₂) and all have seven electrons in their outer shell, which gives them similar reactions: with metals they form ionic metal halides (e.g. 2Na + Cl₂ -> 2NaCl), and with non-metals they form covalent molecular compounds (e.g. H₂ + Cl₂ -> 2HCl). Further down the group an element has a higher relative molecular mass, melting point and boiling point, but reactivity decreases, because the outer shell needing one extra electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so an electron is harder to attract. A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive halogen from an aqueous solution of its salt, e.g. Cl₂ + 2KBr -> 2KCl + Br₂."},{"code":"4.1.3.1","h":"Transition elements compared with Group 1 (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) The transition elements are the block of metals between Group 2 and Group 3. They are all metals with similar properties to one another, but those properties are very different from the Group 1 metals. Transition metals have much higher melting points, higher densities, and are stronger and harder; copper melts at 1085 °C and has a density of 8.96 g/cm³, while sodium melts at 98 °C, has a density of 0.97 g/cm³ and is soft enough to cut with a knife and light enough to float on water. Transition metals are also far less reactive: they react slowly or not at all with oxygen, water and the halogens, whereas Group 1 metals tarnish in air within seconds and fizz violently on water. You must be able to make this comparison and exemplify it using Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni and Cu — iron, for example, rusts only slowly in damp air, while potassium ignites on water."},{"code":"4.1.3.2","h":"Typical properties of the transition metals (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Three properties mark out the transition elements. Many of them form ions with different charges: iron forms Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺, copper forms Cu⁺ and Cu²⁺, chromium forms Cr²⁺ and Cr³⁺, which is why their compounds are named with a Roman numeral, e.g. iron(II) sulfate. Their compounds are usually coloured, unlike the white or colourless compounds of Group 1: copper(II) sulfate solution is blue, iron(II) compounds are pale green, iron(III) compounds are orange-brown, nickel(II) compounds are green and cobalt(II) compounds are pink. Many transition elements and their compounds are also useful as catalysts: iron in the Haber process, nickel in the hydrogenation of vegetable oils and manganese(IV) oxide in the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide. You should be able to exemplify all three properties by reference to compounds of Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni and Cu."}],"examples":[{"title":"Particles in an atom and in its ion","setup":"A magnesium atom has atomic number 12 and mass number 24. Work out the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in (a) a magnesium atom and (b) a magnesium ion, Mg²⁺.","steps":["The atomic number tells you the proton count: 12 protons, and 12 electrons because the atom is neutral overall.","Neutrons = mass number - atomic number = 24 - 12 = 12.","So the magnesium atom has 12 protons, 12 neutrons and 12 electrons.","A 2+ charge means two electrons have been lost: 12 - 2 = 10 electrons.","Nothing in the nucleus changes when an ion forms, so Mg²⁺ has 12 protons, 12 neutrons and 10 electrons."],"cues":["Which two counts can you read straight off the atomic number?","How do you get the neutron count from the two numbers given?","What are the three particle counts for the neutral atom?","A 2+ charge means electrons have been lost or gained, and how many?","Do the proton and neutron counts change when an ion forms?"]},{"title":"Electronic structure from atomic number","setup":"Chlorine has atomic number 17. Write the electronic structure of a chlorine atom as numbers, and state how many electrons are in its outer shell.","steps":["A neutral atom holds one electron for every proton, so chlorine holds 17 electrons.","Fill the lowest energy level first: 2 electrons in the first shell, leaving 17 - 2 = 15.","The second shell takes a maximum of 8, leaving 15 - 8 = 7.","The remaining 7 electrons go into the third shell, so the electronic structure is 2,8,7.","The outer shell therefore holds 7 electrons, which places chlorine in Group 7."],"cues":["How many electrons does a neutral chlorine atom have?","Which shell fills first, and how many does it hold?","What is the maximum for the second shell?","Where do the electrons that are left over go?","What does the outer-shell count tell you about its group?"]},{"title":"Relative atomic mass from percentage abundances","setup":"A sample of copper contains 69% copper-63 atoms and 31% copper-65 atoms. Calculate the relative atomic mass of this sample, giving your answer to one decimal place.","steps":["Multiply the first isotope's mass number by its percentage abundance: 63 × 69 = 4347.","Do the same for the second isotope: 65 × 31 = 2015.","Add the two products together: 4347 + 2015 = 6362.","Divide the total by 100 because the abundances are percentages: 6362 / 100 = 63.62.","Relative atomic mass = 63.6 to one decimal place."],"cues":["What do you do with each isotope's mass number and its abundance?","What is the same calculation for copper-65?","How are the two products combined?","Percentages are shares out of what total?","How should the answer be rounded?"]},{"title":"Balancing a symbol equation, and the half equation (Higher tier)","setup":"Sodium metal burns in chlorine gas to make sodium chloride. (a) Write the balanced symbol equation for the reaction. (b) Higher tier: write the half equation for what happens to the sodium atoms.","steps":["Word equation: sodium + chlorine -> sodium chloride.","Put in the formulae, remembering chlorine is a diatomic molecule: Na + Cl₂ -> NaCl.","There are two chlorine atoms on the left but only one on the right, so write 2NaCl.","That now needs two sodium atoms on the left: 2Na + Cl₂ -> 2NaCl, with 2 Na and 2 Cl on each side.","Each sodium atom loses one electron to form a 1+ ion, so the half equation is Na -> Na⁺ + e⁻."],"cues":["What are the reactants and the product, in words?","What formula must you use for chlorine gas?","How do you fix the chlorine atoms on the right?","What must change on the left-hand side now?","Does a sodium atom lose or gain an electron, and how many?"]},{"title":"Telling a transition metal from an alkali metal (chemistry only)","setup":"A student is given two unlabelled metals, X and Y. X melts at 98 °C, has a density of 0.97 g/cm³, is soft enough to cut with a knife and fizzes violently when dropped into cold water. Y melts at 1085 °C, has a density of 8.96 g/cm³, is hard, and shows no reaction with cold water. Decide which metal is in Group 1 and which is a transition element, and justify the choice.","steps":["Compare the melting points: an alkali metal melts below 200 °C, a transition element at well over 1000 °C.","Compare the densities: alkali metals are less dense than water and float on it, transition elements are many times denser.","Compare hardness and strength: alkali metals are soft and easily cut, transition elements are hard and strong.","Compare the reaction with water: alkali metals react vigorously, transition elements react slowly or not at all.","Every property points the same way, so X is the Group 1 metal (it is sodium) and Y is the transition element (it is copper)."],"cues":["Which melting point belongs to a metal you could melt in boiling water?","Which metal would float on water, and why?","Which of these two metals could be cut with a knife?","Which metal fizzes when it meets cold water?","So which label goes on X, and which on Y?"]},{"title":"Predicting halogen displacement","setup":"A few drops of chlorine water are added to a colourless solution of potassium iodide, and separately to a colourless solution of potassium chloride. Both tubes are shaken. Predict what is seen in each tube and write the balanced symbol equation for any reaction that happens.","steps":["Reactivity falls going down Group 7, so chlorine is more reactive than bromine and iodine.","A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive one from a solution of its salt, so chlorine displaces iodine from the iodide.","The potassium iodide tube darkens to a brown solution as iodine is released: Cl₂ + 2KI -> 2KCl + I₂.","Chlorine cannot displace itself, so the potassium chloride tube stays colourless and no reaction occurs."],"cues":["Which way does halogen reactivity change down the group?","What does a more reactive halogen do to a halide in solution?","What colour appears in the first tube, and what is the equation?","Why does the second tube show nothing at all?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the mass number of an atom?","a":"The total number of protons and neutrons in its nucleus."},{"q":"Why do noble gases not readily react?","a":"Because they already have a stable, full outer shell of electrons."},{"q":"Which separation technique would you use to get pure water from salt solution?","a":"Simple distillation."},{"q":"What happens to reactivity going down Group 7?","a":"It decreases."},{"q":"Name one way the transition metals differ from the Group 1 metals.","a":"Any one of: higher melting point; higher density; harder and stronger; much less reactive with oxygen, water and the halogens."}]},"room":{"facts":["Relative charges: proton +1, neutron 0, electron -1.","Relative masses: proton 1, neutron 1, electron very small (negligible compared with a proton or neutron).","Atomic (proton) number = number of protons = number of electrons in an atom; mass number = protons + neutrons.","Atomic radius is about 0.1 nm (1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m); nuclear radius is about 1 × 10⁻¹⁴ m, less than 1/10 000 of the atom's radius.","Electron shells fill in the order 2, 8, 8 for the first 20 elements, lowest energy level first.","Relative atomic mass: Ar = (mass number × % abundance, added for each isotope) / 100.","Group 1 reactivity increases down the group; Group 7 reactivity decreases down the group.","Noble gases (Group 0) have a stable outer shell of 8 electrons, except helium (2).","(chemistry only) Compared with Group 1 metals, transition metals have higher melting points and densities, are harder and stronger, and react much less readily with oxygen, water and the halogens.","(chemistry only) Many transition elements form ions with different charges, form coloured compounds and are useful as catalysts.","(Higher tier) Half equation: Na -> Na⁺ + e⁻. Ionic equation: Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ -> 2Cl⁻ + Br₂."],"questions":[{"q":"Name the separation technique used to obtain dry salt crystals from a salt solution.","a":"Crystallisation.","sol":"Crystallisation separates a soluble solid from a solution: the solution is gently heated to evaporate some of the solvent until it becomes saturated, then left to cool so pure salt crystals form, which are filtered off and dried.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Filtration only removes an insoluble solid, so it will not work here.","Salt is soluble, so it stays dissolved until the solvent is removed.","The technique involves slowly evaporating some of the solvent until the dissolved solid separates out as the solution cools."],"codes":["4.1.1.2"]},{"q":"Give the relative charges of a proton, a neutron and an electron.","a":"Proton +1, neutron 0, electron -1.","sol":"A proton has a relative charge of +1, a neutron has no charge (0), and an electron has a relative charge of -1. Because the number of electrons equals the number of protons in an atom, the positive and negative charges balance and the atom has no overall charge.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about how the three particles in an atom differ in electric charge.","Remember that an atom's overall charge is zero — think about what that means for how the charges of its particles must relate.","Give three values in total, one for each particle, each as +, - or 0."],"codes":["4.1.1.4"]},{"q":"Define an isotope.","a":"An atom of the same element with a different number of neutrons.","sol":"Isotopes are atoms of the same element (so they have the same number of protons) that have different numbers of neutrons, and therefore different mass numbers.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Isotopes of an element always share the same atomic number.","What changes between isotopes is inside the nucleus, but it is not the proton count.","Think about which subatomic particle affects mass number but not atomic number."],"codes":["4.1.1.5"]},{"q":"Lithium reacts with water to form lithium hydroxide and hydrogen gas. Write the balanced symbol equation for this reaction.","a":"2Li + 2H₂O -> 2LiOH + H₂","sol":"Word equation: lithium + water -> lithium hydroxide + hydrogen. Unbalanced symbol equation: Li + H₂O -> LiOH + H₂. Balancing the hydrogen and lithium atoms on each side gives 2Li + 2H₂O -> 2LiOH + H₂, which has 2 Li, 4 H and 2 O on each side.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Balancing an equation means having the same number of atoms of each element on both sides, as in the Na + Cl₂ worked example.","Count the atoms of lithium, hydrogen and oxygen on each side of the unbalanced equation, then add balancing numbers in front of formulae (never inside them) until they match.","Balance the lithium and the oxygen last, after you have counted the hydrogens on each side."],"codes":["4.1.1.1","4.1.2.5"]},{"q":"State the number of electrons in the outer shell of a Group 7 element.","a":"Seven.","sol":"Elements in Group 7 (the halogens) all have seven electrons in their outer shell, which is why they react in similar ways.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Elements in the same group all have the same number of electrons in their outer shell.","Count across the main groups from Group 1 to find where Group 7 sits, then use the group number itself.","Give a single whole number, not a description of the shell arrangement."],"codes":["4.1.2.6"]},{"q":"Sodium-23 has atomic number 11. Calculate the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in an atom of sodium-23.","a":"11 protons, 12 neutrons, 11 electrons.","sol":"Atomic number = protons = electrons = 11. Neutrons = mass number - atomic number = 23 - 11 = 12. So a sodium-23 atom has 11 protons, 12 neutrons and 11 electrons.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The atomic number gives you two of the three answers immediately.","Neutrons are found by subtracting one of the two numbers from the other.","Neutrons = mass number - atomic number = 23 - 11."],"codes":["4.1.1.4","4.1.1.5"]},{"q":"Work out the electronic structure of an aluminium atom (atomic number 13).","a":"2,8,3","sol":"Aluminium has 13 electrons. The first shell holds a maximum of 2 and the second a maximum of 8 (2 + 8 = 10), leaving 13 - 10 = 3 electrons for the third shell. Electronic structure: 2,8,3.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Electrons fill the lowest available energy level first.","The first shell holds 2 and the second shell holds up to 8.","Work out how many electrons remain after 2 and 8 have been placed: 13 - 2 - 8."],"codes":["4.1.1.7"]},{"q":"Explain why the elements in Group 0 are very unreactive.","a":"They have a stable, full outer shell of electrons, so they do not need to gain, lose or share electrons.","sol":"Group 0 elements (the noble gases) have a stable arrangement of electrons — eight in the outer shell, except helium which has two. Because their outer shell is already stable, their atoms have no tendency to gain, lose or share electrons to react with other atoms, so they are very unreactive and do not easily form molecules.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what makes an atom's electron arrangement stable.","Compare the outer shell of a noble gas with the outer shell of a reactive metal or non-metal.","A stable outer shell means there is no need to do what in a chemical reaction?"],"codes":["4.1.2.4"]},{"q":"Explain why reactivity increases down Group 1.","a":"The outer electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so it is lost more easily.","sol":"Going down Group 1, atoms have more electron shells, so the single outer electron is further from the positively charged nucleus and shielded by more inner shells of electrons. This weakens the attraction between the nucleus and the outer electron, so it is lost more easily, making the atom react more readily. This is why potassium is more reactive than sodium, which is more reactive than lithium.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Compare the number of electron shells in lithium, sodium and potassium.","Think about how far the outer electron is from the nucleus in each case, and what sits in between.","A weaker attraction between nucleus and outer electron makes that electron easier to do what?"],"codes":["4.1.2.5","4.1.2.1"]},{"q":"The transition elements sit between Group 2 and Group 3 of the periodic table. Compare the transition elements with the Group 1 metals in terms of melting point, density, hardness and reactivity with water.","a":"Transition elements have higher melting points and higher densities, are harder and stronger, and react far less readily with water than Group 1 metals.","sol":"Melting point: transition elements such as iron and copper melt at well over 1000 °C, whereas the Group 1 metals melt at low temperatures (sodium at 98 °C). Density: transition elements are dense (copper 8.96 g/cm³), whereas Group 1 metals are less dense than water (sodium 0.97 g/cm³) and float on it. Hardness and strength: transition elements are hard and strong, whereas Group 1 metals are soft and can be cut with a knife. Reactivity with water: transition elements react slowly or not at all with water, whereas Group 1 metals react vigorously, fizzing and giving off hydrogen, with the vigour increasing down the group.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Think about which of these two sets of metals you could cut with a knife and drop into a trough of water in a school lab.","The command word is Compare, so work through the four named properties one at a time and say what each set of metals does for each.","Anchor each comparison to real substances — for example iron or copper against sodium or potassium."],"codes":["4.1.3.1"]},{"q":"Explain why elements found on the left-hand side of the periodic table are usually classed as metals.","a":"They have few electrons in their outer shell, which they lose easily to form a positive ion, and an element that forms positive ions is a metal.","sol":"Elements towards the left and bottom of the periodic table typically have only one, two or three electrons in their outer shell. These outer electrons are lost relatively easily when the atom reacts, leaving a positively charged ion with a stable outer shell below. An element that reacts by forming a positive ion in this way is classed as a metal, unlike non-metals (found towards the right and top), which do not form positive ions.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Look at where metals sit in the periodic table compared with non-metals, and think about how many outer-shell electrons that position tends to give an atom.","A metal is defined by the type of ion its atoms form when they react — think about whether that involves losing or gaining electrons.","Your answer needs two linked parts: how many outer electrons a typical atom there has, and what that means for the type of ion it forms."],"codes":["4.1.2.3"]},{"q":"Describe how the results of the alpha particle scattering experiment led scientists to replace the plum pudding model with the nuclear model.","a":"Most alpha particles passed straight through the gold foil, but some were deflected and a few bounced back, showing that atoms are mostly empty space with mass and positive charge concentrated in a tiny nucleus, not spread evenly as the plum pudding model claimed.","sol":"In the plum pudding model, the atom was thought to be a ball of positive charge with electrons embedded evenly through it, so alpha particles fired at thin gold foil would be expected to pass through with little deflection. In the actual experiment, most alpha particles did pass straight through, showing atoms are mostly empty space; but some were deflected through large angles and a very small number bounced almost straight back, which could only happen if they were repelled by a concentrated region of positive charge containing most of the atom's mass. This evidence could not be explained by the plum pudding model, so it was replaced by the nuclear model, in which a tiny, dense, positively charged nucleus sits at the centre of a mostly empty atom, with electrons some distance away.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["This is an extended response: build a logical chain from what the plum pudding model predicted through to why it was replaced.","What would the plum pudding model have predicted for alpha particles fired at gold foil, if the atom's positive charge were spread evenly through it?","Would a thin, evenly spread sheet of positive charge be able to stop or reverse a fast, heavy alpha particle? What must be true about the atom's structure if some particles behave unexpectedly?"],"codes":["4.1.1.3"]},{"q":"A sample of chlorine contains 75% chlorine-35 atoms and 25% chlorine-37 atoms. Calculate the relative atomic mass of this sample of chlorine, giving your answer to one decimal place.","a":"35.5","sol":"Relative atomic mass = (mass number × % abundance for each isotope) added together, divided by 100 = (35 × 75) + (37 × 25), all divided by 100 = (2625 + 925) / 100 = 3550 / 100 = 35.5.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Each isotope contributes to the average in proportion to how common it is.","Multiply each isotope's mass number by its percentage abundance, then combine the two results.","Divide your total by 100: (35 × 75 + 37 × 25) / 100."],"codes":["4.1.1.6"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Chlorine gas is bubbled through potassium bromide solution. Write the balanced ionic equation for the reaction, write the half equation for the change to the bromide ions, and explain why the reaction happens.","a":"Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ -> 2Cl⁻ + Br₂; half equation 2Br⁻ -> Br₂ + 2e⁻; chlorine is more reactive than bromine, so it displaces bromine from the solution.","sol":"Ionic equation: Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ -> 2Cl⁻ + Br₂ (2 Cl and 2 Br on each side, and a charge of -2 on each side; the K⁺ spectator ions are left out). Half equation for the bromide ions: 2Br⁻ -> Br₂ + 2e⁻. Chlorine is higher up Group 7 than bromine, and reactivity decreases down the group, so chlorine atoms attract electrons more strongly. Chlorine therefore takes the electrons from the bromide ions, becoming chloride ions, and the bromine that is displaced turns the solution orange-brown.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["In a displacement reaction the two halogens swap roles: one ends up as ions, the other as molecules.","Reactivity in Group 7 decreases going down the group, so compare chlorine's position with bromine's; for the half equation, follow only the bromide ions.","Remember halogens exist as diatomic molecules (Cl₂, not Cl), that spectator ions such as K⁺ are left out of an ionic equation, and that a half equation must balance for charge as well as for atoms."],"codes":["4.1.2.6","4.1.1.1"]},{"q":"Explain how Mendeleev's arrangement of the periodic table was later confirmed to be correct, using the idea of an undiscovered element.","a":"He left gaps for elements he predicted existed, and elements later discovered with matching properties filled those gaps.","sol":"Mendeleev arranged known elements mainly in order of atomic weight, but grouped them by their properties, sometimes changing the strict weight order and leaving gaps where no known element fitted. He used these gaps to predict the properties of undiscovered elements. When new elements were later discovered with properties matching his predictions, they fitted neatly into the gaps, providing strong evidence that his arrangement (by properties, not just weight) was correct.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what Mendeleev did when no known element fitted the strict order of atomic weights.","His table was not complete — consider what he left in the spaces where no element was known, and what he said about them.","Explain what happened when chemists later found new elements, and why that supported his table."],"codes":["4.1.2.2"]},{"q":"Iron(II) sulfate solution is pale green, iron(III) sulfate solution is orange-brown, and iron is used in the Haber process. Use this information to describe three typical properties of the transition elements, giving one further example of each property from a different transition element.","a":"They form ions with different charges (e.g. Cu⁺ and Cu²⁺), they form coloured compounds (e.g. blue copper(II) sulfate) and they are useful as catalysts (e.g. nickel in the hydrogenation of vegetable oils).","sol":"The two iron compounds contain Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺, so the first property is that many transition elements form ions with different charges; copper does the same, forming Cu⁺ and Cu²⁺, and chromium forms Cr²⁺ and Cr³⁺. Both solutions are coloured (pale green and orange-brown), so the second property is that transition elements form coloured compounds, unlike the white or colourless compounds of Group 1; copper(II) sulfate solution is blue, nickel(II) compounds are green and cobalt(II) compounds are pink. Iron speeds up the Haber process without being used up, so the third property is that transition elements and their compounds are useful as catalysts; nickel catalyses the hydrogenation of vegetable oils and manganese(IV) oxide catalyses the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Two of the three facts are about the same element behaving in two different ways — look carefully at what is different between the two iron compounds.","Take the information one fact at a time and turn each into a general statement about transition elements, then recall another element that behaves in the same way.","Choose your further examples from Cr, Mn, Co, Ni and Cu, the transition elements named in this specification."],"codes":["4.1.3.2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the atomic number of an atom?","a":"The number of protons in its nucleus."},{"q":"Which model of the atom did the alpha particle scattering experiment disprove?","a":"The plum pudding model."},{"q":"Does reactivity increase or decrease going down Group 7?","a":"It decreases."},{"q":"Iron forms Fe²⁺ ions and Fe³⁺ ions. Which typical property of the transition elements does this show?","a":"That many transition elements form ions with different charges."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Compound","back":"Two or more elements chemically combined in fixed proportions, formed by a chemical reaction and shown by a formula, e.g. H₂O, NaCl. Only a chemical reaction can split it back into elements.","code":"4.1.1.1","open":false},{"front":"Chromatography","back":"Separates substances dissolved in a mixture (e.g. dyes) because they are attracted differently to the moving solvent and the stationary paper, so they travel different distances.","code":"4.1.1.2","open":false},{"front":"Plum pudding model","back":"A ball of positive charge with negative electrons embedded in it; replaced after the alpha particle scattering experiment showed a tiny, dense, positively charged nucleus.","code":"4.1.1.3","open":false},{"front":"Relative charges and masses of subatomic particles","back":"Charges: proton +1, neutron 0, electron -1. Masses: proton 1, neutron 1, electron very small. Protons equal electrons in an atom, so atoms have no overall charge.","code":"4.1.1.4","open":false},{"front":"Isotopes, and the size of an atom","back":"Isotopes: atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons, so different mass numbers. Atomic radius about 0.1 nm (1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m); nuclear radius about 1 × 10⁻¹⁴ m, under 1/10 000 of the atom.","code":"4.1.1.5","open":false},{"front":"Relative atomic mass equation","back":"Ar = (mass number × % abundance, added for each isotope) / 100.","code":"4.1.1.6","open":false},{"front":"Electronic structure rule","back":"Electrons fill the lowest available energy level first: 2 in the first shell, 8 in the second, 8 in the third (first 20 elements), e.g. sodium is 2,8,1.","code":"4.1.1.7","open":false},{"front":"Why elements in a group react similarly","back":"They have the same number of electrons in their outer shell, and it is the outer electrons that control chemical properties.","code":"4.1.2.1","open":false},{"front":"Mendeleev's periodic table","back":"Ordered mainly by atomic weight but grouped by properties; he left gaps for undiscovered elements, whose later discovery with matching properties confirmed his arrangement.","code":"4.1.2.2","open":false},{"front":"Metals and non-metals in the periodic table","back":"Metals (elements that react to form positive ions) are found to the left and bottom; non-metals (which do not form positive ions) are found to the right and top.","code":"4.1.2.3","open":false},{"front":"Group 0 outer shell","back":"A stable outer shell (8 electrons, except helium's 2) makes the noble gases unreactive. Boiling point increases down the group as relative atomic mass increases.","code":"4.1.2.4","open":false},{"front":"Trend down Group 1","back":"Reactivity increases down Group 1: the single outer electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so it is lost more easily.","code":"4.1.2.5","open":false},{"front":"Trend down Group 7","back":"Reactivity decreases down Group 7, but relative molecular mass, melting point and boiling point increase. A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive one from a solution of its salt.","code":"4.1.2.6","open":false},{"front":"Transition elements compared with Group 1 (chemistry only)","back":"Transition metals have higher melting points and densities, and are stronger and harder; they react much less readily with oxygen, water and the halogens. Examples: Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu.","code":"4.1.3.1","open":false},{"front":"Typical properties of the transition elements (chemistry only)","back":"Many form ions with different charges (Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺), form coloured compounds (blue copper(II) sulfate, green nickel(II) compounds) and are useful catalysts (iron in the Haber process).","code":"4.1.3.2","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: the development of the model of the atom","items":["Question: describe how the model of the atom has changed over time, from the plum pudding model to the model used today. (6 marks)","Level 3 (5-6 marks): a coherent, logically structured answer that sequences the stages correctly. The plum pudding model described the atom as a ball of positive charge with electrons embedded in it. The alpha particle scattering experiment showed most alpha particles passed straight through thin gold foil, but some were deflected and a very few bounced back, showing that an atom's mass and positive charge are concentrated at a charged centre, the nucleus; this replaced the plum pudding model with the nuclear model. Niels Bohr then adapted the nuclear model, suggesting electrons orbit the nucleus at specific distances, and his theoretical calculations agreed with experimental observations. Later experiments showed the positive charge of the nucleus could be subdivided into particles of equal positive charge, named protons, and James Chadwick's experimental work, about 20 years after the nucleus became accepted, provided evidence for neutrons within the nucleus, giving the model of protons and neutrons in a nucleus with electrons in shells around it.","Level 2 (3-4 marks): mentions the plum pudding model and the nuclear model and some idea that the scattering experiment's evidence changed the model, and may mention Bohr's specific orbits or protons and neutrons, but the points are not fully linked in the correct sequence, or one stage (such as Chadwick and neutrons) is missing.","Level 1 (1-2 marks): makes an isolated correct statement, such as 'the plum pudding model was replaced by the nuclear model' or 'Bohr said electrons orbit the nucleus', without linking cause and effect or giving the stages in the correct order.","What a bottom-level (Level 1) answer misses: it does not explain why the scattering results (most particles passing straight through, a few bouncing back) disproved the plum pudding model; it does not sequence the stages correctly (plum pudding, then nuclear, then Bohr's specific orbits, then protons, then neutrons); and it does not link each new piece of evidence to the change in the model that it caused."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: transition elements compared with Group 1 (chemistry only)","items":["Question: compare the transition elements with the elements of Group 1, using both their physical properties and their chemical reactions. Refer to named elements in your answer. (6 marks)","Level 3 (5-6 marks): a coherent comparison in which every point names both sides of the difference and is supported by named elements. Physically, transition elements such as iron, nickel and copper have much higher melting points (copper melts at 1085 °C) than the Group 1 metals (sodium melts at 98 °C); transition elements are much denser (copper 8.96 g/cm³, against sodium's 0.97 g/cm³, which is low enough for sodium to float on water); and transition elements are hard and strong while Group 1 metals are soft enough to cut with a knife. Chemically, the transition elements are far less reactive: iron rusts only slowly in damp air and copper does not react with cold water at all, whereas lithium, sodium and potassium tarnish in air within seconds and react vigorously with water to give a hydroxide and hydrogen, the vigour increasing down the group; transition elements also react only slowly with oxygen and the halogens. In addition, many transition elements form ions with different charges (Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺), form coloured compounds and act as catalysts, whereas Group 1 metals form only 1+ ions and white or colourless compounds.","Level 2 (3-4 marks): gives several correct differences, for example that transition metals are harder and melt at higher temperatures and that Group 1 metals are more reactive, but some points describe only one set of metals rather than comparing the two, named elements are missing, or the physical and chemical differences are not separated.","Level 1 (1-2 marks): makes one or two isolated statements, such as 'transition metals are harder' or 'Group 1 metals react with water', with no comparison of the two sets and no named examples.","What a bottom-level (Level 1) answer misses: it states a property of one set of metals without saying what the other set does, so nothing is actually compared; it gives no named elements from Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni or Cu or from Li, Na and K; and it does not separate physical properties (melting point, density, hardness, strength) from chemical reactivity with oxygen, water and the halogens."]},{"kind":"formulae","title":"Key equations, numbers and formulae for Atomic structure and the periodic table","items":["Relative atomic mass: Ar = (mass number of isotope 1 × % abundance) + (mass number of isotope 2 × % abundance) + ..., all divided by 100.","Relative charges: proton +1, neutron 0, electron -1. Relative masses: proton 1, neutron 1, electron very small (negligible compared with a proton or neutron).","Atomic number = number of protons = number of electrons in an atom. Mass number = number of protons + number of neutrons. Neutrons = mass number - atomic number.","Atomic radius: about 0.1 nm (1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m). Nuclear radius: about 1 × 10⁻¹⁴ m, less than 1/10 000 of the atom's radius.","Example symbol equations: 2Na + Cl₂ -> 2NaCl; 4Li + O₂ -> 2Li₂O; 2K + 2H₂O -> 2KOH + H₂; H₂ + Cl₂ -> 2HCl; Cl₂ + 2KBr -> 2KCl + Br₂.","(Higher tier) Ionic equations leave out the spectator ions: Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ -> 2Cl⁻ + Br₂. Half equations show the electrons and balance for charge: Na -> Na⁺ + e⁻; 2Br⁻ -> Br₂ + 2e⁻.","Electronic structure examples: sodium 2,8,1; magnesium 2,8,2; aluminium 2,8,3; chlorine 2,8,7; argon 2,8,8."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Groups 0, 1 and 7 at a glance","items":["Group 0 (noble gases): a stable outer shell of 8 electrons (helium has 2); unreactive and do not easily form molecules; boiling point increases down the group as relative atomic mass increases.","Group 1 (alkali metals): 1 outer electron; soft, low-density metals; reactivity increases down the group (lithium, then sodium, then potassium); with oxygen they give a metal oxide, with chlorine a metal chloride, and with water a metal hydroxide plus hydrogen.","Group 7 (halogens): 7 outer electrons; non-metals existing as molecules of two atoms (Cl₂, Br₂, I₂); with metals they form ionic metal halides, with non-metals covalent molecular compounds; relative molecular mass, melting point and boiling point increase down the group while reactivity decreases.","A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive halogen from an aqueous solution of its salt, e.g. chlorine displaces bromine from potassium bromide solution."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"The transition elements (chemistry only)","items":["Where they are: the block of metals between Group 2 and Group 3. The specification's named examples are Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni and Cu.","Physical properties compared with Group 1: higher melting points (copper 1085 °C against sodium 98 °C), higher densities (copper 8.96 g/cm³ against sodium 0.97 g/cm³), stronger and harder — Group 1 metals are soft enough to cut with a knife and float on water.","Chemical reactivity compared with Group 1: transition elements react slowly or not at all with oxygen, water and the halogens; Group 1 metals tarnish in air at once and react vigorously with water to give a hydroxide and hydrogen.","Ions with different charges: iron forms Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺, copper forms Cu⁺ and Cu²⁺, chromium forms Cr²⁺ and Cr³⁺ — which is why compounds are named with a Roman numeral, e.g. iron(II) sulfate.","Coloured compounds: copper(II) sulfate solution is blue, iron(II) compounds pale green, iron(III) compounds orange-brown, nickel(II) compounds green, cobalt(II) compounds pink — Group 1 compounds are white or colourless.","Useful as catalysts: iron in the Haber process, nickel in the hydrogenation of vegetable oils, manganese(IV) oxide in the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T11:18:58Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"No wrong answer keys: I re-solved every calculation in Python before reading the keys and all agree - Ar(Cl) = (35 x 75 + 37 x 25)/100 = 3550/100 = 35.5, Ar(Cu) = (63 x 69 + 65 x 31)/100 = 6362/100 = 63.62 = 63.6 to 1 dp, sodium-23 = 11 protons / 12 neutrons / 11 electrons, magnesium-24 = 12/12/12 and Mg2+ = 12/12/10, and the electronic structures Na 2,8,1, Mg 2,8,2, Al 2,8,3, Cl 2,8,7, Ar 2,8,8 (and K 2,8,8,1, Ca 2,8,8,2 implied by the 2,8,8 rule) all follow from filling 2 then 8 then 8. Every symbol equation balances atom by atom when parsed mechanically (2Na + Cl2 -> 2NaCl; 4Li + O2 -> 2Li2O; 2K + 2H2O -> 2KOH + H2; 2Li + 2H2O -> 2LiOH + H2 with 2 Li, 4 H, 2 O each side; H2 + Cl2 -> 2HCl; Cl2 + 2KBr -> 2KCl + Br2; Cl2 + 2KI -> 2KCl + I2), and the Higher tier ionic and half equations balance for charge as well as atoms (Cl2 + 2Br- -> 2Cl- + Br2, -2 each side; Na -> Na+ + e-; 2Br- -> Br2 + 2e-). Against the specification: all fifteen ideas of 4.1.1 to 4.1.3 have a lesson section, a card and at least one question; both (chemistry only) ideas 4.1.3.1 and 4.1.3.2 are taught with the named exemplars Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu and tested at indexes 9 and 15; the single (HT only) statement in 4.1.1.1 is flagged \"Higher tier only\" in the lesson and tested by the labelled index 13. 4.1 names no required practical (chemistry's eight sit in 4.2 onwards, chromatography in 4.8), so the absence of a method sheet is correct. Values check out - copper 1085 C and 8.96 g/cm3 against sodium 98 C and 0.97 g/cm3, atomic radius 0.1 nm = 1 x 10-10 m, nuclear radius about 1 x 10-14 m and under 1/10 000 of the atom, Chadwick about 20 years after the nucleus was accepted, about 100 elements - as do the reactivity trends (Group 1 increasing down, Group 7 decreasing down, Group 0 boiling points rising with relative atomic mass), the displacement rule, the transition-metal colours (blue copper(II) sulfate, pale green iron(II), orange-brown iron(III), green nickel(II), pink cobalt(II)), the variable charges (Fe2+/Fe3+, Cu+/Cu2+, Cr2+/Cr3+) and the catalysts (iron in the Haber process, nickel in hydrogenation, manganese(IV) oxide on hydrogen peroxide). Every power is written with Unicode superscripts and there is not a single caret anywhere in the kit, unlike the Trilogy sibling. 16 questions (5 at d1, 6 at d2, 5 at d3), two six-mark extended responses with full levels, 15 cards all within the word limit, a four-question exit ticket, and kit-tools check returns OK. The five faults above are pedagogical rather than factual, so this is comfortably publishable; fix the index 4 hint first."}}},"4.2":{"id":"AQA-8462","topic":"4.2","family":"science","lesson":{"why":"This room covers how ionic, covalent and metallic bonding hold matter together, and how each type of structure explains a substance's physical properties. Explaining properties from structure and bonding is one of AQA's most heavily examined chemistry skills: it appears as short recall questions, as 'explain' questions worth two to four marks on unfamiliar ionic or covalent substances, and as six-mark extended responses comparing structures such as diamond and graphite. You also need to read and criticise dot-and-cross, ball-and-stick and 2D/3D diagrams of structures, and, at Higher tier, evaluate the limitations of the simple particle model used for changes of state. GCSE Chemistry also examines two ideas that Combined Science does not: the sizes of nanoparticles, fine particles and coarse particles with the surface area to volume ratio that goes with them, and the uses and possible risks of nanoparticles, which is where the command word 'Evaluate' turns up most often in this topic.","idea":[{"code":"4.2.1.1","h":"The three types of strong bond","t":"There are three types of strong chemical bond: ionic (between oppositely charged ions), covalent (atoms sharing pairs of electrons) and metallic (atoms sharing delocalised electrons). Ionic bonding occurs between a metal and a non-metal; covalent bonding occurs in non-metallic elements and in compounds of non-metals; metallic bonding occurs in metals and alloys. In exam questions you're expected to identify which type of bond forms from the elements involved, and to explain bonding in terms of electrostatic forces of attraction and the transfer or sharing of electrons, not just name the bond type."},{"code":"4.2.1.2","h":"Ionic bonding: electron transfer","t":"When a metal atom reacts with a non-metal atom, electrons are transferred from the metal's outer shell to the non-metal's outer shell. The metal atom loses electrons and becomes a positively charged ion; the non-metal atom gains electrons and becomes a negatively charged ion. For metals in Groups 1 and 2 and non-metals in Groups 6 and 7, the resulting ions have the electronic structure of a noble gas (Group 0). You need to work out ion charges from the group number, and describe the electron transfer for a given pair of elements, either as a dot and cross diagram or in words: which atom loses how many electrons, and which atom gains them."},{"code":"4.2.1.3","h":"Ionic compounds: giant lattices","t":"An ionic compound is a giant structure of ions held together by strong electrostatic forces of attraction between oppositely charged ions, acting in all directions through the lattice. Sodium chloride is the example you must know, shown as dot and cross, ball and stick or 2D/3D lattice diagrams; each form has limitations (dot and cross doesn't show the 3D arrangement or relative sizes; ball and stick exaggerates the gaps between ions; 2D diagrams can't show the full 3D pattern). From a diagram showing the ratio of ions, you should be able to work out the empirical formula, for example equal numbers of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions give the formula NaCl."},{"code":"4.2.1.4","h":"Covalent bonding: shared electron pairs","t":"When atoms share a pair of electrons, they form a strong covalent bond. Covalently bonded substances fall into three groups: small molecules (such as hydrogen, chlorine or water), very large molecules called polymers, and giant covalent structures (such as diamond and silicon dioxide). You need to draw dot and cross diagrams for hydrogen, chlorine, oxygen, nitrogen, hydrogen chloride, water, ammonia and methane, showing which electrons are shared between which atoms; represent a single covalent bond as a line, in small molecules, in polymer repeating units (the unit in brackets with n outside, where n is a large number) and in giant covalent structures; and deduce a molecular formula from a diagram by counting the atoms shown."},{"code":"4.2.1.5","h":"Metallic bonding: delocalised electrons","t":"Metals are giant structures of atoms arranged in a regular pattern. The outer-shell electrons of metal atoms become delocalised, free to move through the whole structure, and the sharing of these delocalised electrons gives rise to strong metallic bonds. Exam questions ask you to describe this structure, a lattice of positive ions surrounded by a 'sea' of delocalised electrons, and to link it to the properties covered later in this topic, particularly conduction and malleability. Both 'atoms sharing delocalised electrons' and 'positive ions surrounded by delocalised electrons' are accepted ways to describe metallic bonding, and either can be used in an exam answer."},{"code":"4.2.2.1","h":"The three states of matter and the particle model","t":"The three states of matter are solid, liquid and gas; melting and freezing happen at the melting point, boiling and condensing at the boiling point. The simple particle model represents particles as small solid spheres, and particle theory explains melting, boiling, freezing and condensing. The energy needed to change state depends on the strength of the forces between particles, which depends on the type of bonding and structure: stronger forces mean higher melting and boiling points. You should be able to predict states from given melting and boiling point data, explain changes of state in terms of energy transfer and bonding type, and recognise that atoms themselves do not have the bulk properties of materials. (HT only) You must also explain the limitations of the simple model: it has no forces between the particles, it represents all particles as spheres, and those spheres are solid and inelastic, which real particles are not."},{"code":"4.2.2.2","h":"State symbols","t":"In chemical equations, the state of each substance is shown after its formula: (s) for solid, (l) for liquid, (g) for gas, and (aq) for a substance dissolved in water (aqueous). You are expected to include the correct state symbol for every substance in any equation you write for a reaction in this specification, and examiners will credit or penalise a missing or wrong state symbol as part of the equation mark."},{"code":"4.2.2.3","h":"Properties of ionic compounds","t":"Ionic compounds have giant ionic lattices with strong electrostatic forces of attraction acting in all directions between oppositely charged ions. Breaking the very large number of strong bonds in the lattice takes a lot of energy, so ionic compounds have high melting and boiling points. When an ionic compound is melted or dissolved in water, its ions become free to move, so charge can flow and the substance conducts electricity; a solid ionic compound cannot conduct because its ions are fixed in place in the lattice. You only need to know sodium chloride's structure, but must apply this reasoning to any ionic compound described in a question."},{"code":"4.2.2.4","h":"Properties of small molecules","t":"Substances made of small molecules (such as oxygen or water) are usually gases or liquids with relatively low melting and boiling points. This is because only the weak intermolecular forces between molecules, not the strong covalent bonds within them, are overcome on melting or boiling. Intermolecular forces increase with molecule size, so larger molecules have higher melting and boiling points. These substances don't conduct electricity because the molecules carry no overall electric charge. Expect 'explain' questions asking you to compare the melting or boiling points of two molecular substances, or to explain why a molecular substance doesn't conduct."},{"code":"4.2.2.5","h":"Polymers","t":"Polymer molecules are very large, and the atoms within each molecule are linked to other atoms by strong covalent bonds. Unlike small molecules, the intermolecular forces between separate polymer molecules are relatively strong, because the molecules are so large, so polymers are solids at room temperature. You need to recognise a polymer from a diagram of its bonding and structure: look for a repeating unit joined by covalent bonds, often shown with the unit in brackets and 'n' outside to show it repeats many times."},{"code":"4.2.2.6","h":"Giant covalent structures","t":"Giant covalent structures, such as diamond, graphite and silicon dioxide (silica), are solids with very high melting points, because every atom is linked to other atoms by strong covalent bonds throughout the whole structure, and all of these many bonds must be overcome to melt or boil the substance. You need to recognise a giant covalent structure from a diagram: look for a repeating network of atoms joined by single lines (bonds), with no separate small molecules and no free ions."},{"code":"4.2.2.7","h":"Properties of metals and alloys","t":"Metals have giant structures of atoms held together by strong metallic bonding, so most have high melting and boiling points. In a pure metal, atoms are arranged in regular layers that can slide over each other, which is why metals can be bent and shaped rather than shattering. Pure metals are too soft for many uses, so they are mixed with other metals to make alloys; the different-sized atoms in an alloy distort the regular layers, making it harder for the layers to slide over one another, so alloys are harder than the pure metal."},{"code":"4.2.2.8","h":"Metals as conductors","t":"Metals are good conductors of electricity because their delocalised electrons are free to move through the structure and carry electrical charge through the metal. The same delocalised electrons also make metals good conductors of thermal energy, transferring energy quickly through the structure. Both properties trace back to the same feature of metallic bonding, so exam answers should name the delocalised electrons explicitly rather than just saying 'metals conduct well'."},{"code":"4.2.3.1","h":"Diamond","t":"In diamond, every carbon atom forms four covalent bonds with other carbon atoms, building a rigid giant covalent structure. This explains diamond's properties: it is very hard, because the rigid network of strong bonds resists being broken or distorted; it has a very high melting point, because all those covalent bonds must be broken to melt it; and it does not conduct electricity, because there are no ions and no delocalised electrons free to move and carry charge."},{"code":"4.2.3.2","h":"Graphite","t":"In graphite, each carbon atom forms three covalent bonds with three other carbon atoms, forming layers of hexagonal rings which have no covalent bonds between the layers, so the layers can slide over each other. Because each carbon atom uses only three of its four outer electrons in bonding, one electron from each carbon atom is delocalised, free to move through the structure, so graphite conducts electricity and thermal energy. This is the way in which graphite is similar to a metal, even though it is a giant covalent structure."},{"code":"4.2.3.3","h":"Graphene and fullerenes","t":"Graphene is a single layer of graphite, one atom thick, and its strength, flexibility and delocalised electrons make it useful in electronics and composites. Fullerenes are molecules of carbon atoms with hollow shapes, based on hexagonal rings of carbon atoms but which may also contain rings with five or seven carbon atoms; Buckminsterfullerene, C₆₀, a spherical molecule, was the first fullerene discovered. Carbon nanotubes are cylindrical fullerenes with very high length to diameter ratios, useful for nanotechnology, electronics and materials. You need to recognise graphene and fullerenes from diagrams or descriptions, and give examples of their uses."},{"code":"4.2.4.1","h":"Sizes of particles and their properties (chemistry only)","t":"This idea is chemistry only, so it is examined in GCSE Chemistry but not in Combined Science. Nanoscience refers to structures that are 1–100 nm in size, of the order of a few hundred atoms; since an atom is about 0.1 nm (1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m) across, a nanoparticle 1 nm across is about ten atoms wide (1 nm ÷ 0.1 nm = 10) and one 100 nm across is about a thousand atoms wide (100 nm ÷ 0.1 nm = 1000). Nanoparticles are smaller than fine particles (PM2.5), which have diameters between 100 and 2500 nm (1 × 10⁻⁷ m and 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m); coarse particles (PM10), often called dust, have diameters between 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m and 1 × 10⁻⁵ m. The quantitative rule you must be able to use is that as the side of a cube decreases by a factor of 10, the surface area to volume ratio increases by a factor of 10 (for a cube of side L, surface area = 6L², volume = L³, so the ratio is 6/L). Because of this high surface area to volume ratio, nanoparticles may have properties different from those of the same material in bulk, and smaller quantities may be needed to be effective than for materials with normal particle sizes."},{"code":"4.2.4.2","h":"Uses of nanoparticles (chemistry only)","t":"This idea is chemistry only. Nanoparticles have many applications: in medicine, in electronics, in cosmetics and sun creams, as deodorants, and as catalysts, and finding new applications for nanoparticulate materials is an important area of research. You are not asked for specific named examples beyond these, but you are asked to weigh advantages against disadvantages, and, given appropriate information in the question, to evaluate the use of nanoparticles for a stated purpose and to explain that there are possible risks. The advantages usually come back to the high surface area to volume ratio (a small quantity is effective, a catalyst works faster, a sun cream is transparent rather than white); the risks are that their effects on health and on the environment are not fully known, that particles this small may be absorbed into the body or pass into cells, and that long-term effects have not been tested. An 'evaluate' answer must use the information given, give points for and against, and end with a judgement."}],"examples":[{"title":"Finding ion charges from the group number","setup":"Magnesium is in Group 2 of the periodic table and oxygen is in Group 6. Magnesium burns in oxygen to form the ionic compound magnesium oxide. Determine the charge on each ion formed, and use the charges to give the formula of magnesium oxide.","steps":["Magnesium is in Group 2, so a magnesium atom loses 2 electrons to form Mg²⁺.","Oxygen is in Group 6, so an oxygen atom gains 2 electrons to form O²⁻.","The 2+ charge and the 2− charge balance exactly, so the ions combine in a 1:1 ratio.","The formula of magnesium oxide is MgO."],"cues":["Is magnesium a metal or a non-metal, and does it lose or gain electrons?","How many electrons must an oxygen atom gain to fill its outer shell?","What must the total positive and negative charge in the compound add up to?","How do you write a 1:1 ratio of ions as a formula?"]},{"title":"Describing electron transfer in sodium chloride, in words","setup":"Sodium (2,8,1) reacts with chlorine (2,8,7) to make sodium chloride. Describe in words the electron transfer that happens when one sodium atom reacts with one chlorine atom, and say what holds the resulting particles together.","steps":["A sodium atom (electron structure 2,8,1) transfers its 1 outer electron to a chlorine atom (2,8,7).","The sodium atom becomes Na⁺, with the stable electron structure 2,8.","The chlorine atom becomes Cl⁻, with the stable electron structure 2,8,8.","The oppositely charged Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions attract each other strongly, forming an ionic bond."],"cues":["Which of the two atoms is the metal, and which way will the electron move?","What charge is left on the sodium particle once the electron has gone?","What is the chlorine particle's charge and electron structure now?","What force now acts between the two particles you have made?"]},{"title":"Describing covalent bonding in a water molecule, in words","setup":"A water molecule contains one oxygen atom joined to two hydrogen atoms. An oxygen atom has 6 electrons in its outer shell and a hydrogen atom has 1 electron in its outer shell. Explain how the bonding gives every atom in the molecule a full outer shell, and state how many covalent bonds the molecule contains.","steps":["Oxygen needs 2 more electrons to fill its outer shell of 8, and each hydrogen needs 1 more electron to fill its shell of 2.","The oxygen atom shares one pair of electrons with each hydrogen atom, forming two single covalent bonds.","Each shared pair counts towards both atoms' outer shells, so oxygen ends up with 8 outer electrons and each hydrogen ends up with 2.","So the molecule contains 2 covalent bonds, written H₂O or H–O–H."],"cues":["How many more electrons does each atom need for a full outer shell?","If no electrons are transferred, what must the atoms do with them instead?","Whose outer shell does a shared pair of electrons count towards?","How many shared pairs are there in total, and how is that written as a formula?"]},{"title":"Classifying particles and finding a surface area to volume ratio (chemistry only)","setup":"Powder A is made of cube-shaped particles with sides of 1 × 10⁻⁸ m. Powder B is made of cube-shaped particles with sides of 1 × 10⁻⁶ m. Classify the particles in each powder as nanoparticles, fine particles (PM2.5) or coarse particles (PM10), then calculate the surface area to volume ratio of one cube from each powder and state how many times greater one ratio is than the other.","steps":["Converting to nanometres, since 1 nm = 1 × 10⁻⁹ m: the sides are 10 nm in powder A and 1000 nm in powder B.","10 nm lies inside the 1–100 nm range, so powder A is made of nanoparticles; 1000 nm lies between 100 nm and 2500 nm, so powder B is made of fine particles (PM2.5).","For a cube of side L, surface area = 6L² and volume = L³, so the surface area to volume ratio = 6L²/L³ = 6/L.","Powder A: 6 ÷ (1 × 10⁻⁸ m) = 6 × 10⁸ m⁻¹. Powder B: 6 ÷ (1 × 10⁻⁶ m) = 6 × 10⁶ m⁻¹.","The nanoparticle ratio is (6 × 10⁸) ÷ (6 × 10⁶) = 100 times greater, because the side is 100 times smaller."],"cues":["How many nanometres is each side, given the size of one nanometre in metres?","Which size band does each of those two lengths fall into?","What are the surface area and the volume of a cube of side L?","What does 6/L come to for each of the two side lengths?","How do the two ratios compare, and how does that match the change in side length?"]},{"title":"Comparing the boiling points of chlorine and bromine","setup":"Chlorine, Cl₂, and bromine, Br₂, both consist of small molecules. A bromine molecule is larger than a chlorine molecule. Predict which of the two has the higher boiling point and explain your answer in terms of the forces involved.","steps":["On boiling, it is the weak intermolecular forces between the molecules that must be overcome, not the strong covalent bonds inside each molecule.","Intermolecular forces increase with the size of the molecules.","More energy is therefore needed to overcome the forces between the larger bromine molecules than between chlorine molecules.","Bromine has the higher boiling point."],"cues":["When a molecular substance boils, which forces actually break?","What happens to the strength of those forces as molecules get bigger?","Which substance therefore needs more energy to separate its molecules?","So which substance boils at the higher temperature?"]},{"title":"Explaining why graphite conducts electricity but diamond does not","setup":"Diamond and graphite are both giant covalent structures made only of carbon atoms. In diamond each carbon atom forms four covalent bonds; in graphite each carbon atom forms three. Graphite conducts electricity but diamond does not. Explain this difference in terms of structure and bonding.","steps":["In diamond, each carbon atom uses all four of its outer electrons in bonding.","With no delocalised electrons and no ions, diamond has no charged particles free to move, so it cannot conduct electricity.","In graphite, each carbon atom uses only three of its outer electrons in bonding, leaving one electron from each carbon atom delocalised.","These delocalised electrons are free to move through the structure and carry charge, so graphite conducts electricity."],"cues":["How many of carbon's outer electrons are used up by four bonds?","What kind of particle has to be free to move for a substance to conduct?","How many outer electrons are left over on each carbon atom in graphite?","What can that spare electron do, and what does that allow graphite to do?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What type of bonding would you expect between potassium and bromine?","a":"Ionic bonding, because a Group 1 metal is combining with a Group 7 non-metal."},{"q":"What charge does an ion formed by a Group 1 element have?","a":"1+, because it loses one electron."},{"q":"Is diamond a small molecule, a polymer or a giant covalent structure?","a":"A giant covalent structure."},{"q":"Why do metals conduct electricity?","a":"Their delocalised electrons are free to move through the structure and carry charge."},{"q":"A particle has a diameter of 50 nm. Is it a nanoparticle or a fine particle?","a":"A nanoparticle: 50 nm is inside the 1–100 nm range, while fine particles (PM2.5) are 100–2500 nm across."}]},"room":{"facts":["Chemistry Paper 1 examines topics 4.1 to 4.5, so this room is a Paper 1 topic: a written exam of 1 hour 45 minutes, worth 100 marks and 50 % of the GCSE.","The entry code is 8462F for Foundation Tier and 8462H for Higher Tier; content marked (HT only), such as the limitations of the simple particle model, is examined on Higher Tier papers only.","Questions drawing on the knowledge and understanding gained from the eight required practical activities are worth at least 15 % of the marks for the qualification.","Ion charges from group number: Group 1 atoms lose 1 electron to form a 1+ ion; Group 2 atoms lose 2 electrons to form a 2+ ion; Group 6 atoms gain 2 electrons to form a 2− ion; Group 7 atoms gain 1 electron to form a 1− ion.","Ionic bonding is the strong electrostatic force of attraction between oppositely charged ions, acting in all directions in a giant lattice.","Covalent bonding is a shared pair of electrons between two atoms; covalently bonded substances are small molecules, polymers, or giant covalent structures.","Metallic bonding is the strong attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons that are free to move through the structure.","State symbols: (s) solid, (l) liquid, (g) gas, (aq) aqueous (dissolved in water).","In diamond, each carbon atom forms 4 covalent bonds. In graphite, each carbon atom forms 3 covalent bonds and has 1 delocalised electron per atom.","Ionic compounds conduct electricity only when molten or dissolved, because the ions are then free to move; solid ionic compounds do not conduct, because their ions are fixed in the lattice.","Nanoscience deals with structures 1–100 nm in size; fine particles (PM2.5) are 1 × 10⁻⁷ m to 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m across and coarse particles (PM10), or dust, are 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m to 1 × 10⁻⁵ m across.","Surface area to volume ratio of a cube of side L = 6L² ÷ L³ = 6/L, so dividing the side by a factor of 10 multiplies the ratio by a factor of 10."],"questions":[{"q":"Name the type of strong chemical bond in each of these substances: magnesium chloride, ammonia, copper.","a":"Magnesium chloride: ionic. Ammonia: covalent. Copper: metallic.","sol":"Magnesium chloride is a metal combined with a non-metal, so the bonding is ionic (1). Ammonia, NH₃, is a compound of two non-metals, so the bonding is covalent (1). Copper is a metallic element, so the bonding is metallic (1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["For each substance, decide whether the elements in it are metals, non-metals or a mixture of the two.","Each of the three types of strong bond is linked to one of those combinations of elements.","Give one bond type for each of the three substances, in the order they are listed."],"codes":["4.2.1.1"]},{"q":"Magnesium is in Group 2 of the periodic table. Determine the charge on the ion that a magnesium atom forms.","a":"2+ (Mg²⁺)","sol":"A magnesium atom is in Group 2, so it has 2 electrons in its outer shell; it loses both of these to gain the electronic structure of a noble gas, leaving 2 more protons than electrons, so the magnesium ion has a charge of 2+ (1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Magnesium is a metal, so think about what metal atoms do with their outer electrons.","The group number tells you how many electrons an atom of that element has in its outer shell.","Your answer is a single number with a + or − sign after it."],"codes":["4.2.1.2"]},{"q":"Silver nitrate solution reacts with sodium chloride solution to form a precipitate of silver chloride and a solution of sodium nitrate. Complete the equation by giving the state symbol for each of the four substances: AgNO₃(…) + NaCl(…) → AgCl(…) + NaNO₃(…)","a":"AgNO₃(aq) + NaCl(aq) → AgCl(s) + NaNO₃(aq)","sol":"Silver nitrate, sodium chloride and sodium nitrate are all dissolved in water, so each is written (aq) for aqueous (1). Silver chloride is formed as a precipitate, which is a solid, so it is written (s) (1). The completed equation is AgNO₃(aq) + NaCl(aq) → AgCl(s) + NaNO₃(aq).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Three of the four substances are described in the question as solutions.","Think about which state a precipitate is in when it forms in a solution.","Give four state symbols in the order the substances appear in the equation."],"codes":["4.2.2.2"]},{"q":"State what a carbon nanotube is, and give one use of carbon nanotubes.","a":"A cylindrical fullerene with a very high length to diameter ratio; used in nanotechnology, electronics or materials.","sol":"A carbon nanotube is a fullerene shaped like a hollow cylinder, made of hexagonal rings of carbon atoms, with a very high length to diameter ratio (1). Carbon nanotubes are useful for nanotechnology, for electronics or for materials; any one of these is a use (1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Carbon nanotubes belong to one of the groups of hollow carbon molecules in this topic.","Think about the shape the nanotube is named after, and how its length compares with its width.","Describe the shape first, then add one field the nanotubes are used in."],"codes":["4.2.3.3"]},{"q":"Explain, in terms of electron transfer, how a calcium atom and an oxygen atom form the ionic compound calcium oxide, CaO.","a":"Calcium transfers its 2 outer electrons to oxygen, forming Ca²⁺ and O²⁻ ions that are attracted to each other.","sol":"Calcium is a metal in Group 2, so a calcium atom has 2 electrons in its outer shell and transfers both of them to an oxygen atom, which is in Group 6 and needs 2 more electrons for a full outer shell (1). The calcium atom becomes a Ca²⁺ ion and the oxygen atom becomes an O²⁻ ion, each with the electronic structure of a noble gas (1). The oppositely charged ions are held together by a strong electrostatic force of attraction, the ionic bond, and because the charges are equal and opposite the ions combine in a 1:1 ratio, CaO (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The group numbers of calcium and oxygen tell you how many outer electrons each atom has.","Decide which atom loses electrons and which gains them, then how many move.","Your answer should end with what holds the two charged particles together."],"codes":["4.2.1.2"]},{"q":"Ammonia, NH₃, is a small molecule. Describe, in words, the dot and cross diagram you would draw for one molecule of ammonia. Your description should say what is drawn for each atom, how many shared pairs of electrons there are, and how many electrons are shown around the nitrogen atom and around each hydrogen atom.","a":"A nitrogen atom at the centre with three hydrogen atoms round it; three overlaps, each holding one shared pair drawn as one dot and one cross, plus one non-bonding pair on the nitrogen; 8 electrons round the nitrogen and 2 round each hydrogen.","sol":"A dot and cross diagram shows only the outer shell electrons, with the electrons of one element drawn as dots and those of the other as crosses. Draw the nitrogen atom's outer shell in the centre with the outer shell of each of the three hydrogen atoms overlapping it (1). A nitrogen atom has 5 outer electrons and each hydrogen atom has 1, so in each of the three overlaps there is one shared pair, drawn as one dot and one cross, giving three shared pairs, one for each N–H single covalent bond (1). The 2 outer electrons of nitrogen that are not shared are drawn together as a non-bonding (lone) pair on the nitrogen, outside the overlaps (1). Counting the shared electrons for both atoms, the nitrogen shows 8 electrons in its outer shell, a full outer shell, and each hydrogen shows 2, the same as helium (1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A dot and cross diagram shows only the outer shell of each atom, using dots for the electrons of one element and crosses for those of the other.","Use the group of each element to find how many outer electrons each atom brings, and how many more each atom needs for a full outer shell.","Describe what sits at the centre, then what is in each overlap, then what is left over, and finish with a count of the electrons round each atom."],"codes":["4.2.1.4"]},{"q":"A diagram of a giant ionic lattice shows calcium ions, Ca²⁺, and chloride ions, Cl⁻, with twice as many Cl⁻ ions as Ca²⁺ ions. Determine the empirical formula of calcium chloride from this diagram.","a":"CaCl₂","sol":"The positive and negative charges in the compound must balance overall: each Ca²⁺ ion has a charge of 2+ and each Cl⁻ ion a charge of 1−, so two Cl⁻ ions are needed for every Ca²⁺ ion, which matches the 1:2 ratio shown in the diagram (1). The simplest whole-number ratio of ions is therefore 1 Ca²⁺ : 2 Cl⁻, giving the empirical formula CaCl₂ (1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["In any ionic compound the total positive charge and the total negative charge must cancel.","Use the charge on each ion to work out how many of each ion are needed for that to happen.","Write the answer as a formula, with the number of chloride ions as a subscript."],"codes":["4.2.1.3"]},{"q":"A ball and stick model of sodium chloride shows spheres of two sizes joined by sticks, with spaces between the spheres. A dot and cross diagram of sodium chloride shows one sodium ion and one chloride ion with their outer electrons. Explain one limitation of each of these two ways of representing sodium chloride.","a":"The ball and stick model shows gaps and rigid sticks between ions that are really packed closely together and attracted in all directions; the dot and cross diagram shows only one ion of each type, not the giant three-dimensional lattice or the relative sizes of the ions.","sol":"In a ball and stick model the sticks and the spaces between the spheres suggest that the ions are held apart by rods along fixed directions, whereas in the real giant ionic lattice the ions are packed closely together and the electrostatic force of attraction acts in all directions, and the spheres do not show the true relative sizes of the ions (1). A dot and cross diagram shows the electron transfer for a single pair of ions and only their outer electrons, so it does not show that sodium chloride is a giant structure containing a very large number of ions, nor how those ions are arranged in three dimensions (1). Both are still useful: the dot and cross diagram shows where the electrons have gone, and the ball and stick model shows the regular pattern of the lattice.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what each drawing leaves out about a giant ionic structure, not what it gets wrong about the charges.","For the ball and stick model, think about the spaces and the sticks; for the dot and cross diagram, think about how many ions it shows.","Give one clear limitation for each representation in turn, each one saying what the real lattice is like instead."],"codes":["4.2.1.3"]},{"q":"Explain why sodium chloride has a high melting point.","a":"It is a giant ionic lattice with many strong electrostatic forces between oppositely charged ions, which take a lot of energy to break.","sol":"Sodium chloride has a giant ionic lattice, a regular structure containing a very large number of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions (1). The ions are held together by strong electrostatic forces of attraction between oppositely charged ions, acting in all directions through the lattice (1). Melting the compound means breaking a very large number of these strong bonds, which needs a large amount of energy, so the melting point is high (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Name the type of structure sodium chloride has, not only the type of bond.","Think about the strength of the forces between the ions and the direction in which they act.","Finish by linking the number and strength of the forces to the energy needed to melt the solid."],"codes":["4.2.2.3"]},{"q":"Ethene, C₂H₄, is a gas at room temperature. Poly(ethene), which is made from ethene, is a solid at room temperature. Explain this difference in terms of structure and bonding.","a":"Both are covalent molecules, but poly(ethene) molecules are very large so the intermolecular forces between them are relatively strong, while those between small ethene molecules are weak.","sol":"Both substances are made of covalently bonded molecules, and it is the intermolecular forces between the molecules, not the covalent bonds inside them, that have to be overcome to melt or boil them (1). Ethene molecules are small, so the intermolecular forces between them are weak and are easily overcome at room temperature, leaving ethene as a gas (1). Poly(ethene) molecules are very large, so the intermolecular forces between them are relatively strong and a large amount of energy would be needed to separate them, so poly(ethene) is a solid at room temperature (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Decide which forces are overcome when a molecular substance changes state.","Compare the size of an ethene molecule with the size of a poly(ethene) molecule.","Deal with each substance in turn, linking molecule size to force strength and then to state."],"codes":["4.2.2.4","4.2.2.5"]},{"q":"The table gives melting and boiling points. Substance P: melting point −95 °C, boiling point 56 °C. Substance Q: melting point 0 °C, boiling point 100 °C. Substance R: melting point 801 °C, boiling point 1413 °C. Predict the state of each substance at 20 °C and explain your reasoning for substance Q.","a":"P is a liquid, Q is a liquid and R is a solid; Q is a liquid because 20 °C is above its melting point but below its boiling point.","sol":"Substance P is a liquid at 20 °C, because 20 °C is above its melting point of −95 °C but below its boiling point of 56 °C (1). Substance Q is a liquid at 20 °C (1), because 20 °C is above the melting point of 0 °C, so Q has melted, but below the boiling point of 100 °C, so it has not yet boiled (1). Substance R is a solid at 20 °C, because 20 °C is well below its melting point of 801 °C, so it has not melted (1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A substance changes from solid to liquid at its melting point and from liquid to gas at its boiling point.","For each substance, compare 20 °C with both of the temperatures given for it.","Give the state of all three substances, then add the reasoning for the one named in the question."],"codes":["4.2.2.1"]},{"q":"A sample of airborne particles collected beside a road contains particles with a diameter of 8 × 10⁻⁷ m. An atom has a diameter of about 1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m. Determine whether these particles are nanoparticles, fine particles or coarse particles, and calculate how many times wider one particle is than an atom.","a":"They are fine particles (PM2.5); each particle is 8000 times wider than an atom.","sol":"First convert the diameter to nanometres: 1 nm = 1 × 10⁻⁹ m, so 8 × 10⁻⁷ m = 800 nm (1). Fine particles (PM2.5) have diameters between 100 nm and 2500 nm, and 800 nm lies in that range, so these are fine particles, not nanoparticles (1–100 nm) and not coarse particles (1). Number of times wider = particle diameter ÷ atom diameter = (8 × 10⁻⁷ m) ÷ (1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m) = 8000, so one particle is 8000 times wider than an atom (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The size bands for nanoparticles and fine particles are quoted in nanometres, so put the diameter in the same unit.","One nanometre is 1 × 10⁻⁹ m; compare your converted diameter with the band for each type of particle.","For the second part, divide one diameter by the other and give the answer as a number of times."],"codes":["4.2.4.1"]},{"q":"Copper is used for electrical wiring. Bronze, an alloy of copper and tin, is used for bearings and statues. Explain, in terms of structure and bonding, why copper conducts electricity well and why bronze is harder than pure copper.","a":"Copper's delocalised electrons are free to move and carry charge; in bronze the differently sized tin atoms distort the layers so they cannot slide over each other easily.","sol":"Copper is a giant structure of atoms in a regular pattern, and the electrons in the outer shell of each copper atom are delocalised (1). These delocalised electrons are free to move through the whole structure and carry electrical charge through the metal, so copper is a good conductor of electricity (1). In pure copper the atoms are all the same size and are arranged in layers, which can slide over each other, so the metal is soft and easily bent (1). Bronze also contains tin atoms, which are a different size, and these distort the layers so that the layers can no longer slide over one another easily, which makes bronze harder than pure copper (1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Both parts of the answer come from the same giant metallic structure, seen in two different ways.","For conduction, think about which particles in a metal can move; for hardness, think about what the atoms are arranged in.","Answer the conduction part fully first, then the hardness part, and refer to the layers of atoms in both metals."],"codes":["4.2.1.5","4.2.2.7","4.2.2.8"]},{"q":"Compare the structure and bonding of diamond and graphite, and explain why graphite is used as a lubricant but diamond is not.","a":"Both are giant covalent structures of carbon, but graphite's layers have no covalent bonds between them so they slide over each other, while diamond's rigid network of four bonds per atom cannot.","sol":"Diamond and graphite are both giant covalent structures made only of carbon atoms, in which every atom is joined to other atoms by strong covalent bonds (1). In diamond, each carbon atom forms four covalent bonds to neighbouring carbon atoms, giving a rigid three-dimensional network in which no part of the structure can slide over another (1). In graphite, each carbon atom forms only three covalent bonds, producing layers of hexagonal rings, and there are no covalent bonds between the layers (1). The layers of graphite can therefore slide over each other easily, which makes graphite slippery and useful as a lubricant, whereas diamond's rigid structure cannot slide and so is not used in this way (1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Begin from what the two forms of carbon have in common before you separate them.","Count the covalent bonds each carbon atom forms in each structure, and ask what holds graphite's layers together.","Cover both structures and then link the difference in bonding to how easily part of the structure can move."],"codes":["4.2.2.6","4.2.3.1","4.2.3.2"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) In the simple particle model the particles of every substance are drawn as small, solid, inelastic spheres with no forces between them. Sodium chloride melts at 801 °C but oxygen melts at −219 °C. Explain why this model cannot account for the difference between these two melting points, and describe two further ways in which the spheres of the model misrepresent real particles.","a":"The model has no forces between its particles, but it is the strength of those forces that decides the melting point, so the model gives no reason for the difference; it also draws every particle as a sphere and draws those spheres as solid.","sol":"Melting a substance means separating its particles, so the energy needed depends on the strength of the forces between them, which depends on the type of bonding and structure (1). The model shows no forces at all between its particles, so it cannot explain why sodium chloride, a giant lattice held by strong electrostatic forces between oppositely charged ions, needs far more energy to melt than oxygen, which is made of small molecules held together only by weak intermolecular forces (1). The model also represents the particles of every substance as identical spheres, whereas real particles may be atoms, ions or molecules of different sizes, shapes and charges — an O₂ molecule is two atoms joined, not one sphere (1). Finally the spheres are drawn solid and inelastic, whereas an atom is mostly empty space, with electrons around a tiny nucleus, and real particles are not rigid (1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The two melting points are over a thousand degrees apart, so ask first what decides how much energy a substance needs in order to melt.","Compare that quantity with what the model does, and does not, put between its particles.","Deal with the melting points fully first, then turn to the picture of the particles themselves — their shape and what they are made of — and test it against real particles."],"codes":["4.2.2.1"]},{"q":"A solid catalyst is supplied as cubes with sides of 2 × 10⁻³ m. The same material can be supplied as nanoparticle cubes with sides of 2 × 10⁻⁸ m. Calculate the surface area to volume ratio of one cube of each size, and explain why the nanoparticle form is more effective as a catalyst.","a":"3 × 10³ m⁻¹ for the large cubes and 3 × 10⁸ m⁻¹ for the nanoparticle cubes; the far greater surface area to volume ratio exposes much more surface for the reaction, so a smaller quantity is effective.","sol":"For a cube of side L, surface area = 6L² and volume = L³, so the surface area to volume ratio = 6L² ÷ L³ = 6/L (1). Large cube: 6 ÷ (2 × 10⁻³ m) = 3 × 10³ m⁻¹ (1). Nanoparticle cube: 6 ÷ (2 × 10⁻⁸ m) = 3 × 10⁸ m⁻¹, which is 1 × 10⁵ times greater (1). A catalyst works on its surface, so the much higher surface area to volume ratio of the nanoparticles exposes far more surface for the reacting substances, which is why nanoparticles may behave differently from the bulk material and why a smaller quantity of them is needed to be effective (1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Write down expressions for the surface area and the volume of a cube of side L before you put any numbers in.","Simplify 6L² ÷ L³ first, then substitute each side length in metres in turn.","For the last part, think about which part of a catalyst the reaction actually takes place on."],"codes":["4.2.4.1","4.2.4.2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Name the three types of strong chemical bond.","a":"Ionic, covalent and metallic."},{"q":"What is the charge on an ion formed by a Group 7 element?","a":"1−, because it gains one electron."},{"q":"Why does graphite conduct electricity?","a":"One electron from each carbon atom is delocalised and free to move and carry charge."},{"q":"What range of sizes does nanoscience deal with?","a":"Structures 1–100 nm in size, of the order of a few hundred atoms."}]},"cards":[{"code":"4.2.1.1","front":"The three types of strong chemical bond","back":"Ionic: oppositely charged ions. Covalent: atoms sharing pairs of electrons. Metallic: atoms sharing delocalised electrons.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.1.3","front":"Giant ionic lattice, and the limits of its diagrams","back":"A giant regular structure of oppositely charged ions attracted in all directions. Dot and cross shows no 3D arrangement or relative sizes; ball and stick exaggerates the gaps; a 2D diagram cannot show the full 3D pattern.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.1.5","front":"Metallic bonding","back":"A giant regular structure in which outer-shell electrons are delocalised; the strong attraction between the positive metal ions and these shared delocalised electrons is the metallic bond, and the electrons also carry charge and thermal energy.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.2.1","front":"The three states of matter, and the limitations of the simple particle model (HT only)","back":"Solid, liquid, gas; melting and freezing at the melting point, boiling and condensing at the boiling point. Model limitations (HT only): no forces between particles, all particles shown as spheres, and the spheres are solid.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.2.3","front":"Properties of ionic compounds","back":"Giant lattice with many strong electrostatic forces in all directions, so melting and boiling points are high. They conduct only when molten or dissolved, because only then are the ions free to move.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.2.4","front":"Properties of substances made of small molecules","back":"Low melting and boiling points, because only weak intermolecular forces are overcome, not the covalent bonds. Larger molecules mean stronger forces. They do not conduct: the molecules have no overall charge.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.2.5","front":"Why polymers are solids at room temperature","back":"Polymer molecules are very large, with atoms joined by strong covalent bonds, so the intermolecular forces between the molecules are relatively strong.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.2.6","front":"Giant covalent structures","back":"Diamond, graphite and silicon dioxide. Every atom is linked to others by strong covalent bonds throughout the structure, all of which must be overcome, so melting points are very high.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.2.7","front":"Why alloys are harder than pure metals","back":"In a pure metal the layers of atoms slide over each other. Atoms of a different size distort the layers, so they can no longer slide easily.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.2.8","front":"Why metals conduct electricity and thermal energy","back":"The delocalised electrons are free to move through the whole structure: they carry electrical charge through the metal, and they transfer energy through it, so metals also conduct thermal energy well.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.3.1","front":"Diamond","back":"Each carbon atom forms 4 covalent bonds in a rigid giant covalent structure, so diamond is very hard and has a very high melting point. It has no free electrons or ions, so it does not conduct electricity.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.3.2","front":"Graphite","back":"Each carbon atom forms 3 covalent bonds, giving layers of hexagonal rings with no covalent bonds between the layers, so the layers slide over each other. One electron per atom is delocalised, so graphite conducts.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.3.3","front":"Graphene and fullerenes","back":"Graphene is a single layer of graphite, useful in electronics and composites. Fullerenes are hollow carbon molecules based on hexagonal rings, such as C₆₀ and the cylindrical carbon nanotubes.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.4.1","front":"Sizes of nanoparticles, fine particles and coarse particles (chemistry only)","back":"Nanoscience: 1–100 nm. Fine particles (PM2.5): 1 × 10⁻⁷ m to 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m. Coarse particles (PM10), or dust: 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m to 1 × 10⁻⁵ m. Cube of side L: ratio = 6/L.","open":false},{"code":"4.2.4.2","front":"One possible risk of using nanoparticles (chemistry only)","back":"Their effects on health are not fully known; particles this small may be absorbed into the body or pass into cells; their long-term effects on the environment have not been tested.","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: bonding and structure of sodium chloride","items":["Question: explain, in terms of structure and bonding, why sodium chloride has a high melting point, and why it conducts electricity when molten but not when solid. (6 marks)","Level 3 (5–6 marks): a coherent, logically structured answer that fully explains both properties. Sodium chloride is a giant ionic lattice, in which Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions are held together by strong electrostatic forces of attraction acting in all directions. Melting the compound means breaking a very large number of these strong ionic bonds, which requires a large amount of energy, so sodium chloride has a high melting point. In the solid lattice, the ions are held in fixed positions and cannot move, so no charge can flow and solid sodium chloride does not conduct electricity. When sodium chloride is melted, the lattice structure breaks down and the ions become free to move throughout the liquid; because these charged ions are now mobile, they can carry electrical charge from place to place, so molten sodium chloride conducts electricity.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): identifies that sodium chloride is a giant ionic structure with strong forces between ions, and links this to a high melting point, and states that ions must be free to move for the compound to conduct, but does not fully explain both the solid case (fixed ions) and the liquid case (mobile ions), or one of the two properties is only partly explained.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): makes an isolated, correct statement, such as 'sodium chloride has ionic bonds' or 'it conducts when it is a liquid', without explaining the link between the strength and direction of the forces and the melting point, or without explaining the difference in ion mobility between the solid and the liquid.","What a bottom-level (Level 1) answer misses: it does not explain that it is the large number of strong electrostatic forces acting in all directions that require a lot of energy to break, giving the high melting point; and it does not contrast the fixed ions in the solid with the free-moving ions in the liquid to explain the difference in conductivity."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: evaluating nanoparticles in sun cream (chemistry only)","items":["Question: a sun cream contains nanoparticles of titanium dioxide. The same mass of titanium dioxide as normal-sized particles would make the cream white and opaque, while the nanoparticle cream is transparent and blocks the same amount of ultraviolet light using a smaller mass of titanium dioxide. Some scientists are testing whether nanoparticles can pass through skin into the body. Evaluate the use of nanoparticles of titanium dioxide in sun cream. (6 marks)","Level 3 (5–6 marks): a balanced evaluation that uses the information given, gives points for and against and ends with a judgement. In favour: nanoparticles are between 1 and 100 nm in size, so they have a very high surface area to volume ratio and their properties differ from those of the same material in bulk; this is why the cream is transparent rather than white, which makes people more willing to wear it, and why a smaller mass of titanium dioxide gives the same protection from ultraviolet light, which uses less material and may cost less. Against: because the particles are so small they may be absorbed through the skin and pass into cells, and the effects of nanoparticles on health and on the environment have not been fully tested, so the long-term risks are not yet known. Judgement: on the evidence given, the benefit of better ultraviolet protection from a cream people will actually use is worth having, but the use of nanoparticles should continue only while the testing of their effects on the body continues, and the cream should be labelled so that users can choose.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): gives relevant advantages and at least one disadvantage, for example that the cream is transparent and that less material is needed, and that the risks are not fully known, but the points are not clearly linked to the surface area to volume ratio or to the information given in the question, and there is no clear overall judgement.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): makes one or two isolated statements, such as 'nanoparticles are very small' or 'they might be dangerous', with no use of the data given and no balance between advantages and disadvantages.","What a bottom-level (Level 1) answer misses: it does not explain that the high surface area to volume ratio is why a smaller quantity is effective, it does not use the transparency and the reduced mass given in the question as evidence, it does not state that the risk is that the effects are unknown rather than proven, and it reaches no judgement, which an 'evaluate' question always requires."]},{"kind":"formulae","title":"Key facts and figures for Bonding, structure and the properties of matter","items":["Ion charges from group number: Group 1 gives 1+, Group 2 gives 2+, Group 6 gives 2−, Group 7 gives 1−.","State symbols: (s) solid, (l) liquid, (g) gas, (aq) aqueous (dissolved in water).","Diamond: each carbon atom forms 4 covalent bonds. Graphite: each carbon atom forms 3 covalent bonds, with 1 delocalised electron per atom.","Buckminsterfullerene, C₆₀, a spherical molecule, was the first fullerene discovered; carbon nanotubes are cylindrical fullerenes with very high length to diameter ratios.","Empirical formula from a lattice diagram = the simplest whole-number ratio of ions shown, found by balancing the total positive and negative charge.","Surface area to volume ratio of a cube of side L = 6L² ÷ L³ = 6/L; as the side decreases by a factor of 10, the ratio increases by a factor of 10.","Nanoscience: structures 1–100 nm in size, of the order of a few hundred atoms; 1 nm = 1 × 10⁻⁹ m and an atom is about 0.1 nm across.","Fine particles (PM2.5): diameters of 100 nm to 2500 nm, that is 1 × 10⁻⁷ m to 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m. Coarse particles (PM10), often called dust: diameters of 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m to 1 × 10⁻⁵ m."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-18T21:21:04.026703Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Every fault from the first pass is gone — question 11's stem no longer says \"dust\", the nanoparticle-width sentence now derives ten and a thousand atoms correctly (1 ÷ 0.1 = 10, 100 ÷ 0.1 = 1000), the new ammonia dot-and-cross and ball-and-stick/dot-and-cross limitation questions are both accurate, the three re-coded cards match their content, and question 14 is a sound 4-mark Higher-tier explanation using the real melting points of sodium chloride (801 °C) and oxygen (−219 °C); all surface area to volume ratios and size conversions re-solved and correct, superscripts throughout, so this is publishable as it stands."}}},"4.3":{"id":"AQA-8462","topic":"4.3","family":"science","lesson":{"why":"This room is where chemistry becomes arithmetic you can bank marks on. Quantitative chemistry is examined on Paper 1 and its ideas are also drawn on in Paper 2, and the periodic table supplied with both papers is there so you can work out relative formula masses for yourself. Every student has to conserve mass, balance equations, calculate relative formula mass, percentage composition, concentration in g/dm³ and the uncertainty in a set of repeat readings. At Higher tier the moles work sits on top of that: moles = mass ÷ Mr, reacting masses from a balanced equation, balancing numbers worked out from measured masses, and limiting reactants. GCSE Chemistry then examines four things Combined Science does not: percentage yield, atom economy, concentration in mol/dm³ with titration calculations, and the volumes of gases at room temperature and pressure. These calculations carry three and four marks at a time, and the marks are for the working as much as for the number, so you must show the equation, the substitution and the answer with its unit every time.","idea":[{"code":"4.3.1.1","h":"Conservation of mass and balancing equations","t":"In every chemical reaction, no atoms are created or destroyed, so the total mass of the reactants always equals the total mass of the products — this is the law of conservation of mass. Because atoms are only rearranged, symbol equations must be balanced so the same number of atoms of each element appears on both sides. You show this by placing multipliers in normal script in front of a formula, which multiplies the whole formula, or by reading the subscripts already inside a formula; exam questions ask you to balance given equations and to justify why mass is conserved by referring to atoms."},{"code":"4.3.1.2","h":"Relative formula mass and percentage composition","t":"The relative formula mass (Mr) of a compound is found by adding together the relative atomic masses (Ar) of every atom shown in its formula, using the periodic table you are given in the exam. In a balanced equation, the sum of the Mr values of the reactants (in the quantities shown) equals the sum of the Mr values of the products (in the quantities shown) — another way conservation of mass shows up in calculations. You are also expected to calculate the percentage by mass of one element in a compound: divide the total Ar contributed by that element by the Mr of the whole compound, then multiply by 100."},{"code":"4.3.1.3","h":"Mass changes when a gas is involved","t":"Some reactions look as though mass has been gained or lost, but this is because a reactant or product is a gas whose mass was not accounted for on the balance. When a metal burns in oxygen, the solid oxide formed weighs more than the metal because oxygen atoms from the air have joined it; when a metal carbonate is heated, carbon dioxide gas escapes into the open air, so the solid left behind (the metal oxide) weighs less than the carbonate you started with. Exam questions give you a balanced equation and ask you to explain these changes in terms of particles entering or leaving a non-enclosed system."},{"code":"4.3.1.4","h":"Chemical measurements and uncertainty","t":"Every measurement you make in chemistry — a mass, a volume, a temperature — carries some uncertainty, because no instrument or reading is perfectly precise. You are expected to represent how a set of repeat results is spread out and to estimate the uncertainty in them, usually by using the range: the difference between the highest and the lowest reading, taken about the mean. A wide range around the mean suggests more uncertainty in the measurement than a narrow one, and exam questions may ask you to calculate the mean and the range from a results table."},{"code":"4.3.2.1","h":"Moles (Higher tier only)","t":"Higher tier only: chemists count particles in an amount called the mole (mol). One mole of any substance has a mass in grams numerically equal to its relative formula mass, and contains the same number of the stated particles as one mole of anything else — atoms, molecules or ions — a number called the Avogadro constant, 6.02 × 10²³ per mole. This means one mole of carbon atoms (C) contains the same number of particles as the number of molecules in one mole of carbon dioxide (CO₂), even though the masses differ. Amounts in moles apply to atoms, molecules, ions, electrons, formulae and equations. You must be able to convert between mass and moles using moles = mass ÷ Mr, and rearrange this to find mass from moles."},{"code":"4.3.2.2","h":"Amounts of substances in equations (Higher tier only)","t":"Higher tier only: a balanced symbol equation tells you the ratio of moles reacting and forming, not just the ratio of formula units — for example Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂ means one mole of magnesium reacts with two moles of hydrochloric acid to give one mole of magnesium chloride and one mole of hydrogen. To find an unknown mass, convert the given mass to moles using Mr, use the balancing numbers to find the moles of the substance you want, then convert that back to a mass using its own Mr. This lets you calculate masses of reactants or products from a balanced equation and one known mass."},{"code":"4.3.2.3","h":"Using moles to balance equations (Higher tier only)","t":"Higher tier only: if you are given the masses of reactants and products from an experiment rather than a balanced equation, you can work out the balancing numbers yourself. Convert each mass to moles using moles = mass ÷ Mr, then divide every value by the smallest number of moles to turn them into a simple whole-number ratio — that ratio gives you the balancing numbers in front of each formula. This uses the same skill of rearranging moles = mass ÷ Mr to make mass, or Mr, the subject of the equation."},{"code":"4.3.2.4","h":"Limiting reactants (Higher tier only)","t":"Higher tier only: when a reaction uses two reactants, one is often added in excess so that the other is completely used up; the reactant that runs out first is the limiting reactant, because it limits how much product can form even though some of the other reactant is left over unreacted. You should be able to identify which reactant is limiting from the moles or masses given, and explain that the amount of product depends only on the amount of the limiting reactant, in moles or in grams, not on how much of the excess reactant was added."},{"code":"4.3.2.5","h":"Concentration of solutions","t":"Many chemical reactions take place in solutions. The concentration of a solution measures how much solute is dissolved in a given volume of solution, and can be measured as mass per given volume, usually grams per cubic decimetre (g/dm³, where 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³). To find the mass of solute in a known volume, multiply the concentration by the volume in dm³; to find the concentration, divide the mass of solute by the volume of solution in dm³. Higher tier only: you should also be able to explain how the mass of solute and the volume of solution are related to the concentration — more solute in the same volume, or the same solute in a smaller volume, means a more concentrated solution."},{"code":"4.3.3.1","h":"Percentage yield (chemistry only)","t":"Chemistry only: no atoms are gained or lost in a reaction, yet you almost never collect the mass the equation says you could. Three reasons are examined: the reaction may be reversible and so does not go to completion; some product is lost when it is separated from the reaction mixture, for example left behind on the filter paper or in the flask; and some of the reactants may react in ways different to the expected reaction, giving unwanted products. The mass you actually collect is the yield, and comparing it with the maximum theoretical mass as a percentage gives the percentage yield: % yield = (mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product) × 100. Higher tier only: you must also be able to work out that maximum theoretical mass yourself, from a given mass of reactant and the balanced equation, using moles before you can divide."},{"code":"4.3.3.2","h":"Atom economy (chemistry only)","t":"Chemistry only: atom economy (atom utilisation) measures how much of the mass of the starting materials ends up as the useful product rather than as waste. It is calculated from the balanced equation alone: percentage atom economy = (relative formula mass of the desired product from the equation ÷ sum of the relative formula masses of all the reactants from the equation) × 100. A reaction with only one product has an atom economy of 100 %; every extra product lowers it. High atom economy matters for sustainable development, because less of the raw material is wasted, and for economic reasons, because raw materials cost money and the waste has to be separated and disposed of. Higher tier only: given data such as atom economy, percentage yield, rate, the position of equilibrium and how useful the by-products are, you must be able to explain why a particular reaction pathway is chosen for a product."},{"code":"4.3.4","h":"Using concentrations of solutions in mol/dm³ (chemistry only) (Higher tier only)","t":"Chemistry only and Higher tier only: concentration can also be measured in moles of solute per cubic decimetre of solution, mol/dm³. The amount in moles of solute in a given volume is moles = concentration (mol/dm³) × volume (dm³), so concentration = moles ÷ volume, and the mass in grams follows from mass = moles × Mr. This is what makes a titration quantitative: if you know the volumes of two solutions that react completely and the concentration of one of them, you can find the moles of the known solution, use the balancing numbers in the equation to get the moles of the other, and divide by its volume in dm³ to find its concentration. You must also be able to explain the relationship itself — concentration in mol/dm³ rises if more solute is dissolved in the same volume of solution and falls if the same amount of solute is dissolved in a larger volume."},{"code":"4.3.5","h":"Amount of substance and the volumes of gases (chemistry only) (Higher tier only)","t":"Chemistry only and Higher tier only: equal amounts in moles of any gases occupy the same volume under the same conditions of temperature and pressure, so for gases the mole ratio in a balanced equation is also a volume ratio. At room temperature and pressure (20 °C and 1 atmosphere) one mole of any gas occupies 24 dm³, so volume (dm³) = moles × 24 and moles = volume (dm³) ÷ 24. Two kinds of question follow. Given the mass of a gas, convert it to moles with moles = mass ÷ Mr and multiply by 24 to get its volume at room temperature and pressure. Given the volume of one gas in a reaction, convert it to moles, use the balancing numbers to find the moles of another gas, and convert back to a volume — which means changing the subject of the equation confidently in both directions."}],"examples":[{"title":"Balancing the burning of magnesium","setup":"Magnesium burns in air to form magnesium oxide. A student writes the equation as Mg + O₂ → MgO. Balance this symbol equation.","steps":["Mg + O₂ → MgO has 1 Mg and 2 O on the left, but only 1 Mg and 1 O on the right.","Placing a 2 in front of MgO gives 2MgO: now 1 Mg and 2 O on the right, but Mg is unbalanced.","Placing a 2 in front of Mg gives 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO: 2 Mg and 2 O on both sides — balanced."],"cues":["How many atoms of each element are on each side of the equation as it stands?","Which side is short of oxygen atoms, and where can a multiplier be written to put that right?","Adding that multiplier has upset another element — which one, and what needs to change now?"]},{"title":"Percentage of calcium in calcium carbonate","setup":"Limestone is mostly calcium carbonate, CaCO₃. Using the relative atomic masses Ca = 40, C = 12 and O = 16, calculate the relative formula mass of calcium carbonate and then the percentage by mass of calcium in it.","steps":["CaCO₃ contains 1 calcium atom, 1 carbon atom and 3 oxygen atoms.","Mr = 40 + 12 + (3 × 16) = 40 + 12 + 48 = 100.","The calcium in the formula contributes 40 of those 100 units of relative mass.","Percentage of Ca = (40 ÷ 100) × 100 = 40 %."],"cues":["How many atoms of each element does the formula show?","How do the relative atomic masses combine to give the relative formula mass?","Which part of that total comes from calcium alone?","How do you turn that part of the total into a percentage?"]},{"title":"Reacting masses from an equation (Higher tier only)","setup":"Higher tier only. Magnesium burns in oxygen: 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO. A student burns 6 g of magnesium completely. Using the relative atomic masses Mg = 24 and O = 16, calculate the mass of magnesium oxide formed.","steps":["Mr(MgO) = 24 + 16 = 40.","moles of Mg = mass ÷ Ar = 6 ÷ 24 = 0.25 mol.","The equation shows 2 mol of Mg give 2 mol of MgO, a 1 : 1 ratio, so 0.25 mol of MgO forms.","mass of MgO = moles × Mr = 0.25 × 40 = 10 g."],"cues":["What relative formula mass will you need for the product?","What can the mass of magnesium be converted into before the equation can be used?","What do the balancing numbers tell you about the amount of product formed?","How do you turn that amount back into a mass?"]},{"title":"Theoretical mass and percentage yield (chemistry only, Higher tier only)","setup":"Chemistry only, Higher tier only. Calcium carbonate decomposes when heated: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. A student heats 25.0 g of calcium carbonate until it has completely decomposed and collects 12.6 g of calcium oxide. Mr(CaCO₃) = 100 and Mr(CaO) = 56. Calculate the maximum theoretical mass of calcium oxide and the percentage yield of the experiment.","steps":["moles of CaCO₃ = mass ÷ Mr = 25.0 ÷ 100 = 0.250 mol.","The equation shows 1 mol of CaCO₃ gives 1 mol of CaO, so 0.250 mol of CaO could be made.","maximum theoretical mass of CaO = moles × Mr = 0.250 × 56 = 14.0 g.","The student collected 12.6 g, less than the 14.0 g the equation allows, because some of the calcium oxide is lost when the solid is scraped out of the crucible and transferred to the balance.","% yield = (12.6 ÷ 14.0) × 100 = 90.0 %."],"cues":["Which of the two masses can be turned into an amount in moles straight away?","What do the balancing numbers say about the amount of calcium oxide that could form?","How is that amount turned into the largest mass the student could possibly collect?","How does the mass actually collected compare with it, and why is it not the same?","How are the two masses combined to give a percentage?"]},{"title":"Volume of a gas at room temperature and pressure (chemistry only, Higher tier only)","setup":"Chemistry only, Higher tier only. Methane burns completely in oxygen: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O. A burner uses 8.0 g of methane. The volume of one mole of any gas at room temperature and pressure is 24 dm³. Using the relative atomic masses C = 12 and H = 1, calculate the volume of carbon dioxide produced, measured at room temperature and pressure.","steps":["Mr(CH₄) = 12 + (4 × 1) = 16.","moles of CH₄ = mass ÷ Mr = 8.0 ÷ 16 = 0.50 mol.","The equation shows 1 mol of CH₄ gives 1 mol of CO₂, so 0.50 mol of CO₂ is produced, and each mole of any gas occupies 24 dm³ at room temperature and pressure.","volume = moles × 24 = 0.50 × 24 = 12 dm³."],"cues":["What relative formula mass do you need before the mass of methane is any use?","What can the mass of methane now be converted into?","What do the balancing numbers say about the amount of carbon dioxide made?","How is an amount in moles of a gas turned into a volume at room temperature and pressure?"]},{"title":"Concentration from a titration (chemistry only, Higher tier only)","setup":"Chemistry only, Higher tier only. In a titration, 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution needed exactly 20.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid to neutralise it. The equation is NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. Using the relative atomic masses Na = 23, O = 16 and H = 1, calculate the concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution in mol/dm³ and in g/dm³.","steps":["volume of acid = 20.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.0200 dm³.","moles of HCl = concentration × volume = 0.100 × 0.0200 = 0.00200 mol.","The equation shows 1 mol of HCl reacts with 1 mol of NaOH, so the flask held 0.00200 mol of NaOH.","volume of alkali = 25.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.0250 dm³, so concentration of NaOH = moles ÷ volume = 0.00200 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.0800 mol/dm³.","Mr(NaOH) = 23 + 16 + 1 = 40.","concentration in g/dm³ = concentration in mol/dm³ × Mr = 0.0800 × 40 = 3.20 g/dm³."],"cues":["Which unit must a volume be in before it can be used with a concentration in mol/dm³?","Which of the two solutions do you know enough about to find an amount in moles?","What do the balancing numbers tell you about the amount of alkali in the flask?","What else must be converted before that amount can be turned into a concentration?","What do you need to know about sodium hydroxide to change the units of the concentration?","How do moles per cubic decimetre become grams per cubic decimetre?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why must a symbol equation be balanced?","a":"Because atoms are never created or destroyed in a reaction, so the same number of each atom must appear on both sides."},{"q":"What does Mr stand for and how do you find it?","a":"Relative formula mass — add together the relative atomic masses of every atom shown in the formula."},{"q":"Why does the mass of a metal appear to increase when it burns in air?","a":"It combines with oxygen from the air, so the mass of that oxygen joins the solid product and is now included when it is weighed."},{"q":"What is the formula linking moles, mass and Mr? (Higher tier only)","a":"moles = mass ÷ Mr."},{"q":"Write the equation for percentage yield. (chemistry only)","a":"% yield = (mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product) × 100."},{"q":"A reaction has only one product. What is its atom economy? (chemistry only)","a":"100 %, because every atom of the reactants ends up in the desired product."},{"q":"What volume does one mole of any gas occupy at room temperature and pressure? (chemistry only, Higher tier only)","a":"24 dm³, at 20 °C and 1 atmosphere pressure."}]},"room":{"facts":["Quantitative chemistry is examined on Paper 1, which covers topics 4.1 to 4.5: a written exam of 1 hour 45 minutes, worth 100 marks and 50 % of the GCSE. Paper 2 may also draw on the fundamental concepts of 4.1 to 4.3.","The entry code is 8462F for Foundation Tier and 8462H for Higher Tier; content marked (HT only) in this room — moles, reacting masses, using moles to balance equations, limiting reactants, concentration in mol/dm³ and gas volumes — is examined on Higher Tier papers only.","Questions drawing on the knowledge and understanding gained from the eight required practical activities are worth at least 15 % of the marks for the qualification; the titration is Required practical 2.","Law of conservation of mass: the total mass of reactants equals the total mass of products, because atoms are never created or destroyed.","Relative formula mass (Mr) = sum of the relative atomic masses (Ar) of every atom shown in the formula.","Percentage by mass of an element = (total Ar of that element in the formula ÷ Mr of the compound) × 100.","Moles = mass ÷ Mr, so mass = moles × Mr (Higher tier only).","The Avogadro constant is 6.02 × 10²³ particles per mole (Higher tier only).","Concentration (g/dm³) = mass of solute (g) ÷ volume of solution (dm³), and 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³.","The limiting reactant is completely used up first; it determines the maximum amount of product that can form (Higher tier only).","% yield = (mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product) × 100 (chemistry only).","Percentage atom economy = (Mr of the desired product from the equation ÷ sum of the Mr of all the reactants from the equation) × 100 (chemistry only).","Moles = concentration (mol/dm³) × volume (dm³) (chemistry only, Higher tier only).","One mole of any gas occupies 24 dm³ at room temperature and pressure, 20 °C and 1 atmosphere (chemistry only, Higher tier only)."],"questions":[{"q":"State the law of conservation of mass.","a":"The total mass of the reactants equals the total mass of the products, because no atoms are created or destroyed in a chemical reaction.","sol":"In a chemical reaction, atoms are rearranged into new substances but are not created or destroyed, so the total mass of the products always equals the total mass of the reactants.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what happens to atoms during a chemical reaction — are any made or lost?","Your answer should link what happens to atoms to what happens to the total mass.","Your answer is one sentence comparing the mass of reactants with the mass of products."],"codes":["4.3.1.1"]},{"q":"Calculate the relative formula mass (Mr) of water, H₂O. (Ar: H = 1, O = 16)","a":"18","sol":"Mr(H₂O) = (2 × 1) + 16 = 2 + 16 = 18.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Recall what relative formula mass means for a compound's formula.","Use the number of each type of atom shown in the formula together with its Ar value.","Your answer is a single number with no unit."],"codes":["4.3.1.2"]},{"q":"Balance the symbol equation for magnesium burning in oxygen: Mg + O₂ → MgO","a":"2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO","sol":"Count atoms: the left has 1 Mg and 2 O, the right has 1 Mg and 1 O. Add a 2 in front of MgO to give 2 O on the right, then add a 2 in front of Mg to restore equal Mg atoms: 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO, giving 2 Mg and 2 O on each side.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what conservation of mass tells you about the number of each atom on both sides.","Count the atoms of each element separately on the left and on the right before you add any multipliers.","Your answer places a whole number in front of one or more formulae, not inside them."],"codes":["4.3.1.1"]},{"q":"A student measures the temperature of a reaction three times: 24.5 °C, 25.5 °C and 25.0 °C. Calculate the mean and the range of these readings.","a":"Mean = 25.0 °C; range = 1.0 °C","sol":"Mean = (24.5 + 25.5 + 25.0) ÷ 3 = 75.0 ÷ 3 = 25.0 °C. Range = highest − lowest = 25.5 − 24.5 = 1.0 °C.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Recall how a set of repeat measurements can be used to judge how uncertain they are.","One value comes from adding the readings and dividing by how many there are; the other comes from comparing the two extreme readings.","Your answer gives two values, both in °C."],"codes":["4.3.1.4"]},{"q":"Magnesium ribbon is weighed, then burned completely in air, and the solid product is weighed again. Explain why the solid product has a greater mass than the magnesium ribbon.","a":"The magnesium reacts with oxygen from the air to form magnesium oxide, so oxygen atoms have joined the solid, adding to its mass.","sol":"2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO. The magnesium combines with oxygen gas from the air. Because that oxygen was not on the balance at the start, the solid magnesium oxide formed weighs more than the original magnesium ribbon — the oxygen atoms are now part of the solid being weighed.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about where the extra atoms in the solid product could have come from.","Consider which reactant was not sitting on the balance before the reaction took place.","Decide whether the missing substance was on the balance at the start or at the end."],"codes":["4.3.1.3"]},{"q":"Calculate the percentage by mass of calcium in calcium carbonate, CaCO₃. (Ar: Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)","a":"40 %","sol":"Mr(CaCO₃) = 40 + 12 + (3 × 16) = 40 + 12 + 48 = 100. Percentage of Ca = (40 ÷ 100) × 100 = 40 %.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what fraction of the compound's total mass is made up of one element.","Work out the Mr of the whole compound first, then compare it with the mass contributed by calcium alone.","Your answer is a percentage."],"codes":["4.3.1.2"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Calculate the number of moles in 22 g of carbon dioxide, CO₂. (Ar: C = 12, O = 16)","a":"0.50 mol","sol":"Mr(CO₂) = 12 + (2 × 16) = 44. moles = mass ÷ Mr = 22 ÷ 44 = 0.50 mol.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Recall the formula linking mass, moles and relative formula mass.","Find the Mr of the substance first, then use it to convert the given mass.","Your answer is a number of moles, in mol."],"codes":["4.3.2.1"]},{"q":"A solution of copper(II) sulfate has a concentration of 40 g/dm³. (a) Calculate the mass of copper(II) sulfate dissolved in 250 cm³ of this solution. (b) (Higher tier) Water is then added to that 250 cm³ of solution until its total volume is 500 cm³. Explain what happens to the concentration of the solution.","a":"(a) 10 g. (b) The concentration halves, to 20 g/dm³, because the same mass of solute is now spread through a larger volume of solution.","sol":"(a) Convert the volume to dm³: 250 cm³ ÷ 1000 = 0.25 dm³. mass of solute = concentration × volume = 40 × 0.25 = 10 g. (b) concentration = mass of solute ÷ volume of solution. Adding water does not change the mass of solute dissolved, which is still 10 g, but the volume rises to 500 cm³ = 0.50 dm³, so concentration = 10 ÷ 0.50 = 20 g/dm³: dividing the same mass by a larger volume gives a smaller concentration.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the units the concentration is given in, and what unit the volumes must be in to match them.","Convert each volume into cubic decimetres, then use the relationship between concentration, mass of solute and volume of solution, rearranging it when the mass is what you want.","Your answer to (a) is a mass in grams; your answer to (b) says whether the concentration rises or falls and links this to the mass of solute and the volume."],"codes":["4.3.2.5"]},{"q":"A student prepares magnesium sulfate crystals. The maximum theoretical mass of magnesium sulfate that could be made is 6.0 g, and the student obtains 4.8 g of dry crystals. Calculate the percentage yield.","a":"80 %","sol":"% yield = (mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product) × 100 = (4.8 ÷ 6.0) × 100 = 0.80 × 100 = 80 %.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what a percentage yield compares: what was collected against what the equation says was possible.","Recall the equation for percentage yield, and decide which of the two masses given is the actual yield and which is the maximum theoretical yield.","Your answer is a percentage, with no unit of mass."],"codes":["4.3.3.1"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) 6 g of magnesium is burned completely in oxygen. Using the equation 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO, calculate the mass of magnesium oxide produced. (Ar: Mg = 24, O = 16)","a":"10 g","sol":"Ar(Mg) = 24, Mr(MgO) = 24 + 16 = 40. moles of Mg = mass ÷ Ar = 6 ÷ 24 = 0.25 mol. The equation shows a 1 : 1 mole ratio of Mg to MgO, so moles of MgO = 0.25 mol. mass of MgO = moles × Mr = 0.25 × 40 = 10 g.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Think about how the balancing numbers in the equation relate moles of reactant to moles of product.","Convert the given mass to moles first, use the equation's ratio to find the moles of the substance you want, then convert back to a mass.","Your answer is a mass, in grams."],"codes":["4.3.2.2","4.3.1.1"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) 2.4 g of magnesium reacts exactly with 7.1 g of chlorine to form 9.5 g of magnesium chloride. (Ar: Mg = 24, Cl = 35.5) Use this data to determine the simplest whole-number mole ratio of Mg : Cl₂ : MgCl₂, and write the balanced symbol equation.","a":"Mg : Cl₂ : MgCl₂ = 1 : 1 : 1, so the balanced equation is Mg + Cl₂ → MgCl₂","sol":"Ar(Mg) = 24, Mr(Cl₂) = 2 × 35.5 = 71, Mr(MgCl₂) = 24 + (2 × 35.5) = 95. moles Mg = 2.4 ÷ 24 = 0.10 mol; moles Cl₂ = 7.1 ÷ 71 = 0.10 mol; moles MgCl₂ = 9.5 ÷ 95 = 0.10 mol. Dividing each by the smallest value (0.10) gives a ratio of 1 : 1 : 1, so the balanced equation is Mg + Cl₂ → MgCl₂.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Think about how converting each given mass into an amount could reveal a simple ratio.","Find the Mr of each substance, convert every mass to moles, then divide all three values by the smallest one.","Your answer gives a ratio of three numbers and a balanced symbol equation."],"codes":["4.3.2.3"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) 0.2 mol of zinc is added to a solution containing 0.15 mol of hydrochloric acid: Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂. Identify the limiting reactant and calculate the maximum number of moles of hydrogen gas that can form.","a":"Hydrochloric acid is limiting; 0.075 mol of H₂ forms.","sol":"The equation needs 2 mol HCl for every 1 mol Zn. 0.2 mol Zn would need 0.4 mol HCl, but only 0.15 mol HCl is available, so HCl runs out first and is the limiting reactant. The equation shows 2 mol HCl give 1 mol H₂, so moles of H₂ = moles of HCl ÷ 2 = 0.15 ÷ 2 = 0.075 mol.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what it means for a reactant to run out before the other one is fully used.","Compare the mole ratio the equation requires with the moles of each reactant actually given, then use the one that runs out to find the product.","Your answer names one reactant and gives a number of moles of gas."],"codes":["4.3.2.4"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Methane burns completely in oxygen: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O. Calculate the volume of carbon dioxide, measured at room temperature and pressure, produced when 8.0 g of methane burns. The volume of one mole of any gas at room temperature and pressure is 24 dm³. (Ar: C = 12, H = 1)","a":"12 dm³","sol":"Mr(CH₄) = 12 + (4 × 1) = 16. moles of CH₄ = mass ÷ Mr = 8.0 ÷ 16 = 0.50 mol. The equation shows 1 mol of CH₄ gives 1 mol of CO₂, so moles of CO₂ = 0.50 mol. volume = moles × 24 = 0.50 × 24 = 12 dm³.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what links the mass of a gas to an amount in moles, and what links an amount in moles of any gas to the space it fills.","Find the Mr of the fuel and convert its mass to moles, then use the equation's ratio before bringing in the molar volume.","Your answer is a volume, in dm³."],"codes":["4.3.5"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Ammonia is made from nitrogen and hydrogen: N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃. In one run 14.0 g of nitrogen reacts with an excess of hydrogen and 11.9 g of ammonia is collected. (Ar: N = 14, H = 1) Calculate the maximum theoretical mass of ammonia that could be made from this mass of nitrogen, and the percentage yield of the run.","a":"Maximum theoretical mass = 17.0 g; percentage yield = 70.0 %","sol":"Mr(N₂) = 2 × 14 = 28 and Mr(NH₃) = 14 + (3 × 1) = 17. moles of N₂ = mass ÷ Mr = 14.0 ÷ 28 = 0.500 mol. The equation shows 1 mol of N₂ gives 2 mol of NH₃, so moles of NH₃ = 2 × 0.500 = 1.00 mol. maximum theoretical mass of NH₃ = moles × Mr = 1.00 × 17 = 17.0 g. % yield = (mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product) × 100 = (11.9 ÷ 17.0) × 100 = 70.0 %.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Only one of the two masses given belongs to a substance whose amount the equation can follow — think about which one, and why the hydrogen is no use here.","Convert the mass of nitrogen to moles with its Mr, use the balancing numbers to find the moles of ammonia the equation allows and turn that back into a mass, then compare the mass collected with it.","Your answer gives a mass in grams and a percentage, and the reaction being reversible is why the two differ."],"codes":["4.3.3.1","4.3.2.2"]},{"q":"Iron is extracted from iron(III) oxide in the blast furnace: Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂. Calculate the percentage atom economy of this reaction for producing iron. Give your answer to 3 significant figures. (Ar: Fe = 56, O = 16, C = 12)","a":"45.9 %","sol":"Mr of the desired product from the equation: 2Fe = 2 × 56 = 112. Sum of the Mr of the reactants from the equation: Mr(Fe₂O₃) = (2 × 56) + (3 × 16) = 160 and Mr(3CO) = 3 × (12 + 16) = 84, giving 160 + 84 = 244. Percentage atom economy = (112 ÷ 244) × 100 = 45.9 % to 3 significant figures.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what fraction of the mass of the starting materials ends up in the product the furnace is built to make.","Work out the relative formula mass of the wanted product in the quantity the equation shows, and the total relative formula mass of every reactant in the quantities the equation shows.","Your answer is a percentage given to three significant figures."],"codes":["4.3.3.2"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) In a titration, 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution was neutralised by exactly 20.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid. The equation is NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. Calculate the concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution in mol/dm³.","a":"0.0800 mol/dm³","sol":"volume of acid = 20.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.0200 dm³. moles of HCl = concentration × volume = 0.100 × 0.0200 = 0.00200 mol. The equation shows 1 mol HCl reacts with 1 mol NaOH, so moles of NaOH = 0.00200 mol. volume of alkali = 25.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.0250 dm³, so concentration of NaOH = moles ÷ volume = 0.00200 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.0800 mol/dm³.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Think about which of the two solutions you know enough about to work out an amount in moles.","Convert both volumes into cubic decimetres, find the moles of the solution whose concentration you know, then use the equation's ratio to get the moles of the other one.","Your answer is a concentration, in mol/dm³, given to three significant figures."],"codes":["4.3.4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What does the law of conservation of mass tell you about a chemical reaction?","a":"The total mass of products equals the total mass of reactants, because atoms are neither created nor destroyed."},{"q":"How do you calculate the relative formula mass of a compound?","a":"Add together the relative atomic masses of every atom shown in its formula."},{"q":"Write the equation for percentage yield.","a":"% yield = (mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product) × 100."},{"q":"What volume does one mole of any gas occupy at room temperature and pressure? (chemistry only, Higher tier only)","a":"24 dm³, measured at 20 °C and 1 atmosphere pressure."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Law of conservation of mass","back":"The total mass of reactants equals the total mass of products in a chemical reaction, because atoms are never created or destroyed.","code":"4.3.1.1","open":false},{"front":"Balancing a symbol equation","back":"Add whole-number multipliers in normal script in front of formulae until the number of atoms of each element is the same on both sides; subscripts inside a formula are never changed.","code":"4.3.1.1","open":false},{"front":"Relative formula mass (Mr)","back":"The sum of the relative atomic masses of every atom shown in a compound's formula.","code":"4.3.1.2","open":false},{"front":"Percentage by mass of an element","back":"(total Ar of that element in the formula ÷ Mr of the compound) × 100.","code":"4.3.1.2","open":false},{"front":"Apparent mass changes in a non-enclosed system","back":"A metal gains mass burning in air because oxygen atoms join the solid. A heated carbonate loses mass because carbon dioxide escapes into the air.","code":"4.3.1.3","open":false},{"front":"Measuring uncertainty","back":"The range of a set of repeat measurements, about the mean, is used as a measure of how uncertain the result is. Range = highest reading − lowest reading.","code":"4.3.1.4","open":false},{"front":"The mole (Higher tier only)","back":"The unit of amount of substance, symbol mol. One mole of any substance contains 6.02 × 10²³ of the stated particles (the Avogadro constant) and has a mass in grams equal to its Mr; moles = mass ÷ Mr.","code":"4.3.2.1","open":false},{"front":"Reacting masses from an equation (Higher tier only)","back":"Convert the given mass to moles, use the balancing numbers as a mole ratio to find moles of the substance wanted, then convert back to a mass.","code":"4.3.2.2","open":false},{"front":"Finding balancing numbers from masses (Higher tier only)","back":"Convert each given mass to moles, then divide every value by the smallest number of moles to get a simple whole-number ratio.","code":"4.3.2.3","open":false},{"front":"Limiting reactant (Higher tier only)","back":"The reactant that is completely used up first; it limits the maximum amount of product that can form, even if the other reactant is in excess.","code":"4.3.2.4","open":false},{"front":"Concentration of a solution in g/dm³","back":"Concentration (g/dm³) = mass of solute (g) ÷ volume of solution (dm³); 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³. Adding water raises the volume but not the mass of solute, so the concentration falls.","code":"4.3.2.5","open":false},{"front":"Percentage yield (chemistry only)","back":"% yield = (mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass) × 100. Yields fall short because a reaction is reversible, product is lost on separation, or reactants react in unexpected ways.","code":"4.3.3.1","open":false},{"front":"Atom economy (chemistry only)","back":"How much of the starting materials ends up as the useful product: (Mr of desired product ÷ sum of Mr of all reactants, both from the equation) × 100. High atom economy matters for sustainable development and cost.","code":"4.3.3.2","open":false},{"front":"Concentration in mol/dm³ (chemistry only, Higher tier only)","back":"moles = concentration (mol/dm³) × volume (dm³), so concentration = moles ÷ volume. Concentration in g/dm³ = concentration in mol/dm³ × Mr.","code":"4.3.4","open":false},{"front":"Molar volume of a gas (chemistry only, Higher tier only)","back":"One mole of any gas occupies 24 dm³ at room temperature and pressure (20 °C, 1 atmosphere), so volume (dm³) = moles × 24. Equal amounts in moles of gases occupy equal volumes.","code":"4.3.5","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Key equations and figures for Quantitative chemistry","items":["Relative formula mass: Mr = sum of the Ar of every atom shown in the formula. Percentage by mass of an element = (total Ar of that element in the formula ÷ Mr of the compound) × 100.","Uncertainty: mean = sum of the readings ÷ number of readings; range = highest reading − lowest reading, used about the mean as the measure of uncertainty.","(Higher tier only) moles (mol) = mass (g) ÷ Mr, rearranged to mass (g) = moles (mol) × Mr and Mr = mass (g) ÷ moles (mol).","(Higher tier only) The Avogadro constant = 6.02 × 10²³ particles per mole: one mole of any substance contains 6.02 × 10²³ of the stated atoms, molecules or ions.","Concentration (g/dm³) = mass of solute (g) ÷ volume of solution (dm³); mass of solute (g) = concentration (g/dm³) × volume (dm³); 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³, so a volume in cm³ is divided by 1000.","(chemistry only) Percentage yield = (mass of product actually made ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product) × 100.","(chemistry only) Percentage atom economy = (relative formula mass of the desired product from the equation ÷ sum of the relative formula masses of all the reactants from the equation) × 100.","(chemistry only, Higher tier only) moles (mol) = concentration (mol/dm³) × volume (dm³); concentration (mol/dm³) = moles (mol) ÷ volume (dm³); concentration (g/dm³) = concentration (mol/dm³) × Mr.","(chemistry only, Higher tier only) At room temperature and pressure, 20 °C and 1 atmosphere, the volume of one mole of any gas is 24 dm³: volume (dm³) = moles (mol) × 24, and moles (mol) = volume (dm³) ÷ 24.","None of these equations is printed on the paper. The only thing supplied with both Chemistry papers is the periodic table (Appendix A), which gives you every Ar value you need, so each equation above must be recalled."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical 2: (chemistry only) determination of the reacting volumes of solutions of a strong acid and a strong alkali by titration","items":["Aim: determine the volumes of a strong acid and a strong alkali that react exactly together and (Higher tier only) use those reacting volumes and the known concentration of one solution to calculate the concentration of the other in mol/dm³ and in g/dm³.","Method 1: rinse the pipette with a little of the sodium hydroxide solution and run the rinsings to waste, so that any water left in it cannot dilute the alkali.","Method 2: use a pipette and pipette filler to run 25.0 cm³ of the sodium hydroxide solution into a clean conical flask, and stand the flask on a white tile so the colour change is easy to see.","Method 3: add a few drops of a single indicator to the alkali in the flask — phenolphthalein (pink in alkali, colourless in acid) or methyl orange (yellow in alkali, red in acid).","Method 4: rinse the burette with the dilute hydrochloric acid, clamp it vertically below eye level and fill it through a funnel; remove the funnel, then run acid through the tap until the tip below the tap is full and free of air bubbles.","Method 5: read the initial burette volume to the nearest 0.05 cm³, with the bottom of the meniscus at eye level, and record it.","Method 6: run the acid into the flask, swirling constantly, until the indicator changes colour; record the final reading and work out this rough titre as final reading − initial reading.","Method 7: repeat with a fresh 25.0 cm³ of alkali, running the acid in quickly until just before the rough titre and then dropwise, stopping at the first permanent colour change.","Method 8: repeat until at least two titres are concordant — within 0.10 cm³ of each other — then calculate the mean of the concordant titres only, leaving the rough titration out of the mean.","Independent variable: the volume of sodium hydroxide solution placed in the conical flask — the quantity you fix in advance, measured out as 25.0 cm³ with the pipette in every titration.","Dependent variable: the volume of acid run in from the burette that exactly neutralises that alkali (the titre), read at the end point — the first permanent colour change of the indicator.","Control variables: the concentration of the acid, the concentration of the alkali, the indicator used and the number of drops added, and the temperature of the solutions.","Apparatus: 50 cm³ burette with clamp and stand, 25 cm³ pipette and pipette filler, conical flask, white tile, small funnel, beakers, wash bottle of distilled water, phenolphthalein or methyl orange indicator, dilute hydrochloric acid, dilute sodium hydroxide solution, eye protection.","Safety: wear eye protection throughout; dilute hydrochloric acid and dilute sodium hydroxide solution are irritant, so wash any splashes off the skin at once; use a pipette filler and never draw solution up by mouth; fill the burette with it clamped below eye level.","Likely errors: overshooting the end point by not adding the acid dropwise near the end; parallax error from not reading the meniscus at eye level, or reading to the wrong precision; an air bubble in the burette tip that empties during the titration and inflates the titre; leaving the funnel in the burette so extra drops run in; not swirling, so the colour change is reached unevenly; including the rough titre in the mean.","Expected result and analysis: the concordant titres should agree within 0.10 cm³ — for example 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution neutralised by a mean titre of 20.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid. (Higher tier only) moles of HCl = 0.100 × (20.0 ÷ 1000) = 0.00200 mol; NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O reacts 1 : 1, so moles of NaOH = 0.00200 mol; concentration of NaOH = 0.00200 ÷ (25.0 ÷ 1000) = 0.0800 mol/dm³; and with Mr(NaOH) = 23 + 16 + 1 = 40, that is 0.0800 × 40 = 3.20 g/dm³.","Skills assessed: AT 1 (use of appropriate apparatus to make and record a range of measurements accurately, including volume of liquids) and AT 8 (the determination of the concentrations of strong acids and strong alkalis); key opportunities for skills development are WS 2.4, WS 2.6, MS 1a, MS 1c and MS 2a."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: mass change on heating limestone","items":["Question (6 marks): a student heats 10 g of calcium carbonate strongly until no further change occurs, decomposing it completely: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. The carbon dioxide released has a mass of 4.4 g. Calculate the mass of calcium oxide left behind, and explain, in terms of particles, why the mass of the solid decreases during this reaction.","Level 3 (5–6 marks): the mass of calcium oxide is found using conservation of mass: mass of CaO = mass of CaCO₃ − mass of CO₂ = 10 − 4.4 = 5.6 g. This is explained by stating that no atoms are lost or made in the reaction — the carbonate breaks down into calcium oxide and carbon dioxide, and because the reaction is not in a sealed container, the carbon dioxide gas escapes into the surrounding air. Only the solid calcium oxide remains on the balance, so the measured mass of the solid falls, even though the total mass of all the products together (solid plus escaped gas) still equals the mass of carbonate used.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): correctly calculates the mass of calcium oxide as 5.6 g, but the explanation of the mass decrease is only partly linked to particles — for example, stating that carbon dioxide escapes without explaining that it leaves an open, non-enclosed system, or without connecting this back to conservation of mass.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): attempts a calculation or an explanation with significant errors, such as subtracting the wrong values, or states only that mass is lost without reference to a gas leaving the reaction.","What a bottom-level (Level 1) answer misses: it gives a numerical answer or a vague statement about mass loss without explaining, in terms of particles and conservation of mass, that the escaped carbon dioxide still has mass — it has simply left the container and is no longer being weighed, so no mass has truly been lost from the reaction overall."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: choosing a reaction pathway for ethanol (chemistry only) (Higher tier)","items":["Question (6 marks) (Higher tier): ethanol, C₂H₅OH, can be made in two ways. Method 1 is the fermentation of glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂, run in batches at about 30 °C using glucose from sugar cane, slow, and giving a dilute solution of ethanol that must then be separated. Method 2 is the hydration of ethene, C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH, run continuously at high temperature and pressure with a catalyst, fast, using ethene made from crude oil, with unreacted ethene recycled so that almost all of it is converted. (Mr: C₆H₁₂O₆ = 180, C₂H₅OH = 46, CO₂ = 44, C₂H₄ = 28, H₂O = 18.) Calculate the percentage atom economy of each method for making ethanol and evaluate which method a company should choose to make ethanol continuously on a large scale.","Level 3 (5–6 marks): both atom economies are calculated correctly and used, with the other data, to reach a justified judgement. For Method 1 the desired product from the equation is 2C₂H₅OH = 2 × 46 = 92 and the only reactant is 180, so the atom economy = (92 ÷ 180) × 100 = 51.1 %; almost half of the mass of the glucose leaves as carbon dioxide instead of ethanol. For Method 2 the desired product is 46 and the reactants total 28 + 18 = 46, so the atom economy = (46 ÷ 46) × 100 = 100 %, because addition makes ethanol the only product and every atom of the reactants ends up in it. For continuous large-scale production Method 2 should be chosen: its atom economy is 100 % so almost no raw material is wasted, it is fast, it runs continuously rather than in batches, recycling the unreacted ethene makes the yield very high, and the ethanol does not have to be separated from a dilute fermentation mixture. The costs of that choice are recognised: the high temperature and pressure need energy and expensive equipment, and the ethene comes from crude oil, a finite raw material, whereas fermentation uses a renewable one and its carbon dioxide by-product can be collected and sold.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): calculates at least one atom economy correctly, or both with an arithmetic slip, and makes relevant comparisons such as 'Method 2 wastes fewer atoms' or 'fermentation is slow', but the judgement is not fully substantiated — for example the atom economy figures are calculated and then not used in the decision, or only rate is considered and the by-product and raw material are ignored.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): makes isolated correct statements, such as writing down the atom economy equation, calculating one relative formula mass, or saying that fermentation is slow, with no comparison of the two methods and no judgement.","What a bottom-level (Level 1) answer misses: it does not put the relative formula masses into the atom economy equation in the quantities the balanced equation shows (2 × 46 for the fermentation product, 28 + 18 for the hydration reactants), it does not state the two percentages, and it reaches no judgement — or states a preference without linking it to atom economy, rate, yield, by-products and the raw material."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-18T21:32:22Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.94,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file, not the stale copy in the judge prompt: the new Higher-tier question at index 13 re-solves exactly in python — Mr(N₂) = 28, 14.0 ÷ 28 = 0.500 mol, 1 : 2 gives 1.00 mol NH₃, 1.00 × 17 = 17.0 g theoretical and (11.9 ÷ 17.0) × 100 = 70.0 % — and it closes the one real assessment gap, the (HT only) theoretical-mass bullet of 4.3.3.1. All eight earlier problems are gone: the crucible-transfer loss in lesson.examples[3] now agrees with the setup, extras[3] is labelled '(Higher tier)' in both its title and its question, the practical has a pipette-rinse step and separate independent and dependent variables, and the fact list and exit ticket labels are consistent. All 16 answer keys, the six worked examples and both extended answers still re-solve exactly, every power is a Unicode superscript with no carets, all thirteen 4.3 codes are taught, labelled and tested, the four (chemistry only) ideas each have a lesson section, card and question, kit-tools check reports OK, and the difficulty spread is 4 / 5 / 7. Publishable."}}},"4.4":{"id":"AQA-8462","topic":"4.4","family":"science","lesson":{"why":"Chemical changes is the busiest room on Chemistry Paper 1, and three of the eight required practical activities live in it: making a pure, dry soluble salt, titration and the electrolysis of aqueous solutions. Paper 1 examines topics 4.1 to 4.5 in 1 hour 45 minutes for 100 marks, half of the GCSE, and questions drawing on the required practicals are worth at least 15 % of the marks for the whole qualification, so the methods here are examined directly rather than in passing. GCSE Chemistry also goes further than Combined Science in this topic: titrations and the calculations that go with them are examined here and not in Trilogy, and at Higher tier you must define oxidation and reduction in terms of electrons, write ionic equations and half equations for what happens at each electrode, and explain strong and weak acids in terms of the degree of ionisation and the hydrogen ion concentration. Almost every mark in this room is won by writing a correct equation, naming the right salt or the right product at the right electrode, or setting a calculation out line by line.","idea":[{"code":"4.4.1.1","h":"Metals and oxygen: metal oxides","t":"Metals react with oxygen to produce metal oxides, for example 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO or 4Na + O₂ → 2Na₂O. These are oxidation reactions because the metals gain oxygen. In terms of oxygen, oxidation is the gain of oxygen and reduction is the loss of oxygen, and you must be able to explain a reaction either way round using those two definitions. Exam questions ask you to write and balance metal + oxygen equations, name the oxide formed, and identify a reaction as oxidation by saying which substance has gained oxygen."},{"code":"4.4.1.2","h":"The reactivity series","t":"When metals react with other substances the metal atoms form positive ions, and the reactivity of a metal is related to its tendency to form those positive ions: the more readily a metal forms its ion, the more reactive it is. Potassium, sodium, lithium, calcium, magnesium, zinc, iron and copper can be put in order from their reactions with water and with dilute acids at room temperature — reactions with steam are not examined. The non-metals hydrogen and carbon are often included in the series as reference points. A more reactive metal can displace a less reactive metal from a compound, for example Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu. Exam questions give you observations with water or dilute acid and ask you to deduce an order of reactivity, or to explain that order in terms of forming positive ions."},{"code":"4.4.1.3","h":"Extracting metals by reduction with carbon","t":"Unreactive metals such as gold are found in the Earth as the metal itself, but most metals are found as compounds and need chemical reactions to extract them. A metal less reactive than carbon can be extracted from its oxide by reduction with carbon: the oxide loses oxygen and is reduced, while the carbon gains that oxygen and is oxidised, as in 2Fe₂O₃ + 3C → 4Fe + 3CO₂. You only need the reduction of oxides using carbon; the details of industrial extraction processes are not required. Exam questions give information about an extraction and ask you to identify what is oxidised and what is reduced in terms of oxygen, or to evaluate whether carbon reduction would work for a given metal."},{"code":"4.4.1.4","h":"Oxidation and reduction in terms of electrons (Higher tier only)","t":"Higher tier only: the oxygen definitions have an equivalent electron definition — oxidation is the loss of electrons and reduction is the gain of electrons (remembered as OIL RIG). This lets a displacement reaction be written as an ionic equation showing only the species that change, leaving out the spectator ions: Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu becomes Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu. Exam questions at Higher tier ask you to write an ionic equation for a displacement reaction, or to look at a given reaction, symbol equation or half equation and identify which species has lost electrons (oxidised) and which has gained electrons (reduced)."},{"code":"4.4.2.1","h":"Reactions of acids with metals","t":"Acids react with some metals to produce a salt and hydrogen: metal + acid → salt + hydrogen. Knowledge of these reactions is limited to magnesium, zinc and iron with hydrochloric acid and sulfuric acid, for example Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂ and Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂. Higher tier only: you must also explain these as redox reactions in terms of gain or loss of electrons — the metal atoms lose electrons and are oxidised, while the hydrogen ions gain electrons and are reduced, as the ionic equation Zn + 2H⁺ → Zn²⁺ + H₂ shows — and identify which species is oxidised and which is reduced in a given equation."},{"code":"4.4.2.2","h":"Neutralisation of acids and salt production","t":"Acids are neutralised by alkalis, such as soluble metal hydroxides, and by bases, such as insoluble metal hydroxides and metal oxides, to produce a salt and water; metal carbonates neutralise acids to produce a salt, water and carbon dioxide. The particular salt depends on the acid used — hydrochloric acid produces chlorides, nitric acid produces nitrates and sulfuric acid produces sulfates — while the positive ion in the salt comes from the base, alkali or carbonate. Exam questions give you reactants and ask you to predict the products, and to use the formulae of common ions to deduce the formula of the salt, for example Cu(NO₃)₂ from Cu²⁺ and NO₃⁻."},{"code":"4.4.2.3","h":"Making soluble salts","t":"Soluble salts can be made from acids by reacting them with solid insoluble substances such as metals, metal oxides, hydroxides or carbonates. The solid is added to the acid until no more reacts, so the solid is in excess and none of the acid is left; the excess solid is then filtered off, leaving a solution of the salt alone. That salt solution is crystallised to produce the solid salt. Exam questions ask you to describe, from information provided, how to make a pure, dry sample of a named soluble salt, and to explain why each step — the excess solid, the filtering, the crystallising — is needed."},{"code":"4.4.2.3 (Required practical 1)","h":"Required practical 1: preparing a pure, dry sample of a soluble salt","t":"Required practical 1 is the preparation of a pure, dry sample of a soluble salt from an insoluble oxide or carbonate, using a Bunsen burner to heat the dilute acid and a water bath or electric heater to evaporate the solution. Warm the acid, add the insoluble solid a little at a time until it is in excess, filter off what is left, evaporate the filtrate until the solution is close to saturated, then cool it slowly to crystallise and dry the crystals. The practical assesses AT 2, AT 3, AT 4 and AT 6, with skills development in WS 2.3 and WS 2.4. Exam questions ask for the method in order, the reason for a step, or the improvement to a described method."},{"code":"4.4.2.4","h":"The pH scale and neutralisation","t":"Acids produce hydrogen ions, H⁺, in aqueous solutions, and aqueous solutions of alkalis contain hydroxide ions, OH⁻. The pH scale runs from 0 to 14 and measures how acidic or alkaline a solution is; it is measured with universal indicator (or a wide range indicator) for an approximate value, or with a pH probe. A solution of pH 7 is neutral, below 7 is acidic and above 7 is alkaline. In a neutralisation between an acid and an alkali the hydrogen ions react with the hydroxide ions to make water: H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l). Exam questions ask you to classify solutions from pH values or indicator colours and to write or complete this equation."},{"code":"4.4.2.5","h":"Titrations (chemistry only)","t":"Chemistry only: the volumes of acid and alkali solutions that react exactly with each other can be measured by titration using a suitable indicator. You need to describe how to carry out a titration using strong acids and strong alkalis only — sulfuric, hydrochloric and nitric acids — measuring the alkali with a pipette, adding the acid from a burette and using a single indicator such as phenolphthalein or methyl orange, which gives one sharp colour change rather than the range of colours universal indicator gives. Higher tier only: you must also calculate the chemical quantities involved, working in concentrations in mol/dm³ and in g/dm³, using moles = concentration × volume in dm³ and the mole ratio from the balanced equation."},{"code":"4.4.2.5 (Required practical 2)","h":"Required practical 2: reacting volumes by titration (chemistry only)","t":"Chemistry only. Required practical 2 is the determination of the reacting volumes of solutions of a strong acid and a strong alkali by titration, and (Higher tier only) the determination of the concentration of one of those solutions in mol/dm³ and in g/dm³ from the reacting volumes and the known concentration of the other. Accuracy is everything here: a rough titration first, then repeats run dropwise near the end point until at least two titres are concordant, within 0.10 cm³, and only the concordant titres go into the mean. The practical assesses AT 1 and AT 8, with skills development in WS 2.4, WS 2.6, MS 1a, MS 1c and MS 2a."},{"code":"4.4.2.6","h":"Strong and weak acids (Higher tier only)","t":"Higher tier only: a strong acid is completely ionised in aqueous solution — hydrochloric, nitric and sulfuric acids — while a weak acid is only partially ionised, such as ethanoic, citric and carbonic acids. For a given concentration, the stronger the acid the lower the pH, because complete ionisation releases more hydrogen ions. As the pH decreases by one unit, the hydrogen ion concentration increases by a factor of 10, so a drop of two units means 10² = 100 times more H⁺ ions; only whole-number pH values are used. Keep the two pairs of words apart: strong and weak describe the degree of ionisation, while dilute and concentrated describe the amount of acid dissolved in a given volume."},{"code":"4.4.3.1","h":"The process of electrolysis","t":"When an ionic compound is melted or dissolved in water, its ions are free to move about within the liquid or solution, so the liquid or solution conducts electricity and is called an electrolyte. Passing an electric current through an electrolyte makes the ions move to the electrodes: positively charged ions move to the negative electrode, the cathode, and negatively charged ions move to the positive electrode, the anode. At the electrodes the ions are discharged, producing elements, and this process is electrolysis. Higher tier only: throughout electrolysis you must be able to write half equations for the reactions at the electrodes, and you may be given a half equation to complete and balance."},{"code":"4.4.3.2","h":"Electrolysing a molten ionic compound","t":"When a simple binary ionic compound such as lead bromide is electrolysed in the molten state using inert electrodes, the metal is produced at the cathode and the non-metal at the anode: molten lead bromide gives lead at the cathode and bromine at the anode. Anhydrous zinc chloride is a safer alternative for practical work, giving zinc at the cathode and chlorine at the anode. The compound must be molten so that its ions are free to move to the electrodes. Exam questions give you a binary ionic compound and ask you to predict the product at each electrode, which you do by deciding which ion is positive and which is negative."},{"code":"4.4.3.3","h":"Using electrolysis to extract metals","t":"Metals can be extracted from molten compounds using electrolysis, and this method is used when the metal is too reactive to be extracted by reduction with carbon or when the metal reacts with carbon. Large amounts of energy are used, both to melt the compound and to produce the electric current, which makes the process expensive. Aluminium is manufactured by the electrolysis of a molten mixture of aluminium oxide and cryolite, using carbon as the positive electrode. Exam questions ask you to explain why a mixture is used as the electrolyte — the cryolite lowers the melting point, so less energy is needed — and why the positive electrode must be continually replaced, as the oxygen made there reacts with the hot carbon."},{"code":"4.4.3.4","h":"Electrolysis of aqueous solutions","t":"The ions discharged when an aqueous solution is electrolysed using inert electrodes depend on the relative reactivity of the elements involved, because the water molecules themselves break down to produce hydrogen ions and hydroxide ions that can also be discharged. At the cathode, hydrogen is produced if the metal in the compound is more reactive than hydrogen; if the metal is less reactive than hydrogen, the metal itself is produced. At the anode, oxygen is produced from the hydroxide ions unless the solution contains halide ions, in which case the halogen is produced. Exam questions ask you to predict the products at both electrodes for an aqueous solution of a single ionic compound and to justify each prediction."},{"code":"4.4.3.4 (Required practical 3)","h":"Required practical 3: electrolysis of aqueous solutions using inert electrodes","t":"Required practical 3 investigates what happens when aqueous solutions are electrolysed using inert electrodes, and it is an investigation that involves developing a hypothesis: you predict the products from the ions present before you test them. Different solutions are electrolysed in turn and the products are identified — a lighted splint gives a squeaky pop with hydrogen, a glowing splint relights in oxygen, and damp litmus paper is bleached white by chlorine — then the observations are compared with the hypothesis. The practical assesses AT 3, AT 7 and AT 8, with skills development in WS 2.1, WS 2.2, WS 2.3, WS 2.4 and WS 2.6."},{"code":"4.4.3.5","h":"Half equations for the reactions at the electrodes (Higher tier only)","t":"Higher tier only: during electrolysis the positively charged ions at the cathode gain electrons, so the reactions at the cathode are reductions, and the negatively charged ions at the anode lose electrons, so the reactions at the anode are oxidations. These reactions are written as half equations, for example 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂ at the cathode and 4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ at the anode, which may also be written 4OH⁻ − 4e⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O. Check that both the atoms and the charges balance on the two sides. Exam questions ask you to write a half equation for a named electrode reaction, or to complete and balance one you are given."}],"examples":[{"title":"Naming the salt and writing the equation for a carbonate neutralisation","setup":"Copper carbonate, CuCO₃, is added to dilute nitric acid, HNO₃, until no more of it reacts and the fizzing stops. Name the salt produced and write the balanced symbol equation for the reaction.","steps":["Nitric acid always produces a nitrate salt, so the negative ion in the salt is the nitrate ion, NO₃⁻.","The positive ion comes from the carbonate, which contains copper ions, Cu²⁺, so two nitrate ions are needed for each copper ion: the salt is copper nitrate, Cu(NO₃)₂.","An acid reacting with a metal carbonate gives a salt, water and carbon dioxide — the fizzing — so the word equation is copper carbonate + nitric acid → copper nitrate + water + carbon dioxide.","Balancing the symbol equation gives CuCO₃ + 2HNO₃ → Cu(NO₃)₂ + H₂O + CO₂, with 1 Cu, 1 C, 2 H, 2 N and 9 O on each side."],"cues":["Which part of the salt's name is fixed by the acid used?","Where does the positive ion come from, and what charge does it carry?","What three products does an acid always give with a carbonate?","How many nitric acid molecules are needed to supply the nitrate ions in the salt?"]},{"title":"Predicting the products of electrolysing an aqueous solution","setup":"Copper sulfate solution is electrolysed using inert carbon electrodes. Predict the product formed at each electrode and give a reason for each prediction.","steps":["Four ions are available: Cu²⁺ and SO₄²⁻ from the dissolved salt, and H⁺ and OH⁻ from the water molecules that break down.","At the cathode the competition is between Cu²⁺ and H⁺; copper is less reactive than hydrogen, so the copper ions are discharged.","At the anode the competition is between SO₄²⁻ and OH⁻; the solution contains no halide ions, so the hydroxide ions are discharged and oxygen is released.","Testing the prediction: the gas collected at the anode relights a glowing splint, which confirms it is oxygen, and the cathode is left coated in a pink-brown layer of copper."],"cues":["Which ions are present, and which of them did not come from the dissolved salt?","Which of the two positive ions is discharged, and what decides it?","Which of the two negative ions is discharged, and what decides it?","How could you check by experiment that the gas at the anode really is the one you predicted?"]},{"title":"Finding a concentration from a titration (chemistry only, Higher tier only)","setup":"Chemistry only, Higher tier only. In a titration, 25.0 cm³ of 0.200 mol/dm³ sodium hydroxide solution was exactly neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of dilute sulfuric acid. The equation is 2NaOH + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O. Calculate the concentration of the sulfuric acid in mol/dm³ and in g/dm³. (Ar: H = 1, O = 16, S = 32.)","steps":["volume of alkali = 25.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.0250 dm³.","moles of NaOH = concentration × volume = 0.200 × 0.0250 = 0.00500 mol.","The equation shows 2 mol of NaOH react with 1 mol of H₂SO₄, so moles of H₂SO₄ = 0.00500 ÷ 2 = 0.00250 mol.","volume of acid = 20.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.0200 dm³, so concentration of H₂SO₄ = moles ÷ volume = 0.00250 ÷ 0.0200 = 0.125 mol/dm³.","Mr(H₂SO₄) = (2 × 1) + 32 + (4 × 16) = 2 + 32 + 64 = 98.","concentration in g/dm³ = concentration in mol/dm³ × Mr = 0.125 × 98 = 12.25, which is 12.3 g/dm³ to three significant figures, matching the three significant figures of the data."],"cues":["What unit must a volume be in before it can be used with a concentration in mol/dm³?","Which of the two solutions do you know enough about to find an amount in moles?","What do the balancing numbers tell you about the amount of acid in the flask?","What else has to be converted before that amount becomes a concentration?","What do you need to work out before the units can be changed?","How do moles per cubic decimetre become grams per cubic decimetre?"]},{"title":"Comparing hydrogen ion concentrations from pH values (Higher tier only)","setup":"Higher tier only. Solution A is an acid of pH 4 and solution B is a different acid of pH 1, both measured with a pH probe. Determine how many times greater the hydrogen ion concentration of solution B is than that of solution A.","steps":["The lower the pH, the higher the concentration of hydrogen ions, so it is solution B that has the greater H⁺ concentration.","The two solutions differ by 4 − 1 = 3 pH units.","Each fall of one pH unit multiplies the hydrogen ion concentration by 10, so three units multiply it by 10 × 10 × 10 = 10³.","10³ = 1000, so solution B has a hydrogen ion concentration 1000 times greater than solution A."],"cues":["Which of the two solutions must have the greater hydrogen ion concentration?","How far apart are the two pH values?","What does each single pH unit do to the hydrogen ion concentration?","What is that repeated multiplication worth as an ordinary number?"]},{"title":"Writing half equations for a molten compound (Higher tier only)","setup":"Higher tier only. Molten sodium chloride, NaCl, is electrolysed using inert electrodes. Write the half equation for the reaction at the cathode and the half equation for the reaction at the anode, and state which of the two is an oxidation.","steps":["Molten sodium chloride contains Na⁺ ions and Cl⁻ ions, free to move.","The positive Na⁺ ions move to the cathode, where each one gains a single electron: Na⁺ + e⁻ → Na.","The negative Cl⁻ ions move to the anode, where each loses an electron, and the chlorine atoms pair up, so two ions are needed: 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻.","Electrons appear on the right of the anode half equation, so they are being lost there: the anode reaction is the oxidation, and the cathode reaction, where electrons are gained, is a reduction."],"cues":["Which ions are free to move in the molten compound?","How many electrons must a sodium ion gain to become an atom?","Why will one chloride ion not be enough on the left of the anode equation?","On which side of each half equation do the electrons appear, and what does that tell you?"]},{"title":"A metal reacting with water, and what it shows about reactivity","setup":"Potassium is dropped onto cold water in a trough. It melts into a ball and moves across the surface, the gas given off burns with a lilac flame, and the solution left behind turns universal indicator purple. Magnesium, treated the same way, shows no reaction with cold water. Write the balanced symbol equation for the reaction of potassium with water, and explain in terms of ions why potassium is more reactive than magnesium.","steps":["The purple indicator shows an alkali, potassium hydroxide, and the gas that burns is hydrogen: potassium + water → potassium hydroxide + hydrogen.","In symbols, before balancing: K + H₂O → KOH + H₂.","Balancing gives 2K + 2H₂O → 2KOH + H₂, with 2 K, 4 H and 2 O on each side.","When a metal reacts, its atoms form positive ions, K → K⁺ + e⁻; potassium gives up its outer electron far more readily than magnesium does, so it has a much greater tendency to form positive ions and reacts even with cold water, which magnesium does not."],"cues":["What do the purple indicator and the burning gas tell you the products are?","What are the formulae of those products and reactants?","Which atoms are not yet equal on the two sides, and what multipliers fix them?","What does every metal atom do when it reacts, and how does that explain the difference between the two metals?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What type of substance is formed when a metal reacts with oxygen, and what is this type of reaction called?","a":"A metal oxide; the reaction is an oxidation, because the metal gains oxygen."},{"q":"Which two ions react together in a neutralisation between an acid and an alkali, and what do they form?","a":"Hydrogen ions, H⁺, from the acid and hydroxide ions, OH⁻, from the alkali; they form water: H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l)."},{"q":"Why must an ionic compound be melted or dissolved before it can be electrolysed?","a":"Only then are its ions free to move to the electrodes and carry the current."},{"q":"Which acid produces sulfate salts, and where does the positive ion in the salt come from?","a":"Sulfuric acid; the positive ion comes from the base, alkali or carbonate that neutralises it."},{"q":"How is a titre made accurate enough to use? (chemistry only)","a":"Add the acid dropwise near the end point and repeat until two titres are concordant, within 0.10 cm³, then mean only those."},{"q":"State the electron definitions of oxidation and reduction. (Higher tier only)","a":"Oxidation is the loss of electrons; reduction is the gain of electrons."},{"q":"A solution's pH falls by two units. What happens to its hydrogen ion concentration? (Higher tier only)","a":"It increases by a factor of 10 × 10 = 100."}]},"room":{"facts":["Chemical changes is examined on Paper 1, which covers topics 4.1 to 4.5: a written exam of 1 hour 45 minutes, worth 100 marks and 50 % of the GCSE. Paper 2 may draw only on the fundamental concepts of 4.1 to 4.3, so this topic belongs to Paper 1.","The entry codes are 8462F for Foundation Tier and 8462H for Higher Tier; the content marked (HT only) in this room — oxidation and reduction in terms of electrons, ionic equations, half equations, titration calculations, and strong and weak acids — is examined on Higher Tier papers only.","Questions drawing on the knowledge and understanding gained from the eight required practical activities are worth at least 15 % of the marks for the qualification, and three of the eight are in this room: Required practical 1 (a pure, dry soluble salt), Required practical 2 (titration) and Required practical 3 (electrolysis of aqueous solutions).","Oxidation is the gain of oxygen (or the loss of electrons); reduction is the loss of oxygen (or the gain of electrons).","Reactivity series, most to least reactive: potassium, sodium, lithium, calcium, magnesium, carbon, zinc, iron, hydrogen, copper.","Metal + acid → salt + hydrogen; acid + metal oxide or hydroxide → salt + water; acid + metal carbonate → salt + water + carbon dioxide.","Hydrochloric acid gives chloride salts, nitric acid gives nitrate salts and sulfuric acid gives sulfate salts; the positive ion comes from the base, alkali or carbonate.","The pH scale runs from 0 to 14: below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral, above 7 is alkaline, measured with universal indicator or a pH probe.","Neutralisation of an acid by an alkali: H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l).","In a titration only concordant titres, within 0.10 cm³ of each other, are used to work out the mean titre; the rough titration is left out.","(Higher tier only) moles = concentration (mol/dm³) × volume (dm³), and concentration in g/dm³ = concentration in mol/dm³ × Mr.","(Higher tier only) A strong acid is completely ionised in aqueous solution and a weak acid only partially ionised; as the pH falls by one unit the hydrogen ion concentration rises by a factor of 10.","Positive ions move to the cathode (the negative electrode) and negative ions to the anode (the positive electrode), where they are discharged as elements; a molten binary compound gives the metal at the cathode and the non-metal at the anode.","In the electrolysis of an aqueous solution: hydrogen forms at the cathode unless the metal is less reactive than hydrogen, when the metal forms; oxygen forms at the anode unless halide ions are present, when the halogen forms.","Aluminium is manufactured by electrolysing a molten mixture of aluminium oxide and cryolite, with carbon as the positive electrode, which must be replaced continually.","(Higher tier only) Half equations at the electrodes: cathode 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂ (reduction); anode 4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻, which may also be written 4OH⁻ − 4e⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O (oxidation)."],"questions":[{"q":"Sodium is heated in air and reacts with the oxygen in it. Write the word equation for this reaction and state, in terms of oxygen, why the sodium has been oxidised.","a":"sodium + oxygen → sodium oxide; the sodium has gained oxygen.","sol":"Marking points: (1) the word equation sodium + oxygen → sodium oxide; (2) the sodium has gained oxygen, and oxidation is the gain of oxygen. Written in symbols the reaction is 4Na + O₂ → 2Na₂O.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Metals all react with oxygen to give the same class of compound.","Put the two reactants on the left and the single product on the right, then compare what the metal has before the reaction with what it has after it.","Your answer is one word equation followed by one short sentence that uses the word oxygen."],"codes":["4.4.1.1"]},{"q":"Name the salt produced when nitric acid reacts with potassium hydroxide.","a":"Potassium nitrate.","sol":"Marking point: potassium nitrate. Nitric acid always produces a nitrate salt and the positive ion comes from the alkali, so potassium hydroxide supplies the potassium ion: potassium hydroxide + nitric acid → potassium nitrate + water, or HNO₃ + KOH → KNO₃ + H₂O.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["One part of a salt's name is decided by the acid and the other by the alkali.","Match the acid to the ending of the salt it makes, then take the metal from the alkali.","Your answer is a two-word salt name."],"codes":["4.4.2.2"]},{"q":"A solution has a pH of 3. State whether the solution is acidic, neutral or alkaline, and name the ion that acids produce in aqueous solution.","a":"Acidic; hydrogen ions (H⁺).","sol":"Marking points: (1) acidic, because a pH below 7 on the 0 to 14 scale is acidic; (2) hydrogen ions, H⁺, which is what all acids produce in aqueous solution. The lower the pH, the greater the concentration of those ions.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about where pH 7 sits on the scale and which side of it this solution is on.","Recall which ion every acid releases into solution and which ion every alkali releases.","Your answer is one word for the type of solution and the name of one ion."],"codes":["4.4.2.4"]},{"q":"State what is meant by an electrolyte.","a":"An ionic compound that has been melted or dissolved in water, so that its ions are free to move, and which therefore conducts electricity.","sol":"Marking points: (1) an ionic compound that is molten or dissolved in water, so its ions are free to move; (2) it therefore conducts electricity, because the moving ions carry the charge.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what has to happen to an ionic solid before its particles can move at all.","Your definition needs both the state the compound is in and the property this gives it.","Your answer is one sentence that uses the word ions."],"codes":["4.4.3.1"]},{"q":"Give the order of reactivity of these four metals, most reactive first: zinc, calcium, copper, sodium.","a":"Sodium, calcium, zinc, copper.","sol":"Marking point: all four metals in the order sodium, calcium, zinc, copper. The reactivity series you must recall runs potassium, sodium, lithium, calcium, magnesium, zinc, iron, copper, from most to least reactive; picking out only the four named metals leaves them in that order.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["These four metals all appear in the reactivity series you have learned.","Write out the whole series first, then cross out the metals that are not named in the question.","Your answer is a list of four metal names in one order."],"codes":["4.4.1.2"]},{"q":"A student adds a small piece of each of four metals, W, X, Y and Z, to cold water, and then to dilute hydrochloric acid at room temperature. W: no reaction with water, no reaction with acid. X: no reaction with water, steady fizzing with acid. Y: slow fizzing with water, vigorous reaction with acid. Z: very rapid reaction with water, too dangerous to add to acid. Determine the order of reactivity of the four metals, most reactive first, and explain how the results with water show that Y is more reactive than X.","a":"Z, Y, X, W; Y reacts with cold water but X does not, so Y forms positive ions more readily than X.","sol":"Marking points: (1) the order Z, Y, X, W; (2) Y fizzes with cold water while X gives no reaction with water at all, and only reacts once acid is used; (3) reacting with cold water shows a greater tendency to form positive ions, and that tendency is what makes a metal more reactive. W reacts with neither water nor acid, so it is the least reactive of the four.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The two tests are not equally demanding — think about which of them only the more reactive metals pass.","Sort the metals first by what they did with water, then use the acid results to separate the ones that did nothing with water.","Your answer is an ordered list of the four letters, followed by two sentences comparing Y with X."],"codes":["4.4.1.2"]},{"q":"Iron is extracted from iron(III) oxide by heating it with carbon: 2Fe₂O₃ + 3C → 4Fe + 3CO₂. Identify the substance that is reduced and the substance that is oxidised, giving a reason for each in terms of oxygen.","a":"Iron(III) oxide is reduced because it loses oxygen; carbon is oxidised because it gains oxygen.","sol":"Marking points: (1) iron(III) oxide (Fe₂O₃) is reduced; (2) because it loses its oxygen and is left as iron; (3) carbon is oxidised; (4) because it gains that oxygen and becomes carbon dioxide. This is why carbon can be used to extract any metal that is less reactive than carbon itself.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["One element moves from one substance to the other during this reaction.","Follow each substance from the left of the equation to the right and ask whether it has ended up with more oxygen or less.","Your answer names one substance and one reason for each of the two processes."],"codes":["4.4.1.3"]},{"q":"(a) Zinc reacts with dilute sulfuric acid. Write the balanced symbol equation for this reaction, including state symbols. (b) (Higher tier) The ionic equation for the same reaction is Zn + 2H⁺ → Zn²⁺ + H₂. Identify the species that is oxidised and explain your answer in terms of electrons.","a":"(a) Zn(s) + H₂SO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) + H₂(g). (b) Zinc, Zn, is oxidised, because each zinc atom loses two electrons.","sol":"Marking points: (1) the correct products, zinc sulfate and hydrogen, in the equation Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂; (2) the equation balanced, with 1 Zn, 2 H, 1 S and 4 O on each side; (3) all four state symbols correct — Zn(s) + H₂SO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) + H₂(g); (4) zinc (Zn) is oxidised; (5) because each zinc atom loses two electrons to become a Zn²⁺ ion, and oxidation is the loss of electrons.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["An acid and a reactive metal always give the same two kinds of product.","Write the word equation first and turn it into formulae, then for part (b) compare the charge on the zinc before and after the reaction.","Your answer is one balanced equation carrying four state symbols, then the name of one species with a reason that mentions electrons."],"codes":["4.4.2.1"]},{"q":"Describe how to prepare a pure, dry sample of copper sulfate crystals, starting from copper(II) oxide powder and dilute sulfuric acid.","a":"Warm the dilute sulfuric acid, add copper(II) oxide until it is in excess, filter off the excess solid, evaporate the filtrate over a water bath until it is close to saturated, leave it to cool and crystallise, then filter off the crystals and dry them.","sol":"Marking points: (1) warm a measured volume of dilute sulfuric acid gently with a Bunsen burner, without boiling it; (2) add the copper(II) oxide a spatula at a time, stirring, until no more reacts and some solid is left, so the oxide is in excess and all the acid has been used up; (3) filter the mixture to remove the excess copper(II) oxide, keeping the blue filtrate, which is the copper sulfate solution; (4) pour the filtrate into an evaporating basin and heat it over a water bath or with an electric heater to evaporate some of the water, until the solution is close to saturated; (5) leave the solution to cool slowly so that crystals of copper sulfate form; (6) filter off the crystals and dry them, for example between sheets of filter paper. The equation for the reaction is CuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["This is the required practical that begins by reacting an insoluble solid with a warm dilute acid.","Work through the stages in order: reacting the two substances, separating what has not reacted, and turning a solution into a dry solid.","Your answer is a numbered sequence of practical steps, in the order they are carried out."],"codes":["4.4.2.3","4.4.2.3 (Required practical 1)"]},{"q":"(chemistry only) Describe how a student should use a pipette and a burette to find, accurately, the volume of dilute hydrochloric acid that exactly neutralises 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution.","a":"Pipette 25.0 cm³ of the alkali into a conical flask, add a few drops of a single indicator, run acid in from the burette while swirling until the first permanent colour change, record the titre, and repeat until two titres are concordant, then mean those.","sol":"Marking points: (1) use a pipette and pipette filler to measure 25.0 cm³ of the sodium hydroxide solution into a clean conical flask; (2) add a few drops of a single indicator such as phenolphthalein or methyl orange, not universal indicator, and stand the flask on a white tile; (3) fill the burette with the dilute hydrochloric acid, record the initial reading, then run the acid in while swirling the flask, adding it dropwise near the end point, until the first permanent colour change, and work out the titre as final reading − initial reading; (4) repeat the titration until at least two titres are concordant, within 0.10 cm³ of each other, and take the mean of those concordant titres only, leaving out the rough one.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Each piece of apparatus named in the question measures one of the two solutions, and only one of them is measured out in advance.","Set the steps out in order — measuring the alkali, making the end point visible, adding the acid, and making the result reliable.","Your answer is a short method in which the last step says what is done with more than one set of readings."],"codes":["4.4.2.5","4.4.2.5 (Required practical 2)"]},{"q":"Molten zinc chloride is electrolysed using inert electrodes. Predict the product formed at each electrode, and explain why the zinc chloride has to be molten.","a":"Zinc at the cathode and chlorine at the anode; melting the compound lets its ions move to the electrodes.","sol":"Marking points: (1) zinc is produced at the cathode, the negative electrode, because the positive Zn²⁺ ions move there; (2) chlorine is produced at the anode, the positive electrode, because the negative Cl⁻ ions move there; (3) in the solid the ions are held in a lattice and cannot move, but melting frees them, so the liquid conducts and the ions can travel to the electrodes to be discharged.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Only two elements are present in this compound, and the two electrodes carry opposite charges.","Work out the charge on each of the two ions and match each one to the electrode that would attract it, then think about what has to happen to the solid before any ion can travel anywhere.","Your answer names one element at each electrode and gives one reason about how the particles move."],"codes":["4.4.3.2","4.4.3.1"]},{"q":"Sodium chloride solution is electrolysed using inert electrodes, and a gas is collected at each electrode. Predict the gas formed at each electrode, giving a reason for each, and describe the test that would confirm the gas formed at the anode.","a":"Hydrogen at the cathode, because sodium is more reactive than hydrogen; chlorine at the anode, because the solution contains halide ions; damp litmus paper held in the gas is bleached white.","sol":"Marking points: (1) hydrogen is produced at the cathode; (2) because sodium is more reactive than hydrogen, so the hydrogen ions from the water are discharged instead of the sodium ions; (3) chlorine is produced at the anode; (4) because the solution contains chloride ions, which are halide ions, and a halogen is then produced instead of oxygen; (5) hold damp litmus paper (or damp universal indicator paper) in the gas at the anode: chlorine bleaches it white.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["In an aqueous solution the water itself supplies two more kinds of ion, so there is a competition at each electrode.","Apply the cathode rule and the anode rule separately, comparing the metal with hydrogen for one and checking the negative ion for the other.","Your answer names one gas and one reason at each electrode, then one test with the result it gives."],"codes":["4.4.3.4","4.4.3.4 (Required practical 3)"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Magnesium powder is added to copper(II) sulfate solution and a displacement reaction happens: Mg + CuSO₄ → MgSO₄ + Cu. (a) Write the ionic equation for this reaction. (b) Identify which species is oxidised and which is reduced, giving a reason for each in terms of electrons.","a":"(a) Mg + Cu²⁺ → Mg²⁺ + Cu. (b) Magnesium is oxidised because each atom loses two electrons; copper ions are reduced because each gains two electrons.","sol":"Marking points: (1) the sulfate ions, SO₄²⁻, are unchanged on both sides, so they are spectator ions and are left out; (2) the ionic equation Mg + Cu²⁺ → Mg²⁺ + Cu, balanced for atoms and for charge (+2 on each side); (3) magnesium is oxidised, because each magnesium atom loses two electrons to become Mg²⁺, and oxidation is the loss of electrons; (4) the copper ions are reduced, because each Cu²⁺ ion gains two electrons to become a copper atom, and reduction is the gain of electrons.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Not everything written in the full equation actually changes during the reaction.","Write out every ion present, cross out the ones that are identical on both sides, then follow the charge on each remaining species from left to right.","Your answer is one equation showing only the species that change, then one named species with one reason for each of the two processes."],"codes":["4.4.1.4"]},{"q":"(chemistry only, Higher tier) A student titrates 25.0 cm³ portions of a sodium hydroxide solution against 0.100 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid, using methyl orange indicator. The titres obtained were: rough 19.60 cm³, then 18.55 cm³ and 18.45 cm³. The equation is NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. Calculate the concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution in mol/dm³ and in g/dm³. (Ar: H = 1, O = 16, Na = 23.)","a":"0.0740 mol/dm³, which is 2.96 g/dm³.","sol":"Marking points: (1) mean titre from the concordant results only, leaving out the rough titration: (18.55 + 18.45) ÷ 2 = 18.50 cm³; (2) volume of acid = 18.50 ÷ 1000 = 0.01850 dm³; (3) moles of HCl = concentration × volume = 0.100 × 0.01850 = 0.00185 mol; (4) the equation shows 1 mol of HCl reacts with 1 mol of NaOH, so moles of NaOH = 0.00185 mol; (5) volume of alkali = 25.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.0250 dm³, so concentration of NaOH = moles ÷ volume = 0.00185 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.0740 mol/dm³; (6) Mr(NaOH) = 23 + 16 + 1 = 40, so concentration in g/dm³ = 0.0740 × 40 = 2.96 g/dm³.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["A titration is repeated for a reason — look carefully at the three readings before using any of them.","Mark-scheme intermediate steps: only the two concordant titres are averaged, giving a mean titre of 18.50 cm³, and that volume of 0.100 mol/dm³ acid contains 0.00185 mol of HCl.","First line of working: mean titre = (18.55 + 18.45) ÷ 2 = 18.50 cm³, so volume of acid = 18.50 ÷ 1000 = 0.01850 dm³ and moles of HCl = concentration × volume = 0.100 × 0.01850."],"codes":["4.4.2.5","4.4.2.5 (Required practical 2)"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) A student has a solution of hydrochloric acid of pH 1 and adds water to it until its pH is 3. (a) Calculate how many times smaller the hydrogen ion concentration of the diluted solution is. (b) Explain why the diluted solution is still described as a strong acid.","a":"(a) 100 times smaller. (b) Strong describes complete ionisation in aqueous solution, which does not change when water is added; adding water only makes the solution more dilute.","sol":"Marking points: (1) the pH has risen by 3 − 1 = 2 units; (2) each unit of pH is a factor of 10 in hydrogen ion concentration, so the concentration is 10 × 10 = 10² = 100 times smaller; (3) a strong acid is one that is completely ionised in aqueous solution, and hydrochloric acid still ionises completely after water is added; (4) adding water changes how much acid there is in a given volume — dilute rather than concentrated — and dilute and concentrated describe amount of substance, while weak and strong describe the degree of ionisation.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Adding water changes one property of the solution but leaves another one untouched.","Mark-scheme intermediate step: the pH rises by 3 − 1 = 2 units, and one unit of pH is a factor of 10 in the hydrogen ion concentration.","First line of working: change in pH = 3 − 1 = 2 units, so the hydrogen ion concentration falls by a factor of 10 for each of those units — multiply those tens out for (a); begin (b) from what strong means, completely ionised in aqueous solution."],"codes":["4.4.2.6"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Aluminium is manufactured by the electrolysis of a molten mixture of aluminium oxide and cryolite, using carbon electrodes. (a) Explain why the aluminium oxide is mixed with cryolite. (b) Write the half equation for the reaction at the negative electrode and the half equation for the reaction at the positive electrode. (c) Explain why the positive electrode has to be replaced continually.","a":"(a) Cryolite lowers the melting point of the mixture, so less energy is needed. (b) Cathode: Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al; anode: 2O²⁻ → O₂ + 4e⁻. (c) The oxygen made at the anode reacts with the hot carbon to form carbon dioxide, so the electrode burns away.","sol":"Marking points: (1) mixing cryolite with the aluminium oxide lowers the melting point of the electrolyte; (2) so the mixture melts at a much lower temperature and less energy is needed, which reduces the cost; (3) at the negative electrode the aluminium ions gain electrons: Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al (a reduction); (4) at the positive electrode the oxide ions lose electrons: 2O²⁻ → O₂ + 4e⁻ (an oxidation); (5) the oxygen produced there reacts with the hot carbon of the positive electrode; (6) forming carbon dioxide, so the carbon is gradually used up and the electrode must be replaced. Both half equations balance for atoms and for charge.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Two of the three parts are about cost and wear rather than about the chemistry you can see: think about what has to be melted, and what the positive electrode is made of.","Take the three parts in turn: what a mixture does to a melting point, which ion goes to which electrode and how many electrons each one transfers, and what the product at the positive electrode meets when it forms.","Your answer gives one reason with its consequence, two balanced half equations, and one chemical change happening to the electrode."],"codes":["4.4.3.3","4.4.3.5"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What two products are formed when a dilute acid reacts with a metal such as magnesium?","a":"A salt and hydrogen."},{"q":"Which two ions react in a neutralisation between an acid and an alkali, and what do they form?","a":"Hydrogen ions, H⁺, and hydroxide ions, OH⁻; they form water: H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l)."},{"q":"During the electrolysis of an aqueous solution, when is the metal produced at the cathode instead of hydrogen?","a":"When the metal is less reactive than hydrogen."},{"q":"What is the difference between a strong acid and a weak acid? (Higher tier only)","a":"A strong acid is completely ionised in aqueous solution; a weak acid is only partially ionised."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Oxidation and reduction in terms of oxygen","back":"Oxidation is the gain of oxygen; reduction is the loss of oxygen. A metal reacting with oxygen forms its oxide and is oxidised: 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO.","code":"4.4.1.1","open":false},{"front":"The reactivity series","back":"Most to least reactive: potassium, sodium, lithium, calcium, magnesium, carbon, zinc, iron, hydrogen, copper. Reactivity is the tendency to form positive ions. A more reactive metal displaces a less reactive one from its compound.","code":"4.4.1.2","open":false},{"front":"Extracting a metal by reduction with carbon","back":"A metal less reactive than carbon is extracted from its oxide by heating with carbon: the oxide loses oxygen (reduced), the carbon gains it (oxidised). 2Fe₂O₃ + 3C → 4Fe + 3CO₂.","code":"4.4.1.3","open":false},{"front":"Oxidation and reduction in terms of electrons (Higher tier only)","back":"OIL RIG: oxidation is loss of electrons, reduction is gain of electrons. Ionic equation for a displacement, spectator ions left out: Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu, in which zinc is oxidised and copper ions reduced.","code":"4.4.1.4","open":false},{"front":"Metal + acid","back":"Metal + acid → salt + hydrogen, limited to magnesium, zinc and iron with hydrochloric and sulfuric acids: Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂. (Higher tier only) The metal is oxidised, the hydrogen ions reduced.","code":"4.4.2.1","open":false},{"front":"Which acid makes which salt","back":"Hydrochloric acid → chlorides; nitric acid → nitrates; sulfuric acid → sulfates. The positive ion comes from the base, alkali or carbonate. Acid + carbonate → salt + water + carbon dioxide.","code":"4.4.2.2","open":false},{"front":"Making a pure, dry soluble salt (Required practical 1)","back":"Add the insoluble oxide or carbonate to warm dilute acid until it is in excess, filter off the excess, evaporate the filtrate over a water bath until nearly saturated, cool to crystallise, then dry the crystals.","code":"4.4.2.3 (Required practical 1)","open":false},{"front":"The pH scale and neutralisation","back":"pH 0 to 14, measured with universal indicator or a pH probe: below 7 acidic (H⁺ ions), 7 neutral, above 7 alkaline (OH⁻ ions). Neutralisation: H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l).","code":"4.4.2.4","open":false},{"front":"Titration: getting an accurate titre (chemistry only, Required practical 2)","back":"Pipette 25.0 cm³ of alkali into a conical flask, add a few drops of one indicator, run acid in from a burette to the first permanent colour change; mean only concordant titres, within 0.10 cm³.","code":"4.4.2.5 (Required practical 2)","open":false},{"front":"Titration calculation (chemistry only, Higher tier only)","back":"moles = concentration (mol/dm³) × volume (dm³); volume in dm³ = volume in cm³ ÷ 1000. Use the balancing numbers for the mole ratio, then concentration in g/dm³ = concentration in mol/dm³ × Mr.","code":"4.4.2.5","open":false},{"front":"Strong, weak, dilute and concentrated acids (Higher tier only)","back":"Strong = completely ionised (hydrochloric, nitric, sulfuric); weak = partially ionised (ethanoic, citric, carbonic). Dilute and concentrated describe amount of acid per volume. Each fall of one pH unit means ten times more H⁺ ions.","code":"4.4.2.6","open":false},{"front":"Electrolyte, the electrodes, and a molten binary compound","back":"A molten or dissolved ionic compound whose free ions conduct electricity. Positive ions move to the cathode (negative), negative ions to the anode (positive), where they are discharged. Molten binary compound: metal at the cathode, non-metal at the anode.","code":"4.4.3.1","open":false},{"front":"Extracting aluminium","back":"Electrolysis of molten aluminium oxide mixed with cryolite, which lowers the melting point and so the energy needed. The carbon positive electrode reacts with the oxygen made there and must be replaced continually.","code":"4.4.3.3","open":false},{"front":"Electrolysis of aqueous solutions (Required practical 3)","back":"Cathode: hydrogen, unless the metal is less reactive than hydrogen, when the metal forms. Anode: oxygen, unless halide ions are present, when the halogen forms. Water supplies the H⁺ and OH⁻ ions.","code":"4.4.3.4 (Required practical 3)","open":false},{"front":"Half equations at the electrodes (Higher tier only)","back":"Cathode, a reduction: 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂. Anode, an oxidation: 4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻, also written 4OH⁻ − 4e⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O. Atoms and charges must balance.","code":"4.4.3.5","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Key equations and rules for Chemical changes","items":["Oxidation and reduction in terms of oxygen: oxidation = gain of oxygen, reduction = loss of oxygen. Example: 2Fe₂O₃ + 3C → 4Fe + 3CO₂, in which the iron oxide is reduced and the carbon oxidised.","(Higher tier only) Oxidation and reduction in terms of electrons: oxidation = loss of electrons, reduction = gain of electrons. Ionic equation for a displacement: Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu.","Metal + oxygen → metal oxide: 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO; 4Na + O₂ → 2Na₂O.","Metal + water → metal hydroxide + hydrogen: 2K + 2H₂O → 2KOH + H₂ (room temperature only; reactions with steam are not examined).","Metal + acid → salt + hydrogen, for magnesium, zinc and iron with hydrochloric and sulfuric acids: Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂; Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂. (Higher tier only) The ionic equation is, for example, Zn + 2H⁺ → Zn²⁺ + H₂.","Acid + metal oxide or metal hydroxide → salt + water: CuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O; HNO₃ + KOH → KNO₃ + H₂O. Acid + metal carbonate → salt + water + carbon dioxide: CuCO₃ + 2HNO₃ → Cu(NO₃)₂ + H₂O + CO₂.","Neutralisation of an acid by an alkali, as ions: H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l).","(chemistry only, Higher tier only) Titration calculations: moles (mol) = concentration (mol/dm³) × volume (dm³); concentration (mol/dm³) = moles (mol) ÷ volume (dm³); volume (dm³) = volume (cm³) ÷ 1000; concentration (g/dm³) = concentration (mol/dm³) × Mr.","(Higher tier only) pH and hydrogen ion concentration: a fall of one pH unit multiplies the hydrogen ion concentration by 10, so a fall of n units multiplies it by 10ⁿ (whole-number pH values only).","(Higher tier only) Half equations at the electrodes: cathode (reduction) 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂, Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb, Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al; anode (oxidation) 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻, 2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻, 2O²⁻ → O₂ + 4e⁻, 4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ (or 4OH⁻ − 4e⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O).","Overall equation for the extraction of aluminium: 2Al₂O₃ → 4Al + 3O₂.","None of these equations is printed on the exam paper. The only thing supplied with both Chemistry papers is the periodic table (Appendix A), which gives the relative atomic masses needed for a titration calculation, so every equation above must be recalled."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical 1: preparation of a pure, dry sample of a soluble salt from an insoluble oxide or carbonate","items":["Aim: prepare a pure, dry sample of a soluble salt from an insoluble oxide or carbonate, using a Bunsen burner to heat the dilute acid and a water bath or electric heater to evaporate the solution.","Apparatus: measuring cylinder, beaker, Bunsen burner with tripod and gauze, water bath or electric heater, glass rod, spatula, filter funnel and filter paper, conical flask or beaker for the filtrate, evaporating basin, tongs, dilute acid (for example dilute sulfuric acid), an insoluble metal oxide or carbonate (for example copper(II) oxide or copper carbonate), eye protection.","Method step 1: measure a known volume of the dilute acid into a beaker, for example 25 cm³, and warm it gently with the Bunsen burner without letting it boil, then turn the burner off.","Method step 2: add the insoluble oxide or carbonate a spatula measure at a time, stirring with the glass rod, until no more reacts and some solid stays undissolved — the solid is then in excess and all the acid has been used up.","Method step 3: filter the mixture through filter paper in a funnel to remove the excess solid, and keep the filtrate, which is a solution of the salt only.","Method step 4: pour the filtrate into an evaporating basin and heat it over a water bath or with an electric heater to evaporate some of the water, until the solution is close to saturated — a crystal forming on a cold glass rod dipped into it shows this point.","Method step 5: leave the hot, saturated solution to cool slowly so that crystals of the salt grow.","Method step 6: filter off the crystals and dry them between sheets of filter paper, or in a warm oven, rather than by strong heating, which would drive off the water of crystallisation.","Independent variable: the insoluble oxide or carbonate used with the acid (and the acid chosen), which fixes which salt is made.","Dependent variable: the salt produced and the mass of dry crystals obtained.","Control variables: the volume and concentration of the acid, the temperature to which the acid is warmed, the same filtering and crystallising technique, and the same drying method each time.","Safety: wear eye protection throughout; dilute acids are irritant, so wash splashes off the skin at once; do not boil the acid or heat the filtrate to dryness, as the hot solution can spit; use tongs to handle hot apparatus.","Likely errors: adding too little solid, so unreacted acid is carried into the filtrate and the crystals are impure; heating the filtrate too strongly, so it spits and product is lost or the crystals decompose; evaporating to dryness instead of crystallising, which leaves a powder rather than crystals; losing crystals when they are filtered or scraped out of the basin.","Expected result and analysis: a pure, dry sample of coloured or white crystals — blue copper sulfate crystals from copper(II) oxide and dilute sulfuric acid, CuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O. Purity is judged by the crystals having one regular shape and one colour; the yield is found by weighing the dry crystals and can be compared with the maximum mass the acid allows.","Skills assessed: AT 2 (safe use of appropriate heating devices and techniques, including a Bunsen burner and a water bath or electric heater), AT 3 (use of appropriate apparatus and techniques for conducting chemical reactions, including appropriate reagents), AT 4 (safe use of a range of equipment to purify and separate chemical mixtures, including evaporation, filtration and crystallisation) and AT 6 (safe use and careful handling of liquids and solids, including careful mixing of reagents under controlled conditions); key opportunities for skills development are WS 2.3 (apply a knowledge of a range of techniques, instruments, apparatus and materials to select those appropriate to the experiment) and WS 2.4 (carry out experiments appropriately, with due regard for the correct manipulation of apparatus, the accuracy of measurements and health and safety)."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical 2: determination of the reacting volumes of solutions of a strong acid and a strong alkali by titration (chemistry only)","items":["Aim: chemistry only — determine the volumes of a solution of a strong acid and a solution of a strong alkali that react exactly with each other by titration, and (Higher tier only) determine the concentration of one of the solutions in mol/dm³ and in g/dm³ from those reacting volumes and the known concentration of the other.","Apparatus: 50 cm³ burette with clamp and stand, 25 cm³ pipette and pipette filler, conical flask, white tile, small funnel, beakers, wash bottle of distilled water, a single indicator (phenolphthalein or methyl orange), a strong acid (dilute hydrochloric, nitric or sulfuric acid), a strong alkali (dilute sodium hydroxide solution), eye protection.","Method step 1: rinse the pipette with the alkali, then use the pipette and filler to run 25.0 cm³ of the alkali into a clean conical flask, and stand the flask on a white tile.","Method step 2: add a few drops of a single indicator to the alkali — phenolphthalein is pink in alkali and colourless in acid, methyl orange is yellow in alkali and red in acid; universal indicator is not used, because it changes through a range of colours rather than at one point.","Method step 3: rinse the burette with the acid, clamp it vertically, and fill it through a funnel with the burette below eye level; remove the funnel, then run acid through the tap until the tip below the tap is full and free of air bubbles.","Method step 4: read the initial burette volume to the nearest 0.05 cm³, with the bottom of the meniscus at eye level, and record it.","Method step 5: run the acid into the flask, swirling constantly, until the indicator changes colour; record the final reading and work out this rough titre as final reading − initial reading.","Method step 6: repeat with a fresh 25.0 cm³ of alkali, running the acid in quickly until just before the rough titre and then dropwise, stopping at the first permanent colour change.","Method step 7: repeat until at least two titres are concordant, within 0.10 cm³ of each other, then calculate the mean of the concordant titres only, leaving the rough titration out.","Independent variable: the volume of alkali put into the conical flask, which you set yourself with the pipette at 25.0 cm³ for every titration.","Dependent variable: the volume of acid run in from the burette to reach the end point (the titre), worked out as final reading − initial reading and judged by the first permanent colour change of the indicator.","Control variables: the concentration of the acid, the concentration of the alkali, the indicator used and the number of drops added, the same pipette and burette throughout, and the temperature of the solutions.","Safety: wear eye protection throughout; dilute acids and dilute sodium hydroxide solution are irritant, so wash splashes off the skin at once; use a pipette filler and never draw solution up by mouth; fill the burette with it clamped below eye level.","Likely errors: overshooting the end point by not adding the acid dropwise near the end; parallax error from not reading the meniscus at eye level, or recording to the wrong precision; an air bubble in the burette tip that empties during the titration and inflates the titre; leaving the funnel in the burette so that extra drops run in; not swirling, so the end point is missed; including the rough titre in the mean.","Expected result and analysis: the concordant titres agree within 0.10 cm³ — for example titres of 18.55 cm³ and 18.45 cm³ give a mean of 18.50 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid for 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution. (Higher tier only) moles of HCl = 0.100 × (18.50 ÷ 1000) = 0.00185 mol; NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O reacts 1 : 1, so moles of NaOH = 0.00185 mol; concentration of NaOH = 0.00185 ÷ (25.0 ÷ 1000) = 0.0740 mol/dm³; with Mr(NaOH) = 23 + 16 + 1 = 40, that is 0.0740 × 40 = 2.96 g/dm³.","Skills assessed: AT 1 (use of appropriate apparatus to make and record a range of measurements accurately, including the volume of liquids) and AT 8 (the determination of the concentrations of strong acids and strong alkalis); key opportunities for skills development are WS 2.4 (carry out experiments appropriately, with due regard for the correct manipulation of apparatus, the accuracy of measurements and health and safety), WS 2.6 (make and record observations and measurements using a range of apparatus and methods), MS 1a (recognise and use expressions in decimal form), MS 1c (use ratios, fractions and percentages) and MS 2a (use an appropriate number of significant figures)."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical 3: investigate what happens when aqueous solutions are electrolysed using inert electrodes","items":["Aim: investigate what happens when aqueous solutions are electrolysed using inert electrodes; this is an investigation involving developing a hypothesis, so the products are predicted from the ions present before they are tested.","Apparatus: electrolysis cell or small beaker, two inert carbon (graphite) electrodes in a holder or lid, low-voltage DC power supply, connecting leads with crocodile clips, ammeter, two small test tubes to collect the gases over the electrodes, wooden splints and matches, damp blue litmus paper, the aqueous solutions to be tested (for example copper chloride, copper sulfate, sodium chloride and sodium sulfate solutions), eye protection.","Method step 1: use the ions present in each solution, and the rules for aqueous electrolysis, to write a hypothesis predicting the product at each electrode for every solution to be tested.","Method step 2: half-fill the cell with the first solution, dip the two inert carbon electrodes into it so that they do not touch each other, and connect them to the DC power supply through the ammeter.","Method step 3: fill a small test tube with the solution and invert it over each electrode to collect any gas given off.","Method step 4: switch on the power supply, check that a current flows, and observe both electrodes for bubbles, for a coating forming or for a colour change.","Method step 5: test each gas collected — a lighted splint gives a squeaky pop with hydrogen, a glowing splint relights in oxygen, and damp litmus paper is bleached white by chlorine — and note the appearance of any solid formed on an electrode.","Method step 6: switch off, wash and dry the electrodes, and repeat steps 2 to 5 with each of the other solutions, then compare the observations with the hypothesis.","Independent variable: the aqueous solution that is electrolysed.","Dependent variable: the product identified at each electrode.","Control variables: the same inert carbon electrodes, the same voltage and current, the same time of electrolysis, and the same volume and concentration of each solution.","Safety: wear eye protection throughout; work in a well-ventilated room and use small volumes and short runs, because chlorine is toxic; do not let the electrodes touch, which would short-circuit the cell; keep the lighted splint away from the hydrogen until the tube is removed from the cell.","Likely errors: gas escaping before it can be collected, so the test is inconclusive; misreading a test, for example a splint that is lighted rather than glowing; electrodes touching and short-circuiting; too little time allowed for a visible product to form; electrodes not washed between solutions, so a product is carried over.","Expected result and analysis: the observations should match the rules — copper chloride solution gives copper on the cathode and chlorine at the anode; sodium chloride solution gives hydrogen at the cathode and chlorine at the anode; copper sulfate solution gives copper at the cathode and oxygen at the anode; sodium sulfate solution gives hydrogen at the cathode and oxygen at the anode. Each result is compared with the hypothesis, and the pattern is explained by the metal's reactivity against hydrogen at the cathode and by the presence or absence of halide ions at the anode.","Skills assessed: AT 3 (use of appropriate apparatus and techniques for conducting and monitoring chemical reactions), AT 7 (use of appropriate apparatus and techniques to draw, set up and use electrochemical cells for the separation and production of elements and compounds) and AT 8 (use of appropriate qualitative reagents and techniques to analyse and identify unknown samples or products, including gas tests for hydrogen, oxygen and chlorine); key opportunities for skills development are WS 2.1 (use scientific theories and explanations to develop hypotheses), WS 2.2 (plan experiments or devise procedures to test hypotheses), WS 2.3 (apply a knowledge of a range of techniques, instruments, apparatus and materials to select those appropriate to the experiment), WS 2.4 (carry out experiments appropriately, with due regard for the correct manipulation of apparatus, the accuracy of measurements and health and safety) and WS 2.6 (make and record observations and measurements using a range of apparatus and methods)."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: why some metals are reduced with carbon and others must be electrolysed","items":["Question (6 marks): iron is extracted from iron(III) oxide by heating it with carbon, but aluminium cannot be extracted from aluminium oxide in the same way and is obtained by electrolysis instead. Explain, using ideas about reactivity, why the two metals are extracted by different methods, and compare the energy each method needs.","Level 3 (5–6 marks): a coherent, logically structured answer that links each method to the metal's position in the reactivity series relative to carbon and compares the energy demands. Iron is less reactive than carbon, so carbon can take the oxygen away from iron(III) oxide: the oxide is reduced because it loses oxygen and the carbon is oxidised because it gains oxygen, as in 2Fe₂O₃ + 3C → 4Fe + 3CO₂. Aluminium is more reactive than carbon, so carbon cannot remove the oxygen from aluminium oxide, and the compound must instead be melted and electrolysed, where the aluminium ions gain electrons at the negative electrode and are reduced. Electrolysis uses far more energy than carbon reduction, because energy is needed both to melt the compound — which is why cryolite is mixed in, to lower the melting point — and to produce the large electric current, which is why aluminium is more expensive to extract than iron.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): names both methods and links at least one of them correctly to reactivity, for example stating that aluminium is too reactive for carbon to reduce it, but the reasoning is incomplete — the oxygen transfer in the iron reaction is not explained, or the comparison of energy is asserted without saying what the energy is used for.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): makes isolated correct statements, such as saying that the two metals are extracted in different ways, or naming electrolysis for aluminium, with no explanation of why the reactivity series decides the method.","What a bottom-level (Level 1) answer misses: it does not compare either metal with carbon in the reactivity series, which is the whole basis of the choice; it does not say that reduction means the loss of oxygen, so the roles of the iron oxide and the carbon are never identified; and it does not say what the energy in electrolysis is actually used for — melting the compound and producing the current — so no real comparison of cost is made."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: strong, weak, dilute and concentrated acids (Higher tier only)","items":["Question (6 marks, Higher tier only): a student measures the pH of three solutions with a pH probe. Solution 1 is 0.1 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid, pH 1. Solution 2 is 0.1 mol/dm³ ethanoic acid, pH 3. Solution 3 is 1.0 mol/dm³ ethanoic acid, pH 2. Explain these three pH values, using the terms strong, weak, dilute and concentrated, and state how the hydrogen ion concentration of solution 1 compares with that of solution 2.","Level 3 (5–6 marks): a coherent answer that separates the degree of ionisation from the amount of acid and uses the pH rule. Hydrochloric acid is a strong acid, so it is completely ionised in aqueous solution and every acid particle releases a hydrogen ion; at 0.1 mol/dm³ this gives the highest hydrogen ion concentration of the three and so the lowest pH, 1. Ethanoic acid is a weak acid, so it is only partially ionised and most of it stays as un-ionised molecules; at the same concentration of 0.1 mol/dm³ it therefore releases far fewer hydrogen ions and has a higher pH, 3. Solution 3 is the same weak acid but ten times more concentrated, so although it is still only partially ionised there are more acid particles in the same volume and more hydrogen ions are released, which lowers its pH to 2 — it is concentrated but still weak, showing that strong and weak describe the degree of ionisation while dilute and concentrated describe the amount of acid dissolved in a given volume. Solutions 1 and 2 differ by 3 − 1 = 2 pH units, and each pH unit is a factor of 10, so the hydrogen ion concentration of solution 1 is 10² = 100 times greater than that of solution 2.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): correctly links strong to complete ionisation and weak to partial ionisation and uses this to explain the different pH values of solutions 1 and 2, but the treatment of solution 3 confuses concentration with strength, or the comparison of hydrogen ion concentration is given as 'more' rather than worked out as a factor of 100.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): makes isolated correct statements, such as saying that hydrochloric acid is a strong acid or that a lower pH means a more acidic solution, without explaining ionisation or comparing the three solutions.","What a bottom-level (Level 1) answer misses: it treats strong and concentrated as if they meant the same thing, so it cannot explain why a more concentrated weak acid can still have a higher pH than a dilute strong acid; it does not say that a weak acid is only partially ionised; and it does not use the factor-of-ten rule, so no numerical comparison of the hydrogen ion concentrations is made."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-18T21:52:12Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"All six faults from the first judgement are fixed and nothing was broken in the process; the kit is publishable as it stands. I judged the file itself, not the copy embedded in the judge prompt. Re-solved in Python with exact fractions: 0.125 × 98 = 12.25, which rounds half up to 12.3 at three significant figures, so lesson.examples[2] step 6 is now right and consistent with MS 2a on the RP2 sheet; the mean titre is still (18.55 + 18.45)/2 = 18.50 cm³, n(HCl) = 0.100 × 0.01850 = 0.00185 mol, c(NaOH) = 0.00185/0.0250 = 0.0740 mol/dm³ and 0.0740 × 40 = 2.96 g/dm³, so the new hints on index 13 quote the intermediates correctly and stop short of the answer; the pH gap in index 14 is 3 − 1 = 2 units giving a factor of 100, and the unchanged example 3 still gives 10³ = 1000. Index 8 is now d=3, leaving tiers of 5 at d1, 6 at d2 and 5 at d3 across 16 questions, all marks between 1 and 6. Example 2's fourth step is now a real move — collecting the anode gas and relighting a glowing splint to confirm oxygen, with the copper coating mentioned in passing — and its cue asks how the prediction could be checked without naming the test. Cards 8 and 9 and questions 9 and 13 now carry the chemistry-only marker, index 13 keeping its Higher tier label; both card fronts are still items rather than questions and both backs are within the 40-word limit at 35 and 34 words. The RP2 sheet's control variables no longer repeat the alkali volume and now add \"the same pipette and burette throughout\". I re-extracted every equation from the new file and the set is byte-for-byte the one I checked before — 28 symbol, ionic and half equations that all balance for atoms and for charge when parsed mechanically, plus the K + H₂O intermediate that is explicitly labelled \"before balancing\". No answer key changed, every idea of 4.4 is still tested, the chemistry-only and all six HT-only strands are still taught, labelled and tested, the AT, WS and MS codes on the three practical sheets still match section 8.2 and section 7 word for word, there is not one caret in the file, and kit-tools check returns OK. One stylistic carry-over worth knowing rather than fixing: outside the two calculation questions, the third hint still describes the shape of the answer rather than giving a first line, which suits the describe and explain questions it is attached to."}}},"4.5":{"id":"AQA-8462","topic":"4.5","family":"science","lesson":{"why":"This room covers 4.5 Energy changes, examined on Paper 1 of GCSE Chemistry. You will classify reactions as exothermic or endothermic from the temperature change of the surroundings, draw and read reaction profiles that show activation energy, and, on Higher Tier, calculate the overall energy change of a reaction from bond energies supplied in the question. Required practical 4 is examined directly, and this room also holds three ideas that Combined Science students never meet — cells, batteries and fuel cells — where you must interpret reactivity data, evaluate the use of cells and hydrogen fuel cells and, on Higher Tier, write the half equations for the hydrogen fuel cell.","idea":[{"code":"4.5.1.1","h":"Exothermic and endothermic reactions","t":"Energy is conserved in a chemical reaction: the amount of energy in the universe at the end of the reaction is the same as before it took place. If a reaction transfers energy to the surroundings, the product molecules must store less energy than the reactants, by exactly the amount transferred. An exothermic reaction transfers energy to the surroundings, so the temperature of the surroundings increases; exothermic reactions include combustion, many oxidation reactions and neutralisation, and everyday uses include self-heating cans and hand warmers. An endothermic reaction takes in energy from the surroundings, so the temperature of the surroundings decreases; endothermic reactions include thermal decompositions and the reaction of citric acid with sodium hydrogencarbonate, and some sports injury packs are based on endothermic reactions. In the exam you distinguish the two only from the temperature change of the surroundings, and you evaluate uses and applications from information given in the question. This room is limited to measurement of temperature change: you are never asked to calculate an energy change in joules or a value of ΔH."},{"code":"4.5.1.1 (Required practical 4)","h":"Required practical 4: variables that affect temperature changes in reacting solutions","t":"Required practical 4 is an investigation of the variables that affect temperature changes in reacting solutions, such as acid plus metals, acid plus carbonates, neutralisations and displacement of metals. You choose one variable to change — for example the concentration of the acid, the volume of alkali added or the metal used — measure a fixed volume of the first solution into an insulated cup, record the starting temperature, add the second reactant, stir, and record the highest or lowest temperature reached. The temperature change is highest (or lowest) reading minus starting reading, and repeats are combined into a mean after any anomalous result is left out. In the exam you are asked to identify the independent, dependent and control variables, explain why a lidded polystyrene cup is used, spot anomalies, calculate means, plot the temperature change against the variable changed and explain why the measured change is always smaller than the true value. The skills assessed are AT 1, AT 3, AT 5 and AT 6."},{"code":"4.5.1.2","h":"Reaction profiles","t":"Chemical reactions can occur only when reacting particles collide with each other with sufficient energy, and the minimum amount of energy that particles must have to react is called the activation energy. A reaction profile plots energy on the vertical axis against the progress of the reaction on the horizontal axis, with a curved line rising from a horizontal reactants line to a peak and then falling to a horizontal products line. The height of the peak above the reactants is the activation energy; the vertical gap between the reactants line and the products line is the overall energy change. If the products are drawn below the reactants the reaction is exothermic; if they are drawn above the reactants it is endothermic. In the exam you must draw simple labelled profiles for both cases, use a profile to identify the type of reaction, and explain that the activation energy is the energy needed for a reaction to occur."},{"code":"4.5.1.3","h":"The energy change of reactions from bond energies (HT only)","t":"(HT only) During a chemical reaction, energy must be supplied to break bonds in the reactants, and energy is released when bonds in the products are formed. Both totals are worked out from the bond energies supplied in the question: multiply each bond energy by the number of bonds of that type shown in the balanced equation, then add. The overall energy change is the difference between the sum of the energy needed to break bonds in the reactants and the sum of the energy released when bonds in the products are formed — that is, bonds broken minus bonds made, in kJ/mol. In an exothermic reaction the energy released forming new bonds is greater than the energy needed to break existing bonds, so the answer is negative; in an endothermic reaction the energy needed to break existing bonds is greater than the energy released forming new bonds, so the answer is positive. In the exam you must show both totals, the subtraction and the signed answer with its unit, and say what the sign means. No bond energy is ever recalled: the values are always given."},{"code":"4.5.2.1","h":"Cells and batteries (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Cells contain chemicals which react to produce electricity. The voltage produced by a cell depends on a number of factors, including the type of electrode and the type of electrolyte. A simple cell can be made by connecting two different metals in contact with an electrolyte, and the bigger the difference in reactivity between the two metals, the greater the voltage produced. Batteries consist of two or more cells connected together in series to provide a greater voltage. In non-rechargeable cells and batteries the chemical reactions stop when one of the reactants has been used up, and alkaline batteries are non-rechargeable. Rechargeable cells and batteries can be recharged because the chemical reactions are reversed when an external electrical current is supplied. In the exam you interpret data for the relative reactivity of different metals — predicting which pair gives the larger voltage — and evaluate the use of cells. You are not expected to know details of any cells or batteries other than these."},{"code":"4.5.2.2","h":"Fuel cells (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Fuel cells are supplied by an external source of fuel, such as hydrogen, together with oxygen or air. The fuel is oxidised electrochemically within the fuel cell to produce a potential difference, and because the fuel is supplied continuously the cell keeps working as long as the fuel lasts. The overall reaction in a hydrogen fuel cell involves the oxidation of hydrogen to produce water: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, so the only product is water. Hydrogen fuel cells offer a potential alternative to rechargeable cells and batteries. In the exam you evaluate the use of hydrogen fuel cells in comparison with rechargeable cells and batteries, weighing the lack of harmful products and the continuous supply of fuel against the difficulty of storing and transporting a flammable gas and the energy needed to make the hydrogen. (HT only) You must also write the half equations for the electrode reactions: with an acidic electrolyte, 2H₂ → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ at the negative electrode and O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O at the positive electrode."}],"examples":[{"title":"Classifying a reaction from temperature data","setup":"A student pours 25 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid into a polystyrene cup and records the temperature as 20 °C. Sodium hydroxide solution is added and the thermometer reading rises to 32 °C. State whether the reaction is exothermic or endothermic, and justify your answer.","steps":["The solution acts as the surroundings, and its temperature has risen by 32 − 20 = 12 °C.","An increase in the surroundings' temperature means energy has been transferred to the surroundings.","A reaction that transfers energy to the surroundings is exothermic.","So this is an exothermic reaction — and it is a neutralisation, one of the named exothermic reaction types."],"cues":["Which part of this set-up counts as the surroundings, and by how much did its temperature change?","What does a rise in the surroundings' temperature tell you about where the energy went?","Which of the two reaction types matches that direction of energy transfer?","Which named type of exothermic reaction is taking place here?"]},{"title":"Required practical 4: processing the results","setup":"In Required practical 4 a student reacts 25 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid with excess magnesium ribbon in a lidded polystyrene cup, three times. Trial 1: start 21.0 °C, highest 30.2 °C. Trial 2: start 21.0 °C, highest 30.4 °C. Trial 3: start 20.8 °C, highest 26.0 °C. The lid was left off during trial 3. Determine the mean temperature change the student should report.","steps":["Work out each temperature change by subtracting the starting reading from the highest reading: 30.2 − 21.0 = 9.2 °C for the first trial and 30.4 − 21.0 = 9.4 °C for the second.","The third trial gives 26.0 − 20.8 = 5.2 °C, far below the other two.","That result is anomalous: with the lid off, more energy escaped to the air, so it is left out of the mean.","Mean temperature change = (9.2 + 9.4) ÷ 2 = 9.3 °C."],"cues":["How do you turn each pair of readings into a temperature change?","How does the third trial's change compare with the first two?","What was different about trial 3, and what should be done with its result?","Which values go into the mean, and what does it come to?"]},{"title":"Reading a reaction profile","setup":"A reaction profile has energy on the vertical axis and progress of reaction on the horizontal axis. A curved line starts at a horizontal line labelled 'reactants', rises to a peak, then falls to a horizontal line labelled 'products' drawn below the level of the reactants. State whether the reaction is exothermic or endothermic, and state what the height of the peak above the reactants represents.","steps":["Products are drawn lower on the energy axis than reactants, so the products store less energy.","That missing energy must have been transferred to the surroundings, so the reaction is exothermic.","The overall energy change is the vertical drop from the reactants line down to the products line.","The height of the peak measured above the reactants line is the activation energy — the minimum energy colliding particles need to react."],"cues":["Which line sits lower on the energy axis, and what does that say about the energy stored?","Where has that energy difference gone, and what does that make the reaction?","Which vertical distance on the diagram is the overall energy change?","What does the height of the peak above the reactants measure?"]},{"title":"Bond energy calculation — exothermic (Higher tier)","setup":"(Higher tier) Hydrogen reacts with chlorine: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. The bond energies supplied are H–H = 436 kJ/mol, Cl–Cl = 243 kJ/mol and H–Cl = 432 kJ/mol. Calculate the overall energy change for this reaction and state whether it is exothermic or endothermic.","steps":["Bonds broken: one H–H and one Cl–Cl, needing 436 + 243 = 679 kJ/mol.","Bonds made: 2HCl contains two H–Cl bonds, releasing 2 × 432 = 864 kJ/mol.","Energy change = bonds broken − bonds made = 679 − 864 = −185 kJ/mol.","The sign is negative, so more energy was released making bonds than was needed to break them: the reaction is exothermic."],"cues":["Which bonds have to be broken, and what do they cost altogether?","How many bonds of which type are made, and what do they release?","Which way round are the two totals subtracted?","What does the sign of that answer tell you about the reaction?"]},{"title":"Bond energy calculation — endothermic (Higher tier)","setup":"(Higher tier) Nitrogen and oxygen combine at the high temperature inside a car engine: N₂ + O₂ → 2NO. The bond energies supplied are N≡N = 945 kJ/mol, O=O = 498 kJ/mol and N=O = 631 kJ/mol. Calculate the overall energy change for this reaction and state whether it is exothermic or endothermic.","steps":["Bonds broken: the triple bond in N₂ and the double bond in O₂, needing 945 + 498 = 1443 kJ/mol.","Bonds made: 2NO contains two N=O bonds, releasing 2 × 631 = 1262 kJ/mol.","Energy change = bonds broken − bonds made = 1443 − 1262 = +181 kJ/mol.","The sign is positive, so breaking bonds cost more than making bonds gave back: the reaction is endothermic."],"cues":["Which two bonds must be broken, and what is their total?","How many bonds of which type are made, and what do they release?","Which way round are the two totals subtracted?","What does a positive answer tell you about the reaction?"]},{"title":"Half equations for a hydrogen fuel cell (Higher tier, chemistry only)","setup":"(Higher tier) A hydrogen fuel cell is supplied with hydrogen at the negative electrode and oxygen at the positive electrode, with an acidic electrolyte between them. The overall reaction is 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Write the half equation for the reaction at each electrode, and show that together they give the overall equation.","steps":["At the negative electrode the fuel is oxidised, losing electrons: 2H₂ → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻.","Those electrons travel round the external circuit to the positive electrode, where oxygen gains them: O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O.","Adding the two half equations cancels the 4H⁺ and the 4e⁻, because each appears once on the left and once on the right.","What is left is 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, so hydrogen has been oxidised to water and the only product is water."],"cues":["At which electrode does the fuel lose electrons, and what does it turn into?","Where do those electrons go, and what happens to the oxygen there?","What cancels when the two half equations are added together?","What is left once the cancelling is done, and what does it show?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A student adds magnesium to dilute acid and the temperature rises. Is this reaction exothermic or endothermic?","a":"Exothermic — the temperature of the surroundings has increased, showing energy has been transferred to them."},{"q":"On a reaction profile, what does the height from the reactants up to the peak represent?","a":"The activation energy — the minimum energy the particles must have to react when they collide."},{"q":"In a bond energy calculation, what do you do with the energy needed to break bonds and the energy released making bonds to find the overall energy change? (Higher tier)","a":"Subtract the energy released making bonds in the products from the energy needed to break bonds in the reactants."},{"q":"What sign does the overall energy change have for an exothermic reaction? (Higher tier)","a":"Negative — more energy is released forming new bonds than was used breaking old ones."},{"q":"Why does a non-rechargeable battery eventually stop working? (chemistry only)","a":"The chemical reactions inside it stop once one of the reactants has been used up, and they cannot be reversed."},{"q":"What is the only product of the overall reaction in a hydrogen fuel cell? (chemistry only)","a":"Water, made by the oxidation of hydrogen: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O."}]},"room":{"facts":["Energy changes is examined on Paper 1, which covers topics 4.1 to 4.5: a written exam of 1 hour 45 minutes, worth 100 marks and 50 % of the GCSE.","The entry code is 8462F for Foundation Tier and 8462H for Higher Tier; the (HT only) content in this room — calculating the overall energy change from bond energies, and the half equations for the hydrogen fuel cell — is examined on Higher Tier papers only.","Questions drawing on the knowledge and understanding gained from the eight required practical activities are worth at least 15 % of the marks for the qualification; the temperature-change investigation in this room is Required practical 4.","Cells, batteries and fuel cells are marked (chemistry only): they are examined in GCSE Chemistry but not in GCSE Combined Science: Trilogy.","Exothermic reaction: transfers energy to the surroundings, so the temperature of the surroundings increases (combustion, many oxidation reactions, neutralisation).","Endothermic reaction: takes in energy from the surroundings, so the temperature of the surroundings decreases (thermal decompositions, citric acid + sodium hydrogencarbonate).","Activation energy = the minimum amount of energy that particles must have to react when they collide.","Overall energy change = total energy needed to break bonds in the reactants − total energy released making bonds in the products, in kJ/mol (HT only).","A negative overall energy change means exothermic (more energy released making bonds than needed to break them); a positive one means endothermic (HT only).","A simple cell is two different metals in contact with an electrolyte; the greater the difference in reactivity of the two metals, the greater the voltage. A battery is two or more cells connected in series (chemistry only).","Rechargeable cells can be recharged because the chemical reactions are reversed when an external electrical current is supplied; in non-rechargeable cells the reactions stop when a reactant is used up (chemistry only).","The overall reaction in a hydrogen fuel cell is the oxidation of hydrogen to produce water: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O (chemistry only).","Hydrogen fuel cell half equations with an acidic electrolyte: negative electrode 2H₂ → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻; positive electrode O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O (chemistry only, HT only)."],"questions":[{"q":"State what happens to the temperature of the surroundings during an exothermic reaction.","a":"It increases.","sol":"In an exothermic reaction, energy is transferred to the surroundings, so the temperature of the surroundings increases.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about which type of reaction this is and what it does with energy.","Recall the general rule linking this reaction type to a temperature change.","Your answer is a single word describing a direction of change."],"codes":["4.5.1.1"]},{"q":"Name one everyday use of an exothermic reaction.","a":"A self-heating can (or a hand warmer).","sol":"Self-heating cans and hand warmers both use exothermic reactions to release energy and warm up; either one earns the mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think of everyday products that use a chemical reaction to get warm.","Recall the two applications listed alongside the exothermic reaction types.","Your answer names one product, not a type of reaction."],"codes":["4.5.1.1"]},{"q":"State the name given to the minimum amount of energy that particles must have to react when they collide.","a":"Activation energy.","sol":"Chemical reactions can occur only when reacting particles collide with sufficient energy; the minimum amount of energy that particles must have to react is called the activation energy.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This links to the condition a collision must meet before a reaction can happen.","Recall the quantity that a reaction profile shows as the height of the peak above the reactants.","Your answer is a two-word term."],"codes":["4.5.1.2"]},{"q":"State what a battery consists of. (chemistry only)","a":"Two or more cells connected together in series.","sol":"Batteries consist of two or more cells connected together in series, which provides a greater voltage than a single cell.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about how a battery differs from a single cell.","Recall how the cells inside a battery are joined up so that the voltages add.","Your answer is one short phrase about number and connection."],"codes":["4.5.2.1"]},{"q":"Name the substance produced by the overall reaction in a hydrogen fuel cell. (chemistry only)","a":"Water.","sol":"The overall reaction in a hydrogen fuel cell involves the oxidation of hydrogen to produce water: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Water is the only product.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what the two substances fed into the cell are made of.","Recall the overall equation for the oxidation of the fuel in this cell.","Your answer is the name of one substance."],"codes":["4.5.2.2"]},{"q":"A student mixes citric acid with sodium hydrogencarbonate and the temperature of the mixture falls from 21 °C to 15 °C. Explain whether this reaction is exothermic or endothermic.","a":"Endothermic, because the temperature of the surroundings has decreased.","sol":"The temperature of the surroundings (the mixture) has fallen, which means energy has been taken in from the surroundings (1). A reaction that takes in energy from the surroundings is endothermic (1), which is consistent with citric acid and sodium hydrogencarbonate being a named endothermic reaction.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Look at what happened to the temperature of the surroundings, not to the reactants themselves.","Decide which direction energy must have travelled to produce that temperature change.","Your answer names one of the two reaction types and then justifies it from the data."],"codes":["4.5.1.1"]},{"q":"In Required practical 4 a student investigates the reaction between dilute hydrochloric acid and calcium carbonate in an insulated cup. She changes the concentration of the acid each time, keeping the volume of acid and the mass of carbonate the same. Identify the independent variable, the dependent variable and one control variable in this investigation.","a":"Independent: concentration of the acid. Dependent: temperature change of the mixture. Control: volume of acid (or mass of carbonate).","sol":"Independent variable: the concentration of the hydrochloric acid, because that is what the student deliberately changes each time (1). Dependent variable: the temperature change of the reacting mixture, measured with a thermometer or temperature probe (1). Control variable: the volume of acid used, or the mass of calcium carbonate used — either is kept the same so that it cannot affect the result (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Read the stem for what the student alters from one run to the next, and what she keeps fixed.","Recall the three categories used to describe variables in a fair test, and what each one means.","Your answer gives one variable for each of the three categories, in turn."],"codes":["4.5.1.1 (Required practical 4)"]},{"q":"In Required practical 4 the reacting solutions are mixed in a polystyrene cup with a lid rather than in a glass beaker. Explain why, and explain why the temperature rise recorded is still smaller than the true value.","a":"Polystyrene is a poor conductor so reduces energy transfer out; the lid stops energy escaping from the open top; some energy still passes to the cup, thermometer and air, so the recorded rise is too small.","sol":"Polystyrene is a very poor conductor of energy, so it insulates the mixture and reduces the energy transferred out through the walls of the container (1). The lid stops energy escaping from the open surface by evaporation and by warm air rising out of the cup (1). Even so, some energy is still transferred to the cup, the thermometer and the surrounding air during mixing and reading, so the measured temperature rise is always a little smaller than the true rise (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the route energy takes out of a reacting mixture: through the walls, and through the open top.","Compare how well the two container materials pass energy on, and then think about what the lid blocks.","Your answer makes three separate points: one about the material, one about the lid, and one about what is still lost."],"codes":["4.5.1.1 (Required practical 4)"]},{"q":"Describe a reaction profile for an exothermic reaction, giving the position of the reactants, the products and the activation energy.","a":"Reactants are drawn as a line; the curve rises to a peak whose height above the reactants is the activation energy, then falls to a products line drawn below the reactants.","sol":"The reactants are drawn as a horizontal line at a starting energy level (1). A curved line rises from it to a peak, and the height of that peak above the reactants line is the activation energy (1). The curve then falls to a horizontal products line drawn lower than the reactants line, showing that energy has been transferred to the surroundings overall (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about where the product energy level must sit relative to the reactant level for this type of reaction.","Recall the shape of the curve between the two levels and which distance on the diagram is measured as the activation energy.","Your answer describes three features in order: reactants, peak, products."],"codes":["4.5.1.2"]},{"q":"In a reaction, the total energy needed to break the bonds in the reactants is 500 kJ/mol and the total energy released forming bonds in the products is 620 kJ/mol. Calculate the overall energy change and state whether the reaction is exothermic or endothermic. (Higher tier)","a":"−120 kJ/mol; exothermic.","sol":"Energy change = energy to break bonds − energy released making bonds = 500 − 620 = −120 kJ/mol (1). The negative sign shows that more energy was released forming bonds than was needed to break them, so the reaction is exothermic (1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Both totals you need are already given — no bond counting is required here.","Recall which of the two totals is subtracted from the other.","Your answer is a signed number with a unit, followed by one word naming the reaction type."],"codes":["4.5.1.3"]},{"q":"Two simple cells are set up with the same electrolyte. Cell A uses zinc and copper electrodes; cell B uses magnesium and copper electrodes. Explain which cell produces the greater voltage. (chemistry only)","a":"Cell B, because magnesium is more reactive than zinc, so there is a greater difference in reactivity between the two electrode metals.","sol":"The voltage of a simple cell depends on the difference in reactivity between the two metals used as electrodes — the greater the difference, the greater the voltage (1). Magnesium is more reactive than zinc, so the difference in reactivity between magnesium and copper is greater than that between zinc and copper, and cell B therefore produces the greater voltage (1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Only one thing differs between the two cells: the metal paired with copper.","Recall which property of a pair of metals the voltage of a simple cell depends on, then use the reactivity series.","Your answer picks one cell and then justifies the choice by comparing the two metals."],"codes":["4.5.2.1"]},{"q":"Explain why an alkaline battery stops working after a time and cannot be made to work again, whereas the battery in a mobile phone can. (chemistry only)","a":"Alkaline batteries are non-rechargeable: their reactions stop when one reactant is used up and cannot be reversed. A phone battery is rechargeable because its reactions are reversed by an external electrical current.","sol":"In a non-rechargeable cell or battery, such as an alkaline battery, the chemical reactions stop when one of the reactants has been used up (1), and those reactions cannot be reversed, so no more electricity can be produced (1). A rechargeable cell or battery, such as the one in a mobile phone, can be recharged because the chemical reactions in it are reversed when an external electrical current is supplied to it (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what has happened to the chemicals inside a battery that has gone flat.","Recall what an external electrical current does to the reactions in one of these two kinds of battery but not the other.","Your answer deals with the alkaline battery first and then with the phone battery."],"codes":["4.5.2.1"]},{"q":"Describe how a hydrogen fuel cell produces a potential difference. (chemistry only)","a":"Hydrogen fuel and oxygen (or air) are supplied to the cell from an external source; the hydrogen is oxidised electrochemically inside the cell, producing a potential difference; the overall reaction produces water.","sol":"The cell is supplied from an external source with a fuel, hydrogen, together with oxygen or air (1). Inside the cell the hydrogen is oxidised electrochemically rather than burnt, and this electrochemical oxidation produces a potential difference across the cell (1). The overall reaction is the oxidation of hydrogen to produce water, 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, so the cell keeps producing a potential difference for as long as fuel is supplied (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Begin with where the two substances the cell uses come from.","Recall the word the specification uses for how the fuel is changed inside the cell — it is not burning.","Your answer makes three points: the supply, what happens to the fuel, and what the reaction produces."],"codes":["4.5.2.2"]},{"q":"Methane burns in oxygen: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O. Use the bond energies C–H = 413 kJ/mol, O=O = 498 kJ/mol, C=O = 799 kJ/mol and O–H = 463 kJ/mol to calculate the overall energy change for this reaction. (Higher tier)","a":"−802 kJ/mol (exothermic).","sol":"Bonds broken: 4 × C–H + 2 × O=O = (4 × 413) + (2 × 498) = 1652 + 996 = 2648 kJ/mol (1). Bonds made: 2 × C=O + 4 × O–H = (2 × 799) + (4 × 463) = 1598 + 1852 = 3450 kJ/mol (1). Energy change = bonds broken − bonds made = 2648 − 3450 (1) = −802 kJ/mol (1). The negative sign shows the reaction is exothermic, as expected for combustion.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Work from the balanced equation: every atom in CH₄, O₂, CO₂ and H₂O is held by bonds you can count.","Deal with the reactant side and the product side as two separate totals before you combine them.","Your answer shows both totals, the subtraction between them, and a signed value with its unit."],"codes":["4.5.1.3"]},{"q":"Ammonia is made from nitrogen and hydrogen: N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃. Use the bond energies N≡N = 945 kJ/mol, H–H = 436 kJ/mol and N–H = 391 kJ/mol to calculate the overall energy change for this reaction. (Higher tier)","a":"−93 kJ/mol (exothermic).","sol":"Bonds broken: N≡N + 3 × H–H = 945 + (3 × 436) = 945 + 1308 = 2253 kJ/mol (1). Bonds made: each NH₃ molecule has three N–H bonds, so 2NH₃ has six: 6 × 391 = 2346 kJ/mol (1). Energy change = bonds broken − bonds made = 2253 − 2346 = −93 kJ/mol, so the reaction is exothermic (1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Count the bonds from the balanced equation, remembering that one of the product molecules holds more than one bond of the same type.","Total the reactant side and the product side separately before combining them.","Your answer shows the subtraction and gives a signed value with its unit."],"codes":["4.5.1.3"]},{"q":"A hydrogen fuel cell has an acidic electrolyte. Write the half equation for the reaction at the negative electrode, the half equation for the reaction at the positive electrode, and the overall equation for the reaction in the cell. (Higher tier, chemistry only)","a":"Negative electrode: 2H₂ → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻. Positive electrode: O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O. Overall: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O.","sol":"At the negative electrode the hydrogen fuel is oxidised, losing electrons: 2H₂ → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ (1). At the positive electrode oxygen gains those electrons and combines with the hydrogen ions: O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O (1). Adding the two half equations cancels the 4H⁺ and the 4e⁻, which appear on opposite sides, leaving the overall equation 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O (1). (With an alkaline electrolyte the accepted half equations are 2H₂ + 4OH⁻ → 4H₂O + 4e⁻ at the negative electrode and O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻ at the positive electrode, giving the same overall equation.)","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first which electrode the fuel arrives at, and whether the fuel is losing or gaining electrons there.","Balance each half equation for charge as well as for atoms, and make the number of electrons the same in both.","Your answer is three balanced equations, the third obtained by combining the first two."],"codes":["4.5.2.2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What happens to the temperature of the surroundings in an endothermic reaction?","a":"It decreases."},{"q":"What is the activation energy?","a":"The minimum amount of energy that particles must have to react when they collide."},{"q":"What is the overall energy change of a reaction equal to, in words? (Higher tier)","a":"The energy needed to break bonds in the reactants minus the energy released making bonds in the products."},{"q":"What is the overall reaction in a hydrogen fuel cell? (chemistry only)","a":"The oxidation of hydrogen to produce water: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Exothermic reaction","back":"A reaction that transfers energy to the surroundings, so the temperature of the surroundings increases. Examples: combustion, many oxidation reactions, neutralisation.","code":"4.5.1.1","open":false},{"front":"Endothermic reaction","back":"A reaction that takes in energy from the surroundings, so the temperature of the surroundings decreases. Examples: thermal decompositions, citric acid + sodium hydrogencarbonate.","code":"4.5.1.1","open":false},{"front":"Everyday uses of exothermic and endothermic reactions","back":"Exothermic: self-heating cans; exothermic: hand warmers; endothermic: some sports injury packs, which take in energy and so cool the injury.","code":"4.5.1.1","open":true},{"front":"Conservation of energy in a chemical reaction","back":"The amount of energy in the universe at the end of a reaction is the same as before it. If energy is transferred to the surroundings, the products store less energy than the reactants by that amount.","code":"4.5.1.1","open":false},{"front":"Required practical 4","back":"Investigate the variables that affect temperature changes in reacting solutions, eg acid plus metals, acid plus carbonates, neutralisations, displacement of metals. Skills: AT 1, AT 3, AT 5, AT 6.","code":"4.5.1.1 (Required practical 4)","open":false},{"front":"Required practical 4 — the variables","back":"Independent: the factor changed, eg acid concentration or metal used. Dependent: the temperature change of the mixture. Control: volume of solution, mass of solid, starting temperature, same container.","code":"4.5.1.1 (Required practical 4)","open":false},{"front":"Activation energy","back":"The minimum amount of energy that particles must have to react when they collide.","code":"4.5.1.2","open":false},{"front":"Reaction profile — exothermic shape","back":"Curved line rises from the reactants to a peak, then falls to products drawn lower than the reactants. Peak height above the reactants = activation energy.","code":"4.5.1.2","open":false},{"front":"Reaction profile — endothermic shape","back":"Curved line rises from the reactants to a peak, then falls to products drawn higher than the reactants. Peak height above the reactants = activation energy.","code":"4.5.1.2","open":false},{"front":"Breaking bonds and making bonds (HT only)","back":"Energy must be supplied to break bonds in the reactants; energy is released when bonds in the products are formed.","code":"4.5.1.3","open":false},{"front":"Overall energy change from bond energies (HT only)","back":"Energy change = total energy to break bonds in the reactants − total energy released making bonds in the products, in kJ/mol. Negative = exothermic; positive = endothermic.","code":"4.5.1.3","open":false},{"front":"Simple cell (chemistry only)","back":"Two different metals in contact with an electrolyte. Voltage depends on the type of electrode and electrolyte; the greater the difference in reactivity of the two metals, the greater the voltage.","code":"4.5.2.1","open":false},{"front":"Non-rechargeable and rechargeable cells (chemistry only)","back":"Non-rechargeable, eg alkaline: reactions stop when a reactant is used up. Rechargeable: the reactions are reversed when an external electrical current is supplied.","code":"4.5.2.1","open":false},{"front":"Fuel cell (chemistry only)","back":"Supplied by an external source of fuel, eg hydrogen, plus oxygen or air. The fuel is oxidised electrochemically in the cell to produce a potential difference. Overall: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O.","code":"4.5.2.2","open":false},{"front":"Hydrogen fuel cell half equations, acidic electrolyte (HT only, chemistry only)","back":"Negative electrode: 2H₂ → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻. Positive electrode: O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O.","code":"4.5.2.2","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Key equations and relationships for Energy changes","items":["Temperature change = highest (or lowest) temperature reached − starting temperature, in °C. This is the only calculation Required practical 4 asks for: the specification limits 4.5.1.1 to measurement of temperature change, so no energy change in joules and no value of ΔH is ever required.","Mean temperature change = sum of the temperature changes of the repeats ÷ the number of repeats, with any anomalous result left out of both the sum and the count.","(HT only) Overall energy change = total energy needed to break the bonds in the reactants − total energy released when the bonds in the products are formed, in kJ/mol.","(HT only) Total energy to break bonds = Σ (number of bonds of each type in the reactants × that bond energy); total energy released making bonds = Σ (number of bonds of each type in the products × that bond energy). Count the bonds from the balanced equation, including the coefficients: 2NH₃ holds six N–H bonds, not three.","(HT only) Sign of the answer: negative = exothermic (more energy released making bonds than needed to break them); positive = endothermic (more energy needed to break bonds than released making them).","(chemistry only) Overall reaction in a hydrogen fuel cell: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O.","(chemistry only, HT only) Hydrogen fuel cell half equations with an acidic electrolyte: negative electrode 2H₂ → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ (oxidation, electrons lost); positive electrode O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O (reduction, electrons gained). With an alkaline electrolyte: negative electrode 2H₂ + 4OH⁻ → 4H₂O + 4e⁻; positive electrode O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻. Both sets add to the same overall equation.","What is supplied and what must be recalled: none of these relationships is printed on the paper, and no bond energy is ever expected from memory — bond energies are always given in the question. The only insert supplied with both Chemistry papers is the periodic table (Appendix A)."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical activity 4: investigate the variables that affect temperature changes in reacting solutions such as, eg acid plus metals, acid plus carbonates, neutralisations, displacement of metals","items":["Aim: find out how one chosen variable — for example the concentration of the acid, the volume of alkali added, the mass or surface area of the solid, or the metal used — affects the temperature change when two reactants are mixed, using a neutralisation between dilute hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide solution as the worked version below.","Method 1: stand a polystyrene cup inside a glass beaker so that it cannot be knocked over, and put the lid on the cup with the thermometer through the hole in it.","Method 2: use a measuring cylinder to pour 30 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid into the polystyrene cup, and record its starting temperature once the reading is steady.","Method 3: measure 5 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution in a second measuring cylinder, add it to the acid, replace the lid and stir gently with the thermometer.","Method 4: record the highest temperature reached for that addition.","Method 5: repeat step 3 and step 4, adding the alkali in further 5 cm³ portions up to 40 cm³ in total, recording the highest temperature after each addition.","Method 6: work out the temperature change for each addition as highest temperature − starting temperature, then repeat the whole investigation and take a mean of the temperature changes at each volume, leaving out any anomalous result.","Independent variable: the one factor deliberately changed — in this version the volume of sodium hydroxide solution added; in other versions the concentration of the acid, the mass or surface area of the metal or carbonate, or the identity of the metal used for a displacement.","Dependent variable: the temperature change of the reacting mixture, found from thermometer or temperature-probe readings taken before mixing and at the peak (or trough) of the reaction.","Control variables: the volume and concentration of the reactant that is not being changed, the starting temperature of the solutions, the same insulated cup and lid, and the same stirring each time.","Apparatus (standard classroom practice — the specification lists only the AT, WS and MS skills for this practical, not a fixed equipment list): polystyrene cup with lid, glass beaker to hold it upright, thermometer reading to 0.5 °C or a temperature probe, two measuring cylinders, a balance where a solid reactant is used, a spatula and stirring rod, and eye protection.","Safety: wear eye protection throughout; dilute hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide solution are irritant, so wash splashes off the skin at once; reactions of acids with metals give off flammable hydrogen, so keep naked flames away; the cup can become hot, so handle it by the outer beaker.","Likely errors: energy transferred to the cup, the lid, the thermometer and the air makes every recorded temperature change smaller than the true value; taking the reading late means the peak (or trough) temperature is missed; poor stirring gives an uneven temperature through the mixture; reading the thermometer at the wrong angle gives a parallax error; different starting temperatures between runs shift the results.","Expected result and analysis: the temperature change grows as more alkali is added, reaches a maximum when the acid and alkali have exactly neutralised each other, and then falls again as further cold alkali only dilutes and cools the mixture; plotting temperature change against volume added and drawing two straight lines that cross gives the neutralisation point. Worked example of the processing: three repeats of an acid + magnesium run give 30.2 − 21.0 = 9.2 °C, 30.4 − 21.0 = 9.4 °C and 26.0 − 20.8 = 5.2 °C; the third is anomalous (the lid was left off), so the mean reported is (9.2 + 9.4) ÷ 2 = 9.3 °C.","Skills assessed: AT 1 (use of appropriate apparatus to make and record a range of measurements accurately, including mass, temperature and volume of liquids), AT 3 (use of appropriate apparatus and techniques for conducting and monitoring chemical reactions), AT 5 (making and recording of appropriate observations during chemical reactions, including changes in temperature) and AT 6 (safe use and careful handling of gases, liquids and solids, including careful mixing of reagents under controlled conditions, using appropriate apparatus to explore chemical changes).","Key opportunities for skills development: WS 2.1, WS 2.2, WS 2.3, WS 2.4, WS 2.6 and WS 2.7; MS 1a, MS 2a, MS 2b, MS 4a and MS 4c."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: why the burning of hydrogen is exothermic (Higher tier)","items":["Question (6 marks) (Higher tier): hydrogen burns in oxygen, 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Explain, using a reaction profile and the bond energies H–H = 436 kJ/mol, O=O = 498 kJ/mol and O–H = 463 kJ/mol, why this reaction is exothermic.","Level 3 (5–6 marks): a reaction profile for this reaction is drawn with the products lower on the energy axis than the reactants, and a curved line rising from the reactants to a peak — the height of that peak above the reactants being the activation energy — before falling to the lower products level; products below reactants identifies the reaction as exothermic, because energy has been transferred to the surroundings overall. The bond energies confirm this. Breaking the bonds in the reactants needs 2 × H–H + O=O = (2 × 436) + 498 = 872 + 498 = 1370 kJ/mol. Forming the bonds in the products releases 4 × O–H = 4 × 463 = 1852 kJ/mol, because each of the two water molecules contains two O–H bonds. The overall energy change is bonds broken − bonds made = 1370 − 1852 = −482 kJ/mol. The energy change is negative because more energy is released forming the new bonds than is needed to break the old ones, so energy is transferred to the surroundings and the reaction is exothermic.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): the answer identifies the reaction as exothermic and attempts the bond energy calculation, but has a bond-counting or arithmetic slip (commonly counting two O–H bonds instead of four), or gives only the reaction profile or only the calculation without linking the shape of the profile to the sign of the energy change.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): the answer states that the reaction is exothermic, or offers an isolated correct fact such as 'breaking bonds takes in energy' or 'the products are lower than the reactants', with no complete calculation and no labelled profile.","What a bottom-level (Level 1) answer misses: it does not produce both totals — the energy to break bonds and the energy released making bonds — does not carry out the subtraction in the right order to reach −482 kJ/mol, and never links the negative sign to the shape of the profile and to energy being transferred to the surroundings."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: hydrogen fuel cells compared with rechargeable batteries (chemistry only)","items":["Question (6 marks) (chemistry only): a car manufacturer must choose between powering a new car with a hydrogen fuel cell or with rechargeable batteries. The fuel cell is supplied with hydrogen from a tank and with oxygen from the air; the batteries are recharged from the electricity supply. Evaluate the use of a hydrogen fuel cell in this car in comparison with rechargeable batteries.","Level 3 (5–6 marks): the fuel cell is supplied from outside with hydrogen as its fuel and with oxygen or air, and the hydrogen is oxidised electrochemically inside the cell to produce a potential difference, the overall reaction being 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Because water is the only product, the car itself releases no carbon dioxide and no other pollutant at the point of use. The fuel cell can be refilled with hydrogen in a few minutes and keeps working for as long as fuel is supplied, whereas a rechargeable battery has to be recharged over a much longer time and its capacity falls as it ages, after which it must be replaced and its metals recycled or disposed of. Against this, hydrogen is a flammable gas that is difficult and expensive to store and transport, and it has to be manufactured first — usually using electricity or from hydrocarbons — so the fuel cell is only as clean as the process that made its hydrogen, and there are very few places to refuel. Rechargeable batteries can be charged anywhere there is an electricity supply and are already widely available. On balance the fuel cell is the better choice if a long range, quick refuelling and no pollutants at the point of use matter most and a hydrogen supply exists; otherwise rechargeable batteries are the more practical choice today.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): several relevant points are made on both sides — for example that the fuel cell produces only water and refuels quickly, and that hydrogen is hard to store — but the chemistry of the cell is thin (no mention of the fuel being oxidised electrochemically or of the overall equation), or the points are listed without being weighed against each other, or no judgement is reached.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): isolated correct statements such as 'fuel cells produce water' or 'batteries have to be recharged', with no comparison of the two options and no judgement.","What a bottom-level (Level 1) answer misses: it does not explain how the fuel cell works (fuel and oxygen supplied from an external source, the fuel oxidised electrochemically to give a potential difference, water as the only product), it gives points for only one of the two options rather than comparing them, and it does not weigh the advantages against the disadvantages to reach a justified judgement, which is what 'evaluate' demands."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-18T21:48:57.541567Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Re-solved in Python: every bond-energy result checks out (H2+Cl2 -185, N2+O2 +181, CH4+2O2 -802, N2+3H2 -93, 2H2+O2 -482 kJ/mol, and the given-totals question -120 kJ/mol), the Required practical 4 mean is (9.2+9.4)/2 = 9.3 degC with the lid-off 5.2 degC trial correctly excluded, both acidic half equations (2H2 -> 4H+ + 4e-; O2 + 4H+ + 4e- -> 2H2O) and both alkaline ones (2H2 + 4OH- -> 4H2O + 4e-; O2 + 2H2O + 4e- -> 4OH-) balance for atoms and charge and sum to 2H2 + O2 -> 2H2O, every reaction-profile description is right, the (chemistry only) and (HT only) labels match 4.5.2/4.5.1.3 and the fuel-cell half-equation bullet, the paper facts match 2.2 and the 15% practical weighting, and every power and charge is a true Unicode superscript with no caret anywhere - publish as it stands, with the two bond-energy questions' hints worth topping up with the running totals."}}},"4.6":{"id":"AQA-8462","topic":"4.6","family":"science","lesson":{"why":"This room covers rates of reaction and reversible reactions, examined on Paper 2 of GCSE Chemistry, which assesses topics 4.6 to 4.10. Students must calculate mean rate from mass, volume or (HT only) amount-in-moles data, read a rate from the slope of a tangent, explain rate changes using collision theory and activation energy, draw and read the reaction profile of a catalysed reaction, and — for Higher tier — predict how a system at equilibrium responds to changes in concentration, temperature and pressure using Le Chatelier's Principle. Required practical 5 is directly examinable and contributes to the marks reserved for the eight required practical activities across the course.","idea":[{"h":"Calculating mean rate of reaction","t":"Mean rate of reaction is calculated using mean rate of reaction = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken, or mean rate of reaction = quantity of product formed ÷ time taken. Quantity is measured as a mass in grams or a volume in cm³, giving units of g/s or cm³/s. (HT only) quantity of reactants may be given as an amount in moles, and the rate then has units of mol/s. Exam questions give a quantity and a time and ask for the mean rate with correct units, or give a graph of quantity of product formed (or reactant used up) against time to read values from. All students must be able to draw, and interpret, these graphs, and to draw a tangent to the curve and use the slope of that tangent as a measure of the rate of reaction at that point. (HT only) calculate the gradient of that tangent, as change in quantity ÷ change in time read from two points on the tangent line, to give the rate of reaction at one specific time rather than the mean rate over the whole reaction.","code":"4.6.1.1"},{"h":"Factors which affect the rates of chemical reactions","t":"The rate of a reaction is affected by the concentrations of reactants in solution, the pressure of reacting gases, the surface area of solid reactants, the temperature, and the presence of catalysts. You must be able to recall how changing each of these affects the rate: increasing the concentration, the pressure of the gases, the surface area or the temperature speeds the reaction up, and a catalyst speeds it up when it is present — a catalyst is either present or absent, not increased. Exam questions ask you to state the factors, to pick out which factor a set of results has tested, or to say which factor a suggested change alters — for example that crushing a solid changes its surface area, not its concentration. The explanation of why each factor works is collision theory, covered in 4.6.1.3.","code":"4.6.1.2"},{"h":"Required practical 5: how changes in concentration affect the rates of reactions","t":"Required practical 5 investigates how changes in concentration affect the rates of reactions, by a method involving measuring the volume of a gas produced and a method involving a change in colour or turbidity, and it must be an investigation involving developing a hypothesis — you write down, before you start, how you expect the rate to change as concentration increases, and what you will measure to test that. In the gas method, dilute hydrochloric acid of a known concentration reacts with a fixed length of magnesium ribbon and the hydrogen is collected in a gas syringe, the volume being recorded at set times. In the turbidity method, sodium thiosulfate solution and dilute hydrochloric acid are mixed over a paper cross and you time how long the cross takes to disappear behind the sulfur precipitate. The practical assesses AT 1, AT 3, AT 5 and AT 6. Exam questions ask you to describe either method, identify the independent, dependent and control variables, suggest improvements, or process and plot the results.","code":"4.6.1.2 (Required practical 5)"},{"h":"Collision theory and activation energy","t":"Collision theory explains how the various factors affect rates of reaction. According to this theory, chemical reactions can occur only when reacting particles collide with each other and with sufficient energy; the minimum amount of energy that particles must have to react is called the activation energy. Increasing the concentration of reactants in solution, the pressure of reacting gases, and the surface area of solid reactants increases the frequency of collisions and so increases the rate of reaction. Increasing the temperature increases the frequency of collisions and makes the collisions more energetic, and so increases the rate of reaction. Exam questions ask you to predict and explain, using collision theory, the effects of changing concentration, pressure and temperature; to explain the effect of the size of the pieces of a reacting solid in terms of surface area to volume ratio (small pieces have a larger surface area to volume ratio, so more of the solid is exposed to collisions); and to use simple ideas about proportionality, such as that doubling the concentration roughly doubles the frequency of collisions and so roughly doubles the rate.","code":"4.6.1.3"},{"h":"Catalysts and the reaction profile of a catalysed reaction","t":"Catalysts change the rate of chemical reactions but are not used up during the reaction; different reactions need different catalysts, and enzymes act as catalysts in biological systems. Catalysts increase the rate of reaction by providing a different pathway for the reaction that has a lower activation energy. A reaction profile for a catalysed reaction is drawn with energy on the vertical axis and the progress of the reaction on the horizontal axis: the reactants and the products stay at exactly the same energy levels as in the uncatalysed reaction, and the overall energy change is unchanged, but the catalysed curve has a lower hump, so the activation energy — measured from the reactants up to the top of the hump — is smaller. You should be able to identify catalysts in reactions from their effect on the rate of reaction and because they are not included in the chemical equation for the reaction, and explain catalytic action in terms of activation energy. You do not need to know the names of catalysts other than those specified in the subject content.","code":"4.6.1.4"},{"h":"Reversible reactions","t":"In some chemical reactions, the products of the reaction can react to produce the original reactants. Such reactions are called reversible reactions and are represented A + B ⇌ C + D, where the double arrow replaces the single arrow of a one-way reaction. The direction of reversible reactions can be changed by changing the conditions: heating hydrated copper(II) sulfate drives off water to give white anhydrous copper(II) sulfate, and adding water back to the anhydrous solid re-forms the blue hydrated compound. Exam questions ask you to recognise the ⇌ symbol, to state what it tells you about a given reaction, and to describe a change of conditions that reverses the direction of a named reversible reaction.","code":"4.6.2.1"},{"h":"Energy changes and reversible reactions","t":"If a reversible reaction is exothermic in one direction, it is endothermic in the opposite direction, and the same amount of energy is transferred in each case. So if the forward reaction gives out 41 kJ, the reverse reaction takes in exactly 41 kJ. Heating hydrated copper(II) sulfate to drive off the water is endothermic, and adding water to the anhydrous solid is exothermic, transferring the same amount of energy back. Exam questions give the energy change of one direction of a reversible reaction and ask you to state both the type of energy change in the opposite direction and how the amount of energy transferred compares.","code":"4.6.2.2"},{"h":"Equilibrium","t":"When a reversible reaction occurs in apparatus which prevents the escape of reactants and products — a closed system, such as a sealed flask — equilibrium is reached when the forward and reverse reactions occur at exactly the same rate. Equilibrium is dynamic: both reactions are still happening, but because they happen at the same rate the amounts of reactants and products present no longer change. Exam questions test whether you understand that equilibrium is not a reaction that has stopped, and that it can only be reached in a closed system, since anything that escapes can never react back.","code":"4.6.2.3"},{"h":"The effect of changing conditions on equilibrium (HT only)","t":"(HT only) The relative amounts of all the reactants and products at equilibrium depend on the conditions of the reaction. If a system is at equilibrium and a change is made to any of the conditions, then the system responds to counteract the change, settling at a new equilibrium position. The effects of changing conditions on a system at equilibrium can be predicted using Le Chatelier's Principle. Exam questions give a reversible reaction and a change in condition — concentration, temperature or pressure — together with the information you need, such as which direction is exothermic, and ask you to make a qualitative prediction about how the position of equilibrium shifts and what happens to the amounts present.","code":"4.6.2.4"},{"h":"The effect of changing concentration (HT only)","t":"(HT only) If the concentration of one of the reactants or products is changed, the system is no longer at equilibrium and the concentrations of all the substances will change until equilibrium is reached again. If the concentration of a reactant is increased, more products will be formed until equilibrium is reached again. If the concentration of a product is decreased, more reactants will react until equilibrium is reached again. Exam questions give data about a specific equilibrium — often a colour change, such as the deepening red of FeSCN²⁺ — and ask you to interpret it and predict the effect of a stated concentration change on the amounts of reactants or products present at the new equilibrium.","code":"4.6.2.5"},{"h":"The effect of temperature changes on equilibrium (HT only)","t":"(HT only) If the temperature of a system at equilibrium is increased, the relative amount of products at equilibrium increases for an endothermic reaction and decreases for an exothermic reaction. If the temperature of a system at equilibrium is decreased, the relative amount of products at equilibrium decreases for an endothermic reaction and increases for an exothermic reaction. The short way to think about it: raising the temperature pushes the equilibrium in the endothermic direction, lowering it pushes the equilibrium in the exothermic direction. Exam questions give the direction that is exothermic (or endothermic) for a named reversible reaction and a stated temperature change, and ask you to predict and explain the resulting change in the amounts of reactants and products at the new equilibrium.","code":"4.6.2.6"},{"h":"The effect of pressure changes on equilibrium (HT only)","t":"(HT only) For gaseous reactions at equilibrium, an increase in pressure causes the equilibrium position to shift towards the side with the smaller number of molecules as shown by the symbol equation for that reaction, and a decrease in pressure causes the equilibrium position to shift towards the side with the larger number of molecules as shown by the symbol equation for that reaction. The working is always the same: count the gas molecules on each side of the balanced symbol equation, remembering the big numbers in front, then decide which side is the smaller. Exam questions give a balanced symbol equation and a stated pressure change and ask you to predict and explain the direction of the shift; if both sides have the same number of gas molecules, a pressure change does not move the equilibrium position at all.","code":"4.6.2.7"}],"examples":[{"title":"Mean rate from a change in mass","setup":"0.20 g of magnesium ribbon reacts completely with excess dilute hydrochloric acid. The magnesium has all disappeared after 25 s. Calculate the mean rate of this reaction in g/s.","steps":["Magnesium is a reactant that is used up, so the formula needed is mean rate of reaction = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken.","The quantity here is a mass in grams and the time is in seconds, so the answer will be in g/s.","mean rate of reaction = 0.20 g ÷ 25 s.","mean rate of reaction = 0.008 g/s."],"cues":["Is the quantity you are given a reactant used up or a product formed, and which of the two mean-rate formulae does that pick out?","What unit must the answer carry, given that a mass in grams is divided by a time in seconds?","Which two numbers go into the formula, and which way round?","What is 0.20 divided by 25?"]},{"title":"Mean rate from a volume of gas","setup":"A student collects the oxygen given off as hydrogen peroxide decomposes. After 60 s, 84 cm³ of oxygen has been collected in a gas syringe. Calculate the mean rate of this reaction in cm³/s, giving your answer to 2 significant figures.","steps":["Oxygen is the product being made, so use mean rate of reaction = quantity of product formed ÷ time taken.","mean rate of reaction = 84 cm³ ÷ 60 s.","mean rate of reaction = 1.4 cm³/s (2 significant figures)."],"cues":["Is the oxygen a reactant being used up or a product being formed, and which formula follows from that?","Which number is divided by which, and what units go with each?","What is 84 divided by 60, rounded to two significant figures?"]},{"title":"(HT only) Rate at a specific time from the gradient of a tangent","setup":"A graph of the volume of hydrogen collected against time curves steeply upwards at first and then flattens. A tangent is drawn to the curve at t = 20 s. Two points are read from the tangent line itself: at t = 10 s the tangent is at 24 cm³, and at t = 30 s the tangent is at 64 cm³. (HT only) Calculate the rate of reaction at t = 20 s.","steps":["The rate at one specific time is the gradient of the tangent, so use gradient = change in volume ÷ change in time.","change in volume = 64 − 24 = 40 cm³.","change in time = 30 − 10 = 20 s.","rate of reaction at t = 20 s = 40 cm³ ÷ 20 s = 2 cm³/s."],"cues":["The question asks for the rate at one moment, not the mean rate — what feature of the graph gives that?","How far does the tangent line rise between the two points quoted?","How much time passes between the two points quoted?","What is the rise divided by the run, and what unit does it carry?"]},{"title":"(HT only) Mean rate in mol/s","setup":"Calcium carbonate reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid and the carbon dioxide given off is collected. 0.024 mol of carbon dioxide is collected in 40 s. (HT only) Calculate the mean rate of this reaction in mol/s, giving your answer in standard form.","steps":["Carbon dioxide is the product formed, so use mean rate of reaction = quantity of product formed ÷ time taken.","On Higher Tier the quantity may be an amount in moles, so dividing moles by seconds gives a rate in mol/s.","mean rate of reaction = 0.024 mol ÷ 40 s.","mean rate of reaction = 6.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol/s."],"cues":["Which of the two mean-rate formulae matches a quantity of something that is being made?","What unit does the answer carry when an amount in moles is divided by a time in seconds?","Which number is divided by which?","What is that division as a decimal, and how is it written in standard form?"]},{"title":"Surface area and collision theory","setup":"Two experiments are carried out at the same temperature. In experiment A, a single 2 g lump of calcium carbonate is added to 50 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid. In experiment B, 2 g of small calcium carbonate chips is added to 50 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid of the same concentration. Explain, using collision theory, why experiment B is faster.","steps":["Splitting the same mass into small chips exposes far more of the solid, so the chips have a larger surface area to volume ratio than the lump.","Acid particles can only react with the carbonate particles that are exposed at the surface of the solid.","With more of the solid exposed, collisions between acid particles and carbonate particles happen more frequently in experiment B.","A greater frequency of collisions means more successful collisions each second, so experiment B has the greater rate of reaction."],"cues":["The two experiments use the same mass of the same solid — what property of the solid has changed?","Where on a lump of solid can the acid actually react?","How does the exposed surface change how often acid and carbonate particles meet?","How does the frequency of collisions decide the rate?"]},{"title":"(HT only) Le Chatelier's Principle and a pressure change","setup":"In the Haber process, nitrogen and hydrogen reach equilibrium with ammonia in a sealed vessel: N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g). The forward reaction is exothermic. The pressure of the mixture is increased. (HT only) Predict and explain the effect of this change on the equilibrium yield of ammonia.","steps":["Count the gas molecules shown by the symbol equation: 1 + 3 = 4 on the left-hand side and 2 on the right-hand side.","Le Chatelier's Principle says the system responds to counteract the increase in pressure.","It counteracts a higher pressure by shifting towards the side with the smaller number of gas molecules, which here is the ammonia side.","The equilibrium position shifts to the right, so the yield of ammonia increases."],"cues":["What do you need to count on each side of the symbol equation before a pressure question can be answered?","What does a system at equilibrium always do when a condition is changed?","Which side of this equation would a shift towards fewer gas molecules mean?","What does that shift do to the amount of ammonia present?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What are the units of mean rate of reaction when the quantity is measured as a volume in cm³?","a":"cm³/s."},{"q":"Which factor increases both the frequency and the energy of particle collisions?","a":"Temperature."},{"q":"Is a catalyst included in the chemical equation for the reaction it speeds up?","a":"No — a catalyst is not used up and does not appear in the chemical equation."},{"q":"In the symbol A + B ⇌ C + D, what does the ⇌ tell you about the reaction?","a":"That it is reversible — the products can react to produce the original reactants."},{"q":"What must be true of the forward and reverse reactions for a closed system to be at equilibrium?","a":"They occur at exactly the same rate."},{"q":"(HT only) According to Le Chatelier's Principle, what does a system at equilibrium do when a condition is changed?","a":"It responds to counteract the change, settling at a new equilibrium position."}]},"room":{"facts":["The rate and extent of chemical change is examined on Paper 2, which assesses topics 4.6 to 4.10: a written exam of 1 hour 45 minutes, worth 100 marks and 50 % of the GCSE.","The entry code is 8462F for Foundation Tier and 8462H for Higher Tier; the (HT only) content in this room — quantity in moles with rate in mol/s, calculating the gradient of a tangent, and the whole of Le Chatelier's Principle — is examined on Higher Tier papers only.","Questions drawing on the knowledge and understanding gained from the eight required practical activities are worth at least 15 % of the marks for the qualification; the concentration and rate investigation in this room is Required practical 5.","mean rate of reaction = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken, or quantity of product formed ÷ time taken; units g/s or cm³/s (HT only: quantity in moles, units mol/s).","The rate of reaction at a specific time is given by the slope of the tangent drawn to the curve at that time (HT only: calculated as change in quantity ÷ change in time from two points on the tangent).","Factors that affect rate: the concentrations of reactants in solution, the pressure of reacting gases, the surface area of solid reactants, the temperature, and the presence of catalysts.","Increasing concentration, pressure or surface area increases the frequency of collisions between particles; increasing temperature increases both the frequency and the energy of collisions.","Activation energy is the minimum amount of energy that particles must have to react when they collide.","A catalyst increases rate by providing a different pathway for the reaction that has a lower activation energy; it is not used up in the reaction and does not appear in the chemical equation.","A + B ⇌ C + D: if the forward reaction is exothermic, the reverse reaction is endothermic, transferring exactly the same amount of energy.","Equilibrium is reached in apparatus which prevents the escape of reactants and products when the forward and reverse reactions occur at exactly the same rate.","(HT only) Le Chatelier's Principle: if a condition of a system at equilibrium is changed, the system responds to counteract that change — increasing a reactant's concentration forms more product, raising the temperature favours the endothermic direction, and raising the pressure shifts a gaseous equilibrium towards the side with the smaller number of molecules."],"questions":[{"q":"State what is meant by the activation energy of a reaction.","a":"The minimum amount of energy that particles must have to react when they collide.","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): the minimum amount of energy that particles must have when they collide for a reaction to take place.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This term appears whenever you use collision theory to explain why a reaction happens, or doesn't happen, when particles meet.","Think about what has to be true of a collision for it to actually lead to a reaction, rather than the particles just bouncing apart.","Give a one-sentence definition, not a description of the whole reaction."],"codes":["4.6.1.3"]},{"q":"State three factors, other than the presence of a catalyst, that affect the rate of a chemical reaction.","a":"Any three of: concentration of reactants in solution, pressure of reacting gases, surface area of solid reactants, temperature.","sol":"One mark each for any three of the following, to a maximum of 3 marks: the concentrations of reactants in solution; the pressure of reacting gases; the surface area of solid reactants; the temperature.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["This is the list of conditions you learned that change how fast a reaction happens.","Split the list into changes made to the reactants themselves and changes made to the conditions they react under.","Write three short phrases, not full sentences, and do not repeat the catalyst the question has ruled out."],"codes":["4.6.1.2"]},{"q":"A 1.2 g piece of zinc reacts completely with excess dilute sulfuric acid in 75 s. Calculate the mean rate of this reaction in g/s.","a":"0.016 g/s","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): mean rate of reaction = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken = 1.2 g ÷ 75 s. Marking point 2 (1 mark): mean rate of reaction = 0.016 g/s.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This uses the relationship linking a quantity used up or formed to the time taken.","The zinc is the reactant used up, so divide the mass given by the time given.","Your first line of working is 1.2 ÷ 75."],"codes":["4.6.1.1"]},{"q":"Sulfur dioxide reacts with oxygen to form sulfur trioxide: 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃. State what the ⇌ symbol tells you about this reaction.","a":"The reaction is reversible: the products can react to produce the original reactants.","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): the reaction is reversible — the product, sulfur trioxide, can react to produce the original reactants, sulfur dioxide and oxygen, as well as the reactants forming the product.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what makes this different from a single, one-way reaction arrow.","Consider what can happen to the substances written on the right-hand side of the equation.","Name the type of reaction first, then say in one clause what the substances on the right can do."],"codes":["4.6.2.1"]},{"q":"A student adds a 3 cm length of magnesium ribbon to excess dilute hydrochloric acid. The student repeats the experiment with the same mass of magnesium powder, at the same temperature and with acid of the same concentration. Explain, in terms of surface area to volume ratio, why the reaction with the powder is faster.","a":"The powder has a larger surface area to volume ratio, so more of the magnesium is exposed to the acid, collisions between acid particles and magnesium particles are more frequent and the rate of reaction is greater.","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): dividing the same mass of magnesium into fine powder gives the solid a much larger surface area to volume ratio than a single piece of ribbon has. Marking point 2 (1 mark): the acid particles can react only with the magnesium particles exposed at the surface of the solid, so far more of the magnesium is available to be collided with. Marking point 3 (1 mark): collisions between acid particles and magnesium particles therefore happen more frequently, so more successful collisions occur each second and the rate of reaction is greater.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Both experiments use the same mass of the same metal at the same temperature, so ask what breaking the solid up has changed about the solid itself.","Think about where on a piece of solid the acid is able to react, and how that changes when the same mass is spread over far more surface.","Set the answer out in three moves: what happens to the ratio named in the question, what that does to how often particles meet, and what that does to the rate."],"codes":["4.6.1.3"]},{"q":"Explain, using collision theory, why increasing the concentration of a reactant in solution, and increasing the pressure of reacting gases, both increase the rate of reaction.","a":"Both changes put more reactant particles into the same volume, so the particles collide more frequently, more collisions each second have at least the activation energy and the rate increases.","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): increasing the concentration of a solution, and increasing the pressure of a mixture of gases, both put more reactant particles into the same volume, so the particles are closer together. Marking point 2 (1 mark): the particles therefore collide with each other more frequently. Marking point 3 (1 mark): a greater frequency of collisions means more collisions each second have at least the activation energy, so the rate of reaction increases.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what concentration tells you about how crowded the particles are in a solution, and what pressure tells you about how crowded they are in a gas.","Link that crowding to how often the particles meet each other.","Work through three stages in order: crowding, then how often particles meet, then what that does to the rate."],"codes":["4.6.1.3"]},{"q":"Explain, using collision theory, why increasing the temperature increases the rate of a reaction.","a":"The particles move faster, so they collide more frequently, and the collisions are more energetic, so a greater proportion of collisions have at least the activation energy; both effects increase the rate.","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): increasing the temperature gives the particles more energy, so they move faster and collide more frequently. Marking point 2 (1 mark): the collisions are also more energetic, so a greater proportion of them have energy equal to or greater than the activation energy. Marking point 3 (1 mark): both effects mean more successful collisions occur each second, so the rate of reaction increases.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Temperature is the one factor on the list that changes two different things about the collisions.","Deal with how often the particles meet and with how hard they hit as two separate ideas.","Structure the answer as two effects and then one consequence for the rate."],"codes":["4.6.1.3"]},{"q":"Explain, in terms of activation energy, why adding a catalyst increases the rate of a reaction.","a":"A catalyst provides a different reaction pathway with a lower activation energy, so a greater proportion of collisions have enough energy to react and the rate increases.","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): a catalyst provides a different pathway for the reaction. Marking point 2 (1 mark): this pathway has a lower activation energy than the uncatalysed pathway. Marking point 3 (1 mark): a greater proportion of colliding particles therefore have at least the activation energy, so more collisions are successful each second and the rate of reaction increases; the catalyst itself is not used up.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what a catalyst changes about the route the reaction takes, rather than about concentration or temperature.","Link that change of route to the minimum energy a collision needs.","Move from a statement about the pathway to a statement about how many collisions now succeed."],"codes":["4.6.1.4"]},{"q":"A student wants to investigate how changing the concentration of sodium thiosulfate solution affects the rate of its reaction with dilute hydrochloric acid, which produces a cloudy sulfur precipitate. Describe how the student could carry out this investigation, including the measurement taken and how the student would know when to stop timing.","a":"Place a diluted volume of sodium thiosulfate solution over a paper cross, add a fixed volume of dilute hydrochloric acid and start timing, then stop timing and record the time when the cross can no longer be seen through the cloudy mixture; repeat for different concentrations.","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): measure out sodium thiosulfate solution and water into a conical flask standing on a piece of paper marked with a black cross, varying the volume of water to change the concentration while keeping the total volume of solution the same. Marking point 2 (1 mark): add a fixed volume of dilute hydrochloric acid and start the stopwatch immediately, swirling to mix. Marking point 3 (1 mark): look down at the cross through the solution and stop the stopwatch at the moment the cross can no longer be seen, recording that time. Marking point 4 (1 mark): repeat for a range of thiosulfate concentrations, keeping the volume and concentration of the acid and the temperature the same, and repeat each concentration to check the results.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Recall the version of Required practical 5 that follows a change in colour or turbidity rather than a volume of gas.","Think about what is diluted to change the concentration, what must stay the same each time, and what visible change marks the end point.","Set the answer out as a sequence: prepare and set up, add the second reagent and start timing, then say what you watch for to stop timing, and finish with what is repeated."],"codes":["4.6.1.2 (Required practical 5)"]},{"q":"A reversible reaction takes place in a sealed container. Explain what is meant by the reaction reaching equilibrium.","a":"The forward and reverse reactions are still happening, but at exactly the same rate, so the amounts of reactants and products stay constant.","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): the forward reaction and the reverse reaction occur at exactly the same rate as each other. Marking point 2 (1 mark): both reactions are still taking place, but because their rates are equal the amounts of reactants and products present no longer change; this can only happen in a closed system, where nothing can escape.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what 'sealed container' tells you about whether reactants and products can escape.","Compare the two reaction rates rather than asking whether the reaction has stopped.","Say something about the two rates, then something about the amounts present."],"codes":["4.6.2.3"]},{"q":"Heating hydrated copper(II) sulfate drives off water to form anhydrous copper(II) sulfate, and this forward reaction is endothermic. State and explain what can be said about the energy change of the reverse reaction.","a":"The reverse reaction is exothermic, and it transfers exactly the same amount of energy as the forward reaction takes in.","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): the reverse reaction, in which water is added back to the anhydrous solid, is exothermic. Marking point 2 (1 mark): exactly the same amount of energy is transferred in each direction, because if a reversible reaction is exothermic in one direction it is endothermic in the other and the same amount of energy is transferred in each case.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Recall the rule that links the energy change of the two directions of any reversible reaction.","There are two things to say: the type of energy change, and how the amount of energy compares with the forward reaction.","Name the type of energy change first, then make the comparison of amounts."],"codes":["4.6.2.2"]},{"q":"A student reacts marble chips with excess dilute hydrochloric acid and plots a graph of the volume of gas produced against time. A tangent drawn to the curve at t = 15 s passes through the points (5 s, 18 cm³) and (25 s, 54 cm³). Calculate the rate of reaction at t = 15 s. (Higher tier)","a":"1.8 cm³/s","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): the rate at a specific time is the gradient of the tangent, so gradient = change in volume ÷ change in time = (54 − 18) cm³ ÷ (25 − 5) s = 36 cm³ ÷ 20 s. Marking point 2 (1 mark): rate of reaction at t = 15 s = 1.8 cm³/s.","m":2,"d":3,"hints":["The question asks for the rate at one moment rather than the mean rate, which on Higher Tier means working with the tangent that has been drawn for you.","Take both readings from the straight tangent line, not from the curve, and treat the calculation as rise over run.","Your first line of working is (54 − 18) ÷ (25 − 5)."],"codes":["4.6.1.1"]},{"q":"0.035 mol of carbon dioxide is collected in 25 s when sodium carbonate reacts with excess dilute hydrochloric acid. Calculate the mean rate of this reaction in mol/s. Give your answer in standard form. (Higher tier)","a":"1.4 × 10⁻³ mol/s","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): mean rate of reaction = quantity of product formed ÷ time taken = 0.035 mol ÷ 25 s. Marking point 2 (1 mark): mean rate of reaction = 0.0014 mol/s = 1.4 × 10⁻³ mol/s.","m":2,"d":3,"hints":["The same mean-rate relationship is used when the quantity is given as an amount in moles — a Higher Tier variation.","Divide the amount of product by the time, then convert the decimal you get into standard form.","Your first line of working is 0.035 ÷ 25."],"codes":["4.6.1.1"]},{"q":"Methanol is manufactured from carbon monoxide and hydrogen in a reversible reaction: CO(g) + 2H₂(g) ⇌ CH₃OH(g). The pressure of the mixture at equilibrium is decreased. Use Le Chatelier's Principle to predict and explain the effect of this change on the equilibrium yield of methanol. (Higher tier)","a":"The equilibrium position shifts towards the side with the larger number of gas molecules, the carbon monoxide and hydrogen side, so the yield of methanol decreases.","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): there are 3 gas molecules on the left-hand side (1 CO + 2 H₂) and 1 gas molecule on the right-hand side (1 CH₃OH). Marking point 2 (1 mark): by Le Chatelier's Principle the system responds to counteract the decrease in pressure by shifting towards the side with the larger number of molecules. Marking point 3 (1 mark): that is the left-hand side, so the equilibrium position shifts to the left and the yield of methanol decreases.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["This is a Higher Tier equilibrium question, so the reasoning is Le Chatelier's Principle rather than collision theory.","Count what the symbol equation shows on each side, remembering the large numbers in front of the formulae, before deciding anything.","Give the count, then the rule about which side the system moves towards when the pressure falls, then what happens to the methanol."],"codes":["4.6.2.4","4.6.2.7"]},{"q":"In the equilibrium Fe³⁺(aq) + SCN⁻(aq) ⇌ FeSCN²⁺(aq), the FeSCN²⁺(aq) ion is deep red. A student adds more Fe³⁺(aq) to the equilibrium mixture. Predict the effect on the amount of FeSCN²⁺(aq) present at the new equilibrium, and explain your answer. (Higher tier)","a":"The amount of FeSCN²⁺(aq) increases, because increasing the concentration of a reactant makes more product form until equilibrium is reached again.","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): the amount of FeSCN²⁺(aq) at the new equilibrium increases, so the mixture goes a deeper red. Marking point 2 (1 mark): adding Fe³⁺(aq) increases the concentration of a reactant, so the system is no longer at equilibrium and more product is formed until equilibrium is reached again.","m":2,"d":3,"hints":["This Higher Tier question is about disturbing an equilibrium by changing a concentration, not a temperature or a pressure.","Work out which side of the equation the added substance sits on, then recall what the system does when a reactant's concentration is raised.","State whether the amount goes up or down first, then give the reasoning in terms of reactants and products."],"codes":["4.6.2.5"]},{"q":"Nitrogen dioxide and dinitrogen tetroxide are in equilibrium: 2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g). The forward reaction is exothermic. Predict and explain the effect of decreasing the temperature on the amount of N₂O₄ present at equilibrium. (Higher tier)","a":"The amount of N₂O₄ increases, because lowering the temperature shifts the equilibrium in the exothermic direction, which is the forward direction.","sol":"Marking point 1 (1 mark): the forward reaction, towards N₂O₄, is exothermic, so the reverse reaction, towards NO₂, is endothermic. Marking point 2 (1 mark): decreasing the temperature makes the system respond to counteract the change by shifting in the exothermic direction, which releases energy. Marking point 3 (1 mark): that is the forward direction, so the relative amount of N₂O₄ at the new equilibrium increases and the amount of NO₂ decreases.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Recall how a temperature change moves an equilibrium, depending on which direction is exothermic and which is endothermic.","Work out which direction of this particular reaction is endothermic before deciding which way a falling temperature pushes the system.","State the direction of the shift, then say what it does to the amount of N₂O₄."],"codes":["4.6.2.6","4.6.2.4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write the equation for mean rate of reaction using the quantity of product formed.","a":"mean rate of reaction = quantity of product formed ÷ time taken."},{"q":"Small pieces of a solid reactant react faster than one large lump of the same mass. Which property of the solid is greater for the small pieces?","a":"The surface area to volume ratio."},{"q":"What effect does a catalyst have on the activation energy of a reaction?","a":"It lowers it, by providing a different reaction pathway with a lower activation energy."},{"q":"In a reversible reaction at equilibrium in a closed system, what is true about the rates of the forward and reverse reactions?","a":"They are exactly the same."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Mean rate of reaction (equation and units)","back":"mean rate of reaction = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken = quantity of product formed ÷ time taken. Units: g/s or cm³/s (HT only: quantity in moles, units mol/s).","code":"4.6.1.1","open":false},{"front":"Rate at a specific time from a tangent","back":"All students: draw a tangent to the curve at that time and use its slope as the rate. HT only: calculate that slope, as change in quantity ÷ change in time read from two points on the tangent.","code":"4.6.1.1","open":false},{"front":"Factors which affect the rates of chemical reactions","back":"The concentrations of reactants in solution, the pressure of reacting gases, the surface area of solid reactants, the temperature, and the presence of catalysts.","code":"4.6.1.2","open":false},{"front":"Name one method used in Required practical 5 to follow the rate of a reaction","back":"Measuring the volume of gas produced at set times with a gas syringe or an inverted measuring cylinder over water; timing how long a paper cross takes to disappear as the mixture turns cloudy (turbidity).","code":"4.6.1.2 (Required practical 5)","open":true},{"front":"Activation energy","back":"The minimum amount of energy that particles must have to react when they collide.","code":"4.6.1.3","open":false},{"front":"Collision theory: what each factor changes","back":"Increasing concentration, pressure or surface area increases the frequency of collisions; smaller pieces of a solid have a larger surface area to volume ratio. Increasing temperature increases the frequency of collisions and makes them more energetic.","code":"4.6.1.3","open":false},{"front":"Catalyst","back":"A substance that increases the rate of a reaction by providing a different pathway with a lower activation energy, and is not used up during the reaction.","code":"4.6.1.4","open":false},{"front":"Reaction profile for a catalysed reaction","back":"Reactants and products sit at the same energy levels as without the catalyst, and the overall energy change is unchanged, but the catalysed curve has a lower hump: a smaller activation energy.","code":"4.6.1.4","open":false},{"front":"Reversible reaction (general form)","back":"A + B ⇌ C + D. The products of the reaction can react to produce the original reactants; the direction can be changed by changing the conditions.","code":"4.6.2.1","open":false},{"front":"Energy changes in a reversible reaction","back":"If a reversible reaction is exothermic in one direction, it is endothermic in the opposite direction, and the same amount of energy is transferred in each case.","code":"4.6.2.2","open":false},{"front":"Equilibrium","back":"In apparatus which prevents the escape of reactants and products, equilibrium is reached when the forward and reverse reactions occur at exactly the same rate, so the amounts present stay constant.","code":"4.6.2.3","open":false},{"front":"Le Chatelier's Principle (HT only)","back":"(HT only) If a system at equilibrium has any condition changed, the system responds to counteract that change. It predicts the effect of changing concentration, temperature or pressure.","code":"4.6.2.4","open":false},{"front":"Changing concentration at equilibrium (HT only)","back":"(HT only) Increase a reactant's concentration and more products form; decrease a product's concentration and more reactants react — in each case until equilibrium is reached again.","code":"4.6.2.5","open":false},{"front":"Changing temperature at equilibrium (HT only)","back":"(HT only) Raise the temperature and the relative amount of products increases for an endothermic reaction and decreases for an exothermic one; lower the temperature and the opposite happens.","code":"4.6.2.6","open":false},{"front":"Changing pressure at equilibrium (HT only, gases)","back":"(HT only) An increase in pressure shifts the equilibrium position towards the side with the smaller number of molecules shown by the symbol equation; a decrease shifts it towards the side with the larger number.","code":"4.6.2.7","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Key equations and relationships for The rate and extent of chemical change","items":["mean rate of reaction = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken. Quantity is a mass in grams or a volume in cm³, so the rate comes out in g/s or cm³/s.","mean rate of reaction = quantity of product formed ÷ time taken. Same units; choose this version when the data describe something being made rather than used up.","(HT only) The quantity may be given as an amount in moles, and the units of rate of reaction are then mol/s. Nothing else about the relationship changes.","Rate at a specific time = the slope of the tangent drawn to the curve of quantity against time at that time. Drawing the tangent and using its slope as a measure of rate is required of all students.","(HT only) gradient of the tangent = change in quantity ÷ change in time, read from two points on the tangent line itself (never from the curve). For example (54 − 18) cm³ ÷ (25 − 5) s = 36 cm³ ÷ 20 s = 1.8 cm³/s.","Collision theory in words: rate depends on the frequency of collisions and on the proportion of collisions with energy of at least the activation energy. Concentration, pressure and surface area change only the first; temperature changes both.","Simple proportionality: doubling the concentration of a reactant in solution roughly doubles the frequency of collisions, and so roughly doubles the rate of reaction.","Reversible reaction: A + B ⇌ C + D. Exothermic in one direction means endothermic in the other, with the same amount of energy transferred each way.","At equilibrium in a closed system: rate of the forward reaction = rate of the reverse reaction.","(HT only) Le Chatelier's Principle: change a condition of a system at equilibrium and the system responds to counteract the change. Concentration — raise a reactant's concentration, more product forms; lower a product's concentration, more reactant reacts. Temperature — raise it and the equilibrium shifts in the endothermic direction, lower it and it shifts in the exothermic direction. Pressure (gases) — raise it and the equilibrium shifts towards the side with the smaller number of molecules shown by the symbol equation; lower it and it shifts towards the side with the larger number.","What is supplied and what must be recalled: none of these relationships is printed on the paper, so all of them must be recalled. The only insert supplied with both Chemistry papers is the periodic table (Appendix A)."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical activity 5: investigate how changes in concentration affect the rates of reactions by a method involving measuring the volume of a gas produced and a method involving a change in colour or turbidity","items":["Aim and hypothesis: the specification says this should be an investigation involving developing a hypothesis, so before any apparatus is touched, write down how you expect the rate to change as concentration increases — for example 'the rate of reaction is directly proportional to the concentration of the acid, because more particles in the same volume collide more often' — and state the measurement you will use to test it.","Method A step 1 (gas volume): use a measuring cylinder to put dilute hydrochloric acid and distilled water into a conical flask in the volumes that give the acid concentration being tested, keeping the total volume of liquid the same in every run.","Method A step 2 (gas volume): clean a 3 cm length of magnesium ribbon of the same mass with emery paper so no oxide layer remains; use the same length and mass of ribbon in every run.","Method A step 3 (gas volume): drop the magnesium into the acid, immediately push in the bung carrying the delivery tube to the gas syringe (or to an inverted, water-filled measuring cylinder in a trough) and start the stopwatch as the bung goes in.","Method A step 4 (gas volume): record the volume of hydrogen collected every 10 s until no more gas is produced, then repeat the whole run at each of the other acid concentrations.","Method A step 5 (gas volume): for each concentration plot volume of gas against time, compare the steepness of the curves, and calculate the mean rate of reaction as volume of gas produced ÷ time taken; a tangent drawn to a curve gives the rate at one specific time, and (HT only) its gradient gives that rate as a number.","Method B step 1 (colour or turbidity): use a measuring cylinder to put sodium thiosulfate solution and distilled water into a conical flask in the volumes that give the thiosulfate concentration being tested, keeping the total volume the same in every run, and stand the flask on a piece of paper marked with a black cross.","Method B step 2 (colour or turbidity): add a fixed volume of dilute hydrochloric acid, start the stopwatch at the moment the acid goes in, and swirl once to mix.","Method B step 3 (colour or turbidity): look down through the solution at the cross and stop the stopwatch the instant the cross can no longer be seen behind the pale yellow sulfur precipitate; record the time.","Method B step 4 (colour or turbidity): repeat at each of the other thiosulfate concentrations, keeping everything else the same, and repeat each concentration to check that the times are reproducible; the shorter the time, the faster the reaction, so 1 ÷ time is a measure of rate.","Independent variable: the concentration of one reactant in solution — the hydrochloric acid in Method A, the sodium thiosulfate solution in Method B — changed by diluting a stock solution with distilled water while keeping the total volume constant.","Dependent variable: Method A, the volume of gas collected at each set time; Method B, the time taken for the cross to disappear from view.","Control variables: the temperature of the solutions, the total volume of the diluted solution, and the fixed amount of the other reactant — the length and mass of the cleaned magnesium ribbon in Method A, the volume and concentration of the hydrochloric acid in Method B — together with the same apparatus, the same swirling and the same observer.","Apparatus (standard classroom practice — the specification lists the AT, WS and MS skills for this practical, not a fixed equipment list): conical flask, bung with delivery tube, gas syringe or measuring cylinder inverted over water in a trough, measuring cylinders for each solution and for the water, stopwatch, paper marked with a black cross, magnesium ribbon with a ruler and emery paper, and eye protection.","Safety: wear eye protection throughout; dilute hydrochloric acid is an irritant, so wash splashes off at once; the reaction of magnesium with acid gives off flammable hydrogen, so keep naked flames away; the thiosulfate and acid reaction gives off toxic sulfur dioxide, so work in a well-ventilated room or a fume cupboard and dispose of the mixture promptly.","Likely errors: judging the exact moment the cross disappears is subjective and differs between observers; gas escapes if the bung is not pushed in the instant the magnesium is added, so the early volumes are too low; an uncleaned magnesium ribbon, or one cut to a different length, changes the amount of reactant; the reaction warms the mixture, so the temperature does not stay perfectly constant; reading a measuring cylinder off the meniscus gives a parallax error.","Expected result and how it is analysed: the more concentrated the solution, the faster the reaction — in Method A the curve of gas volume against time is steeper at the start and levels off sooner, and in Method B the time for the cross to disappear is shorter. Plot mean rate (or 1 ÷ time) against concentration: a straight line through the origin supports the hypothesis that rate is proportional to concentration. Worked example of the processing: a run collecting 84 cm³ of gas in 60 s has a mean rate of 84 ÷ 60 = 1.4 cm³/s, while a tangent drawn at t = 15 s passing through (5 s, 18 cm³) and (25 s, 54 cm³) gives a rate at that instant of (54 − 18) ÷ (25 − 5) = 36 ÷ 20 = 1.8 cm³/s.","Skills assessed: AT 1 (use of appropriate apparatus to make and record a range of measurements accurately, including mass, time, temperature and volume of liquids and gases), AT 3 (use of appropriate apparatus and techniques for conducting and monitoring chemical reactions), AT 5 (making and recording of appropriate observations during chemical reactions including the measurement of rates of reaction by a variety of methods such as production of gas and colour change) and AT 6 (safe use and careful handling of gases, liquids and solids, including careful mixing of reagents under controlled conditions, using appropriate apparatus to explore chemical changes).","Key opportunities for skills development: WS 2.1, WS 2.2, WS 2.3, WS 2.4, WS 2.6 and WS 2.7; MS 1a, MS 1c, MS 1d, MS 2a, MS 2b, MS 4a, MS 4b, MS 4c, MS 4d and MS 4e."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: the effect of increasing temperature on the equilibrium yield of sulfur trioxide (HT only)","items":["Question (6 marks) (HT only): sulfur dioxide reacts with oxygen in a reversible reaction to form sulfur trioxide, 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g). The forward reaction is exothermic. A chemical plant increases the temperature of the reaction mixture at equilibrium. Predict and explain the effect of this change on the amount of sulfur trioxide present at the new equilibrium.","Level 3 (5–6 marks) model answer: increasing the temperature decreases the amount of sulfur trioxide at the new equilibrium. By Le Chatelier's Principle, when a condition of a system at equilibrium is changed the system responds to counteract that change. Increasing the temperature adds energy to the system, so the equilibrium shifts in the direction that takes energy in — the endothermic direction — to counteract the rise in temperature. Because the forward reaction, towards sulfur trioxide, is exothermic, the reverse reaction, back towards sulfur dioxide and oxygen, must be endothermic, and the same amount of energy is transferred in each direction. The equilibrium position therefore shifts to the left, so once the new equilibrium is reached there is less sulfur trioxide and more sulfur dioxide and oxygen. Both reactions are still occurring at the new equilibrium, at equal rates to each other.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): correctly states that the amount of sulfur trioxide decreases and links this to Le Chatelier's Principle or to the forward reaction being exothermic, but does not fully explain both which direction is endothermic and why the system shifts that way, or muddles which direction is exothermic and which is endothermic.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): states that increasing the temperature changes the position of equilibrium, or that the forward reaction is exothermic, without correctly giving the direction of the shift or the resulting change in the amount of sulfur trioxide.","What a bottom-level (Level 1) answer misses: it does not name Le Chatelier's Principle as the reason the system responds to the change, does not identify the reverse reaction as the endothermic direction, and does not connect the shift to a decrease, rather than an increase, in the amount of sulfur trioxide."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: plan the gas-volume method of Required practical 5","items":["Question (6 marks): a student has a stock solution of dilute hydrochloric acid, magnesium ribbon, and the usual laboratory apparatus. Describe an investigation the student could carry out to test the hypothesis that the rate of reaction between magnesium and hydrochloric acid increases as the concentration of the acid increases, using a method that measures the volume of gas produced. Include the variables that must be controlled and how the results would be used.","Level 3 (5–6 marks) model answer: measure out, with a measuring cylinder, a set volume of the stock hydrochloric acid and add distilled water to give the first concentration to be tested, keeping the total volume of liquid the same for every concentration so that only the concentration changes. Pour this into a conical flask. Clean a 3 cm length of magnesium ribbon with emery paper and check its mass, using the same length and mass each time. Drop the ribbon into the acid, immediately push in a bung carrying a delivery tube to a gas syringe, and start a stopwatch as the bung goes in, so that no hydrogen escapes before it can be measured. Record the volume of hydrogen in the syringe every 10 s until no more gas is given off. Repeat the whole run for at least four more concentrations of acid, and repeat each concentration to check the results are reproducible. The independent variable is the concentration of the acid, the dependent variable is the volume of gas collected at each time, and the control variables are the temperature of the acid, the total volume of liquid, and the length, mass and cleanliness of the magnesium ribbon. Plot volume of gas against time for each concentration on the same axes: the more concentrated the acid, the steeper the curve at the start and the sooner it levels off. Calculate a mean rate for each run as volume of gas produced ÷ time taken, or draw a tangent to each curve at the same early time and use its slope as the rate at that moment, then plot rate against concentration; a straight line through the origin supports the hypothesis.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): describes a workable method with the acid diluted and the gas collected and timed, but leaves out the control variables or the fixed total volume, or says only that the results are compared rather than how a rate is obtained from them.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): gives isolated correct points, such as that the acid is diluted or that a gas syringe collects the hydrogen, with no ordered method, no variables identified and nothing said about how the results would be processed.","What a bottom-level (Level 1) answer misses: it does not keep the total volume of liquid the same so that concentration is the only variable changed, does not identify the independent, dependent and control variables, does not seal the flask at the instant the magnesium is added, and does not turn the readings into a rate that can be plotted against concentration to test the hypothesis."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-18T22:16:31Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Re-judged the kit file itself: every fix lands and every number re-solves in Python — 1.2 ÷ 75 = 0.016 g/s, 0.035 ÷ 25 = 0.0014 = 1.4 × 10⁻³ mol/s, (54 − 18) ÷ (25 − 5) = 36 ÷ 20 = 1.8 cm³/s, and the untouched worked examples still give 0.008 g/s, 1.4 cm³/s, 2 cm³/s and 6.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol/s; Zn + H₂SO₄, Na₂CO₃ + HCl, 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃ and CO + 2H₂ ⇌ CH₃OH all balance, and the new pressure prediction is right (3 gas molecules to 1, so a decrease in pressure shifts the equilibrium left and the methanol yield falls); question 3 is now 1 mark for one marking point, question 11 one checkable Higher-tier answer, exit[1] single-answer, lesson.idea[1] correct about catalysts, all 12 idea codes still tested, 16 questions at 4/7/5 across the tiers, cards, facts, formulae, the Required practical 5 sheet (8.2.5 verbatim, AT 1/3/5/6, WS 2.1–2.4/2.6/2.7, MS 1a–4e) and both extended responses unchanged and still correct, and no power anywhere uses a caret; publish it, but put the all-tier tangent-drawing question back before a Foundation class sees it."}}},"4.7":{"id":"AQA-8462","topic":"4.7","family":"science","lesson":{"why":"Organic chemistry is examined on Paper 2 of GCSE Chemistry, and the separate science asks far more of it than Combined Science does: as well as crude oil, fractional distillation, combustion and cracking, you must know the alkenes and their addition reactions with hydrogen, water and the halogens, the alcohols and the carboxylic acids with their functional groups and reactions, addition and condensation polymerisation, amino acids and the naturally occurring polymers. Examiners set structured questions that ask you to write and balance symbol equations, to draw fully displayed structural formulae and repeating units, to give the conditions for a named reaction, and (on Higher Tier) to explain condensation polymerisation and why a carboxylic acid is a weak acid. Marks are commonly lost by confusing alkanes with alkenes, by equations that do not balance, by structures in which a carbon atom does not have four bonds, and by giving a reaction without its conditions, so this room drills the families, the equations, the structures and the conditions that the mark schemes reward.","idea":[{"code":"4.7.1.1","h":"Crude oil, hydrocarbons and alkanes","t":"Crude oil is a finite resource drilled from rocks, formed from the remains of an ancient biomass of plankton buried in mud over millions of years. It is a mixture of a very large number of compounds, most of which are hydrocarbons — molecules made of hydrogen and carbon atoms only, with no other elements present. Most of the hydrocarbons in crude oil belong to one family, the alkanes, whose general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂: substitute a value of n to predict a formula, or check a given formula (for example C₅H₁₂) against the pattern to confirm it fits. You must recognise the first four alkanes — methane (CH₄), ethane (C₂H₆), propane (C₃H₈) and butane (C₄H₁₀) — from a molecular or structural formula, but you do not need to name any alkane beyond butane."},{"code":"4.7.1.2","h":"Fractional distillation and petrochemicals","t":"Crude oil is not very useful until its hydrocarbons are separated by fractional distillation. The oil is heated until it vaporises, and the vapour rises through a fractionating column that is hot at the bottom and cool at the top; each fraction condenses back to a liquid at the height where the column temperature has fallen to that fraction's boiling point, so short-chain, low-boiling fractions condense near the top and long-chain, high-boiling fractions condense near the bottom. Exam questions ask you to explain the separation in terms of evaporation and condensation, not just to name the process. The fractions supply fuels such as petrol, diesel oil, kerosene, heavy fuel oil and liquefied petroleum gases, and feedstock for the petrochemical industry, which makes solvents, lubricants, polymers and detergents; you do not need the names of individual fractions beyond this list. The huge variety of carbon compounds made from them exists because carbon atoms can bond together into families of similar compounds."},{"code":"4.7.1.3","h":"Properties of hydrocarbons and combustion","t":"As the number of carbon atoms in a hydrocarbon molecule increases, three properties change in a predictable direction: boiling point increases, viscosity increases (the liquid becomes thicker and flows less easily), and flammability decreases (longer molecules are harder to ignite). You must be able to state these three trends for a given pair of hydrocarbons, not describe one molecule alone. Combustion of a hydrocarbon fuel is an oxidation reaction that releases energy; in complete combustion, with plenty of oxygen, the carbon is oxidised to carbon dioxide and the hydrogen is oxidised to water. You must be able to write and balance the symbol equation for complete combustion given only the formula of the hydrocarbon, checking that the number of C, H and O atoms matches on both sides."},{"code":"4.7.1.4","h":"Cracking and alkenes","t":"Cracking breaks long-chain hydrocarbons, which are in low demand as fuels, into smaller, more useful molecules, because there is high demand for fuels made of small molecules. Catalytic cracking passes vaporised hydrocarbon over a hot powdered catalyst at a moderate temperature; steam cracking mixes vaporised hydrocarbon with steam and heats it to a very high temperature. Both methods produce a mixture of alkanes and alkenes, and you must be able to balance an equation for cracking when given the formulae of reactants and products, using the fact that atoms are conserved. Alkenes are more reactive than alkanes and turn orange/brown bromine water colourless — the standard test used to identify them — and they are useful as the starting materials for polymers and other chemicals, which is why cracking matters for modern life. In GCSE Chemistry, unlike Combined Science, you also need the names and formulae of the first four alkenes, which are set out in 4.7.2.1."},{"code":"4.7.2.1","h":"Structure and formulae of alkenes (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Alkenes are hydrocarbons that contain a carbon–carbon double bond, C=C, and their homologous series has the general formula CₙH₂ₙ. An alkene is described as unsaturated because its molecule holds two fewer hydrogen atoms than the alkane with the same number of carbon atoms: compare ethene, C₂H₄, with ethane, C₂H₆. The first four alkenes are ethene (C₂H₄), propene (C₃H₆), butene (C₄H₈) and pentene (C₅H₁₀), and you must recognise an alkene from its name, from a molecular formula that fits CₙH₂ₙ, or from a displayed structural formula showing the double bond. In the displayed formula of ethene, the two carbon atoms are joined by a double line and each carries two hydrogen atoms; in propene, one carbon of the double bond carries two hydrogen atoms, the other carries one hydrogen atom and a CH₃ group. Every carbon atom must end up with four bonds in total — that is the check an examiner applies to any structure you draw."},{"code":"4.7.2.2","h":"Reactions of alkenes (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) The functional group of the alkenes is the C=C double bond, and it is the functional group, not the length of the chain, that decides how a family of organic compounds reacts. Alkenes burn in oxygen like any other hydrocarbon — complete combustion gives carbon dioxide and water, as in C₂H₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 2H₂O — but in air they tend to burn with smoky flames because combustion is incomplete. Their characteristic reactions are addition reactions, in which atoms add across the double bond so that it becomes a single carbon–carbon bond: with hydrogen over a nickel catalyst at about 150 °C an alkene becomes the alkane (C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆); with steam, using a phosphoric acid catalyst at about 300 °C and a high pressure, it becomes an alcohol (C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH); with chlorine, bromine or iodine at room temperature it becomes a dihalogeno compound (C₂H₄ + Br₂ → C₂H₄Br₂). You must be able to draw the fully displayed structural formulae of the first four alkenes and of these addition products, giving every carbon atom four bonds."},{"code":"4.7.2.3","h":"Alcohols (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Alcohols all contain the functional group –OH. The first four are methanol (CH₃OH), ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH, also written C₂H₅OH), propanol (C₃H₇OH) and butanol (C₄H₉OH), and you must recognise an alcohol from its name or from a formula written either way or as a displayed structure. You must be able to describe four things they do: they react with sodium, which fizzes and gets smaller as hydrogen is given off; they burn in air, the complete combustion of ethanol being C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O (combustion is the only reaction of alcohols for which you are expected to write an equation); they dissolve in water to form neutral solutions with a pH of about 7; and they are oxidised by an oxidising agent to carboxylic acids, ethanol giving ethanoic acid. Their main uses are as fuels and as solvents, and ethanol is the alcohol in alcoholic drinks. Ethanol is also made as an aqueous solution by fermenting a sugar solution with yeast, whose enzymes work best at about 25–35 °C in the absence of air."},{"code":"4.7.2.4","h":"Carboxylic acids (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Carboxylic acids all contain the functional group –COOH. The first four are methanoic acid (HCOOH), ethanoic acid (CH₃COOH), propanoic acid (C₂H₅COOH) and butanoic acid (C₃H₇COOH), and you must recognise one from its name or from a formula or displayed structure. You must be able to describe three reactions: with carbonates they fizz, giving a salt, water and carbon dioxide, which turns limewater milky; in water they dissolve to give acidic solutions; and with alcohols, using an acid catalyst, they produce esters — ethanoic acid with ethanol gives ethyl ethanoate, the only ester you need to name, which has a sweet, fruity smell. You are not expected to write balanced equations for these reactions. (HT only) You must also explain why carboxylic acids are weak acids: in aqueous solution only a small proportion of their molecules ionise to release H⁺ ions, so the concentration of H⁺ ions is lower and the pH higher than for a strong acid of the same concentration."},{"code":"4.7.3.1","h":"Addition polymerisation (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Alkenes are used to make polymers such as poly(ethene) and poly(propene) by addition polymerisation, in which many small molecules called monomers join together to make one very large molecule called a polymer. You can recognise a monomer that will polymerise this way by its functional group: it must contain a C=C double bond. In the reaction the double bond opens, leaving a spare bond on each carbon atom, and these spare bonds join the monomers end to end. Because no other molecule is produced, the repeating unit of an addition polymer contains exactly the same atoms as the monomer, so poly(ethene) has the repeating unit –CH₂–CH₂– and poly(propene) has the repeating unit –CH₂–CH(CH₃)–. Exam questions ask you to draw the formation of the polymer from a given alkene monomer, written as n CH₂=CH₂ → –(CH₂–CH₂)ₙ–, with a bond drawn out of each end of the repeating unit and the brackets and the n in place, and to work back from a repeating unit to the monomer it came from."},{"code":"4.7.3.2","h":"Condensation polymerisation (chemistry only) (HT only)","t":"(chemistry only) (HT only) Condensation polymerisation involves monomers with two functional groups. When these monomers react they join together and usually lose a small molecule such as water, which is what makes the reaction a condensation. The simplest polymers of this kind are made from two different monomers, each carrying two of the same functional group. For example, ethanediol, HOCH₂CH₂OH, has an –OH group at each end and hexanedioic acid, HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH, has a –COOH group at each end; an –OH reacts with a –COOH to form an ester link, and because each molecule still has a free group at its other end the two monomers join alternately into a very long chain — a polyester — releasing one water molecule for every link formed: n HOCH₂CH₂OH + n HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH → [–OCH₂CH₂OOC(CH₂)₄CO–]ₙ + 2n H₂O. This is the same long-chain form: it writes the polymer as n repeating units and leaves the groups on the two ends of the chain out, so it counts one water molecule for every monomer molecule used, while the 2n monomer molecules in one chain in fact make (2n − 1) links and release (2n − 1) water molecules. You must be able to explain this by referring to the functional groups in the monomers and the repeating unit in the polymer, and to contrast it with addition polymerisation, where no small molecule is lost."},{"code":"4.7.3.3","h":"Amino acids (chemistry only) (HT only)","t":"(chemistry only) (HT only) Amino acids are different from the monomers of a polyester because a single amino acid molecule carries two different functional groups: a basic amino group, –NH₂, and an acidic carboxyl group, –COOH. The simplest is glycine, H₂NCH₂COOH. Amino acids react by condensation polymerisation: the –COOH group of one molecule reacts with the –NH₂ group of the next, a molecule of water is lost at every link, and the molecules join end to end to give a polypeptide, n H₂NCH₂COOH → [–HNCH₂CO–]ₙ + n H₂O. That is the long-chain form of the equation: the polymer is written as n repeating units, the –H and the –OH left on the two ends of the chain are not written in, and so one water molecule is counted for every unit. Count the links themselves and a chain of n amino acids has only (n − 1) of them, because the molecule at each end of the chain is joined on one side only, so n amino acids release (n − 1) water molecules — six glycine molecules release five — and writing the end groups in gives n H₂NCH₂COOH → H[–HNCH₂CO–]ₙOH + (n − 1) H₂O. Both equations balance, and in a chain long enough to be a real protein n and (n − 1) differ by too little to matter. Because each amino acid still has a free –NH₂ at one end and a free –COOH at the other, the chain can keep growing. When different amino acids are combined in the same chain the product is a protein, and it is the order of the different amino acids along the chain that makes one protein different from another."},{"code":"4.7.3.4","h":"DNA and other naturally occurring polymers (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Not all polymers are manufactured: several of the largest molecules in living things are natural polymers. DNA, deoxyribonucleic acid, is a large molecule essential for life because it encodes the genetic instructions for the development and functioning of living organisms and viruses. Most DNA molecules consist of two polymer chains wound into a double helix, and each chain is made from four different monomers called nucleotides. Other naturally occurring polymers important for life are proteins, which are made from amino acids, and starch and cellulose, which are both made from sugars — specifically from glucose. The examinable skill here is naming the monomer for each polymer: nucleotides for DNA, amino acids for proteins, and glucose for starch and for cellulose."}],"examples":[{"title":"Balance the equation for the complete combustion of propane","setup":"A gas heater burns propane, C₃H₈, completely in plenty of air. The unbalanced equation is C₃H₈ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O. Write the balanced symbol equation for this reaction, using whole-number coefficients.","steps":["Balance carbon first: 3 carbon atoms in C₃H₈ need 3CO₂, giving C₃H₈ + O₂ → 3CO₂ + H₂O.","Balance hydrogen next: 8 hydrogen atoms in C₃H₈ need 4H₂O, giving C₃H₈ + O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O.","Count the oxygen atoms now on the right-hand side: (3 × 2) + 4 = 10, so 5O₂ is needed on the left.","The balanced equation is C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O, with 3 C, 8 H and 10 O atoms on each side."],"cues":["Which element is easiest to balance first, and how many carbon dioxide molecules does it need?","How many water molecules do the hydrogen atoms of propane require?","How many oxygen atoms are now on the product side, and how many O₂ molecules supply them?","Does every element balance in the finished equation?"]},{"title":"Balance an equation for cracking decane","setup":"Decane, C₁₀H₂₂, is cracked. One of the two products is octane, C₈H₁₈, and the other is a single hydrocarbon. Write the balanced equation for the cracking reaction and identify the homologous series and the name of the second product.","steps":["Carbon atoms are conserved: 10 on the left, 8 of them used by C₈H₁₈, leaving 2 for the second product.","Hydrogen atoms are conserved: 22 on the left, 18 of them used by C₈H₁₈, leaving 4 for the second product.","The second product is therefore C₂H₄, so the equation is C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄.","C₂H₄ fits the general formula CₙH₂ₙ with n = 2, so it is an alkene, and the first alkene is ethene."],"cues":["How many carbon atoms must the second product contain?","How many hydrogen atoms are left over for the second product?","What is the formula of the second product, and what does the full equation look like?","Which homologous series does that formula belong to, and what is the compound called?"]},{"title":"Add bromine across the double bond of propene","setup":"Propene, C₃H₆, is shaken with orange/brown bromine water at room temperature. Write the balanced symbol equation for the addition reaction, describe the fully displayed structure of the product, and state what is seen.","steps":["Bromine adds across the C=C double bond: one bromine atom joins each of the two carbon atoms of the double bond, which becomes a single bond.","The equation is C₃H₆ + Br₂ → C₃H₆Br₂, which balances with 3 C, 6 H and 2 Br atoms on each side.","In the displayed structure the first carbon atom holds two hydrogen atoms and one bromine atom, the middle carbon atom holds one hydrogen atom and one bromine atom, and the third carbon atom holds three hydrogen atoms; all the carbon–carbon bonds are now single, and every carbon atom has four bonds.","The orange/brown colour of the bromine water disappears and the mixture becomes colourless, which is the test that shows propene is an alkene."],"cues":["Which bond is attacked, and where do the two bromine atoms end up?","What is the formula of the product, and does the equation balance?","How many hydrogen and bromine atoms does each carbon atom hold in the product?","What colour change does the observer record?"]},{"title":"Balance the equation for the complete combustion of ethanol","setup":"Ethanol, C₂H₅OH, is burned as a fuel in plenty of air. The unbalanced equation is C₂H₅OH + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O. Remember that the ethanol molecule itself already contains one oxygen atom. Write the balanced symbol equation.","steps":["Balance carbon: the 2 carbon atoms in C₂H₅OH need 2CO₂.","Balance hydrogen: C₂H₅OH holds 6 hydrogen atoms altogether, 5 in the C₂H₅ group and 1 in the –OH group, so 3H₂O is needed.","Count the oxygen atoms needed on the right: (2 × 2) + 3 = 7; one of them comes from the ethanol molecule itself, so the oxygen gas must supply 6 atoms.","6 oxygen atoms means 3O₂, so the balanced equation is C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O."],"cues":["How many carbon dioxide molecules do the carbon atoms of ethanol require?","How many hydrogen atoms does one ethanol molecule hold altogether?","How many oxygen atoms are needed on the right, and how many of them must come from O₂?","How many O₂ molecules is that, and what is the finished equation?"]},{"title":"Draw the repeating unit of poly(propene)","setup":"Poly(propene) is made by addition polymerisation from the monomer propene, C₃H₆, whose displayed formula is CH₂=CH–CH₃. Describe the repeating unit of poly(propene) and write the equation for the polymerisation using n.","steps":["The C=C double bond in each monomer opens, leaving a single bond between those two carbon atoms and one spare bond on each of them.","The spare bonds join the monomers end to end, and no other molecule is produced, so the repeating unit contains exactly the same atoms as one propene molecule.","The repeating unit is –CH₂–CH(CH₃)–: a carbon atom carrying two hydrogen atoms joined to a carbon atom carrying one hydrogen atom and a CH₃ side group, with a bond drawn out of each end of the unit.","The equation is written n CH₂=CHCH₃ → –(CH₂–CH(CH₃))ₙ–, with the brackets enclosing the repeating unit and n outside them."],"cues":["What happens to the double bond when the monomer reacts?","What holds the monomers together, and is any other product formed?","Which atoms appear in the repeating unit, and how are they arranged?","How is the whole polymerisation written as one equation using n?"]},{"title":"Work out the water lost when glycine polymerises (Higher tier)","setup":"(Higher tier) Glycine is an amino acid with the formula H₂NCH₂COOH: it has an –NH₂ group at one end of the molecule and a –COOH group at the other. Six glycine molecules react together in a condensation polymerisation to form one polypeptide chain. Explain how the chain forms and determine how many molecules of water are released.","steps":["A link forms when the –COOH group of one glycine molecule reacts with the –NH₂ group of the next, joining the two molecules and releasing one small molecule of water.","Because each monomer carries one group of each kind, every molecule can react at both ends, so the molecules join end to end into a chain instead of stopping after a single link.","Six molecules joined in a line need one link fewer than the number of molecules, so the chain contains 5 links.","One water molecule leaves per link, so 5 molecules of water are released, and what is left is a polypeptide whose repeating unit is –HNCH₂CO–, with a free –NH₂ at one end of the chain and a free –COOH at the other: in general, n H₂NCH₂COOH → H[–HNCH₂CO–]ₙOH + (n − 1) H₂O, which is the long-chain equation n H₂NCH₂COOH → [–HNCH₂CO–]ₙ + n H₂O with the end groups of the chain written in."],"cues":["Which two functional groups react when two glycine molecules join, and what small molecule leaves?","Why does the reaction carry on instead of stopping at a pair of molecules?","How many links are there in a chain of six molecules?","How many water molecules leave altogether, and what is left behind?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the general formula of the alkenes?","a":"CₙH₂ₙ"},{"q":"What are the conditions for the reaction of an alkene with hydrogen?","a":"A nickel catalyst at about 150 °C."},{"q":"What is produced when an alkene reacts with steam?","a":"An alcohol — ethene gives ethanol."},{"q":"What is seen when sodium is added to ethanol?","a":"Effervescence as hydrogen is given off, and the sodium gets smaller."},{"q":"(Higher tier) What small molecule is lost each time a link forms in condensation polymerisation?","a":"Water."},{"q":"Which monomers make up DNA, and which make up starch and cellulose?","a":"DNA is made from nucleotides; starch and cellulose are made from glucose."}]},"room":{"facts":["Organic chemistry is examined on Paper 2, which covers topics 4.6 to 4.10: a written exam of 1 hour 45 minutes, worth 100 marks and 50 % of the GCSE.","The entry code is 8462F for Foundation Tier and 8462H for Higher Tier; the (HT only) content in this room — condensation polymerisation, amino acids, and why carboxylic acids are weak acids — is examined on Higher Tier papers only.","Questions drawing on the knowledge and understanding gained from the eight required practical activities are worth at least 15 % of the marks for the qualification; topic 4.7 itself names no required practical.","Everything in 4.7.2 and 4.7.3 is marked (chemistry only): alkenes and their reactions, alcohols, carboxylic acids and the polymers are examined in GCSE Chemistry but not in GCSE Combined Science: Trilogy.","Alkanes: general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ — the first four are methane (CH₄), ethane (C₂H₆), propane (C₃H₈) and butane (C₄H₁₀).","Alkenes: general formula CₙH₂ₙ, functional group C=C, unsaturated — the first four are ethene (C₂H₄), propene (C₃H₆), butene (C₄H₈) and pentene (C₅H₁₀).","Complete combustion of a hydrocarbon: hydrocarbon + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water.","As chain length increases: boiling point increases, viscosity increases, flammability decreases.","Bromine water test: orange/brown → colourless with an alkene; stays orange/brown with an alkane.","Addition reactions of alkenes: with hydrogen (nickel catalyst, about 150 °C) → alkane; with steam (phosphoric acid catalyst, about 300 °C, high pressure) → alcohol; with chlorine, bromine or iodine at room temperature → a dihalogeno compound. In every case the double bond becomes a single bond.","Alcohols: functional group –OH; methanol, ethanol (C₂H₅OH), propanol and butanol. They react with sodium to give hydrogen, burn to give carbon dioxide and water, dissolve to give neutral solutions, and are oxidised to carboxylic acids. Uses: fuels and solvents, and ethanol in alcoholic drinks.","Fermentation: a sugar solution plus yeast, at about 25–35 °C, in the absence of air, gives an aqueous solution of ethanol.","Carboxylic acids: functional group –COOH; methanoic, ethanoic (CH₃COOH), propanoic and butanoic acids. With carbonates they give a salt, water and carbon dioxide; with alcohols and an acid catalyst they give esters — ethanoic acid plus ethanol gives ethyl ethanoate. (HT only) They are weak acids because they ionise only partially in water.","Addition polymerisation: n CH₂=CH₂ → –(CH₂–CH₂)ₙ–; the repeating unit has the same atoms as the monomer because no other molecule is formed.","Condensation polymerisation (HT only): monomers with two functional groups join and lose a small molecule, usually water — ethanediol + hexanedioic acid → a polyester + water; glycine, H₂NCH₂COOH, → a polypeptide + water.","DNA is two polymer chains in a double helix, made from four different monomers called nucleotides; proteins are made from amino acids, and starch and cellulose from glucose."],"questions":[{"q":"State what is meant by the term hydrocarbon.","a":"A molecule made of hydrogen and carbon atoms only.","sol":"A hydrocarbon is a molecule made up of hydrogen and carbon atoms only, with no other elements present.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the family of compounds that make up most of crude oil.","Recall the short definition of this term rather than an example of it.","Answer in one sentence naming the two elements allowed."],"codes":["4.7.1.1"]},{"q":"Give the general formula for the homologous series of alkenes and explain why alkenes are described as unsaturated.","a":"CₙH₂ₙ; an alkene contains a carbon–carbon double bond, so its molecule holds two fewer hydrogen atoms than the alkane with the same number of carbon atoms.","sol":"The alkenes form a homologous series with the general formula CₙH₂ₙ, where n is the number of carbon atoms. Every alkene molecule contains one carbon–carbon double bond, C=C, and therefore holds two fewer hydrogen atoms than the alkane with the same number of carbon atoms — ethene is C₂H₄ while ethane is C₂H₆ — and a molecule that does not hold as many hydrogen atoms as it could is described as unsaturated.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the one feature of an alkene molecule that an alkane of the same chain length does not have.","Compare the number of hydrogen atoms in an alkene with the number in the alkane that has the same number of carbon atoms.","Give a formula written in terms of n, then one sentence of explanation."],"codes":["4.7.2.1"]},{"q":"Name the functional group present in the alcohols and the functional group present in the carboxylic acids.","a":"Alcohols: –OH. Carboxylic acids: –COOH.","sol":"Every alcohol contains the functional group –OH, which is why ethanol is written CH₃CH₂OH. Every carboxylic acid contains the functional group –COOH, which is why ethanoic acid is written CH₃COOH. It is the functional group, rather than the length of the carbon chain, that decides how the whole homologous series reacts.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the group of atoms that every member of each family has in common.","Recall the group written at the end of the formula of ethanol, then the one written at the end of the formula of ethanoic acid.","Write two groups of atoms, one for each family."],"codes":["4.7.2.3","4.7.2.4"]},{"q":"State the colour change seen when bromine water reacts with an alkene.","a":"Orange/brown to colourless.","sol":"Bromine water is orange/brown; an alkene reacts with it and the colour changes to colourless, which is used as the chemical test for alkenes.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the test used to tell alkanes and alkenes apart.","Recall the colour of bromine water before it meets a C=C double bond, and after.","State a starting colour and a finishing colour."],"codes":["4.7.1.4"]},{"q":"Name the monomers from which most DNA molecules are made, and describe the shape of a DNA molecule.","a":"Nucleotides — four different ones; the molecule is two polymer chains in the form of a double helix.","sol":"Most DNA molecules are made of two polymer chains twisted around each other in the form of a double helix. Each chain is built from monomers called nucleotides, and there are four different nucleotides. DNA is essential for life because it encodes the genetic instructions for the development and functioning of living organisms and viruses.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the small units that are joined together to build each of the two chains.","Recall the name given to those monomers, and how many different kinds there are, then picture how the two chains are arranged in space.","Name one type of monomer and describe the shape in two or three words."],"codes":["4.7.3.4"]},{"q":"Explain, in terms of evaporation and condensation, how fractional distillation separates crude oil into fractions.","a":"Crude oil is vaporised by heating; the vapours rise up a column that is cooler higher up, and each fraction condenses back to a liquid where the column temperature falls to its boiling point, so lower-boiling fractions condense higher up and higher-boiling fractions condense lower down.","sol":"Crude oil is heated so that most of its hydrocarbons evaporate. The vapours rise through a fractionating column, which is hot at the bottom and progressively cooler towards the top. As a vapour rises it cools, and each fraction condenses back into a liquid at the height where the column temperature matches its boiling point: short-chain hydrocarbons, which have low boiling points, condense near the top, while long-chain hydrocarbons, which have high boiling points, condense near the bottom.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what happens to crude oil as it is heated, and what happens to a hot vapour as it moves up a column that gets cooler.","Link the boiling point of a fraction to the height in the column at which it turns back into a liquid.","Write two or three linked sentences moving from heating, through rising vapour, to condensing at different heights."],"codes":["4.7.1.2"]},{"q":"Hexane (C₆H₁₄) and decane (C₁₀H₂₂) are compared. State and explain the difference in their boiling points, viscosity and flammability.","a":"Decane has the longer chain, so it has a higher boiling point, is more viscous, and is less flammable than hexane.","sol":"Decane has more carbon atoms than hexane, so its molecules are larger. Larger hydrocarbon molecules have a higher boiling point, are more viscous, and are less flammable, so decane has a higher boiling point than hexane, is more viscous than hexane, and is less flammable than hexane.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about which of the two named hydrocarbons has the longer carbon chain and so the larger molecules.","Apply the three trends linking molecular size to boiling point, viscosity and flammability to this pair.","Give three separate comparisons, one for each property."],"codes":["4.7.1.3"]},{"q":"Describe the reaction of ethene with hydrogen, give the conditions used, and name the product.","a":"Hydrogen adds across the C=C double bond, which becomes a single carbon–carbon bond; a nickel catalyst at about 150 °C is used; the product is ethane, C₂H₆.","sol":"This is an addition reaction: one hydrogen atom adds to each of the two carbon atoms of the double bond, so the carbon–carbon double bond becomes a single bond and the molecule becomes saturated. The conditions are a catalyst, nickel, at a temperature of about 150 °C. The product is ethane: C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆, in which each carbon atom is bonded to three hydrogen atoms and to the other carbon atom, giving every carbon atom four bonds.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about which bond in the ethene molecule the hydrogen atoms attack, and what that bond becomes afterwards.","Work out where each of the two hydrogen atoms ends up, then recall the catalyst and temperature this addition needs.","Give one sentence on the bond, one on the conditions and the name of the product."],"codes":["4.7.2.1","4.7.2.2"]},{"q":"Describe what is seen, and name the gas produced, when a small piece of sodium is added to ethanol, and name the product formed when ethanol reacts with an oxidising agent.","a":"Effervescence — bubbles of gas are given off; the sodium gradually gets smaller and disappears; the gas is hydrogen; the oxidising agent oxidises ethanol to ethanoic acid.","sol":"Sodium reacts with ethanol. Bubbles of gas stream from the surface of the metal, so the mixture effervesces (fizzes), and the piece of sodium gradually becomes smaller and finally disappears as it is used up. The gas given off is hydrogen. The solution left behind contains sodium ethoxide, although you are not expected to write an equation for this reaction, and the first four alcohols all behave in the same way. An alcohol is also oxidised by an oxidising agent to the carboxylic acid with the same number of carbon atoms, so ethanol is oxidised to ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH; no equation is expected for this reaction either.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what you would see happening at the surface of the metal, and what happens to the piece of metal itself.","Recall that this alcohol reacts with sodium in a similar way to water and think about which gas that reaction gives off, then decide which family of organic compounds an alcohol is turned into by an oxidising agent.","Give two observations and the name of one gas, then work the last mark out from the step that an oxidising agent oxidises an alcohol to the carboxylic acid with the same number of carbon atoms — ethanol has two carbon atoms."],"codes":["4.7.2.3"]},{"q":"Describe what happens when ethanoic acid is added to sodium carbonate, and name the products.","a":"The mixture fizzes and the solid dissolves; carbon dioxide is given off; the other products are sodium ethanoate (a salt) and water.","sol":"Carboxylic acids react with carbonates in the same way as other acids. Bubbles of gas are given off, so the mixture effervesces, and the solid carbonate dissolves. The gas is carbon dioxide, which can be shown by bubbling it through limewater, which turns milky. The other products are a salt, sodium ethanoate, and water. The equation is 2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂, although you are not expected to write balanced equations for the reactions of carboxylic acids.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what any acid does when it is added to a carbonate.","Recall the three products of an acid plus a carbonate, and decide which of them is the gas that causes the fizzing.","Give one observation and then name the products."],"codes":["4.7.2.4"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Explain how ethanediol, HOCH₂CH₂OH, and hexanedioic acid, HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH, react together to produce a polyester.","a":"Each monomer has two functional groups — an –OH at each end of ethanediol and a –COOH at each end of hexanedioic acid — so an –OH reacts with a –COOH to form an ester link; because a group is still free at the other end of each molecule, the two monomers join alternately into a long chain, and one molecule of water is lost every time a link is formed.","sol":"Condensation polymerisation needs monomers that each carry two functional groups. Ethanediol has an –OH group at each end of its molecule and hexanedioic acid has a –COOH group at each end of its molecule. An –OH group of one monomer reacts with a –COOH group of the other to form an ester link, which joins the two molecules together. Because each molecule still has a free group at its other end, the two kinds of monomer go on joining alternately to build a very long chain, a polyester. Every time a link is formed a small molecule of water is released, which is why the reaction is called a condensation: n HOCH₂CH₂OH + n HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH → [–OCH₂CH₂OOC(CH₂)₄CO–]ₙ + 2n H₂O.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about how many functional groups each of these two monomers carries, and where on the molecule they are.","Decide which group on one monomer reacts with which group on the other, and what kind of link that makes between them.","Write one sentence about the functional groups, one about the link and how the chain builds up, and one about what leaves each time a link forms."],"codes":["4.7.3.2"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Explain why ethanoic acid is described as a weak acid.","a":"In aqueous solution only a small proportion of its molecules ionise, so the concentration of H⁺ ions is low, and its pH is higher than that of a strong acid of the same concentration.","sol":"When ethanoic acid dissolves in water it ionises only partially: only a small proportion of the molecules split up to release H⁺ ions, and most stay as whole molecules. Because of this the concentration of H⁺ ions in the solution is low compared with a strong acid of the same concentration, in which the molecules ionise completely. A lower concentration of H⁺ ions means a higher pH, so a solution of ethanoic acid has a higher pH than a solution of a strong acid of the same concentration. This is why all the carboxylic acids are weak acids.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what happens to the molecules of this acid when it dissolves in water, and how completely it happens.","Link how many molecules break up to the concentration of the ion that makes a solution acidic.","Build the answer in three linked steps, from what happens to the molecules in water, through the concentration of the ion that makes a solution acidic, to the pH — all compared with a strong acid of the same concentration."],"codes":["4.7.2.4"]},{"q":"A student burns butane, C₄H₁₀, completely in air. Write the balanced symbol equation for this reaction, using whole-number coefficients.","a":"2C₄H₁₀ + 13O₂ → 8CO₂ + 10H₂O","sol":"Balancing carbon needs 4CO₂ per C₄H₁₀, and balancing hydrogen needs 5H₂O per C₄H₁₀, giving C₄H₁₀ + 6.5O₂ → 4CO₂ + 5H₂O. Because 6.5 is not a whole number, every coefficient is doubled: 2C₄H₁₀ + 13O₂ → 8CO₂ + 10H₂O. Checking the doubled equation: 8 C, 20 H and 26 O atoms on each side.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what to do if balancing the oxygen atoms for one molecule of the hydrocarbon gives a fractional coefficient.","Balance carbon and hydrogen first, then work out the oxygen needed, doubling every coefficient if that number is not a whole number.","Your first line is the carbon balance: the 4 carbon atoms in C₄H₁₀ need 4CO₂; carry on to hydrogen and then to oxygen from there."],"codes":["4.7.1.3"]},{"q":"Ethanol can be manufactured by reacting ethene with steam, or by fermentation. Describe the reaction of ethene with steam, giving the conditions used, and compare this route with fermentation.","a":"Steam adds across the C=C double bond of ethene to give ethanol, C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH, using a phosphoric acid catalyst at about 300 °C and a high pressure. Fermentation instead uses a sugar solution with yeast at about 25–35 °C in the absence of air. Both produce ethanol, but the ethene route runs continuously and gives ethanol as the only product from a finite crude-oil feedstock, while fermentation is a batch process giving a dilute aqueous solution of ethanol from a renewable plant crop.","sol":"Reacting ethene with steam is an addition reaction: the water molecule adds across the carbon–carbon double bond, which becomes a single bond, and the product is ethanol, C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH. The conditions are a catalyst of phosphoric acid, a high temperature of about 300 °C and a high pressure. In fermentation, a sugar solution is mixed with yeast, whose enzymes convert the sugar into ethanol and carbon dioxide; the conditions are a warm temperature of about 25–35 °C and the absence of air. Comparing the two: both give ethanol, but the ethene route runs continuously and quickly and ethanol is its only product, whereas fermentation is slow, is carried out in batches and produces only a dilute aqueous solution of ethanol, which must then be concentrated by fractional distillation. The ethene comes from cracking fractions of crude oil, which is finite, while the sugar for fermentation comes from a plant crop, which is renewable.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Think about which bond in ethene the water molecule adds across, and what that bond becomes.","Recall the catalyst, temperature and pressure for the ethene route, then the three conditions fermentation needs.","Give the reaction and its conditions first, then set at least one clear difference between the two routes side by side."],"codes":["4.7.2.2","4.7.2.3"]},{"q":"A molecule of poly(ethene) has a relative molecular mass of 42 000. The repeating unit of poly(ethene) is –CH₂–CH₂–. Calculate the number of ethene monomer units in this molecule. Relative atomic masses: H = 1, C = 12.","a":"1500 monomer units","sol":"The repeating unit of an addition polymer contains the same atoms as the monomer, so –CH₂–CH₂– has the same atoms as one molecule of ethene, C₂H₄. Its relative formula mass is (2 × 12) + (4 × 1) = 24 + 4 = 28. Number of monomer units = relative molecular mass of the polymer ÷ relative formula mass of the repeating unit = 42 000 ÷ 28 = 1500 monomer units.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Think about how the atoms in the repeating unit of an addition polymer compare with the atoms in the monomer it was made from.","Find the relative formula mass of one repeating unit first, then decide which mass to divide by which.","The relative formula mass of the repeating unit is (2 × 12) + (4 × 1) = 28, so your first line of working is: number of units = 42 000 ÷ 28."],"codes":["4.7.3.1"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Compare addition polymerisation, as in the formation of poly(ethene) from ethene, with the condensation polymerisation of the amino acid glycine, H₂NCH₂COOH.","a":"In addition polymerisation many ethene monomers join because the C=C double bond opens; no other product is formed, so the repeating unit –CH₂–CH₂– has the same atoms as the monomer. Glycine has two different functional groups, –NH₂ and –COOH; the –COOH of one molecule reacts with the –NH₂ of the next, and a molecule of water is lost at every link, so the repeating unit –HNCH₂CO– has fewer atoms than the monomer. Both reactions join many small monomer molecules end to end into one very large polymer molecule.","sol":"Addition polymerisation: ethene monomers each contain a C=C double bond, which opens to leave a spare bond on each carbon atom, and these bonds join the monomers end to end, n CH₂=CH₂ → –(CH₂–CH₂)ₙ–. No other molecule is produced, so the repeating unit contains exactly the same atoms as the monomer. Condensation polymerisation: glycine carries two different functional groups, an amino group –NH₂ at one end and a carboxyl group –COOH at the other. The –COOH group of one molecule reacts with the –NH₂ group of the next to join them, and each time a link is formed a small molecule of water is lost, n H₂NCH₂COOH → [–HNCH₂CO–]ₙ + n H₂O, so the repeating unit contains fewer atoms than the monomer. That equation is written in the long-chain form, which leaves the –H and the –OH on the two ends of the chain out; counting the links, n amino acids make only (n − 1) links and release (n − 1) water molecules — six glycine molecules release five — as the full form n H₂NCH₂COOH → H[–HNCH₂CO–]ₙOH + (n − 1) H₂O shows. What the two have in common is that many small monomer molecules join end to end to make one very large polymer molecule; where they differ is in the functional groups involved (one C=C, or two different groups), and in whether a small molecule is lost. Combining different amino acids in the same chain gives a protein rather than a polypeptide of one repeating unit.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Think about the functional group each monomer brings to the reaction: one double bond, or two groups at opposite ends of the molecule.","For each polymerisation, work out what happens to those groups, whether anything else is produced, and how the repeating unit compares with the monomer.","Take each polymerisation in turn with its monomer, its link and its repeating unit, then finish with what the two processes have in common."],"codes":["4.7.3.1","4.7.3.3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Give the general formula of the alkenes.","a":"CₙH₂ₙ"},{"q":"Name the functional group in a carboxylic acid.","a":"–COOH"},{"q":"(Higher tier) Name the small molecule lost each time a link is formed in condensation polymerisation.","a":"Water."},{"q":"Name the monomers from which proteins are made.","a":"Amino acids."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Hydrocarbon","back":"A molecule made of hydrogen and carbon atoms only.","code":"4.7.1.1","open":false},{"front":"General formula of the alkanes","back":"CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ — n is the number of carbon atoms. The first four are methane (CH₄), ethane (C₂H₆), propane (C₃H₈) and butane (C₄H₁₀).","code":"4.7.1.1","open":false},{"front":"Fractional distillation","back":"Separates crude oil into fractions by evaporation and condensation: vapour rises up a column that cools with height, and each fraction condenses where the temperature matches its boiling point.","code":"4.7.1.2","open":false},{"front":"Trend with increasing chain length","back":"Boiling point increases, viscosity increases, flammability decreases.","code":"4.7.1.3","open":false},{"front":"Cracking","back":"Breaking long-chain hydrocarbons into smaller, more useful molecules, by catalytic cracking (hot catalyst) or steam cracking (steam, very high temperature). The products are alkanes and alkenes.","code":"4.7.1.4","open":false},{"front":"Bromine water test","back":"Shake the sample with orange/brown bromine water: an alkene turns it colourless; an alkane leaves it orange/brown.","code":"4.7.1.4","open":false},{"front":"Alkenes","back":"Hydrocarbons with a C=C double bond; general formula CₙH₂ₙ; unsaturated (two fewer hydrogen atoms than the alkane of the same chain length). First four: ethene C₂H₄, propene C₃H₆, butene C₄H₈, pentene C₅H₁₀.","code":"4.7.2.1","open":false},{"front":"Addition reactions of alkenes and their conditions","back":"Hydrogen (nickel catalyst, about 150 °C) → alkane; steam (phosphoric acid catalyst, about 300 °C, high pressure) → alcohol; halogens at room temperature → a dihalogeno compound. Each adds across the C=C, which becomes single.","code":"4.7.2.2","open":false},{"front":"Alcohols","back":"Functional group –OH; methanol, ethanol, propanol, butanol. With sodium → hydrogen; burn in air → carbon dioxide + water; dissolve to give neutral solutions; oxidised to carboxylic acids. Used as fuels and solvents; ethanol in alcoholic drinks.","code":"4.7.2.3","open":false},{"front":"Fermentation of sugar solution","back":"Sugar solution plus yeast, at about 25–35 °C, in the absence of air, gives an aqueous solution of ethanol.","code":"4.7.2.3","open":false},{"front":"Carboxylic acids","back":"Functional group –COOH; first four methanoic, ethanoic, propanoic, butanoic. With carbonates → salt + water + carbon dioxide; with alcohols (acid catalyst) → esters, such as ethyl ethanoate. (HT only) Weak acids: only partly ionised in water.","code":"4.7.2.4","open":false},{"front":"Addition polymerisation","back":"Many alkene monomers join as the C=C opens: n CH₂=CH₂ → –(CH₂–CH₂)ₙ–. No other molecule is formed, so the repeating unit has the same atoms as the monomer.","code":"4.7.3.1","open":false},{"front":"Condensation polymerisation (HT only)","back":"Monomers with two functional groups join and lose a small molecule, usually water. Ethanediol (two –OH) with hexanedioic acid (two –COOH) gives a polyester plus water.","code":"4.7.3.2","open":false},{"front":"Amino acids (HT only)","back":"One molecule carries two different groups, –NH₂ and –COOH; glycine is H₂NCH₂COOH. They polymerise by condensation to polypeptides, losing water at every link; different amino acids in one chain give proteins.","code":"4.7.3.3","open":false},{"front":"DNA and other naturally occurring polymers","back":"DNA: two polymer chains in a double helix, made from four different monomers called nucleotides; it encodes genetic instructions. Proteins are made from amino acids; starch and cellulose from glucose.","code":"4.7.3.4","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Key equations, general formulae and structures for Organic chemistry","items":["General formulae: alkanes CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ (saturated, single C–C bonds only); alkenes CₙH₂ₙ (unsaturated, one C=C double bond) (chemistry only). Substitute n to predict a formula, or test a given formula against the pattern to name the family.","Complete combustion of a hydrocarbon: hydrocarbon + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water. Worked examples: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O; C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O; 2C₄H₁₀ + 13O₂ → 8CO₂ + 10H₂O. Balance carbon, then hydrogen, then oxygen, and double every coefficient if the oxygen figure comes out as a fraction.","Cracking: balance by conserving atoms, for example C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄ and C₁₂H₂₆ → C₆H₁₄ + C₆H₁₂. Check that the carbon atoms and the hydrogen atoms each total the same on both sides.","(chemistry only) Combustion of an alkene: C₂H₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 2H₂O for complete combustion; in air alkenes tend to burn with smoky flames because combustion is incomplete.","(chemistry only) Addition of hydrogen (nickel catalyst, about 150 °C): C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆. The double bond becomes a single bond and the product is the alkane.","(chemistry only) Addition of water as steam (phosphoric acid catalyst, about 300 °C, high pressure): C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH. The product is an alcohol.","(chemistry only) Addition of the halogens at room temperature: C₂H₄ + Cl₂ → C₂H₄Cl₂; C₂H₄ + Br₂ → C₂H₄Br₂; C₂H₄ + I₂ → C₂H₄I₂; and with propene, C₃H₆ + Br₂ → C₃H₆Br₂. In every displayed structure of the product, each of the two carbon atoms that shared the double bond gains one halogen atom, and every carbon atom ends up with four bonds.","(chemistry only) Complete combustion of an alcohol — the only alcohol reaction you are asked to write an equation for: C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O; 2CH₃OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 4H₂O. Count the oxygen atom inside the alcohol molecule when balancing.","(chemistry only) Addition polymerisation: n CH₂=CH₂ → –(CH₂–CH₂)ₙ– for poly(ethene), and n CH₂=CHCH₃ → –(CH₂–CH(CH₃))ₙ– for poly(propene). Draw a bond out of each end of the repeating unit, brackets round it and n outside.","(chemistry only) Number of monomer units in an addition polymer = relative molecular mass of the polymer ÷ relative formula mass of the repeating unit; the repeating unit has the same atoms as the monomer, so for poly(ethene) that relative formula mass is (2 × 12) + (4 × 1) = 28.","(chemistry only) (HT only) Condensation polymerisation of a polyester: n HOCH₂CH₂OH + n HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH → [–OCH₂CH₂OOC(CH₂)₄CO–]ₙ + 2n H₂O — one water molecule for each ester link formed. The equation is written in the long-chain form, which leaves the groups on the two ends of the chain out; counting the links, the 2n monomer molecules in one chain make (2n − 1) links and release (2n − 1) water molecules.","(chemistry only) (HT only) Condensation polymerisation of an amino acid: n H₂NCH₂COOH → [–HNCH₂CO–]ₙ + n H₂O — the long-chain form, which writes the polymer as n repeating units and leaves the –H and the –OH on the two ends of the chain out. A chain of n amino acids has only (n − 1) links and so releases (n − 1) water molecules: a chain built from six glycine molecules has five links and releases five water molecules, and with the end groups written in the equation is n H₂NCH₂COOH → H[–HNCH₂CO–]ₙOH + (n − 1) H₂O. Both forms balance.","(chemistry only) Reactions you should describe but are not asked to write equations for: alcohol + sodium → hydrogen; alcohol + oxidising agent → carboxylic acid (ethanol → ethanoic acid); carboxylic acid + carbonate → salt + water + carbon dioxide (for example 2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂); carboxylic acid + alcohol, with an acid catalyst, → an ester (ethanoic acid + ethanol → ethyl ethanoate, CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH → CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O); fermentation of glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂.","What is supplied and what must be recalled: none of these equations is printed on the paper — all of them must be written from the formulae given in the question. The only insert supplied with both Chemistry papers is the periodic table (Appendix A), which gives the relative atomic masses used in any relative formula mass calculation."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: how cracking is carried out and why it matters for modern life","items":["Question (6 marks): cracking is used to convert large hydrocarbon molecules from crude oil into smaller, more useful molecules. Explain how cracking is carried out and why it is important for modern life.","Level 3 (5–6 marks) model answer: long-chain hydrocarbon fractions from crude oil are in low demand as fuels, while there is high demand for fuels made of small molecules, so cracking is used to break the large, less useful molecules into smaller ones. In catalytic cracking, the vaporised hydrocarbon is passed over a hot powdered catalyst at a moderate temperature; in steam cracking, the vaporised hydrocarbon is mixed with steam and heated to a very high temperature. Both methods break the long chains apart, producing a mixture of smaller molecules that includes alkanes, which can be used directly as fuels, and alkenes, which are shown to be present because they turn orange/brown bromine water colourless, unlike alkanes. For example, C₁₀H₂₂ can be cracked to give C₈H₁₈ and C₂H₄, a balanced equation in which the total number of carbon and hydrogen atoms is conserved. Cracking therefore matters for modern life in two ways: it increases the supply of small hydrocarbon molecules needed as fuels, and it supplies alkenes as the starting materials for making polymers such as poly(ethene) and many other chemicals that everyday products depend on.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): the answer describes cracking as breaking large hydrocarbons into smaller ones and names one method or one use (as a fuel, or for making polymers), or gives an example equation, but does not link the conditions of both methods, the bromine water test and both uses into one coherent explanation.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): the answer gives an isolated correct fact, such as that cracking makes smaller molecules or that alkenes turn bromine water colourless, without describing a method or explaining why cracking is needed.","What a bottom-level answer misses: it does not name or describe the conditions for catalytic cracking or steam cracking, does not explain the mismatch between the supply of large molecules and the demand for small ones, and does not connect the alkenes produced to their use in making polymers."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: the two ways of manufacturing ethanol (chemistry only)","items":["Question (6 marks) (chemistry only): a company can manufacture ethanol either by reacting ethene with steam or by fermenting a sugar solution. Evaluate these two methods of manufacturing ethanol.","Level 3 (5–6 marks) model answer: in the first method, ethene obtained by cracking a fraction of crude oil is reacted with steam, and the water adds across the carbon–carbon double bond, which becomes a single bond: C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH. The conditions are a phosphoric acid catalyst, a high temperature of about 300 °C and a high pressure. Every atom of the ethene and the steam ends up in the ethanol, so no other product is formed, and the process can be run continuously and quickly, with no need to concentrate a dilute product afterwards. Against this, the ethene comes from crude oil, which is a finite resource, and maintaining a high temperature and high pressure is expensive. In the second method, a sugar solution obtained from a plant crop is mixed with yeast, whose enzymes convert the sugar into ethanol and carbon dioxide, at a warm temperature of about 25–35 °C and in the absence of air. This uses mild conditions, little energy and a renewable raw material, but it is slow, it is carried out in batches rather than continuously, and it produces only a dilute aqueous solution of ethanol, which then has to be concentrated by fractional distillation. On balance, fermentation is the better choice where a plant crop is available, energy is expensive and a renewable source matters, while the ethene route is better where large amounts of pure ethanol are needed quickly and a supply of ethene from crude oil is available.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): the answer describes both methods with some correct conditions — for example steam and a catalyst for one, yeast and a warm temperature for the other — and gives at least one advantage or disadvantage, but the chemistry is thin (no addition across the double bond, or no equation), the points are listed rather than weighed against each other, or no judgement is reached.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): isolated correct statements such as that ethene and steam make ethanol, or that yeast is used in fermentation, with no conditions, no comparison of the two methods and no judgement.","What a bottom-level answer misses: it does not give the conditions for either method (catalyst, about 300 °C and high pressure; yeast, about 25–35 °C and no air), it does not say that the water adds across the C=C double bond, it does not contrast a continuous process giving pure ethanol from a finite feedstock with a batch process giving aqueous ethanol from a renewable one, and it reaches no justified judgement, which is what evaluate demands."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-18T22:20:28Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"The three faults from the first pass are properly fixed and nothing new is broken: every symbol equation in the kit balances, including both condensation forms checked symbolically for general n (n H₂NCH₂COOH → H[–HNCH₂CO–]ₙOH + (n − 1) H₂O and the long-chain n H₂O form, and the implied polyester (2n − 1) form), so this is publishable as it stands, with the polyester solution the only passage still worth a one-clause edit."}}},"4.8":{"id":"AQA-8462","topic":"4.8","family":"science","lesson":{"why":"Chemical analysis is the room where chemistry becomes detective work, and in GCSE Chemistry it is far bigger than it is in Combined Science. You need to judge purity from melting point and boiling point data, recognise a formulation, run paper chromatography and calculate Rf values in Required practical 6, and recall all four gas tests exactly. On top of that sits section 4.8.3, marked (chemistry only): flame tests for five metal ions, the sodium hydroxide precipitate tests with the balanced and ionic equations behind them, the tests for carbonate, halide and sulfate ions, Required practical 7 on unknown single ionic compounds, and the instrumental methods — above all flame emission spectroscopy — that have replaced many of these tests in real laboratories. Expect short recall questions on every test, calculation questions on Rf values to an appropriate number of significant figures, equation questions on the hydroxide precipitates, and extended questions asking you to identify an unknown compound from a set of results or to interpret a line spectrum against a reference set.","idea":[{"code":"4.8.1.1","h":"Pure substances","t":"In chemistry, a pure substance is a single element or compound, not mixed with any other substance — this is different from the everyday use of 'pure', which can just mean nothing has been added (eg pure milk). A pure element or compound melts and boils at one exact, sharp temperature; a mixture melts and boils over a range of temperatures. Exam questions give melting point or boiling point data and ask you to decide, and explain, whether a sample is pure or a mixture — always compare the sharpness or range of the data, not just whether the numbers 'look close' to a textbook value."},{"code":"4.8.1.2","h":"Formulations","t":"A formulation is a mixture that has been designed as a useful product, made by mixing components in carefully measured quantities so the product has the properties it needs — each ingredient does a specific job. Formulation types named in the specification are fuels, cleaning agents, paints, medicines, alloys, fertilisers and foods. In the exam you will be given information about a mixture's composition and asked to identify whether it is a formulation and to justify this from the data; you do not need to know the names of components in proprietary products, only that each named ingredient serves a stated purpose."},{"code":"4.8.1.3","h":"Chromatography and Rf values","t":"Chromatography separates mixtures because it has a stationary phase (the paper) and a mobile phase (the solvent); separation depends on how each substance distributes itself between the two phases, so substances that are more soluble in the solvent and less strongly attracted to the paper travel further and the mixture separates into spots. A pure compound gives a single spot in all solvents, whereas a mixture may separate into different spots depending on the solvent. Compare the distance moved by a compound (centre of spot from the origin) with the distance moved by the solvent to get its Rf value: Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent. Rf has no unit and always lies between 0 and 1, and you must give it to an appropriate number of significant figures. Matching an unknown's Rf against known values measured in the same solvent helps identify it."},{"code":"4.8.1.3 (Required practical 6)","h":"Required practical 6: paper chromatography and Rf values","t":"Required practical 6 asks you to investigate how paper chromatography can be used to separate and tell the difference between coloured substances, and to calculate Rf values. The details that earn marks are the ones students most often lose: the origin line is drawn in pencil because ink would dissolve and travel with the solvent, the solvent level in the tank must start below the origin line so the spots are not washed off into the solvent, the tank is covered so the solvent does not evaporate, and the solvent front is marked in pencil the moment the paper is taken out. You then measure from the origin to the solvent front and from the origin to the centre of each spot, and divide. The skills the specification lists for this activity are AT 1 and AT 4, with WS 2.4 and WS 2.6."},{"code":"4.8.2.1","h":"Test for hydrogen","t":"Collect the gas in a test tube and hold a lit (burning) splint at the open end of the tube. If hydrogen is present it burns rapidly, giving a squeaky pop sound as it reacts with oxygen in the air. Exam questions often describe an observation (eg 'a pop was heard') and ask you to name the gas, or ask you to describe the test and expected result for a given gas — always give both the method (a lit splint at the open end of the tube) and the result (the pop) for full marks."},{"code":"4.8.2.2","h":"Test for oxygen","t":"Collect the gas in a test tube and insert a glowing (not flaming) wooden splint into the tube. If oxygen is present, the splint relights. Do not confuse this with the hydrogen test: oxygen relights a glowing splint, hydrogen makes a lit splint pop — examiners often swap these round in questions to check you keep the method and the gas the right way round. The same test is used at the positive electrode in electrolysis, but only when the gas there is oxygen: an aqueous solution gives oxygen at the positive electrode unless the solution contains halide ions, in which case the halogen is produced instead — chlorine from a chloride solution, which bleaches damp litmus paper and does not relight a glowing splint. Name the gas from the test that works: a relighting glowing splint identifies oxygen and bleached damp litmus identifies chlorine, whichever electrode the gas came from."},{"code":"4.8.2.3","h":"Test for carbon dioxide","t":"Shake the gas with, or bubble it through, an aqueous solution of calcium hydroxide, known as limewater. If carbon dioxide is present the limewater turns milky (cloudy), because insoluble calcium carbonate forms: CO₂ + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O. Calcium hydroxide is only slightly soluble in water, so limewater is a dilute solution of the very compound that appears as a white precipitate when sodium hydroxide solution is added to a solution containing calcium ions: dissolved it is limewater, and once there is more of it than the water can hold it is the white solid. This test is the second half of the test for carbonates, and it appears in questions on combustion, respiration and thermal decomposition, where you may be asked to describe how you would confirm that a gas produced is carbon dioxide, or to explain a given observation."},{"code":"4.8.2.4","h":"Test for chlorine","t":"Hold a piece of damp (moist) litmus paper in the gas. If chlorine is present the litmus paper is bleached and turns white, because chlorine has a bleaching action rather than simply turning the paper red or blue as an acid or an alkali would. The paper must be damp, because the chlorine has to dissolve in the water on the paper before it can act. This is different from the acid–base litmus behaviour you have met before, so exam questions often test whether you remember that chlorine bleaches litmus completely rather than colouring it."},{"code":"4.8.3.1","h":"Flame tests (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Flame tests identify some metal ions (cations). Clean a nichrome or platinum wire loop by dipping it in dilute hydrochloric acid and holding it in a roaring Bunsen flame until no colour shows, dip it in the solid or solution and hold it in the edge of a blue flame. Learn the five colours exactly as the specification gives them: lithium compounds crimson, sodium compounds yellow, potassium compounds lilac, calcium compounds orange-red and copper compounds green. The weakness of the method is that if a sample contains a mixture of ions some flame colours can be masked — the strong yellow of sodium in particular hides the others. Flame colours of other metal ions are not required knowledge, and you must be able to identify species from the results of the tests in 4.8.3.1 to 4.8.3.5."},{"code":"4.8.3.2","h":"Metal hydroxides (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Sodium hydroxide solution identifies some metal ions in solution because the metal hydroxides are insoluble and appear as precipitates. Aluminium, calcium and magnesium ions all give white precipitates, so a white precipitate alone does not identify the ion: add excess sodium hydroxide solution, and only the aluminium hydroxide precipitate dissolves again. The white calcium hydroxide precipitate is the same compound as limewater: Ca(OH)₂ is only slightly soluble, so a dilute solution of it is limewater and anything beyond that solubility shows as the white precipitate. Copper(II) gives a blue precipitate, iron(II) a green precipitate and iron(III) a brown precipitate. You must be able to write balanced equations for the reactions that produce the insoluble hydroxides, for example CuSO₄ + 2NaOH → Cu(OH)₂ + Na₂SO₄ and FeCl₃ + 3NaOH → Fe(OH)₃ + 3NaCl. The same reactions written as ionic equations, which the specification's 'Write' command word lists as Higher tier, are Cu²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Cu(OH)₂(s) and Fe³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₃(s): the metal ion takes one hydroxide ion for each unit of its positive charge, so the two sides balance for both atoms and charge. You are not expected to write equations for the production of sodium aluminate."},{"code":"4.8.3.3","h":"Carbonates (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Carbonates react with dilute acids to form carbon dioxide gas, so the test for a carbonate is done in two stages: add dilute acid to the sample and look for effervescence (fizzing), then pass the gas given off through limewater, which turns milky if the gas is carbon dioxide. Both stages are needed, because fizzing on its own does not prove which gas has been released. An example equation is Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂, and the ionic equation for the gas-producing step, a Higher tier way of writing it, is CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂."},{"code":"4.8.3.4","h":"Halides (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Halide ions in solution produce precipitates with silver nitrate solution in the presence of dilute nitric acid. Silver chloride is white, silver bromide is cream and silver iodide is yellow — the colours deepen as you go down the group, which is a useful way to remember them. The dilute nitric acid is added first to remove carbonate ions, which would otherwise give a white precipitate of silver carbonate and a false positive; nitric acid is used rather than hydrochloric acid because hydrochloric acid would add chloride ions of its own. The ionic equation for the precipitation is Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s) (Higher tier), and the full equation for a sodium chloride solution is NaCl + AgNO₃ → AgCl + NaNO₃."},{"code":"4.8.3.5","h":"Sulfates (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Sulfate ions in solution produce a white precipitate with barium chloride solution in the presence of dilute hydrochloric acid. The acid is added first, for the same reason as in the halide test: it removes carbonate ions that would otherwise give a white precipitate of barium carbonate. Hydrochloric acid is chosen here because it does not add sulfate ions; sulfuric acid obviously could not be used. The precipitate is barium sulfate, and the ionic equation for the reaction is Ba²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → BaSO₄(s) (Higher tier), or in full Na₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄ + 2NaCl. Note that both this test and the halide test give a white precipitate, so the reagent you used is part of your answer, not just the colour you saw."},{"code":"4.8.3.5 (Required practical 7)","h":"Required practical 7: chemical tests to identify the ions in unknown single ionic compounds (chemistry only)","t":"Required practical 7, which is GCSE Chemistry only, is the use of chemical tests to identify the ions in unknown single ionic compounds, covering every ion from Flame tests through to Sulfates. The working order matters: identify the cation first with a flame test and with sodium hydroxide solution, then take fresh samples of the solution for each anion test — dilute acid for a carbonate, dilute nitric acid with silver nitrate for a halide, dilute hydrochloric acid with barium chloride for a sulfate. Always use a fresh sample for each test, because a reagent added earlier can mask or ruin the next result. The skills the specification lists against this activity in section 8.2.7 are AT 2 and AT 8, with WS 2.4 and WS 2.6."},{"code":"4.8.3.6","h":"Instrumental methods (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Elements and compounds can also be detected and identified using instrumental methods — machines rather than test tubes. The three advantages the specification wants are that instrumental methods are accurate, sensitive and rapid: accurate because the result does not depend on someone judging a colour by eye, sensitive because they work on very small amounts of a sample, and rapid because many samples can be analysed quickly. This is why forensic scientists and drug control scientists rely on them. You should be able to state advantages of instrumental methods compared with the chemical tests in this specification, and the comparison is always against those tests — masked flame colours and two different white precipitates are exactly the weaknesses instruments remove."},{"code":"4.8.3.7","h":"Flame emission spectroscopy (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Flame emission spectroscopy is an example of an instrumental method used to analyse metal ions in solutions. The sample is put into a flame and the light given out is passed through a spectroscope. The output is a line spectrum, which can be analysed to identify the metal ions in the solution and to measure their concentrations — the positions of the lines identify the ions and the intensity of the lines gives their concentration. It succeeds where a flame test fails, because in a mixture the separate sets of lines are still visible even though one flame colour would mask another. In the exam you will be given an instrumental result as a chart or a table together with a reference set in the same form, and asked to interpret it: compare each line in the sample with the reference wavelengths one at a time."}],"examples":[{"title":"Deciding whether a sample is pure from melting point data","setup":"A solid organic compound is heated gently in a melting point tube. It starts to melt at 118 °C and has completely melted by 127 °C. A data book gives the melting point of the pure compound as 121 °C. State and explain whether this sample is pure.","steps":["A pure element or compound melts sharply at one specific temperature, not across a spread of temperatures.","This sample melts across a range: 127 °C − 118 °C, a spread of 9 °C.","Because it melts over a range instead of sharply at the data book value, the sample is impure — it is a mixture, not a pure compound."],"cues":["What melting behaviour would you expect from a genuinely pure compound?","How wide is the melting range of this sample?","Is the sample pure or impure, and what is your reason?"]},{"title":"Calculating an Rf value from a chromatogram","setup":"On a dried chromatogram, the centre of a blue dye spot has travelled 3.6 cm up the paper from the pencil line, and the solvent front has travelled 4.8 cm up the paper from the same pencil line. Calculate the Rf value of the blue dye.","steps":["Recall the equation from the specification: Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent.","Substituting the two measurements gives Rf = 3.6 ÷ 4.8.","Rf = 0.75, which has no unit and lies between 0 and 1 as every Rf value must."],"cues":["Which equation links the two measurements you have been given?","Which measurement is the divisor if the answer is to come out below 1?","What is the value of that division, and what check can you make on it?"]},{"title":"Recognising a formulation","setup":"A hand cream is made from water, an oil, a preservative, a fragrance and a thickener, each added in a carefully measured amount. Explain whether the hand cream is a formulation.","steps":["Each ingredient has a distinct purpose: the oil moisturises, the preservative stops microbial growth, the fragrance adds scent, the thickener sets the texture.","The components are combined in carefully measured quantities so that the product has the properties it needs.","Conclusion: this is a formulation — a mixture designed and measured to give a product its required properties."],"cues":["Does every named ingredient do a job of its own, and what is each job?","Why does it matter that the amounts are measured rather than approximate?","Is the hand cream a formulation, and what definition are you applying?"]},{"title":"Identifying an unknown ionic compound from its test results","setup":"A white solid X dissolves in water to give a colourless solution. A flame test on X gives a crimson flame. To a fresh sample of the solution, dilute nitric acid is added and then silver nitrate solution, and a cream precipitate forms. Identify X and give its formula.","steps":["A crimson flame test colour shows that the cation in X is the lithium ion, Li⁺.","A cream precipitate with silver nitrate in dilute nitric acid shows that the anion is the bromide ion, Br⁻, since silver chloride would be white and silver iodide yellow.","One Li⁺ balances one Br⁻, so X is lithium bromide, LiBr."],"cues":["Which metal ion does a crimson flame colour point to?","Which halide ion gives a cream precipitate rather than a white or a yellow one?","How many of each ion are needed for the compound to be neutral overall?"]},{"title":"Writing the equations for a hydroxide precipitate","setup":"Copper(II) sulfate solution, CuSO₄(aq), is added to sodium hydroxide solution, NaOH(aq), and a blue precipitate forms. Write the balanced symbol equation for this reaction, and then the ionic equation for the formation of the precipitate.","steps":["The blue precipitate is copper(II) hydroxide, Cu(OH)₂, and the other product is sodium sulfate, Na₂SO₄.","Balancing gives CuSO₄(aq) + 2NaOH(aq) → Cu(OH)₂(s) + Na₂SO₄(aq), with 1 Cu, 1 S, 6 O, 2 Na and 2 H on each side.","Sodium ions and sulfate ions appear unchanged on both sides, so they are spectator ions and are cancelled.","The ionic equation is Cu²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Cu(OH)₂(s); the charge is (+2) + 2(−1) = 0 on the left and 0 on the right, so charge balances as well as atoms."],"cues":["What are the names and formulae of the two products?","How many hydroxide ions does each copper(II) ion need, and does every element then balance?","Which ions are unchanged on both sides of the full equation?","What is left once those ions are cancelled, and does the charge balance?"]},{"title":"Interpreting a flame emission spectrum","setup":"A solution of metal ions is analysed by flame emission spectroscopy. Its line spectrum shows lines at 589 nm and 766 nm. A reference set recorded on the same instrument gives sodium 589 nm, potassium 766 nm, lithium 671 nm and calcium 423 nm. State which metal ions the solution contains, and explain how the result shows this.","steps":["Compare each line in the sample spectrum with the reference wavelengths one at a time.","The line at 589 nm matches the sodium reference line, so sodium ions are present.","The line at 766 nm matches the potassium reference line, so potassium ions are present.","No other reference wavelength appears in the sample, so lithium and calcium ions are absent; the solution contains sodium ions and potassium ions only, and a flame test could not have shown this because the yellow sodium colour would have masked the lilac of potassium."],"cues":["How should the sample spectrum and the reference set be used together?","Which reference line does the first of the sample's two lines match?","Which reference line does the second of the sample's lines match?","Which reference ions are absent, and what advantage does this method have over a flame test?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Would a pure compound produce one spot or several spots on a chromatogram, tested in several different solvents?","a":"One spot, in every solvent."},{"q":"Which type of splint (lit or glowing) is used to test for hydrogen?","a":"A lit (burning) splint — it should produce a pop."},{"q":"What happens to limewater when carbon dioxide is present?","a":"It turns milky (cloudy)."},{"q":"Aluminium, calcium and magnesium ions all give a white precipitate with sodium hydroxide solution. Which one dissolves again in excess sodium hydroxide solution?","a":"Aluminium hydroxide — so a white precipitate that redissolves shows aluminium ions."},{"q":"What colour precipitate does silver nitrate solution, with dilute nitric acid, give with bromide ions?","a":"Cream."},{"q":"What is the output of a flame emission spectroscope, and what two things can it tell you?","a":"A line spectrum; it identifies the metal ions present and measures their concentrations."}]},"room":{"facts":["Chemical analysis is examined on Paper 2, which covers topics 4.6 to 4.10: a written exam of 1 hour 45 minutes, worth 100 marks and 50 % of the GCSE; Paper 2 questions may also draw on fundamental concepts from 4.1 to 4.3.","The entry code is 8462F for Foundation Tier and 8462H for Higher tier; the whole of section 4.8.3 — identification of ions by chemical and spectroscopic means — is marked (chemistry only), so it is examined in GCSE Chemistry but not in GCSE Combined Science: Trilogy.","Questions drawing on the knowledge and understanding gained from the eight required practical activities are worth at least 15 % of the marks for the qualification; this room holds two of them, Required practical 6 (paper chromatography) and Required practical 7 (chemical tests for ions), and practical 7 is GCSE Chemistry only.","Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent, both measured from the origin, the substance to the centre of its spot; Rf has no unit and always lies between 0 and 1.","A pure element or compound melts and boils at one specific, sharp temperature; a mixture melts and boils over a range of temperatures.","A formulation is a mixture designed as a useful product, made by mixing components in carefully measured quantities, each for a particular purpose: fuels, cleaning agents, paints, medicines, alloys, fertilisers and foods.","The four gas tests: hydrogen — a lit splint gives a squeaky pop; oxygen — a glowing splint relights; carbon dioxide — limewater (aqueous calcium hydroxide) turns milky; chlorine — damp litmus paper is bleached white.","Flame test colours: lithium crimson, sodium yellow, potassium lilac, calcium orange-red, copper green. In a mixture some flame colours can be masked.","With sodium hydroxide solution: aluminium, calcium and magnesium ions give white precipitates, and only aluminium hydroxide dissolves in excess; copper(II) gives blue, iron(II) green and iron(III) brown. The white calcium hydroxide precipitate is the same compound as limewater, which is a dilute solution of Ca(OH)₂.","Equations for the insoluble hydroxides: CuSO₄ + 2NaOH → Cu(OH)₂ + Na₂SO₄ and FeCl₃ + 3NaOH → Fe(OH)₃ + 3NaCl; as ionic equations (Higher tier), Cu²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Cu(OH)₂(s) and Fe³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₃(s).","Carbonates: dilute acid gives fizzing and the gas turns limewater milky. Halides: dilute nitric acid then silver nitrate solution — chloride white, bromide cream, iodide yellow. Sulfates: dilute hydrochloric acid then barium chloride solution — a white precipitate.","Instrumental methods are accurate, sensitive and rapid. Flame emission spectroscopy puts the sample into a flame and passes the light given out through a spectroscope; the line spectrum identifies the metal ions and measures their concentrations."],"questions":[{"q":"State what is meant by a pure substance in chemistry.","a":"A single element or compound, not mixed with any other substance.","sol":"In chemistry, a pure substance is a single element or a single compound that is not mixed with any other substance. (This is not the everyday meaning of 'pure', which can mean only that nothing has been added.)","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This is the chemist's meaning of the word, which is not the meaning printed on a carton of milk.","Decide how many different kinds of substance a sample is allowed to contain and still count as pure.","Your answer is one sentence in two parts: what the sample consists of, and what it is not."],"codes":["4.8.1.1"]},{"q":"Write the equation used to calculate the Rf value of a substance in paper chromatography.","a":"Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent.","sol":"Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent. Both distances are measured from the origin (the pencil line), the substance being measured to the centre of its spot.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the two measurements you take off a dried chromatogram with a ruler.","Both are measured from the same pencil line, and the value that results is never greater than 1.","Write it as a ratio of those two measurements, choosing which goes on top so the value stays below 1."],"codes":["4.8.1.3"]},{"q":"Describe the test for hydrogen gas and state the result of a positive test.","a":"Hold a lit (burning) splint at the open end of a test tube of the gas; hydrogen burns rapidly with a squeaky pop.","sol":"Collect the gas in a test tube. Hold a lit (burning) splint at the open end of the tube (1 mark). If hydrogen is present it burns rapidly and produces a squeaky pop sound (1 mark).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This gas test uses a wooden splint, and the evidence it gives you is a sound rather than a colour change.","Decide whether the splint must already be alight or only glowing, and where it has to be held.","Give one sentence for the method and one short phrase for what you hear."],"codes":["4.8.2.1"]},{"q":"State the flame colour produced in a flame test by lithium compounds, by potassium compounds and by copper compounds.","a":"Lithium crimson; potassium lilac; copper green.","sol":"Lithium compounds result in a crimson flame (1 mark). Potassium compounds result in a lilac flame (1 mark). Copper compounds result in a green flame (1 mark).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["A flame test identifies the metal ion (cation) in a compound, and each of these three gives a colour of its own.","Take them one at a time; only one of the three is at the blue-green end of the visible spectrum.","Set your answer out as three short 'metal — colour' pairs."],"codes":["4.8.3.1"]},{"q":"Dilute nitric acid and then silver nitrate solution are added to separate solutions of chloride ions, bromide ions and iodide ions. Name the colour of the precipitate formed in each case.","a":"Chloride white; bromide cream; iodide yellow.","sol":"Silver chloride is white (1 mark). Silver bromide is cream (1 mark). Silver iodide is yellow (1 mark).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["All three precipitates are silver halides, and their colours deepen as you go down the group.","Take the three halide ions in order and recall the colour that goes with each.","Give three short 'ion — colour' pairs."],"codes":["4.8.3.4"]},{"q":"Describe the test for oxygen gas and the test for carbon dioxide gas, giving the result of each test.","a":"Oxygen: insert a glowing splint into the tube — it relights. Carbon dioxide: bubble the gas through limewater — it turns milky (cloudy).","sol":"Oxygen: insert a glowing (not flaming) splint into a test tube of the gas (1 mark); the splint relights (1 mark). Carbon dioxide: shake the gas with, or bubble it through, limewater, an aqueous solution of calcium hydroxide (1 mark); the limewater turns milky, or cloudy (1 mark).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["One of these tests uses a splint and the other uses a solution, so treat them as two separate tests.","For the splint, decide what state it must be in; for the solution, recall the chemical name behind its common name.","Write four short statements: method then observation for the first gas, then method then observation for the second."],"codes":["4.8.2.2","4.8.2.3"]},{"q":"Explain how paper chromatography separates the different dyes in a mixture of food colourings.","a":"Different dyes are more or less soluble in the solvent and more or less attracted to the paper, so they travel different distances and separate into different spots.","sol":"As the solvent (mobile phase) moves through the paper (stationary phase), the dyes in the mixture dissolve in the solvent to different extents and have different attractions to the paper (1 mark). Dyes that are more soluble in the solvent and less attracted to the paper are carried further; dyes that are less soluble and more attracted to the paper stay closer to the origin (1 mark). Because each dye travels a different distance, the mixture separates into separate spots (1 mark).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is about what happens as the solvent moves through the stationary phase.","Think about why different dyes would end up at different distances from the start — compare their solubility and how strongly they interact with the paper.","Structure your answer as: what moves, why different substances behave differently, then the result."],"codes":["4.8.1.3"]},{"q":"A sample of a solid melts between 132 °C and 141 °C. Explain what this tells you about the purity of the sample.","a":"It is impure (a mixture), because a pure substance melts at one sharp, specific temperature, not over a range.","sol":"A pure element or compound melts at one specific, sharp temperature (1 mark). This sample melts over a range of temperatures, from 132 °C to 141 °C, rather than sharply at a single temperature, so it must be impure — a mixture rather than a pure substance (1 mark).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Melting point data is one of the ways the specification says pure substances can be told from mixtures.","Compare the melting behaviour described here with what a single element or compound would do.","State your conclusion first, then justify it from the shape of the data rather than from the actual temperatures."],"codes":["4.8.1.1"]},{"q":"A paint is made by mixing a pigment, a solvent, a binder and a preservative in carefully measured amounts. Explain why this makes the paint a formulation.","a":"It is a mixture designed as a useful product, made from components each with a specific purpose, measured carefully to give the required properties.","sol":"A formulation is a mixture that has been designed as a useful product, made by mixing components in carefully measured quantities so the product has the properties it needs (1 mark). This paint fits that description: each named component (pigment, solvent, binder, preservative) has its own particular purpose and they are combined in measured amounts, so it is a formulation (1 mark).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["This links to the definition of a formulation given in the specification.","Check whether each ingredient has its own job and whether the amounts are controlled rather than approximate.","Give the definition, then apply it to the ingredients listed in the question."],"codes":["4.8.1.2"]},{"q":"A colourless solution is thought to contain sulfate ions. Describe the test you would carry out and state the result of a positive test.","a":"Add dilute hydrochloric acid, then barium chloride solution; a white precipitate shows sulfate ions.","sol":"Add dilute hydrochloric acid to a sample of the solution (1 mark), then add barium chloride solution (1 mark). A white precipitate, of barium sulfate, shows that sulfate ions are present (1 mark). The acid is added first so that any carbonate ions present cannot give a white precipitate and a false positive.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is a precipitation test, and like the halide test it has to be done in the presence of an acid.","One reagent supplies the ion that joins the sulfate ion; the acid is chosen so that it cannot add the ion being tested for.","Set the answer out as acid first, then reagent, then the observation."],"codes":["4.8.3.5"]},{"q":"Describe the test for chlorine gas and state the result of a positive test.","a":"Hold damp litmus paper in the gas; the litmus paper is bleached and turns white.","sol":"Hold a piece of damp (moist) litmus paper in the gas (1 mark). If chlorine is present the litmus paper is bleached and turns white (1 mark). The paper must be damp because the chlorine has to dissolve in the water on the paper.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["This gas test uses paper rather than a splint, and the paper must not be dry.","Recall what happens to the colour of the paper: it is not turned red and it is not turned blue.","Give the method in one sentence, then describe the final appearance of the paper in a short phrase."],"codes":["4.8.2.4"]},{"q":"On a chromatogram, a dye spot has moved 3.2 cm from the origin and the solvent front has moved 8.0 cm from the origin. Calculate the Rf value of the dye.","a":"Rf = 0.40","sol":"Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent (1 mark) = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = 0.40, to 2 significant figures, with no unit (1 mark).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Both distances are already measured from the origin, so nothing has to be subtracted first.","An Rf value has no unit, so the two lengths simply divide; decide which is the divisor so that the answer comes out below 1.","The first line of working is Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 — set it up, then evaluate it."],"codes":["4.8.1.3","4.8.1.3 (Required practical 6)"]},{"q":"A student carries out Required practical 6. On the dried chromatogram the centre of a green spot is 6.1 cm above the pencil line and the solvent front is 9.5 cm above the pencil line. Determine the Rf value of the green pigment, giving your answer to 2 significant figures.","a":"Rf = 0.64","sol":"Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent (1 mark) = 6.1 ÷ 9.5 = 0.642105…, which rounds to 0.64 to 2 significant figures (1 mark).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Both lengths are measured from the same pencil line, and the one for the pigment is taken to the centre of its spot.","Carry out the division in full and only round at the very end, to the number of significant figures the question asks for.","The first line of working is Rf = 6.1 ÷ 9.5 — evaluate that, then round it."],"codes":["4.8.1.3 (Required practical 6)"]},{"q":"A white solid Y is a single ionic compound. A flame test on Y gives a yellow flame. When dilute hydrochloric acid is added to a fresh sample of Y it fizzes, and the gas given off turns limewater milky. Identify compound Y, give its formula, and explain how each test result supports your answer.","a":"Sodium carbonate, Na₂CO₃.","sol":"The yellow flame colour shows that the cation is the sodium ion, Na⁺ (1 mark). Fizzing with a dilute acid, giving a gas that turns limewater milky, shows that carbon dioxide is released and therefore that the anion is the carbonate ion, CO₃²⁻ (1 mark). Two sodium ions balance one carbonate ion, so Y is sodium carbonate (1 mark), formula Na₂CO₃ (1 mark). The reaction with the acid is Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Take the two results in turn: one of them identifies the positive ion and the other identifies the negative ion.","Decide which gas turns limewater milky, then which class of compound releases that gas when a dilute acid is added.","Write down the cation from the flame colour and the anion from the gas test, then combine them so that the charges balance."],"codes":["4.8.3.1","4.8.3.3","4.8.3.5 (Required practical 7)"]},{"q":"Iron(III) chloride solution reacts with sodium hydroxide solution to form a brown precipitate. Write the balanced symbol equation for this reaction, and then write the ionic equation for the formation of the precipitate (Higher tier).","a":"FeCl₃ + 3NaOH → Fe(OH)₃ + 3NaCl; Fe³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₃(s)","sol":"The brown precipitate is iron(III) hydroxide, Fe(OH)₃, and the soluble product is sodium chloride, so the equation is FeCl₃ + NaOH → Fe(OH)₃ + NaCl with the correct formulae (1 mark). Balanced: FeCl₃ + 3NaOH → Fe(OH)₃ + 3NaCl — 1 Fe, 3 Cl, 3 Na, 3 O and 3 H on each side (1 mark). Cancelling the spectator sodium ions and chloride ions gives the ionic equation Fe³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₃(s), which balances for atoms and for charge, since (+3) + 3(−1) = 0 on the left and 0 on the right (1 mark).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Sodium hydroxide solution supplies the ion that joins the metal ion to make the insoluble solid; the other product stays in solution.","Write the formula of the precipitate from the charges on its two ions, then balance the sodium and the chlorine.","For the ionic equation, cross out the ions that appear unchanged on both sides, then check that the total charge matches on each side."],"codes":["4.8.3.2"]},{"q":"Flame emission spectroscopy is used to analyse a solution of metal ions. Explain why an instrumental method such as this is used in place of the chemical tests in this specification, and describe how the instrument produces a result that identifies the metal ions present.","a":"Instrumental methods are accurate, sensitive and rapid; the sample is put into a flame, the light given out is passed through a spectroscope, and the line spectrum produced is compared with reference spectra to identify the ions and measure their concentrations.","sol":"Advantages: instrumental methods are accurate, because the result does not depend on someone judging a colour by eye (1 mark); they are sensitive, so they work on very small amounts of sample, and they are rapid (1 mark). Method: the sample is put into a flame and the light given out is passed through a spectroscope (1 mark). The output is a line spectrum; comparing the positions of its lines with a reference set identifies the metal ions in the solution, and the intensity of the lines measures their concentrations (1 mark). A flame test could not separate the ions in a mixture, because one flame colour can mask another.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The specification gives three single words for the advantages of instrumental methods over the chemical tests in this course.","For the second part, follow the sample through the instrument: what is done to it, what comes out of it, and what the output is compared against.","Give the advantages as a short list, then describe the instrument as a sequence from what happens to the sample to what is finally read."],"codes":["4.8.3.6","4.8.3.7"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the equation for the Rf value of a substance?","a":"Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent."},{"q":"What colour precipitate forms when sodium hydroxide solution is added to a solution containing iron(II) ions?","a":"Green."},{"q":"Which two reagents identify sulfate ions in solution, and what is seen?","a":"Dilute hydrochloric acid then barium chloride solution; a white precipitate."},{"q":"What three advantages do instrumental methods have over the chemical tests in this specification?","a":"They are accurate, sensitive and rapid."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Pure substance (chemistry definition)","back":"A single element or compound, not mixed with any other substance. It melts and boils at one sharp, specific temperature; a mixture melts and boils over a range.","code":"4.8.1.1","open":false},{"front":"Formulation","back":"A mixture designed as a useful product, made by mixing components in carefully measured quantities, each with a particular purpose: fuels, cleaning agents, paints, medicines, alloys, fertilisers, foods.","code":"4.8.1.2","open":false},{"front":"Rf value equation","back":"Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent, both measured from the origin, the substance to the centre of its spot. No unit; always between 0 and 1.","code":"4.8.1.3","open":false},{"front":"Stationary phase and mobile phase in chromatography","back":"The paper is the stationary phase and the solvent the mobile phase; separation depends on how substances distribute between them. A pure compound gives a single spot in all solvents.","code":"4.8.1.3","open":false},{"front":"Test for hydrogen","back":"Hold a lit (burning) splint at the open end of the test tube. Hydrogen burns rapidly with a squeaky pop.","code":"4.8.2.1","open":false},{"front":"Test for oxygen","back":"Insert a glowing splint into the test tube. The splint relights if oxygen is present.","code":"4.8.2.2","open":false},{"front":"Test for carbon dioxide","back":"Shake the gas with, or bubble it through, limewater (aqueous calcium hydroxide). The limewater turns milky (cloudy). Limewater is a dilute solution of the same Ca(OH)₂ that is the white precipitate in the sodium hydroxide test.","code":"4.8.2.3","open":false},{"front":"Test for chlorine","back":"Hold damp litmus paper in the gas. The litmus paper is bleached and turns white.","code":"4.8.2.4","open":false},{"front":"Flame test colours","back":"Lithium crimson, sodium yellow, potassium lilac, calcium orange-red, copper green. In a mixture of ions some flame colours can be masked.","code":"4.8.3.1","open":false},{"front":"Sodium hydroxide solution test for metal ions","back":"White precipitates: aluminium, calcium, magnesium — only Al(OH)₃ dissolves in excess. Coloured: copper(II) blue, iron(II) green, iron(III) brown. Ionic (Higher tier): Fe³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₃(s).","code":"4.8.3.2","open":false},{"front":"Flame emission spectroscopy","back":"An instrumental method for metal ions in solution: the sample is put into a flame and the light given out is passed through a spectroscope. The line spectrum identifies the ions and measures their concentrations.","code":"4.8.3.7","open":false},{"front":"Test for carbonate ions","back":"Add dilute acid: the sample fizzes and the gas given off turns limewater milky, showing carbon dioxide. Example: Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂.","code":"4.8.3.3","open":false},{"front":"Test for halide ions","back":"Add dilute nitric acid, then silver nitrate solution: silver chloride white, silver bromide cream, silver iodide yellow. Ionic (Higher tier): Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s).","code":"4.8.3.4","open":false},{"front":"Test for sulfate ions","back":"Add dilute hydrochloric acid, then barium chloride solution: a white precipitate of barium sulfate. Ionic (Higher tier): Ba²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → BaSO₄(s).","code":"4.8.3.5","open":false},{"front":"Name one advantage of instrumental methods over the chemical tests in this specification","back":"They are accurate, not dependent on judging a colour by eye; they are sensitive, so a very small sample is enough; they are rapid, so many samples can be analysed quickly.","code":"4.8.3.6","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Key equations, tests and values for Chemical analysis","items":["Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent — both distances measured from the origin, the substance to the centre of its spot. Rf has no unit and always lies between 0 and 1. Not supplied on any equation sheet; it must be recalled.","Gas tests: hydrogen — lit splint at the open end of the tube, squeaky pop; oxygen — glowing splint, relights; carbon dioxide — limewater (aqueous calcium hydroxide), turns milky; chlorine — damp litmus paper, bleached white.","Carbon dioxide with limewater: CO₂ + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O — the insoluble calcium carbonate is what makes the limewater look milky.","Flame tests: lithium crimson, sodium yellow, potassium lilac, calcium orange-red, copper green.","White hydroxide precipitates with sodium hydroxide solution: aluminium, calcium and magnesium; only Al(OH)₃ dissolves in excess sodium hydroxide. Coloured precipitates: Cu(OH)₂ blue, Fe(OH)₂ green, Fe(OH)₃ brown.","Balanced symbol equations for the insoluble hydroxides: MgCl₂ + 2NaOH → Mg(OH)₂ + 2NaCl; CuSO₄ + 2NaOH → Cu(OH)₂ + Na₂SO₄; FeSO₄ + 2NaOH → Fe(OH)₂ + Na₂SO₄; FeCl₃ + 3NaOH → Fe(OH)₃ + 3NaCl; AlCl₃ + 3NaOH → Al(OH)₃ + 3NaCl.","The same reactions as ionic equations, which the specification's 'Write' command word lists as Higher tier: Mg²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Mg(OH)₂(s); Ca²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Ca(OH)₂(s); Al³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Al(OH)₃(s); Cu²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Cu(OH)₂(s); Fe²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₂(s); Fe³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₃(s). Each balances for atoms and for charge — the metal ion takes one OH⁻ for every unit of positive charge, so both sides are neutral. Calcium hydroxide is the one of these that is slightly soluble in water: limewater is a dilute solution of Ca(OH)₂, and the same compound is the white precipitate here once more of it is present than the water can dissolve.","Carbonates with dilute acid: Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂ and CaCO₃ + 2HNO₃ → Ca(NO₃)₂ + H₂O + CO₂; ionic (Higher tier) CO₃²⁻(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) → H₂O(l) + CO₂(g).","Halides with dilute nitric acid and silver nitrate solution: NaCl + AgNO₃ → AgCl + NaNO₃ and KBr + AgNO₃ → AgBr + KNO₃; ionic (Higher tier) Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s), Ag⁺(aq) + Br⁻(aq) → AgBr(s), Ag⁺(aq) + I⁻(aq) → AgI(s). Colours: chloride white, bromide cream, iodide yellow.","Sulfates with dilute hydrochloric acid and barium chloride solution: Na₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄ + 2NaCl; ionic (Higher tier) Ba²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → BaSO₄(s). The precipitate is white.","Instrumental methods are accurate, sensitive and rapid. Flame emission spectroscopy: sample into a flame → light given out → spectroscope → line spectrum, whose line positions identify the metal ions and whose line intensities measure their concentrations."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical activity 6: investigate how paper chromatography can be used to separate and tell the difference between coloured substances. Students should calculate Rf values","items":["Aim: investigate how paper chromatography can be used to separate and tell the difference between coloured substances — for example food colourings or the inks in felt-tip pens — and calculate Rf values for the separated spots.","Apparatus (standard classroom practice; the specification lists the AT, WS and MS skills for this activity rather than a fixed equipment list): chromatography paper, a pencil and a ruler, a fine capillary tube or glass rod for spotting, the coloured samples and any reference substances, a 250 cm³ beaker or a chromatography tank, a suitable solvent such as water or a water and ethanol mixture, a watch glass or lid, a splint or rod to suspend the paper, and eye protection.","Method step 1: draw a line across the chromatography paper about 2 cm from the bottom edge — the origin. Use a pencil, not a pen, because pencil does not dissolve in the solvent and so cannot move up the paper with it.","Method step 2: using a fine capillary tube, put a small spot of each coloured substance, and of each reference substance, on the origin line, well apart from one another. Keep the spots small, letting each one dry before adding more of the same sample, so the spots stay tight and do not overlap as they rise.","Method step 3: pour solvent into the beaker or tank to a depth that will stay below the origin line when the paper is stood in it, and check the depth against the paper before the paper goes in.","Method step 4: stand the paper in the solvent so that only the strip below the origin line dips in, keeping the paper upright and not touching the sides, and cover the beaker with a watch glass or lid so the solvent does not evaporate from the tank.","Method step 5: leave the paper undisturbed until the solvent has risen most of the way up the paper, then take the paper out before the solvent reaches the top edge and immediately mark the position of the solvent front in pencil, because the solvent evaporates and the front becomes impossible to find once the paper dries.","Method step 6: let the paper dry, then measure in millimetres the distance from the origin to the solvent front, and the distance from the origin to the centre of each spot, and calculate Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent for each spot, giving each answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.","Independent variable: the substance spotted onto the origin line — each coloured sample and each reference substance (the investigation can instead be repeated with different solvents, in which case the solvent is the independent variable).","Dependent variable: the distance each substance travels up the paper from the origin, which is used to calculate its Rf value.","Control variables: the same solvent, the same type and size of chromatography paper, the same distance of the origin line above the solvent surface, the same size of spot, the same temperature, and the same running distance of the solvent front (all spots on one chromatogram share a single solvent front, which is why comparisons must be made within one run or between runs made under identical conditions).","Safety: wear eye protection. If an organic solvent such as ethanol or propanone is used it is flammable and harmful, so keep it away from naked flames, work in a well-ventilated room and keep the tank covered.","Likely errors: drawing the origin line in ink, so the line itself dissolves and travels with the solvent; starting with the solvent level above the origin line, so the samples wash straight off into the solvent instead of separating; leaving the paper so long that the solvent front runs off the top edge, which makes the Rf values impossible to calculate; failing to mark the solvent front before the paper dries; measuring to the edge of a spot instead of consistently to its centre; and letting the paper touch the side of the tank, which distorts the solvent front.","Expected result and how it is analysed: a sample that is a single pure compound produces one spot, whichever solvent is used; a mixture separates into two or more spots, one for each coloured compound in it. Calculate each Rf value and compare it with reference values measured in the same solvent to identify the compounds. Worked example of the processing: if a spot's centre is 3.6 cm from the origin and the solvent front is 4.8 cm from the origin, Rf = 3.6 ÷ 4.8 = 0.75. Every Rf value should come out between 0 and 1; a value above 1 means a measurement or the division has gone wrong.","Skills assessed (specification section 8.2.6): AT 1 — use of appropriate apparatus to make and record a range of measurements accurately; AT 4 — safe use of a range of equipment to purify and/or separate chemical mixtures including chromatography.","Key opportunities for skills development (specification section 8.2.6): WS 2.4 — carry out experiments appropriately having due regard for the correct manipulation of apparatus, the accuracy of measurements and health and safety considerations; WS 2.6 — make and record observations and measurements using a range of apparatus and methods. The topic also develops MS 1a, MS 1c, MS 1d and MS 2a through the Rf calculation."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical activity 7: use of chemical tests to identify the ions in unknown single ionic compounds covering the ions from sections Flame tests to Sulfates (chemistry only)","items":["Aim: use chemical tests to identify the cation and the anion in each of several unknown single ionic compounds, covering the ions in specification sections 4.8.3.1 Flame tests to 4.8.3.5 Sulfates. Practicals 2 and 7 are GCSE Chemistry only.","Apparatus (standard classroom practice; the specification lists the AT and WS skills for this activity rather than a fixed equipment list): the unknown solids, test tubes in a rack, a Bunsen burner, heatproof mat and tongs, a nichrome or platinum wire loop (or flame test rods), teat pipettes, a spatula, distilled water, dilute hydrochloric acid, dilute nitric acid, sodium hydroxide solution, silver nitrate solution, barium chloride solution, limewater with a delivery tube and bung, and eye protection.","Method step 1: dissolve a spatula measure of the unknown solid in distilled water in a test tube to make a solution, and keep some of the solid dry for the flame test. Divide the solution between several test tubes so that every test that follows is done on a fresh sample.","Method step 2 (flame test, cation): clean the wire loop by dipping it in dilute hydrochloric acid and holding it in a roaring blue Bunsen flame until it gives no colour. Dip the clean loop in the solid and hold it at the edge of a blue flame. Crimson shows lithium, yellow sodium, lilac potassium, orange-red calcium and green copper. Clean the loop again between samples.","Method step 3 (sodium hydroxide, cation): to a fresh sample of the solution add a few drops of sodium hydroxide solution and record the precipitate. A blue precipitate shows copper(II), green shows iron(II) and brown shows iron(III). A white precipitate means aluminium, calcium or magnesium ions.","Method step 4 (sodium hydroxide in excess): if the precipitate is white, go on adding sodium hydroxide solution until it is in excess. Only aluminium hydroxide dissolves again to give a colourless solution; calcium and magnesium hydroxides stay as white precipitates (calcium hydroxide is only slightly soluble, and a dilute solution of that same compound is the limewater used in step 5), and these two are then told apart by the flame test, since calcium gives an orange-red flame and magnesium gives none of the listed colours.","Method step 5 (carbonate, anion): to a fresh sample of the solid or solution add dilute hydrochloric acid and look for effervescence. If the sample fizzes, pass the gas through limewater: limewater that turns milky confirms carbon dioxide and therefore a carbonate.","Method step 6 (halide, anion): to a fresh sample of the solution add dilute nitric acid, then silver nitrate solution. A white precipitate shows chloride, cream shows bromide and yellow shows iodide. The nitric acid is added first to remove carbonate ions, which would otherwise give a white precipitate and a false positive, and nitric acid is used rather than hydrochloric acid because hydrochloric acid would add chloride ions of its own.","Method step 7 (sulfate, anion): to another fresh sample of the solution add dilute hydrochloric acid, then barium chloride solution. A white precipitate shows sulfate ions. Again the acid goes in first to remove carbonate ions, and hydrochloric acid is used because it cannot add sulfate ions.","Method step 8: record every observation in a results table with a row for each unknown and a column for each test, then put the cation and the anion together in the ratio that makes the compound neutral overall, and name the compound and write its formula.","Independent variable: the unknown compound being tested (and, within each unknown, the test being carried out).","Dependent variable: the observation each test gives — the flame colour, and the colour of any precipitate or the gas evolved.","Control variables: the same volume of solution and the same number of drops of each reagent in every test, the same concentration of each reagent, a freshly cleaned wire loop and a fresh sample of solution for every test, and the same background against which the flame colour is judged.","Safety: wear eye protection throughout. Tie hair back and keep loose clothing away from the Bunsen flame; use tongs and a heatproof mat, and let the wire loop cool before putting it down. Sodium hydroxide solution is corrosive and silver nitrate solution stains skin and clothing, so wash any splashes off at once. Barium chloride solution is toxic, so wash hands afterwards and do not pipette by mouth.","Likely errors: using a dirty wire loop, so a trace of sodium left on it gives a yellow flame that masks the true colour; testing a mixture, in which a strong flame colour such as sodium's masks a weaker one; using the same sample of solution for more than one test, so a reagent added earlier ruins the next result; forgetting the acid before the silver nitrate or the barium chloride, so a carbonate gives a white precipitate and a false positive; using hydrochloric acid before silver nitrate, which introduces chloride ions; and adding too little sodium hydroxide solution to tell a dissolving aluminium hydroxide precipitate from one that does not dissolve.","Expected result and how it is analysed: each unknown gives one cation result and one anion result. Combine them so that the charges cancel — for example a yellow flame with fizzing and limewater turning milky gives sodium carbonate, Na₂CO₃, and a crimson flame with a cream precipitate gives lithium bromide, LiBr. Support a hydroxide result with an equation, for example CuSO₄ + 2NaOH → Cu(OH)₂ + Na₂SO₄, whose ionic form (Higher tier) is Cu²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Cu(OH)₂(s).","Skills assessed: the specification gives this activity two different AT lists, so the sheet has to say which one it follows. Section 8.2.7 lists AT 2 — safe use of a Bunsen burner; AT 8 — use of appropriate qualitative reagents and techniques to analyse and identify unknown samples or products including gas tests, flame tests, precipitation reactions. Section 4.8.3.5 lists AT 1 and AT 8 instead. This sheet follows 8.2.7, because that is the section that sets out in full what the practical must cover and a Bunsen burner is needed for the flame test; learn both lines, since either may be quoted.","Key opportunities for skills development (specification section 8.2.7): WS 2.4 — carry out experiments appropriately having due regard for the correct manipulation of apparatus, the accuracy of measurements and health and safety considerations; WS 2.6 — make and record observations and measurements using a range of apparatus and methods."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: using chromatography to decide whether a food colouring is a pure compound or a mixture","items":["Question: Two green food colourings, A and B, are analysed by paper chromatography in the same solvent. Colouring A produces one spot, a green spot with an Rf value of 0.55. Colouring B produces two spots, a blue spot with an Rf value of 0.30 and a yellow spot with an Rf value of 0.80. Explain what these results show about the purity of each colouring, and describe how paper chromatography achieves this separation. [6 marks]","Level 3 (5–6 marks) model answer: Colouring A produces only one spot in this solvent, and a pure compound produces a single spot in all solvents, so colouring A is likely to be a single pure dye. Colouring B produces two spots, blue at Rf 0.30 and yellow at Rf 0.80; a mixture can separate into different spots while a pure compound cannot, so colouring B must be a mixture of at least two different dyes. The separation happens because chromatography has a stationary phase, the paper, and a mobile phase, the solvent: as the solvent moves up the paper, each dye distributes itself between the two phases according to how soluble it is in the solvent and how strongly it is attracted to the paper. A dye that is more soluble in the solvent and less strongly attracted to the paper is carried further and so has the larger Rf value, like the yellow dye at 0.80, while a dye that is less soluble and more strongly attracted to the paper travels a shorter distance and has the smaller Rf value, like the blue dye at 0.30. Because the two dyes travel different distances, they separate into distinct spots, which is how chromatography both separates a mixture and shows whether the sample was pure to begin with. To be certain that colouring A really is pure, it should be run again in one or two other solvents, because a single spot in every solvent is the test the specification sets.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): the answer correctly says that the one-spot colouring is likely to be pure and the two-spot colouring is a mixture, and explains that there is a stationary phase and a mobile phase, but it does not link the two different Rf values to differences in solubility in the solvent or in attraction to the paper, or it deals with only one of the two colourings in detail.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): the answer states that more than one spot means a mixture, or names the stationary and mobile phases, as an isolated fact, without applying it to both colourings or explaining why different substances travel different distances.","What a bottom-level answer misses: it does not reach a separate purity conclusion for each colouring using its own Rf data, and it does not explain the mechanism — that differing solubility in the solvent and differing attraction to the paper make substances travel different distances and so give them different Rf values."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: identifying the ions in an unknown single ionic compound (chemistry only)","items":["Question: A technician finds an unlabelled bottle of a white ionic solid that dissolves in water to give a colourless solution. Describe how you would use chemical tests to identify both the metal ion and the negative ion in the solid, giving the result you would expect for each test you describe. [6 marks]","Level 3 (5–6 marks) model answer: First dissolve a spatula measure of the solid in distilled water and divide the solution between several test tubes, keeping some solid dry, so that every test is carried out on a fresh sample and no reagent can spoil a later result. To find the metal ion, clean a nichrome wire loop in dilute hydrochloric acid and hold it in a roaring Bunsen flame until it gives no colour, then dip it in the dry solid and hold it at the edge of a blue flame: a crimson flame shows lithium ions, yellow shows sodium, lilac shows potassium, orange-red shows calcium and green shows copper. Then add a few drops of sodium hydroxide solution to a fresh sample of the solution: a blue precipitate shows copper(II) ions, a green precipitate iron(II) ions and a brown precipitate iron(III) ions, while a white precipitate means aluminium, calcium or magnesium ions, and only the aluminium hydroxide precipitate dissolves again when sodium hydroxide solution is added in excess. To find the negative ion, add dilute hydrochloric acid to a fresh sample: if it fizzes and the gas turns limewater milky the solid is a carbonate, because carbonates react with dilute acids to give carbon dioxide. Add dilute nitric acid and then silver nitrate solution to another fresh sample: a white precipitate shows chloride ions, a cream precipitate bromide ions and a yellow precipitate iodide ions. Add dilute hydrochloric acid and then barium chloride solution to a further fresh sample: a white precipitate shows sulfate ions. In both of these last two tests the acid is added first so that any carbonate present cannot give a white precipitate and a false positive, and the acid is chosen so that it does not add the very ion being tested for. Finally, combine the cation and the anion in the ratio that makes the compound neutral and write its name and formula, for example a yellow flame together with fizzing and milky limewater gives sodium carbonate, Na₂CO₃.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): the answer describes a correct cation test and a correct anion test with their results, for example the flame test colours and the silver nitrate precipitate colours, but it leaves out the reagents or the results for the other anions, does not use excess sodium hydroxide to separate the three white precipitates, or does not say that a fresh sample and an acid are needed before the precipitation tests.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): the answer names one test, such as 'do a flame test' or 'add sodium hydroxide', with at most a vague result, and does not distinguish the metal ion tests from the negative ion tests or give the colours that identify particular ions.","What a bottom-level answer misses: it does not organise the work as one set of tests for the cation and another for the anion, it does not give the specific colour that identifies each ion, it omits the acid that must precede the silver nitrate and barium chloride tests and the use of excess sodium hydroxide to identify aluminium, and it never combines the two results into a named compound."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:03:09Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"Every fault from the first pass is properly fixed and nothing new has been broken. Re-checked from the kit file, not the stale judge.md copy: 24 distinct symbol and ionic equations re-parsed in Python and all balance for atoms and for charge (the 25th match, FeCl₃ + NaOH → Fe(OH)₃ + NaCl in room.questions[14].sol, is the deliberate unbalanced skeleton the following mark-scheme line balances); Rf re-solved as 3.6/4.8 = 0.75, 3.2/8.0 = 0.40 and 6.1/9.5 = 0.642105… → 0.64 to 2 sf; lesson.idea[5] now matches spec 4.4.3.4 (oxygen at the anode unless halide ions are present, chlorine from a chloride solution, identified by bleached damp litmus) and names the gas each test identifies; the silver halide and barium sulfate ionic equations carry the Higher tier label in lesson.idea[11], lesson.idea[12], cards[12] and cards[13]; questions 11 and 12 sit at d=2 with the spread 5/8/3, still at least three at each tier; extras[2] item 16 now sets out both AT lists (8.2.7 AT 2 and AT 8, 4.8.3.5 AT 1 and AT 8), says it follows 8.2.7 and why, which is the right reading since 8.2.7 is the section that states what must be covered and a Bunsen is needed for the flame test; calcium hydroxide is reconciled as the slightly soluble hydroxide whose dilute solution is limewater in lesson.idea[6], lesson.idea[9], room.facts[8], cards[6] (36 words, within the 40-word limit), extras[0] item 6 and extras[2] item 5. Unchanged material re-verified: all five flame colours, the three white precipitates and aluminium hydroxide redissolving in excess, the carbonate/halide/sulfate reagents and colours, the four gas tests, the illustrative emission lines (Na 589, K 766, Li 671, Ca 423 nm, all genuine principal lines), Paper 2 at 1 h 45 min, 100 marks, 50 %, 8462F/8462H, eight required practicals and the 15 % practical share, RP6 as AT 1 and AT 4 with WS 2.4 and 2.6 from 8.2.6, all 16 idea codes tested, 16 questions, 15 cards within their word limits, four exit questions, no carets and Unicode subscripts and superscripts throughout. Publish it."}}},"4.9":{"id":"AQA-8462","topic":"4.9","family":"science","lesson":{"why":"Chemistry of the atmosphere is examined on Paper 2 of GCSE Chemistry, and almost every mark in it is for explaining rather than calculating. You must recall the proportions of the gases in today's atmosphere, describe the one theory of the early atmosphere you are required to know, explain how algae and plants raised the percentage of oxygen and lowered the percentage of carbon dioxide, explain the greenhouse effect in terms of short and long wavelength radiation, judge the quality of evidence in a report about climate change, and predict and explain the pollutants released when a fuel burns. Examiners set one-mark and two-mark recall questions on the figures, four-mark descriptions of the volcanic theory and of how carbon dioxide fell, and six-mark extended responses on the evidence for climate change or on the pollutants from a named fuel; the mathematics in this room is limited to ratios, fractions and percentages (MS 1c).","idea":[{"code":"4.9.1.1","h":"The proportions of different gases in today's atmosphere","t":"For 200 million years the proportions of the different gases in the atmosphere have been much the same as they are today: about four-fifths (approximately 80 %) nitrogen, about one-fifth (approximately 20 %) oxygen, and small proportions of various other gases, including carbon dioxide, water vapour and noble gases. Learn the two main figures as fractions and as percentages, because a question may give you either. The skill attached to this statement is MS 1c, using ratios, fractions and percentages: you may be given the volume of a gas in a sample of air, or the ratio in which two gases are mixed, and asked to work out a percentage, or to compare a sample with the proportions expected in today's atmosphere."},{"code":"4.9.1.2","h":"The Earth's early atmosphere and the evidence for it","t":"Theories about what was in the Earth's early atmosphere and how the atmosphere formed have changed and developed over time, and the evidence is limited because of the timescale of 4.6 billion years. Only one theory is required: during the first billion years of the Earth's existence there was intense volcanic activity that released the gases which formed the early atmosphere, and water vapour that condensed to form the oceans. At the start of this period the atmosphere may have been like the atmospheres of Mars and Venus today, consisting of mainly carbon dioxide with little or no oxygen gas. Volcanoes also produced nitrogen, which gradually built up in the atmosphere, and there may have been small proportions of methane and ammonia. When the oceans formed, carbon dioxide dissolved in the water and carbonates were precipitated, producing sediments and reducing the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere. No knowledge of other theories is required: in the exam you are given information and asked to interpret the evidence and evaluate the theories, so the marks come from using what you are given, not from recalling a rival theory."},{"code":"4.9.1.3","h":"How oxygen increased","t":"Algae and plants produced the oxygen that is now in the atmosphere by photosynthesis, which can be represented by the equation carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen, and in symbols by 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, with light absorbed by chlorophyll. Algae first produced oxygen about 2.7 billion years ago and soon after this oxygen appeared in the atmosphere. Over the next billion years plants evolved and the percentage of oxygen gradually increased to a level that enabled animals to evolve. The examinable skill is to describe that sequence in the right order and to use the equation as your evidence: oxygen is on the products side, so every photosynthesising organism added oxygen to the air while taking carbon dioxide out of it."},{"code":"4.9.1.4","h":"How carbon dioxide decreased","t":"Algae and plants decreased the percentage of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere by photosynthesis. Carbon dioxide was also decreased by the formation of sedimentary rocks and fossil fuels that contain carbon. You must be able to describe the main changes in the atmosphere over time with some of their likely causes, and to describe and explain the formation of four named deposits. Limestone formed from carbonates precipitated when carbon dioxide dissolved in the oceans, and from the shells and skeletons of marine organisms, which settled as sediment and were buried and compressed into sedimentary rock. Coal formed from thick layers of dead plant material buried in the absence of oxygen so that it did not decay completely, then compressed over millions of years. Crude oil and natural gas formed from the remains of plankton buried in mud and subjected to high pressure and temperature over millions of years. Carbon held in rock or in fossil fuel is carbon that is no longer in the air, which is why the percentage of carbon dioxide fell."},{"code":"4.9.2.1","h":"Greenhouse gases and the greenhouse effect","t":"Greenhouse gases in the atmosphere maintain temperatures on Earth high enough to support life, and the three you must name are water vapour, carbon dioxide and methane. You are required to describe the greenhouse effect in terms of the interaction of short and long wavelength radiation with matter, so a full answer follows the energy: short wavelength radiation from the Sun passes through the atmosphere and is absorbed by the Earth's surface, which warms; the warmed surface radiates energy back out at a longer wavelength; greenhouse gas molecules absorb this long wavelength radiation and re-emit it in all directions, so some of it returns to the surface and warms it further. Naming the gases alone will not earn the marks on this statement — the wavelengths and what the molecules do to them must both appear."},{"code":"4.9.2.2","h":"Human activities, the evidence base and its uncertainties","t":"Some human activities increase the amounts of the greenhouse gases carbon dioxide and methane, and you must be able to recall two activities for each: carbon dioxide from burning fossil fuels for energy and transport and from deforestation, methane from livestock farming and from decomposing waste in landfill (growing rice in flooded paddy fields is also accepted). Based on peer-reviewed evidence, many scientists believe that human activities will cause the temperature of the Earth's atmosphere to increase at the surface and that this will result in global climate change. However, it is difficult to model such complex systems, and this leads to simplified models, speculation and opinions presented in the media that may be based on only parts of the evidence and which may be biased. The examinable skills are to evaluate the quality of evidence in a report given to you, to describe the uncertainties in the evidence base, and to recognise the importance of peer review of results and of communicating results to a wide range of audiences."},{"code":"4.9.2.3","h":"Global climate change","t":"An increase in average global temperature is a major cause of climate change, and there are several potential effects. The four you should be able to describe briefly are: sea levels rising, because water expands as it warms and land ice melts, which threatens low-lying land; more frequent or more severe extreme weather events, such as storms, floods and heatwaves; changes to the amount, timing and distribution of rainfall, which affects how much food can be grown and where; and changes to the migration patterns and habitats of wildlife, so that some species move and some may not survive. You must also be able to discuss the scale, the risk and the environmental implications: the changes act on the whole planet, are slow to reverse, and fall hardest on the places least able to cope."},{"code":"4.9.2.4","h":"The carbon footprint and its reduction","t":"The carbon footprint is the total amount of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases emitted over the full life cycle of a product, service or event — not only while it is in use, but also while it is being made, transported and disposed of. It can be reduced by reducing emissions of carbon dioxide and methane, for example by using renewable or nuclear energy in place of fossil fuels, improving energy efficiency and insulation, using public transport, cutting meat and dairy consumption, and capturing the methane given off by landfill. You must also be able to give reasons why such actions may be limited: the cost of the new technology, the fact that the technology or the infrastructure does not yet exist everywhere, the inconvenience of changing how people live, and the lack of international agreements to make everyone act together."},{"code":"4.9.3.1","h":"Atmospheric pollutants from fuels","t":"The combustion of fuels is a major source of atmospheric pollutants. Most fuels, including coal, contain carbon and/or hydrogen and may also contain some sulfur. When there is plenty of air the carbon and hydrogen burn completely to carbon dioxide and water vapour, for example CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O. When the supply of air is limited, combustion is incomplete: some carbon is only partly oxidised to carbon monoxide, 2CH₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO + 4H₂O, and some is released as solid carbon (soot), CH₄ + O₂ → C + 2H₂O, which together with unburned hydrocarbons forms particulates. Any sulfur in the fuel burns to sulfur dioxide, S + O₂ → SO₂. The high temperature inside an engine or furnace also makes nitrogen and oxygen from the air itself react, N₂ + O₂ → 2NO, forming oxides of nitrogen. In the exam you are given the composition of a fuel and the conditions it is burned in, and asked to predict the products."},{"code":"4.9.3.2","h":"Properties and effects of atmospheric pollutants","t":"Carbon monoxide is a toxic gas; it is colourless and odourless and so is not easily detected, which is why people can be poisoned by a faulty appliance without any warning. Sulfur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen cause respiratory problems in humans, because they irritate the lungs and airways, and they cause acid rain: they dissolve in water in the atmosphere to form acidic solutions that fall as rain, damaging trees, making lakes and rivers acidic enough to kill fish, and corroding limestone buildings and metal structures. Particulates cause global dimming, because they reflect and absorb sunlight so that less of it reaches the Earth's surface, and they cause health problems for humans, because they are small enough to be breathed deep into the lungs. The command is to describe and explain the problems, so each answer needs the effect and the reason for it."}],"examples":[{"title":"Using a ratio to find the percentage of a gas in air","setup":"A sample of air has a total volume of 60 cm³. A student is told that the nitrogen and the oxygen in the sample are in the ratio 4 : 1 by volume, and that the other gases make up so little of the sample that they can be ignored. Work out the volume of nitrogen in the sample, and the percentage of the sample that is nitrogen.","steps":["A ratio of 4 : 1 divides the sample into 4 + 1 = 5 equal parts.","One part is 60 cm³ ÷ 5 = 12 cm³, and nitrogen is 4 parts, so the volume of nitrogen is 4 × 12 cm³ = 48 cm³.","Write the nitrogen as a fraction of the whole sample: 48 cm³ ÷ 60 cm³ = 0.8.","Turn the fraction into a percentage: 0.8 × 100 = 80 %, the approximate percentage of nitrogen in today's atmosphere."],"cues":["How many equal parts does this ratio divide the sample into?","What is one part worth in cm³, and how many of those parts are nitrogen?","What fraction of the whole sample is that volume of nitrogen?","What is that fraction written as a percentage, and does it match the air you breathe?"]},{"title":"Interpreting evidence about the Earth's early atmosphere","setup":"A student is given three pieces of information. (1) The gases released by volcanoes today are mainly carbon dioxide and water vapour, with some nitrogen. (2) The atmospheres of Mars and Venus today consist of mainly carbon dioxide with little or no oxygen gas. (3) Almost no rock from the first billion years of the Earth's existence still survives. Use this information to explain what the Earth's early atmosphere was probably like, and why scientists cannot be certain.","steps":["Apply the first piece of information to the past: intense volcanic activity in the Earth's first billion years would have released mainly carbon dioxide and water vapour, with some nitrogen.","As the Earth cooled the water vapour condensed to form the oceans, so what remained in the atmosphere was mainly carbon dioxide, with little or no oxygen and nitrogen building up gradually.","The second piece of information supports this picture, because two planets that have kept their early atmospheres are still mainly carbon dioxide with little or no oxygen gas.","The third piece of information explains the uncertainty: over such a timescale almost no direct evidence survives, so theories about the early atmosphere have changed and developed over time and cannot be tested directly."],"cues":["Which gases would intense volcanic activity have released into the early atmosphere?","What happened to the water vapour as the Earth cooled, and which gas was left behind?","How does the information about Mars and Venus support that idea?","Why can scientists not be certain, given the third piece of information?"]},{"title":"Explaining how four carbon-containing deposits formed","setup":"A student is given a list of four carbon-containing deposits found in the Earth's crust: limestone, coal, crude oil and natural gas. For each deposit the student must say what it formed from, and then explain what the formation of these deposits did to the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.","steps":["Limestone: carbon dioxide dissolved in the oceans and carbonates were precipitated, producing sediments, alongside the shells and skeletons of marine organisms; burial and compression turned that sediment into sedimentary rock.","Coal: thick layers of dead plant material were buried in the absence of oxygen, so they did not decay completely, and heat and pressure over millions of years turned them into coal.","Crude oil and natural gas: the remains of plankton, the tiny plants and animals of the sea, were buried in mud and, under high pressure and temperature over millions of years, formed crude oil and natural gas.","In every case the carbon came from carbon dioxide that had dissolved in the ocean or that algae and plants had taken from the air, so locking it into rock and fossil fuel reduced the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere."],"cues":["Which deposit forms from precipitated carbonates and shells on the sea bed, and what turns the sediment into rock?","Which deposit forms from plant material, and what stops that material decaying completely?","Whose remains form crude oil and natural gas, and what conditions are needed?","Where did the carbon in all four deposits come from, and what did burying it do to the air?"]},{"title":"Predicting the products of burning methane in a restricted air supply","setup":"A gas fire burns methane, CH₄, in a room where the flue has become blocked, so the supply of air to the flame is restricted and the flame burns yellow and smoky. Predict the products released, writing a balanced symbol equation for complete combustion and one for the incomplete combustion that produces carbon monoxide, and state why the blocked flue makes the fire dangerous.","steps":["With plenty of air methane burns completely: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O, giving carbon dioxide and water vapour, with 1 C, 4 H and 4 O atoms on each side.","With the air supply restricted there is not enough oxygen to oxidise every carbon atom fully, so carbon monoxide forms instead: 2CH₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO + 4H₂O, with 2 C, 8 H and 6 O atoms on each side.","If even less oxygen is available some carbon is not oxidised at all and is released as solid carbon, or soot: CH₄ + O₂ → C + 2H₂O; the soot and any unburned hydrocarbons form particulates, which is why the flame is yellow and smoky.","The blocked flue is dangerous because carbon monoxide is toxic and is colourless and odourless, so the people in the room cannot detect it and may be poisoned without warning."],"cues":["What are the products when methane has plenty of oxygen, and what is the balanced equation?","If there is not enough oxygen for every carbon atom to reach carbon dioxide, what forms instead?","What is released when some of the carbon is not oxidised at all, and what does it explain about the flame?","Why does the gas made by the restricted supply put the people in the room at risk?"]},{"title":"Explaining the greenhouse effect from a diagram","setup":"A diagram shows an arrow of radiation coming from the Sun to the Earth's surface labelled 'short wavelength radiation', and arrows leaving the surface labelled 'long wavelength radiation'. Some of the outgoing arrows are shown meeting molecules of carbon dioxide, methane and water vapour and turning back towards the ground. Use the diagram to explain how greenhouse gases maintain temperatures on Earth high enough to support life.","steps":["Radiation of short wavelength travels from the Sun through the atmosphere and is absorbed by the ground, which warms up.","The warmed ground radiates energy back out, but at a longer wavelength than the radiation that arrived.","Molecules of carbon dioxide, methane and water vapour absorb that long wavelength radiation instead of letting it escape to space.","They re-emit the energy in all directions, so some of it returns to the ground and warms it further, keeping temperatures on Earth high enough to support life."],"cues":["What happens to the short wavelength radiation when it reaches the ground?","How does the radiation leaving the ground differ from the radiation that arrived?","What do the greenhouse gas molecules do to that outgoing radiation?","Where does the energy go once the molecules re-emit it, and what is the effect on the ground?"]},{"title":"Calculating the reduction in a carbon footprint","setup":"A school works out that its carbon footprint is 120 000 kg of carbon dioxide equivalent each year. Of this, 45 000 kg comes from heating the buildings with a gas boiler. Replacing the boiler with an electric heat pump run on renewable electricity would cut the heating emissions by 60 %. Calculate the school's new annual carbon footprint and the percentage by which its total footprint would fall, then give one reason why the school may not be able to make the change.","steps":["Work out the saving on heating: 60 % of 45 000 kg = 0.6 × 45 000 = 27 000 kg of carbon dioxide equivalent each year.","Take the saving off the total: 120 000 kg − 27 000 kg = 93 000 kg, the school's new annual carbon footprint.","Write the saving as a percentage of the original total: (27 000 ÷ 120 000) × 100 = 22.5 %.","Give a limitation: a heat pump is expensive to buy and install, so the school may not have the money for it, even though the change would cut its footprint by 22.5 %."],"cues":["How many kilograms of carbon dioxide equivalent does the heat pump save each year?","What is the new annual total once that saving is taken off?","What percentage of the original total does that saving represent?","Why might a school be unable to go ahead with a change like this?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What are the approximate proportions of nitrogen and of oxygen in today's atmosphere?","a":"About four-fifths (approximately 80 %) nitrogen and about one-fifth (approximately 20 %) oxygen, with small proportions of other gases."},{"q":"According to the one theory you are required to know, what was the Earth's early atmosphere mainly made of?","a":"Mainly carbon dioxide, with little or no oxygen gas."},{"q":"Which three gases does the specification name as greenhouse gases?","a":"Water vapour, carbon dioxide and methane."},{"q":"Why is carbon monoxide particularly dangerous?","a":"It is a toxic gas, and it is colourless and odourless, so it is not easily detected."},{"q":"What is the carbon footprint of a product?","a":"The total amount of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases emitted over its full life cycle."}]},"room":{"facts":["Chemistry of the atmosphere is examined on Paper 2, which covers topics 4.6 to 4.10: a written exam of 1 hour 45 minutes, worth 100 marks and 50 % of the GCSE.","The entry codes are 8462F for Foundation Tier and 8462H for Higher Tier; topic 4.9 contains no (HT only) and no (chemistry only) content, so everything in this room is examined on both tiers.","Questions drawing on the knowledge and understanding gained from the eight required practical activities are worth at least 15 % of the marks for the qualification; topic 4.9 itself names no required practical.","For 200 million years the proportions of the gases in the atmosphere have been much the same as today: about four-fifths (approximately 80 %) nitrogen, about one-fifth (approximately 20 %) oxygen, and small proportions of other gases, including carbon dioxide, water vapour and noble gases.","Photosynthesis, the source of the oxygen in the atmosphere: carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen, or 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂.","The early atmosphere: intense volcanic activity in the first billion years released the gases that formed it — mainly carbon dioxide with little or no oxygen, plus nitrogen that built up gradually and small proportions of methane and ammonia — and water vapour that condensed to form the oceans. Evidence is limited because of the timescale of 4.6 billion years.","Algae first produced oxygen about 2.7 billion years ago; over the next billion years plants evolved and the percentage of oxygen gradually increased to a level that enabled animals to evolve.","Carbon dioxide decreased by photosynthesis and by the formation of sedimentary rocks (limestone) and of the fossil fuels coal, crude oil and natural gas, which all contain carbon.","The greenhouse gases named in the specification are water vapour, carbon dioxide and methane; they maintain temperatures on Earth high enough to support life.","The carbon footprint is the total amount of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases emitted over the full life cycle of a product, service or event.","Pollutants from burning fuels: complete combustion of carbon and hydrogen gives carbon dioxide and water vapour; incomplete combustion gives carbon monoxide and carbon (soot); sulfur in the fuel gives sulfur dioxide, S + O₂ → SO₂; the high temperature makes nitrogen and oxygen from the air react, N₂ + O₂ → 2NO.","Carbon monoxide is toxic, colourless and odourless, so it is not easily detected; sulfur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen cause respiratory problems and acid rain; particulates cause global dimming and health problems for humans."],"questions":[{"q":"State the approximate percentages of nitrogen and of oxygen in today's atmosphere.","a":"About 80 % nitrogen and about 20 % oxygen.","sol":"Nitrogen makes up about four-fifths of the atmosphere, approximately 80 %. Oxygen makes up about one-fifth, approximately 20 %. The small proportions that remain are other gases, including carbon dioxide, water vapour and noble gases.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two gases make up almost all of the air; decide which they are before you think about any numbers.","The specification gives each of these proportions as a simple fraction of the whole atmosphere, so work each fraction out as a percentage.","Write one percentage for each of the two gases, to the nearest ten per cent."],"codes":["4.9.1.1"]},{"q":"Name two greenhouse gases other than carbon dioxide.","a":"Water vapour and methane.","sol":"The greenhouse gases named in the specification are water vapour, carbon dioxide and methane, so the two other than carbon dioxide are water vapour and methane. Greenhouse gases in the atmosphere maintain temperatures on Earth high enough to support life.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A greenhouse gas is one whose molecules absorb the radiation leaving the Earth's surface; only a short list is named in this topic.","Rule out the two gases that make up almost all of the air: nitrogen and oxygen are not greenhouse gases.","Give two names only — each answer is a gas, not a source of a gas."],"codes":["4.9.2.1"]},{"q":"State what is meant by the carbon footprint of a product.","a":"The total amount of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases emitted over the full life cycle of the product.","sol":"The carbon footprint is the total amount of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases emitted, and it is measured over the full life cycle of the product — while it is being made and transported, while it is in use, and when it is disposed of — not only while it is being used.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A carbon footprint is a total of emissions, not a rate, a concentration or a temperature.","Decide which stages of a product's existence the total has to cover, from the raw materials to the end of its life.","Your definition needs two parts: what is added up, and the period over which it is added up."],"codes":["4.9.2.4"]},{"q":"Give two reasons why carbon monoxide is a particularly dangerous atmospheric pollutant.","a":"It is toxic, and it is colourless and odourless so it is not easily detected.","sol":"Carbon monoxide is a toxic gas, so breathing it in harms or kills. It is also colourless and odourless, so it is not easily detected: a person cannot see it or smell it and may be poisoned without any warning, for example from an appliance burning gas with a restricted air supply.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["One reason is about what the gas does to the body; the other is about whether a person knows it is there.","For the second reason, think about whether a person sitting in the room would have any way of knowing the gas was there.","Give one short statement for each of the two reasons."],"codes":["4.9.3.2"]},{"q":"A 250 cm³ sample of dry air is passed repeatedly over heated copper until the volume stops falling. The copper reacts with all of the oxygen in the sample. The final volume of gas is 200 cm³. Calculate the percentage of oxygen in the air sample.","a":"20 %","sol":"Volume of oxygen removed = 250 cm³ − 200 cm³ = 50 cm³. Percentage of oxygen = (volume of oxygen ÷ total volume of the sample) × 100 = (50 cm³ ÷ 250 cm³) × 100 = 20 %. This agrees with the approximately 20 % oxygen expected in today's atmosphere.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The copper removes only one of the gases, so the fall in volume belongs to that gas alone.","Find the volume that has disappeared, then write it as a fraction of the volume you started with.","Multiply that fraction by 100 and give the answer as a percentage."],"codes":["4.9.1.1"]},{"q":"Describe the theory of how the Earth's early atmosphere was formed, and give one reason why scientists cannot be certain about it.","a":"Intense volcanic activity in the first billion years released the gases that formed the atmosphere, mainly carbon dioxide with little or no oxygen, and water vapour that condensed to form the oceans; the evidence is limited because of the timescale of 4.6 billion years.","sol":"During the first billion years of the Earth's existence there was intense volcanic activity, which released the gases that formed the early atmosphere. The atmosphere was then mainly carbon dioxide with little or no oxygen gas, so the Earth may have been like Mars and Venus are today. The volcanoes also released water vapour, which condensed as the Earth cooled to form the oceans, and nitrogen, which gradually built up in the atmosphere; there may also have been small proportions of methane and ammonia. Scientists cannot be certain because the evidence for the early atmosphere is limited by the timescale of 4.6 billion years, which is why theories about it have changed and developed over time.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what was happening to the Earth's crust during its first billion years.","Sort the material released into two groups: what stayed in the atmosphere as a gas, and what did not stay a gas once the Earth cooled.","Write three sentences on how the atmosphere formed and what it contained, then one sentence on the limits of the evidence."],"codes":["4.9.1.2"]},{"q":"Explain, using the equation for photosynthesis, how the percentage of oxygen in the atmosphere increased.","a":"Algae and then plants photosynthesised — 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ — and oxygen is a product, so it was released into the air and the percentage gradually increased.","sol":"Algae first produced oxygen about 2.7 billion years ago by photosynthesis: carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen, or 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂. Oxygen appears on the products side of the equation, so it was released into the atmosphere, and oxygen appeared in the atmosphere soon after algae evolved. Over the next billion years plants evolved and photosynthesised as well, so the percentage of oxygen gradually increased until it reached a level that enabled animals to evolve.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The oxygen in the air was made by living things, not released by the Earth itself.","Look at which side of the photosynthesis equation oxygen appears on, and say what that means for the air around the organism.","Put your answer in time order: the first organisms, then the equation, then what happened to the percentage over the next billion years."],"codes":["4.9.1.3"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which the percentage of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere decreased over geological time, naming the deposits in which the carbon was locked away.","a":"Photosynthesis by algae and plants removed it, and it was locked into sedimentary rock such as limestone and into the fossil fuels coal, crude oil and natural gas.","sol":"First, algae and plants decreased the percentage of carbon dioxide by photosynthesis, taking carbon dioxide out of the atmosphere and building the carbon into their own tissues. Second, carbon dioxide dissolved in the oceans and carbonates were precipitated, producing sediments that were buried and compressed into sedimentary rock, for example limestone, which also formed from the shells and skeletons of marine organisms. The buried remains of organisms also formed fossil fuels that contain carbon: coal from thick layers of dead plant material buried in the absence of oxygen, and crude oil and natural gas from the remains of plankton buried in mud. Carbon held in rock or in fossil fuel is no longer in the air, so the percentage of carbon dioxide fell.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["One route is biological and involves living organisms; the other buries the carbon in the ground.","For the second route, follow the carbon dioxide into the oceans and then into what settles on the sea bed, and follow the buried remains of organisms.","Name a rock for one route and the three fossil fuels for the other, then finish by saying what this does to the percentage in the air."],"codes":["4.9.1.4"]},{"q":"State two human activities that increase the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere and two that increase the amount of methane.","a":"Carbon dioxide: burning fossil fuels and deforestation. Methane: livestock farming and decomposing waste in landfill.","sol":"Carbon dioxide is increased by burning fossil fuels, for example in power stations, vehicles and home heating, and by deforestation, because felling trees means less carbon dioxide is removed from the air by photosynthesis and burning the wood releases more. Methane is increased by livestock farming, because cattle produce methane as they digest their food, and by waste decomposing in landfill sites; growing rice in flooded paddy fields is also accepted as a source of methane.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The answer wants everyday large-scale human activities, not chemical equations.","Take each gas in turn and ask which industries release that particular gas in the greatest quantity.","Write four short activities, each clearly labelled with the gas it increases."],"codes":["4.9.2.2"]},{"q":"A fuel oil contains carbon, hydrogen and a small proportion of sulfur. It is burned in a boiler in which the supply of air is limited. Predict the gases released into the atmosphere, and for each gas state where it comes from.","a":"Carbon dioxide and water vapour from the carbon and hydrogen burning, carbon monoxide from incomplete combustion of the carbon, sulfur dioxide from the sulfur, and oxides of nitrogen from the air.","sol":"Carbon dioxide is released, because some of the carbon in the oil is fully oxidised. Water vapour is released, because the hydrogen in the oil burns to water. Carbon monoxide is released, because the supply of air is limited so combustion is incomplete and some carbon is only partly oxidised. Sulfur dioxide is released, because the sulfur in the fuel burns: S + O₂ → SO₂. Oxides of nitrogen are released, because the high temperature inside the boiler makes nitrogen and oxygen from the air itself react: N₂ + O₂ → 2NO. Solid carbon (soot) and unburned hydrocarbons are also released, but as particulates rather than as gases.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Work through the fuel element by element, and then remember that the air itself takes part in the burning.","A limited supply of air means some of the carbon is only partly oxidised, so decide what that carbon becomes.","List one gas per line with a short reason for each, and include the gas that does not come from the fuel at all."],"codes":["4.9.3.1"]},{"q":"Describe four potential effects of global climate change.","a":"Rising sea levels, more frequent or severe extreme weather, changes to the amount and distribution of rainfall, and changes to wildlife migration patterns and habitats.","sol":"Sea levels rise, because sea water expands as it warms and land ice melts, so low-lying land and coastal cities may be flooded. Extreme weather events such as storms, floods and heatwaves may become more frequent or more severe. The amount, timing and distribution of rainfall change, so some regions receive too much water and others too little to grow food. The migration patterns and habitats of wildlife change, so species move to new areas and some may not survive.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A warmer planet changes more than the temperature; think broadly about which different parts of the natural world are affected.","For each part you choose, say what actually changes rather than just naming the part.","Give four separate effects, each in one short sentence."],"codes":["4.9.2.3"]},{"q":"Describe and explain the problems caused by increased amounts of sulfur dioxide, oxides of nitrogen and particulates in the air.","a":"Sulfur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen cause respiratory problems and acid rain; particulates cause global dimming and health problems in humans.","sol":"Sulfur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen cause respiratory problems in humans, because they irritate the lungs and airways. They also cause acid rain, because they dissolve in water in the atmosphere to form acidic solutions that fall as rain, damaging trees, making lakes and rivers acidic enough to kill fish, and corroding limestone buildings and metal structures. Particulates, which are solid particles such as soot together with unburned hydrocarbons, cause global dimming, because they reflect and absorb sunlight so that less of it reaches the Earth's surface. Particulates also cause health problems for humans, because they are small enough to be breathed deep into the lungs and damage them.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Deal with the two gases first and the solid particles second.","Each of these pollutants harms people in one way and harms something outside the body in another, so look for both kinds of problem.","Give two problems for the gases and two for the particulates, with a reason for each."],"codes":["4.9.3.2"]},{"q":"Methane and carbon dioxide are both greenhouse gases. Explain why increasing the amounts of these gases in the atmosphere is expected to increase the temperature of the Earth's atmosphere at the surface.","a":"Greenhouse gases absorb the long wavelength radiation the warmed surface emits and re-emit it in all directions, so more of these gases means more energy returned to the surface and a higher surface temperature.","sol":"Short wavelength radiation from the Sun passes through the atmosphere and is absorbed by the Earth's surface, which warms up. The warmed surface radiates energy back out at a longer wavelength. Molecules of greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide and methane absorb this long wavelength radiation and re-emit it in all directions, so some of it returns to the surface instead of escaping to space. If human activities increase the amounts of carbon dioxide and methane, more of the long wavelength radiation leaving the surface is absorbed and less escapes, so more energy is returned to the surface and the temperature at the surface is expected to rise. This is why, based on peer-reviewed evidence, many scientists believe human activities will cause global climate change.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Follow the energy: start with what arrives from the Sun and what the surface sends back out afterwards.","The two wavelengths behave differently in the atmosphere, so decide which one the greenhouse gas molecules can absorb and what they then do with it.","Finish by saying what changes when there are more of these molecules in the atmosphere."],"codes":["4.9.2.1","4.9.2.2"]},{"q":"A magazine article claims that global climate change is not caused by human activity. The article is written by a journalist and is based on temperature measurements from one weather station over five years, which have not been published in a scientific journal. Evaluate the reliability of this claim.","a":"It is unreliable: measurements from one station over five years are too small a sample to show a global trend, and the work has not been peer reviewed, unlike the wide evidence base behind the scientific view.","sol":"The claim is not reliable. The evidence base is far too small: measurements from a single weather station over five years cannot show a global trend, because global climate change is about the average global temperature over a long period, and a short run of data from one place is easily affected by local weather. The work has also not been peer reviewed, so no other scientists have checked the measurements, the method or the conclusion for mistakes or bias, and a journalist writing for a magazine may be presenting only part of the evidence or may be biased. By contrast, the view that human activities will increase the temperature of the Earth's atmosphere at the surface is based on peer-reviewed evidence gathered by many independent scientists. It is fair to add that modelling a system as complex as global climate is difficult, so the models are simplified and their predictions carry real uncertainty; but that uncertainty does not make one small unreviewed data set more trustworthy, so the claim should not be accepted.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what a claim about global climate would have to be based on before it could be tested at all — how much of the planet, and how long a period.","Compare the amount of data and the amount of checking behind this article with the amount behind the view held by most scientists.","Reach a clear judgement at the end rather than stopping at a list of weaknesses, and be fair to the side you are rejecting as well as the side you accept."],"codes":["4.9.2.2"]},{"q":"Explain why the atmosphere today contains a much smaller proportion of carbon dioxide than the Earth's early atmosphere did.","a":"Carbon dioxide dissolved in the new oceans and was precipitated as carbonates that became sedimentary rock, photosynthesis by algae and plants removed more, and carbon was buried as coal, crude oil and natural gas, so little of the original carbon dioxide is left in the air.","sol":"The early atmosphere was mainly carbon dioxide, released by intense volcanic activity during the Earth's first billion years, with little or no oxygen. Volcanoes also released water vapour, which condensed as the Earth cooled to form the oceans. Carbon dioxide then dissolved in the ocean water, and carbonates were precipitated, producing sediments; buried and compressed, these sediments became sedimentary rock such as limestone, which also formed from the shells and skeletons of marine organisms, so the carbon was locked out of the atmosphere. Once algae evolved about 2.7 billion years ago, and plants after them, photosynthesis removed further carbon dioxide from the atmosphere and converted the carbon into the tissues of the organisms. Some of those remains were buried: thick layers of plant material became coal, and plankton buried in mud became crude oil and natural gas, storing still more carbon underground. Because all three routes moved carbon out of the air and into rock or fossil fuel, and because carbon dioxide is not replaced as fast as it was removed, only a small proportion of carbon dioxide remains in today's atmosphere.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Follow the carbon: where it started, and every place it ended up.","Three separate routes remove it — one needs the oceans to form, one needs living organisms, and one needs burial over millions of years.","Work in time order from the early atmosphere to today, and finish with the small proportion of carbon dioxide in the air now."],"codes":["4.9.1.2","4.9.1.4"]},{"q":"A bus company runs its fleet on diesel. Suggest two actions the company could take to reduce the carbon footprint of its buses, and explain why such actions may be limited.","a":"Replace the diesel buses with electric buses charged on renewable electricity, and cut fuel use through better maintenance, route planning and driver training; the actions are limited by the cost of the new vehicles and by charging technology and infrastructure not being available everywhere.","sol":"Actions: the company could replace its diesel buses with electric buses charged using electricity from renewable sources, so that no carbon dioxide is released while the buses are running; and it could reduce the fuel each bus burns by maintaining the engines well, planning shorter routes and training drivers to drive more economically, so less carbon dioxide is released per journey. Limitations: new low-carbon vehicles are expensive to buy, so a company may not be able to afford them; the charging infrastructure may not exist along the routes, or the range of the buses may not be enough for a day's service; and because a carbon footprint covers the full life cycle, making the new buses and generating the electricity still cause emissions, so the footprint is reduced rather than removed.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A carbon footprint falls when less carbon dioxide and methane are emitted over the whole life cycle, so look for ways to burn less fuel or none at all.","Give one action that changes what the buses run on and one that changes how much fuel they use for the same work.","For the limitations, think about what the company would have to pay for and what would have to exist before the change could work, giving one sentence for each."],"codes":["4.9.2.4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What percentage of today's atmosphere is nitrogen?","a":"About 80 % (four-fifths)."},{"q":"Which gas made up most of the Earth's early atmosphere?","a":"Carbon dioxide."},{"q":"How long ago did algae first produce oxygen?","a":"About 2.7 billion years ago."},{"q":"Which pollutant gas from burning fuels is toxic, colourless and odourless?","a":"Carbon monoxide."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Today's atmosphere: the proportions of the gases","back":"About four-fifths (approximately 80 %) nitrogen, about one-fifth (approximately 20 %) oxygen, and small proportions of other gases, including carbon dioxide, water vapour and noble gases.","code":"4.9.1.1","open":false},{"front":"How long the proportions of the gases in the atmosphere have been much the same as today","back":"200 million years.","code":"4.9.1.1","open":false},{"front":"The Earth's early atmosphere (the one theory required)","back":"Intense volcanic activity in the first billion years released gases that formed the atmosphere: mainly carbon dioxide with little or no oxygen, like Mars and Venus today. Water vapour condensed to form the oceans.","code":"4.9.1.2","open":false},{"front":"Why the evidence about the Earth's early atmosphere is limited","back":"The timescale is 4.6 billion years, so very little evidence survives; theories about the early atmosphere have changed and developed over time.","code":"4.9.1.2","open":false},{"front":"The photosynthesis equation","back":"Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen; in symbols 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂.","code":"4.9.1.3","open":false},{"front":"When algae first produced oxygen","back":"About 2.7 billion years ago; oxygen appeared in the atmosphere soon after, and over the next billion years plants evolved and the percentage of oxygen gradually increased.","code":"4.9.1.3","open":false},{"front":"How the percentage of carbon dioxide decreased","back":"Photosynthesis by algae and plants removed it; carbon dioxide also dissolved in the oceans, and carbon was locked into sedimentary rocks such as limestone and into the fossil fuels coal, crude oil and natural gas.","code":"4.9.1.4","open":false},{"front":"The greenhouse gases named in the specification","back":"Water vapour, carbon dioxide and methane. They maintain temperatures on Earth high enough to support life.","code":"4.9.2.1","open":false},{"front":"The greenhouse effect","back":"Short wavelength radiation passes through the atmosphere and warms the surface; the surface radiates long wavelength radiation, which greenhouse gas molecules absorb and re-emit in all directions, so some returns and warms the surface further.","code":"4.9.2.1","open":false},{"front":"Human activities that increase carbon dioxide","back":"Burning fossil fuels for energy and transport; deforestation, which leaves fewer trees to photosynthesise.","code":"4.9.2.2","open":true},{"front":"Human activities that increase methane","back":"Livestock farming, as cattle digest their food; decomposing waste in landfill sites; growing rice in flooded paddy fields.","code":"4.9.2.2","open":true},{"front":"Potential effects of global climate change","back":"Sea levels rise, flooding low-lying land; extreme weather becomes more frequent or severe; the amount and distribution of rainfall changes.","code":"4.9.2.3","open":true},{"front":"Carbon footprint","back":"The total amount of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases emitted over the full life cycle of a product, service or event.","code":"4.9.2.4","open":false},{"front":"Products of complete and of incomplete combustion","back":"Complete: carbon dioxide and water vapour. Incomplete (limited air): carbon monoxide and solid carbon (soot), which with unburned hydrocarbons forms particulates.","code":"4.9.3.1","open":false},{"front":"Effects of the main atmospheric pollutants","back":"Carbon monoxide: toxic, colourless and odourless, so not easily detected. Sulfur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen: respiratory problems and acid rain. Particulates: global dimming and health problems.","code":"4.9.3.2","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Key equations and figures for Chemistry of the atmosphere","items":["Photosynthesis, the equation the specification uses for how oxygen entered the atmosphere: carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen; in symbols 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, with light absorbed by chlorophyll. Check the balance: 6 C, 12 H and 18 O atoms on each side.","Complete combustion of a fuel containing carbon and hydrogen: fuel + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water. Worked examples: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O; C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O; and for a liquid fuel, 2C₈H₁₈ + 25O₂ → 16CO₂ + 18H₂O.","Incomplete combustion, when the supply of air is limited: 2CH₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO + 4H₂O releases carbon monoxide, and CH₄ + O₂ → C + 2H₂O releases solid carbon (soot). Carbon itself burns as C + O₂ → CO₂ in plenty of oxygen and as 2C + O₂ → 2CO in a limited supply.","Sulfur impurity in a fuel burning: S + O₂ → SO₂. This is the source of the sulfur dioxide released when coal or fuel oil is burned.","Oxides of nitrogen, formed from the air itself at the high temperature of combustion, not from the fuel: N₂ + O₂ → 2NO, and the nitrogen monoxide is then oxidised further in the air, 2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂.","Proportion of a gas in a sample of air (MS 1c): percentage = (volume of the gas ÷ total volume of the sample) × 100. A nitrogen to oxygen ratio of 4 : 1 means 4 parts out of 5, which is four-fifths, or 80 %.","Figures to recall exactly as the specification gives them: 200 million years (how long the proportions have been much the same as today); about four-fifths, approximately 80 %, nitrogen and about one-fifth, approximately 20 %, oxygen; 4.6 billion years (the timescale that limits the evidence for the early atmosphere); about 2.7 billion years ago (algae first produced oxygen); the first billion years (intense volcanic activity) and the next billion years (plants evolving and oxygen increasing).","What is supplied and what must be recalled: the only insert provided with either Chemistry paper is the periodic table (Appendix A). None of the equations or figures above is given to you — the photosynthesis equation, the combustion equations and the proportions of the gases must all be recalled."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: the evidence for human-caused climate change and the risks it brings","items":["Question (6 marks): some newspapers report that scientists disagree about whether human activity is causing global climate change. Explain why most scientists conclude that human activities are increasing the temperature of the Earth's atmosphere at the surface, why reports in the media may give a different impression, and discuss the risks that global climate change brings.","Level 3 (5–6 marks) model answer: human activities are increasing the amounts of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere — burning fossil fuels for energy and transport and clearing forests increase carbon dioxide, while livestock farming, decomposing waste in landfill and flooded rice paddies increase methane. Greenhouse gases let the short wavelength radiation from the Sun pass through to warm the Earth's surface, but they absorb the long wavelength radiation the surface radiates back out and re-emit it in all directions, so more of these gases means more energy returned to the surface and a higher surface temperature. Most scientists accept this because the conclusion is based on peer-reviewed evidence: results are checked by other scientists for mistakes and bias before they are published, and the same conclusion comes from many independent sets of data. Media reports can give a different impression because global climate is an extremely complex system that is difficult to model, so the models used are simplified and their predictions carry real uncertainty; an article may quote only part of the evidence, may present speculation or opinion as though it were data, and may be biased by the interests of whoever wrote or paid for it. The risks are serious and global in scale: rising sea levels, from sea water expanding as it warms and from melting ice, threaten low-lying land and coastal cities; extreme weather events may become more frequent or more severe; the amount and distribution of rainfall may change so that some regions flood while others cannot grow enough food; and wildlife migration patterns and habitats change, so some species may not survive. Because the risk falls on the whole planet and the changes would be slow to reverse, the uncertainty in the models is a reason to act carefully rather than a reason to do nothing.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): the answer explains that human activities increase carbon dioxide or methane and gives some of the greenhouse mechanism, or describes two or three effects of climate change, but does not link the mechanism, the peer-reviewed evidence base and the risks into one developed argument, and treats the media point as a bare assertion that reports are biased.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): the answer gives isolated correct statements, such as naming a greenhouse gas or saying that sea levels will rise, without explaining how the gases warm the surface or why the evidence is trusted.","What a bottom-level answer misses: it does not describe the interaction of short and long wavelength radiation with the greenhouse gas molecules, does not say what peer review actually checks or why many independent data sets matter, does not identify the real uncertainty that comes from simplified models of a complex system, and does not discuss the scale of the risk — that the effects are global and hard to reverse — so it reads as a list of facts rather than a reasoned judgement."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: the pollutants released when a coal-fired power station burns coal","items":["Question (6 marks): a power station burns coal that contains carbon, some hydrogen and a small proportion of sulfur. The air supply to parts of the furnace is limited. Describe the pollutants released into the atmosphere, explain how each one is produced, and describe the problems they cause.","Level 3 (5–6 marks) model answer: where there is plenty of air, the carbon and hydrogen in the coal burn completely to carbon dioxide and water vapour; carbon dioxide is a greenhouse gas, so releasing more of it contributes to global climate change. Where the air supply is limited, combustion is incomplete: some of the carbon is only partly oxidised and is released as carbon monoxide, and some is not oxidised at all and leaves as solid carbon, or soot, which together with unburned hydrocarbons forms particulates. Carbon monoxide is a toxic gas, and because it is colourless and odourless it is not easily detected, so it can poison people without any warning. Particulates cause global dimming, because they reflect and absorb sunlight so that less of it reaches the Earth's surface, and they cause health problems in humans because they are small enough to be breathed deep into the lungs. The sulfur in the coal burns as well, S + O₂ → SO₂, releasing sulfur dioxide. In addition, the high temperature inside the furnace makes nitrogen and oxygen from the air itself react, N₂ + O₂ → 2NO, so oxides of nitrogen are released even though the fuel contains no nitrogen. Sulfur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen cause respiratory problems in humans because they irritate the airways, and they cause acid rain: they dissolve in water in the atmosphere to give acidic solutions that damage trees, make lakes and rivers acidic enough to kill fish, and corrode limestone buildings and metal structures.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): the answer names most of the pollutants and links some of them to their sources, for example sulfur to sulfur dioxide and a limited air supply to carbon monoxide, and gives some of the problems, but does not explain where the oxides of nitrogen come from, or gives the problems as a list without saying why each pollutant causes them.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): the answer names one or two pollutants and one problem, such as that burning coal causes acid rain, without explaining how the pollutant is formed or why it has that effect.","What a bottom-level answer misses: it does not distinguish complete from incomplete combustion, so it cannot explain carbon monoxide or soot; it does not realise that the oxides of nitrogen come from the nitrogen and oxygen of the air at high temperature rather than from the fuel; it gives no equations; and it does not pair each pollutant with the problem it causes — respiratory problems and acid rain for the two gases, global dimming and health problems for the particulates, toxicity and poor detectability for carbon monoxide."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T07:58:32Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"A clean, specification-tight room: all eleven symbol equations balance atom for atom, both worked-example calculations and the one calculation question re-solve exactly (80 %, 22.5 %, 20 %), every figure (200 million years, 4.6 billion years, 2.7 billion years, four-fifths/80 %, one-fifth/20 %) matches spec 4.9.1-4.9.3 word for word, room.facts states Paper 2 / 1 h 45 / 100 marks / 50 % / 8462F and 8462H correctly with no required practical and no HT-only or chemistry-only content in 4.9, no worked example's data is reused in a bank question, and every power is a Unicode superscript and every formula uses Unicode subscripts with no caret anywhere - publish it, with the open climate-change card the only thing a teacher might tidy."}}},"4.10":{"id":"AQA-8462","topic":"4.10","family":"science","lesson":{"why":"This room exists because 'Using resources' is examined on Paper 2 of GCSE Chemistry and is where the chemistry of the course is judged against the real world: how potable water is produced and tested in Required practical 8, how waste water is treated, how a life cycle assessment is read and criticised, and — as (chemistry only) content that Combined Science students never meet — how corrosion is prevented, what alloys, ceramics, polymers and composites are used for, and how ammonia and NPK fertilisers are manufactured. It mixes one-mark recall with data work, balanced equations, percentage and yield arithmetic and (HT only) equilibrium reasoning, and it carries several of the paper's six-mark evaluate questions.","idea":[{"code":"4.10.1.1","h":"Earth's resources and sustainable development","t":"Humans rely on the Earth's resources for warmth, shelter, food and transport, and natural resources such as timber and wild food are increasingly supplemented or replaced by agricultural and synthetic products. A resource is finite if it is used up faster than natural processes replace it (like crude oil and metal ores) and renewable if it is replenished on a human timescale (like timber from a managed forest). In exams you may be given a chart, graph or table of reserves and use rates and asked to classify a resource as finite or renewable, or to use orders of magnitude (comparing powers of ten) to judge how significant a quantity of resource use really is, and to say why chemistry supports sustainable development: meeting today's needs without compromising future generations' ability to meet theirs."},{"code":"4.10.1.2","h":"Potable water","t":"Potable water is water that is safe to drink because it has sufficiently low levels of dissolved salts and microbes; it is not pure water in the chemical sense, since dissolved substances remain. In the UK, most potable water is produced from fresh water collected in rivers, lakes and the ground: an appropriate source is chosen, the water is passed through filter beds to remove solid particles, then it is sterilised with chlorine, ozone or ultraviolet light to kill microbes. Where fresh water is scarce, salty water or sea water must be desalinated instead, by distillation or by a membrane process such as reverse osmosis; both require large amounts of energy, which is why exam answers must give a reason for each step, not just name it."},{"code":"4.10.1.2 (Required practical 8)","h":"Required practical 8: analysis and purification of water samples","t":"Required practical 8 analyses and purifies water samples from different sources, and it is the practical this room is examined on. You measure the pH of each sample with universal indicator or a pH meter (AT 3); you find the dissolved solids by weighing a clean, dry evaporating basin, evaporating a measured volume of the sample to dryness over a water bath or with gentle heating (AT 2), letting it cool and reweighing, so the increase in mass is the mass of the solids dissolved in that volume; and you purify a sample by distillation (AT 4), collecting the distillate and checking its boiling point, which is 100 °C for pure water. Exam questions ask you to describe these steps, to process the mass and volume readings into a mass of dissolved solids per dm³, to compare samples and to evaluate the method (WS 2.3 to 2.7) — for example why the basin must cool before it is reweighed, or why the sample taken has to be representative of the source."},{"code":"4.10.1.3","h":"Waste water treatment","t":"Urban living and industrial processes create large volumes of waste water that must be treated before release into rivers or the sea. Sewage and agricultural waste water need organic matter and harmful microbes removed; industrial waste water may also need harmful chemicals removed. Sewage treatment happens in a fixed order: screening and grit removal takes out large solids and grit; sedimentation lets solids settle to produce sewage sludge and a liquid effluent; the sludge is broken down by anaerobic digestion (without oxygen); and the effluent is cleaned by aerobic biological treatment (with oxygen). Exam questions often ask you to comment on the relative ease of obtaining potable water from waste, ground and salt water, and waste and ground water usually need less energy than desalinating salt water."},{"code":"4.10.1.4","h":"Alternative methods of extracting metals (HT only)","t":"(HT only) As high-grade copper ores run out, phytomining and bioleaching extract copper from low-grade ores without the digging, moving and disposal of large amounts of rock that traditional mining needs. In phytomining, plants absorb metal compounds from the ground as they grow; the plants are harvested and burned, leaving an ash that contains the metal compounds. In bioleaching, bacteria act on the ore to produce a leachate solution containing dissolved metal compounds. Either way, the metal compounds still have to be processed to obtain the metal: copper is obtained from solutions of copper compounds by displacement using scrap iron, Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu, because iron is the more reactive metal, or by electrolysis. Exam questions ask you to evaluate these biological methods against traditional mining, using data on energy, yield or environmental impact that you are given."},{"code":"4.10.2.1","h":"Life cycle assessment","t":"A life cycle assessment (LCA) evaluates the environmental impact of a product across four stages: extracting and processing raw materials; manufacturing and packaging; use and operation during its lifetime; and disposal at the end of its life, including transport and distribution at every stage. Quantities such as water use, energy use, resource use and some wastes can be measured fairly easily and compared numerically, but giving a number to pollution or harm is much harder and involves value judgements, so an LCA is never purely objective. A shortened or selective LCA can be used to give a quick comparison, but it can also be misused to support a pre-decided conclusion, such as an advertising claim. You should be able to interpret a given LCA, or carry out a simple comparative LCA, for products such as plastic and paper shopping bags."},{"code":"4.10.2.2","h":"Ways of reducing the use of resources","t":"Reducing use, reusing products and recycling materials by end users all cut the amount of limited resources and energy used, and reduce waste and environmental impact, because obtaining raw materials by quarrying and mining itself damages the environment. Some products, such as glass bottles, can be reused directly (washed and refilled); others are recycled — glass is crushed and melted to make new glass products, and metals are melted and recast or reformed into different products. How much separation and processing recycling needs depends on the material and on how pure the final product must be; for example, scrap steel can be mixed straight into iron from a blast furnace, cutting the amount of new iron ore that has to be extracted. Exam questions ask you to evaluate reduction strategies using data you are given, weighing benefits against costs."},{"code":"4.10.3.1","h":"Corrosion and its prevention (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Corrosion is the destruction of materials by chemical reactions with substances in the environment, and rusting is the example you must know: both air and water are necessary for iron to rust, and the rust formed is hydrated iron(III) oxide — iron + oxygen + water → hydrated iron(III) oxide. You must be able to describe and interpret the three-tube experiment: a nail in tap water open to the air rusts, a nail under boiled water sealed beneath a layer of oil does not, and a nail in dry air over calcium chloride does not, so each condition on its own is not enough. Corrosion is prevented either by a coating that acts as a barrier keeping air and water off the metal — greasing, painting or electroplating, while aluminium carries its own protective oxide coating — or by a reactive coating: zinc galvanising iron gives sacrificial protection, because zinc is the more reactive metal and corrodes in preference to the iron even where the coating is scratched."},{"code":"4.10.3.2","h":"Alloys as useful materials (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Most metals in everyday use are alloys, and you must recall a use of each alloy named in the specification. Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin, used for statues, ornaments and bells; brass is an alloy of copper and zinc, used for musical instruments, taps and door fittings. Gold used as jewellery is usually an alloy with silver, copper and zinc, and the proportion of gold is measured in carats, which are twenty-fourths: 24 carat is 100 % gold and 18 carat is 75 % gold, so carat questions are ratio and percentage arithmetic. Steels are alloys of iron containing specific amounts of carbon and other metals: high carbon steel is strong but brittle (cutting tools), low carbon steel is softer and more easily shaped (car bodies and nails), and stainless steels containing chromium and nickel are hard and resistant to corrosion (cutlery and chemical plant). Aluminium alloys are low density, which is why they are used for aircraft. You may also be given data on an alloy that is not named here and asked to interpret and evaluate its composition and uses."},{"code":"4.10.3.3","h":"Ceramics, polymers and composites (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Most of the glass we use is soda-lime glass, made by heating a mixture of sand, sodium carbonate and limestone; borosilicate glass, made from sand and boron trioxide, melts at higher temperatures than soda-lime glass. Clay ceramics, including pottery and bricks, are made by shaping wet clay and then heating it in a furnace. The properties of a polymer depend on the monomer it is made from and on the conditions under which it is made: low density (LD) and high density (HD) poly(ethene) are both produced from ethene, but under different reaction conditions: LD poly(ethene) is made at a high temperature and a very high pressure with a trace of oxygen as an initiator, while HD poly(ethene) is made at a lower temperature and a lower pressure with a catalyst, which is why their properties differ. Thermosoftening polymers are separate chains held together by weak intermolecular forces, so they melt when they are heated; thermosetting polymers have cross-links of strong covalent bonds between the chains, so they do not melt. Most composites are made of two materials, a matrix or binder surrounding and binding together fibres or fragments of the reinforcement — fibreglass, carbon fibre and reinforced concrete are examples. Given data you must compare the physical properties of glass and clay ceramics, polymers, composites and metals quantitatively, and select a material for a stated use."},{"code":"4.10.4.1","h":"The Haber process (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) The Haber process manufactures ammonia, which is used to make nitrogen-based fertilisers. The raw materials are nitrogen, obtained from the air, and hydrogen, obtained from natural gas (hydrocarbons). The purified gases are passed over a catalyst of iron at a high temperature of about 450 °C and a high pressure of about 200 atmospheres: nitrogen + hydrogen ⇌ ammonia, N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃. The reaction is reversible, so some of the ammonia produced breaks back down into nitrogen and hydrogen; on cooling the ammonia liquefies and is removed, and the remaining hydrogen and nitrogen are recycled. (HT only) You must apply dynamic equilibrium to these conditions and explain the trade-off between rate and yield: the forward reaction is exothermic, so a lower temperature would give a higher equilibrium yield but far too slowly, and about 450 °C is the compromise; the forward reaction turns four molecules of gas into two, so a higher pressure raises both the yield and the rate, but about 200 atmospheres is as high as the plant can safely and affordably be built for; the iron catalyst speeds the reaction up without changing the position of equilibrium; and you may be asked to interpret graphs of reaction conditions against rate or yield."},{"code":"4.10.4.2","h":"Production and uses of NPK fertilisers (chemistry only)","t":"(chemistry only) Compounds of nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium are used as fertilisers to improve agricultural productivity, and NPK fertilisers contain compounds of all three elements: they are formulations of various salts containing appropriate percentages of the elements, made industrially from a variety of raw materials in several integrated processes. Ammonia from the Haber process can be used to manufacture ammonium salts and nitric acid; neutralising ammonia with nitric acid gives ammonium nitrate, NH₃ + HNO₃ → NH₄NO₃, with sulfuric acid gives ammonium sulfate, 2NH₃ + H₂SO₄ → (NH₄)₂SO₄, and with phosphoric acid gives ammonium phosphate, 3NH₃ + H₃PO₄ → (NH₄)₃PO₄. Potassium chloride, potassium sulfate and phosphate rock are obtained by mining, but phosphate rock cannot be used directly as a fertiliser because it is insoluble, so it is treated with an acid to produce soluble salts: you must recall that nitric acid gives phosphoric acid and calcium nitrate, sulfuric acid gives single superphosphate (a mixture of calcium phosphate and calcium sulfate) and phosphoric acid gives triple superphosphate (calcium phosphate). Given information, you must compare this continuous industrial production with a laboratory preparation of the same salt by titration and crystallisation, which is a small-scale batch process."}],"examples":[{"title":"Finding the mass of dissolved solids in a water sample (Required practical 8)","setup":"In Required practical 8 a student tests water from three sources. For sample B she weighs a clean, dry evaporating basin and finds a mass of 46.85 g. She measures 50.0 cm³ of sample B into the basin, evaporates it to dryness over a water bath, lets the basin cool and reweighs it: basin plus residue now has a mass of 47.21 g. The pH of sample B, measured with a pH meter, is 6.5. Calculate the mass of dissolved solids in 1 dm³ of sample B and say what the results show about the sample.","steps":["The white solid left behind is the dissolved solids, so their mass is 47.21 − 46.85 = 0.36 g.","1 dm³ is 1000 cm³, and 1000 ÷ 50.0 = 20, so a full dm³ would leave 20 times that residue.","Mass of dissolved solids in 1 dm³ = 0.36 × 20 = 7.2 g.","The pH of 6.5 is close to neutral, so acidity is not the problem with this sample; it is the dissolved solids that are.","Distilling the sample would leave those 7.2 g of solids in the flask, and a distillate that boils at 100 °C shows the water collected is pure."],"cues":["Which two masses do you subtract, and what is the solid that is left?","How many 50.0 cm³ portions make up 1 dm³?","What mass of dissolved solids does a full dm³ of this sample contain?","Does the pH reading suggest anything else is wrong with this sample?","Which purification step removes those solids, and how would you check the water collected is pure?"]},{"title":"Interpreting an experiment on the conditions needed for rusting","setup":"A student sets up three sealed boiling tubes, each holding an identical clean iron nail. Tube A: the nail is half covered by tap water, with ordinary air above it. Tube B: the nail is covered by distilled water that has been boiled to drive out dissolved air, with a layer of oil floating on top. Tube C: the nail sits in dry air above granules of calcium chloride, a drying agent. All three tubes are left for one week at room temperature, after which only the nail in tube A has rusted. Explain what this experiment shows and give the word equation for the reaction in tube A.","steps":["The nail that rusted, in tube A, is the one that had both air and water, so air and water together are enough to rust iron; the other two tubes have to settle whether each of them on its own is needed.","Tube B gives the nail water but no air, because boiling drove the dissolved oxygen out and the oil layer stops more air dissolving, and this nail does not rust.","Tube C gives the nail air but no water, because the calcium chloride absorbs water vapour and keeps the air dry, and this nail does not rust.","Comparing the three tubes shows that both air and water are necessary for iron to rust, since removing either one stops it.","The reaction in tube A is iron + oxygen + water → hydrated iron(III) oxide, and this rusting is an example of corrosion: the destruction of a material by chemical reactions with substances in the environment."],"cues":["What does the single nail that did rust already establish, and what is left for the other two tubes to settle?","What has been removed from tube B, and by which two precautions?","What has been removed from tube C, and by what?","What does comparing the three results allow you to conclude?","What is the word equation for the change in tube A, and what is this type of reaction called?"]},{"title":"Working out the mass of gold in an 18 carat bracelet","setup":"A jeweller sells a bracelet made of 18 carat gold with a mass of 24.0 g; the rest of the alloy is silver, copper and zinc. Pure gold is 24 carat, that is 100 % gold. Calculate the mass of gold in the bracelet and the total mass of the other metals, and explain why jewellery is made from this alloy rather than from pure gold.","steps":["Carats are twenty-fourths, so 18 carat gold is 18 ÷ 24 = 0.75 of the mass, that is 75 % gold.","Mass of gold = 0.75 × 24.0 = 18.0 g.","Mass of the silver, copper and zinc together = 24.0 − 18.0 = 6.0 g.","Pure gold is very soft, so alloying it with silver, copper and zinc makes the bracelet harder and more hard-wearing, which is why most metals in everyday use are alloys."],"cues":["What fraction of the alloy is gold if 24 carat is pure gold?","What mass does that fraction of the bracelet come to?","What mass of metal is left once the gold is accounted for?","Why does the jeweller not simply use pure gold?"]},{"title":"Percentage yield of ammonia in the Haber process","setup":"In the Haber process purified nitrogen and hydrogen pass over an iron catalyst at about 450 °C and about 200 atmospheres: N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃. In one day a plant feeds in 280 tonnes of nitrogen with hydrogen in excess and collects 238 tonnes of liquid ammonia. Relative formula masses: N₂ = 28, NH₃ = 17. Calculate the percentage yield of ammonia.","steps":["Working in tonnes per relative formula mass, the nitrogen fed in is 280 ÷ 28 = 10 units of N₂.","The equation shows that 1 N₂ gives 2 NH₃, so 10 units of N₂ could give at most 20 units of NH₃.","Maximum theoretical mass of ammonia = 20 × 17 = 340 tonnes, against the 238 tonnes actually collected.","Percentage yield = 238 ÷ 340 × 100 = 70 %.","The yield is below 100 % because the reaction is reversible and does not go to completion, so the unreacted nitrogen and hydrogen are recycled through the reactor."],"cues":["How many relative formula masses of nitrogen, in tonnes, are fed in?","What does the 1 : 2 ratio in the equation make that worth in units of ammonia?","What mass of ammonia could that produce, and how does it compare with the mass collected?","How do you turn those two masses into a percentage yield?","Why can this reaction never give a 100 % yield in one pass?"]},{"title":"Making ammonium phosphate fertiliser, and its nitrogen content","setup":"A fertiliser works neutralises ammonia from the Haber process with phosphoric acid, H₃PO₄, to make ammonium phosphate for an NPK formulation. Write the balanced symbol equation for the reaction and calculate the percentage by mass of nitrogen in ammonium phosphate. Relative atomic masses: H = 1, N = 14, O = 16, P = 31.","steps":["Ammonia is the base and phosphoric acid the acid, so the salt is ammonium phosphate, (NH₄)₃PO₄: three NH₄⁺ ions balance one PO₄³⁻ ion.","Three ammonia molecules are therefore needed per acid molecule: 3NH₃ + H₃PO₄ → (NH₄)₃PO₄, which has 3 N, 12 H, 1 P and 4 O on each side.","Relative formula mass of (NH₄)₃PO₄ = 3 × (14 + 4 × 1) + 31 + 4 × 16 = 149.","Nitrogen in one formula unit = 3 × 14 = 42, so the percentage by mass of nitrogen = 42 ÷ 149 × 100 = 28.2 %."],"cues":["Which salt does this neutralisation make, and what is its formula?","How many ammonia molecules are needed to balance one phosphoric acid molecule?","What is the relative formula mass of the salt?","Which part of that mass is nitrogen, and what percentage is it?"]},{"title":"Comparing a plastic and a paper shopping bag with a life cycle assessment","setup":"A supermarket is given these figures. Plastic bag: made from crude oil, 1.5 MJ of energy to manufacture, typically used 4 times, takes decades to break down in landfill. Paper bag: made from wood pulp from a managed forest, 5.4 MJ of energy to manufacture, typically used once, biodegrades within months and is easily recycled. Compare the two bags stage by stage, using energy per use, and explain why these figures cannot settle the question on their own.","steps":["Raw materials: crude oil is a finite resource, while wood pulp from a managed forest is renewable, so the paper bag is better at this stage.","Manufacture: the plastic bag takes less energy to make, 1.5 MJ against 5.4 MJ for the paper bag.","Use: energy per use is 1.5 ÷ 4 = 0.375 MJ for the plastic bag but 5.4 ÷ 1 = 5.4 MJ for the paper bag, so counting reuse widens the gap in the plastic bag's favour.","Disposal: the paper bag biodegrades within months and is easily recycled, while the plastic bag persists in landfill for decades.","No single stage decides it: energy and resource use can be measured, but putting a number on litter and on the harm plastic does in the environment needs value judgements, so an LCA is not purely objective and a selective one could be used to support an advertising claim."],"cues":["Which raw material is finite and which is renewable?","Which bag costs less energy to manufacture, and by how much?","How does counting the typical number of uses change that comparison?","What happens to each bag once it is thrown away?","Why can these figures alone not decide which bag is better for the environment?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What makes water 'potable' rather than pure?","a":"It is safe to drink, with sufficiently low levels of dissolved salts and microbes, but it still contains dissolved substances, unlike pure water."},{"q":"Put the four sewage treatment stages in order.","a":"Screening and grit removal, then sedimentation, then anaerobic digestion of the sludge, then aerobic biological treatment of the effluent."},{"q":"Which two substances must both be present for iron to rust, and which metal is used to galvanise iron?","a":"Air (oxygen) and water; zinc, which is more reactive than iron and so gives sacrificial protection."},{"q":"State the equation and the conditions used in the Haber process.","a":"N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, over an iron catalyst at about 450 °C and about 200 atmospheres."},{"q":"Name the products formed when phosphate rock is treated with nitric acid, with sulfuric acid and with phosphoric acid.","a":"Nitric acid: phosphoric acid and calcium nitrate. Sulfuric acid: single superphosphate, a mixture of calcium phosphate and calcium sulfate. Phosphoric acid: triple superphosphate, calcium phosphate."},{"q":"What are the four stages of a life cycle assessment?","a":"Extracting and processing raw materials; manufacturing and packaging; use and operation; disposal, with transport and distribution at each stage."}]},"room":{"facts":["Using resources is examined on Paper 2, which covers topics 4.6 to 4.10: a written exam of 1 hour 45 minutes, worth 100 marks and 50 % of the GCSE.","The entry code is 8462F for Foundation Tier and 8462H for Higher Tier; the (HT only) content in this room — phytomining and bioleaching, and the equilibrium reasoning behind the Haber process conditions — is examined on Higher Tier papers only.","Questions drawing on the knowledge and understanding gained from the eight required practical activities are worth at least 15 % of the marks for the qualification; this room carries Required practical 8, the analysis and purification of water samples.","Sections 4.10.3 (corrosion, alloys, ceramics, polymers and composites) and 4.10.4 (the Haber process and NPK fertilisers) are marked (chemistry only): they are examined in GCSE Chemistry but not in GCSE Combined Science: Trilogy.","Potable water is safe to drink (low levels of dissolved salts and microbes) but is not pure water, since it still contains dissolved substances.","Producing potable water from fresh water: choose an appropriate source → pass through filter beds → sterilise (chlorine, ozone or ultraviolet light).","Where fresh water is limited, salty or sea water is desalinated by distillation or by a membrane process such as reverse osmosis; both use large amounts of energy.","Sewage treatment, in order: screening and grit removal → sedimentation (produces sewage sludge and effluent) → anaerobic digestion of the sludge → aerobic biological treatment of the effluent.","A life cycle assessment (LCA) considers four stages: extracting and processing raw materials; manufacturing and packaging; use and operation; disposal, including transport and distribution throughout.","Reducing, reusing and recycling materials all cut the use of limited resources, energy and waste, but an LCA is not purely objective because assigning numbers to pollution effects needs value judgements.","(HT only) Phytomining (plants absorb metal compounds, then are burned to leave an ash containing them) and bioleaching (bacteria produce a leachate solution containing metal compounds) extract copper from low-grade ores; the metal is then obtained by displacement with scrap iron, Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu, or by electrolysis.","Corrosion is the destruction of materials by chemical reactions with substances in the environment; both air and water are necessary for iron to rust: iron + oxygen + water → hydrated iron(III) oxide. Barrier coatings (greasing, painting, electroplating, aluminium's own oxide layer) or sacrificial protection with a more reactive metal, such as zinc galvanising iron, prevent it.","Bronze is copper and tin; brass is copper and zinc; jewellery gold is an alloy with silver, copper and zinc, measured in carats, 24 carat being 100 % gold and 18 carat 75 % gold. High carbon steel is strong but brittle, low carbon steel is softer and more easily shaped, stainless steels (chromium and nickel) are hard and corrosion-resistant, and aluminium alloys are low density.","Soda-lime glass is made by heating sand, sodium carbonate and limestone; borosilicate glass, from sand and boron trioxide, melts at higher temperatures; clay ceramics are shaped from wet clay and heated in a furnace; thermosoftening polymers melt when heated and thermosetting polymers do not; a composite is a reinforcement of fibres or fragments held in a matrix or binder.","The Haber process: N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, with nitrogen from the air and hydrogen from natural gas, passed over an iron catalyst at about 450 °C and about 200 atmospheres; on cooling the ammonia liquefies and is removed, and the unreacted gases are recycled.","NPK fertilisers are formulations of salts containing appropriate percentages of nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium. Ammonia makes ammonium salts and nitric acid; phosphate rock is mined but must be treated with nitric acid (giving phosphoric acid and calcium nitrate), sulfuric acid (single superphosphate) or phosphoric acid (triple superphosphate)."],"questions":[{"q":"State what is meant by potable water.","a":"Water that is safe to drink.","sol":"Potable water is water that is safe to drink, because it has sufficiently low levels of dissolved salts and microbes. It is not pure water in the chemical sense, since it still contains some dissolved substances.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a person drinking the water needs from it, rather than what a chemist would call pure.","Recall the definition the specification gives for this term.","Your answer is a one-sentence definition."],"codes":["4.10.1.2"]},{"q":"Name the two metals in bronze and the two metals in brass.","a":"Bronze: copper and tin. Brass: copper and zinc.","sol":"Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin. Brass is an alloy of copper and zinc. One mark for each alloy correctly described, and both metals must be right for the mark.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Both of these alloys are built on the same base metal, which you need in each answer.","Recall which second metal is added to that base metal in each case.","Your answer is two pairs of metals, one pair for each alloy."],"codes":["4.10.3.2"]},{"q":"State the two substances that must both be present for iron to rust.","a":"Air (oxygen) and water.","sol":"Both air and water are necessary for iron to rust: one mark for air or oxygen, one mark for water. Rusting is an example of corrosion, the destruction of materials by chemical reactions with substances in the environment.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about where iron objects rust fastest and where they stay bright.","Recall the two conditions the three-tube experiment is designed to remove one at a time.","Your answer names two substances, one mark each."],"codes":["4.10.3.1"]},{"q":"State, in order, the four stages included in a life cycle assessment (LCA) of a product.","a":"Raw materials; manufacture and packaging; use; disposal.","sol":"An LCA considers: (1) extracting and processing raw materials, (2) manufacturing and packaging, (3) use and operation during the product's lifetime, and (4) disposal at the end of its useful life, including transport and distribution at each stage.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the life of a product, from before it is made to after it is thrown away.","Recall the four named stages from the LCA content, in the order a product actually goes through them.","Your answer lists four stages in order, one mark each."],"codes":["4.10.2.1"]},{"q":"State the temperature, the pressure and the catalyst used in the Haber process.","a":"About 450 °C, about 200 atmospheres, and an iron catalyst.","sol":"The purified gases are passed over a catalyst of iron (1) at a high temperature of about 450 °C (1) and a high pressure of about 200 atmospheres (1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["These are the three conditions quoted for the industrial reactor, two of them numerical.","Recall the figure for the temperature, the figure for the pressure, and the metal used to speed the reaction up.","Your answer gives two values with their units and one named metal, one mark each."],"codes":["4.10.4.1"]},{"q":"Explain the difference between a finite resource and a renewable resource. Use crude oil and timber as examples.","a":"Crude oil is finite because it is not replaced as fast as it is used; timber from a managed forest is renewable because it is replanted and regrows within a human timescale.","sol":"A finite resource is not replenished as fast as it is used up, so it will eventually run out (1). Crude oil is finite because it takes millions of years to form, far longer than the time in which it is being used (1). A renewable resource is replaced by natural processes at least as fast as it is used, so timber from a managed forest, which is replanted and regrows within a few tens of years, is renewable (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about how quickly each resource is replaced compared with how quickly it is used.","Define each type of resource in terms of that comparison of timescales, then attach the right example to each definition.","Your answer is two linked sentences, one for each type of resource, each carrying its example."],"codes":["4.10.1.1"]},{"q":"Describe, in order, the main stages of sewage treatment.","a":"Screening and grit removal; sedimentation; anaerobic digestion of sludge; aerobic treatment of effluent.","sol":"Sewage treatment happens in this order: screening and grit removal takes out large solid objects and grit (1); sedimentation allows the remaining solids to settle, producing sewage sludge and effluent (1); the sewage sludge is broken down by anaerobic digestion (1); and the effluent undergoes aerobic biological treatment before release into the environment (1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["This is the fixed sequence covered under waste water treatment.","Work through what happens to the solids first, then what happens to each of the two products that settling produces.","Your answer lists four stages in the correct order, one mark each."],"codes":["4.10.1.3"]},{"q":"Explain how galvanising iron with zinc protects the iron from rusting, even where the zinc coating has been scratched.","a":"The zinc coating keeps out air and water, and because zinc is more reactive than iron the zinc corrodes in preference to the iron (sacrificial protection).","sol":"The layer of zinc first acts as a barrier, keeping air and water away from the iron surface (1). Zinc is more reactive than iron (1), so where the coating is scratched the zinc reacts with the air and water in preference to the iron, corroding instead of it; this is sacrificial protection, and the iron does not rust until all the zinc has gone (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A coating can protect a metal in two quite different ways, and zinc does both.","Compare the reactivity of the two metals to explain what happens once the coating is broken.","Your answer is two or three linked sentences, starting from what the intact coating does."],"codes":["4.10.3.1"]},{"q":"Explain the difference between thermosoftening and thermosetting polymers in terms of their structures.","a":"Thermosoftening polymers are separate chains held by weak intermolecular forces, so they melt when heated; thermosetting polymers have cross-links of strong covalent bonds between chains, so they do not melt.","sol":"A thermosoftening polymer consists of individual polymer chains held together only by weak intermolecular forces (1), which are overcome when the polymer is heated, so the chains can move past one another and the polymer melts (1). A thermosetting polymer has cross-links, strong covalent bonds joining the chains together, so the chains cannot separate and the polymer does not melt when it is heated (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The exam word here is structure: think about what holds one chain to the next in each type of polymer.","Say what heating does to the forces or bonds between the chains in each case, and what that allows the chains to do.","Your answer treats the two types in turn, each time naming what joins the chains before saying what heating does."],"codes":["4.10.3.3"]},{"q":"Describe how you would find the mass of dissolved solids in a sample of water, as in Required practical 8.","a":"Weigh a dry evaporating basin, add a measured volume of the sample, evaporate it to dryness, cool it and reweigh; the increase in mass is the mass of dissolved solids in that volume.","sol":"Weigh a clean, dry evaporating basin and record its mass (1). Measure a known volume of the water sample, for example 25.0 cm³, into the basin (1). Heat gently, using a water bath or an electric heater, until all the water has evaporated and the basin is dry (1). Allow the basin to cool and reweigh it; the increase in mass from the empty basin is the mass of dissolved solids in that volume of sample, which can be scaled to a mass per dm³ (1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The dissolved solids are the part of the sample that cannot evaporate, so they must be separated from the water that can.","Think what has to be measured before heating as well as after, and why the volume of sample taken must be known.","Your answer is a numbered sequence of four steps, ending with the comparison of two masses."],"codes":["4.10.1.2 (Required practical 8)"]},{"q":"Write balanced symbol equations for the reactions of ammonia with nitric acid and with sulfuric acid, and name the salt made in each case.","a":"NH₃ + HNO₃ → NH₄NO₃, ammonium nitrate; 2NH₃ + H₂SO₄ → (NH₄)₂SO₄, ammonium sulfate.","sol":"Ammonia with nitric acid: NH₃ + HNO₃ → NH₄NO₃ (1), and the salt is ammonium nitrate (1). Ammonia with sulfuric acid: 2NH₃ + H₂SO₄ → (NH₄)₂SO₄ (1), and the salt is ammonium sulfate (1). Each equation must balance: in the second, two ammonia molecules are needed because sulfuric acid provides two hydrogen ions, giving 2 N, 8 H, 1 S and 4 O on each side.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Ammonia is a base, so each of these is a neutralisation, and the name of each salt comes from the acid used.","Work out the formula of each salt from the ammonium ion and the acid's negative ion, then balance the ammonia.","The first equation starts NH₃ + HNO₃ → , and the salt in the second contains two ammonium ions for each sulfate ion."],"codes":["4.10.4.2"]},{"q":"A fertiliser plant passes 56 tonnes of nitrogen, with hydrogen in excess, through a Haber process reactor and collects 51 tonnes of ammonia. The equation is N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, and the relative formula masses are N₂ = 28 and NH₃ = 17. Calculate the percentage yield of ammonia.","a":"75 %","sol":"Number of relative formula masses of nitrogen, in tonnes: 56 ÷ 28 = 2 (1). The equation shows 1 N₂ gives 2 NH₃, so the maximum is 4 units of ammonia, a theoretical mass of 4 × 17 = 68 tonnes (1). Percentage yield = actual ÷ theoretical × 100 = 51 ÷ 68 × 100 = 75 % (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Percentage yield compares the mass actually collected with the largest mass the equation allows from the nitrogen supplied.","Divide the mass of nitrogen by its relative formula mass, use the 1 : 2 ratio in the equation to reach the theoretical mass of ammonia, which comes to 68 tonnes, and only then compare the two masses.","The first line of working is 56 ÷ 28 = 2 units of N₂."],"codes":["4.10.4.1"]},{"q":"A 9 carat gold chain has a mass of 12.0 g. Pure gold is 24 carat. Calculate the mass of gold in the chain.","a":"4.5 g","sol":"Carats are twenty-fourths of the mass, so 9 carat gold is 9 ÷ 24 = 0.375 gold (1), that is 37.5 % gold (1). Mass of gold = 0.375 × 12.0 = 4.5 g (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Carats measure the proportion of gold in the alloy against a fixed scale for pure gold.","Turn the carat figure into a fraction or a percentage of the total mass first, which comes to 37.5 %, then apply it to the mass of the chain.","The first line of working is 9 ÷ 24 = 0.375."],"codes":["4.10.3.2"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Copper is obtained from a leachate solution of copper(II) sulfate by adding scrap iron: Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu. Relative atomic masses: O = 16, S = 32, Fe = 56, Cu = 63.5. Calculate the atom economy of this reaction for making copper. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"29.5 %","sol":"Relative formula mass of CuSO₄ = 63.5 + 32 + (4 × 16) = 159.5 (1). Total relative formula mass of the reactants = 56 + 159.5 = 215.5 (1). Atom economy = relative formula mass of the desired product ÷ total relative formula mass of the reactants × 100 = 63.5 ÷ 215.5 × 100 (1) = 29.5 % (1). The rest of the mass leaves as iron(II) sulfate solution, which is why the leachate route still has to be judged against the energy and rock moved by traditional mining.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Atom economy asks what fraction of the mass fed into the reaction ends up in the product you actually want.","Work out the relative formula mass of the copper(II) sulfate, add the iron to get the reactant total of 215.5, and compare the mass of copper with it.","The first line of working is Mr(CuSO₄) = 63.5 + 32 + (4 × 16)."],"codes":["4.10.1.4"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Explain why the Haber process is operated at about 450 °C and about 200 atmospheres, rather than at a much lower temperature and a much higher pressure.","a":"450 °C is a compromise between a low temperature, which gives a high yield but a very slow reaction, and a high temperature, which is fast but gives a low yield; 200 atmospheres is as high a pressure as the plant can safely and economically be built and run for, even though a higher pressure would give more ammonia.","sol":"Level 3 (5–6 marks): the forward reaction is exothermic, so lowering the temperature would shift the equilibrium towards ammonia and raise the yield, but at a low temperature the rate of reaction is so slow that ammonia would be produced uneconomically slowly; about 450 °C is therefore a compromise that accepts a lower equilibrium yield in return for a workable rate, helped by the iron catalyst, which speeds up the reaction without changing the position of equilibrium. The forward reaction turns four molecules of gas into two, so increasing the pressure shifts the equilibrium towards ammonia and also increases the rate by pushing the molecules closer together; a much higher pressure would therefore give more ammonia, but the vessels and pipework needed to contain it safely, and the energy needed to compress the gas, become too expensive and too dangerous, so about 200 atmospheres is used. Unreacted nitrogen and hydrogen are recycled, so ammonia is still obtained economically overall. Level 2 (3–4 marks): links the temperature to the trade-off between rate and yield, or the pressure to the number of gas molecules, but not both, or does not say why the higher pressure is rejected. Level 1 (1–2 marks): isolated correct statements, such as 'a higher pressure gives more ammonia' or 'the catalyst speeds it up', with no explanation of the compromise.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The conditions chosen are never the ones that give the best yield on their own; two different demands are being balanced.","Take temperature and pressure in turn: for each, say which way the equilibrium moves, what happens to the rate, and what the drawback of the more extreme condition is.","Your answer is a levels-marked response dealing with temperature, then pressure, then the catalyst and the recycling, not a list of conditions."],"codes":["4.10.4.1"]},{"q":"A council is given these figures for supplying glass bottles: making a new bottle from raw materials uses 8.0 MJ per bottle; making a bottle from crushed recycled glass uses 5.2 MJ per bottle; collecting, washing and refilling a returned bottle uses 1.4 MJ per bottle. Evaluate these three options as ways of reducing the use of limited resources.","a":"Reuse saves the most energy (6.6 MJ per bottle, 82.5 %) and uses no new raw material, recycling saves 2.8 MJ (35 %) and cuts quarrying, and making new bottles is the worst option; but the figures ignore collection, transport, breakage and the sorting glass needs, so a judgement has to weigh those too.","sol":"Level 3 (5–6 marks): making a new bottle from raw materials is the worst option, because sand, sodium carbonate and limestone must be quarried, which damages the environment, and 8.0 MJ of energy, most of it from limited resources, is used for every bottle. Recycling saves 8.0 − 5.2 = 2.8 MJ per bottle, a saving of 35 %, and means far less raw material has to be quarried, although the glass still has to be collected, sorted (for example by colour) and melted. Reuse is the best of the three on these figures: washing and refilling a bottle uses only 1.4 MJ, a saving of 6.6 MJ or 82.5 % against a new bottle, and needs no new raw material at all. The figures are only part of an assessment, however: they say nothing about the energy used collecting and transporting bottles back to the plant, about bottles broken in handling, or about the environmental harm of quarrying, which cannot easily be given a number, so the ranking reuse, then recycling, then new bottles is a judgement rather than a purely objective result. Level 2 (3–4 marks): ranks the three options correctly and uses at least one of the energy differences, but does not compare them as percentages or does not question what the figures leave out. Level 1 (1–2 marks): states that reuse or recycling saves energy, with no use of the data and no evaluation.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Three options are being compared against the same alternative, so each one is worth judging by what it saves and by what it still costs.","Use the figures to work out both the energy saved per bottle and that saving as a percentage, then ask what a life cycle assessment would count that these three numbers do not.","Your answer is a levels-marked response that ranks the options with the data and then weighs what the data leave out."],"codes":["4.10.2.1","4.10.2.2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What does 'potable' mean?","a":"Safe to drink (though not chemically pure)."},{"q":"Which two substances must both be present for iron to rust?","a":"Air (oxygen) and water."},{"q":"What are the conditions used in the Haber process?","a":"An iron catalyst, about 450 °C and about 200 atmospheres."},{"q":"Which acid is used to treat phosphate rock to make single superphosphate?","a":"Sulfuric acid."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Potable water","code":"4.10.1.2","open":false,"back":"Water that is safe to drink because it has sufficiently low levels of dissolved salts and microbes; it is not chemically pure water, since it still contains dissolved substances."},{"front":"Producing potable water","code":"4.10.1.2","open":false,"back":"Fresh water: choose an appropriate source, pass through filter beds, sterilise with chlorine, ozone or ultraviolet light. Salty water: desalinate by distillation or reverse osmosis; both need large amounts of energy."},{"front":"What Required practical 8 measures in a water sample","code":"4.10.1.2 (Required practical 8)","open":false,"back":"pH, with universal indicator or a pH meter; dissolved solids, as the mass left when a measured volume is evaporated to dryness; and purity after distillation, shown by a distillate boiling at 100 °C."},{"front":"Sewage treatment order","code":"4.10.1.3","open":false,"back":"Screening and grit removal, then sedimentation (produces sewage sludge and effluent), then anaerobic digestion of the sludge, then aerobic biological treatment of the effluent."},{"front":"Finite resources, renewable resources and sustainable development","code":"4.10.1.1","open":false,"back":"Finite: not replaced as fast as it is used, so it runs out (crude oil, metal ores). Renewable: replaced at least as fast as used (timber). Sustainable development meets current needs without compromising future generations."},{"front":"Phytomining and bioleaching (HT only)","code":"4.10.1.4","open":false,"back":"Phytomining: plants absorb metal compounds, then are harvested and burned to give an ash containing them. Bioleaching: bacteria produce a leachate solution of metal compounds. Copper is then obtained by displacement with scrap iron or by electrolysis."},{"front":"Four stages of a life cycle assessment (LCA)","code":"4.10.2.1","open":false,"back":"Extracting and processing raw materials; manufacturing and packaging; use and operation; disposal, including transport and distribution at each stage."},{"front":"Reduce, reuse, recycle","code":"4.10.2.2","open":false,"back":"Reducing use, reusing products (washing and refilling a glass bottle) and recycling materials (crushing and melting glass into new glass products, or melting and recasting metals) all cut resource use, energy use and waste."},{"front":"Rusting and how corrosion is prevented (chemistry only)","code":"4.10.3.1","open":false,"back":"Both air and water are needed: iron + oxygen + water → hydrated iron(III) oxide. Prevent it with a barrier (greasing, painting, electroplating; aluminium's own oxide layer) or sacrificial protection by a more reactive metal, eg zinc galvanising iron."},{"front":"Bronze, brass and jewellery gold (chemistry only)","code":"4.10.3.2","open":false,"back":"Bronze: copper and tin. Brass: copper and zinc. Jewellery gold: an alloy with silver, copper and zinc, measured in carats — 24 carat is 100 % gold, 18 carat is 75 % gold."},{"front":"Steels and aluminium alloys (chemistry only)","code":"4.10.3.2","open":false,"back":"Steels are iron with set amounts of carbon and other metals: high carbon steel strong but brittle, low carbon steel softer and easily shaped, stainless steels (chromium and nickel) hard and corrosion-resistant. Aluminium alloys are low density."},{"front":"Soda-lime glass, borosilicate glass and clay ceramics (chemistry only)","code":"4.10.3.3","open":false,"back":"Soda-lime glass: heat sand, sodium carbonate and limestone. Borosilicate glass: sand and boron trioxide, melting at higher temperatures. Clay ceramics, including pottery and bricks: shape wet clay, then heat in a furnace."},{"front":"Thermosoftening and thermosetting polymers, and composites (chemistry only)","code":"4.10.3.3","open":false,"back":"Thermosoftening polymers melt when heated; thermosetting polymers, cross-linked between chains, do not. LD and HD poly(ethene) are both made from ethene under different conditions. A composite is reinforcement fibres or fragments held in a matrix or binder."},{"front":"The Haber process (chemistry only)","code":"4.10.4.1","open":false,"back":"N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃. Purified nitrogen (from the air) and hydrogen (from natural gas) pass over an iron catalyst at about 450 °C and about 200 atmospheres; cooling liquefies the ammonia, and unreacted gases are recycled."},{"front":"NPK fertilisers (chemistry only)","code":"4.10.4.2","open":false,"back":"Formulations of salts giving appropriate percentages of nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium. Ammonia makes ammonium salts and nitric acid; mined phosphate rock is insoluble, so it is treated with nitric or sulfuric acid to give soluble salts."}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Key equations, values and relationships for Using resources","items":["The Haber process: nitrogen + hydrogen ⇌ ammonia, N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃. Conditions: an iron catalyst, about 450 °C, about 200 atmospheres. Nitrogen comes from the air and hydrogen from natural gas. Ammonia is the only product, so the atom economy of the reaction is 100 %.","Rusting: iron + oxygen + water → hydrated iron(III) oxide. Rust is hydrated iron(III) oxide, Fe₂O₃·xH₂O; written for the monohydrate the balanced equation is 4Fe + 3O₂ + 2H₂O → 2Fe₂O₃·H₂O (4 Fe, 8 O and 4 H on each side). Only the word equation is required; both air and water must be present.","(HT only) Obtaining copper from a leachate solution with scrap iron: Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu, or copper sulfate solution + iron → iron sulfate solution + copper. Iron is the more reactive metal, so it displaces the copper.","Ammonium salts for fertilisers, all neutralisations of ammonia: NH₃ + HNO₃ → NH₄NO₃ (ammonium nitrate); 2NH₃ + H₂SO₄ → (NH₄)₂SO₄ (ammonium sulfate); 3NH₃ + H₃PO₄ → (NH₄)₃PO₄ (ammonium phosphate). Check each one by counting atoms: the second, for example, has 2 N, 8 H, 1 S and 4 O on each side.","Phosphate rock is mined but cannot be used directly as a fertiliser because it is insoluble, so it is treated with an acid to give soluble salts. 4.10.4.2 asks for the names of the products, not for equations, so the names are what must be recalled. With nitric acid: phosphoric acid and calcium nitrate. With sulfuric acid: single superphosphate, a mixture of calcium phosphate and calcium sulfate. With phosphoric acid: triple superphosphate, calcium phosphate.","Percentage yield = (mass of product actually obtained ÷ maximum theoretical mass of product) × 100, giving a percentage. The maximum theoretical mass comes from the balancing numbers in the equation and the relative formula masses.","Atom economy = (relative formula mass of the desired product ÷ sum of the relative formula masses of all the reactants) × 100, giving a percentage. For calcium oxide from the thermal decomposition of limestone, CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂, it is 56 ÷ 100 × 100 = 56.0 %.","Carats: proportion of gold = carats ÷ 24, so 24 carat = 100 % gold, 18 carat = 18 ÷ 24 = 75 % gold and 9 carat = 9 ÷ 24 = 37.5 % gold. Mass of gold = proportion × total mass, in grams.","Required practical 8 processing: mass of dissolved solids = (mass of basin + residue) − (mass of empty basin), in grams; mass per dm³ = that mass × (1000 ÷ volume of sample in cm³). Pure water boils at 100 °C, so the boiling point of the distillate is the purity check.","Orders of magnitude: round two quantities to the nearest power of ten and divide to see how big the difference between them is — a reserve of about 10¹¹ tonnes against a rate of use of about 10⁹ tonnes a year is a ratio of about 10², so the reserve would last of the order of a hundred years. That figure says how long the reserve will last; it does not decide whether the resource is finite or renewable, which turns on whether natural processes replace the resource as fast as it is used, so an oil reserve lasting centuries is still finite.","What is supplied and what must be recalled: none of these equations or relationships is printed on the paper, so all of them must be recalled. The only insert supplied with both Chemistry papers is the periodic table (Appendix A)."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical activity 8: analysis and purification of water samples from different sources, including pH, dissolved solids and distillation","items":["Aim: to analyse water samples from different sources (for example tap water, pond or river water, and sea water or a salt solution) by measuring their pH and the mass of dissolved solids they contain, and then to purify one sample by distillation and test the purity of the water collected.","Method step 1 (sampling): collect each sample in a clean container, taking it in a way that is representative of the source — for example stirring or taking from mid-depth rather than from the surface film — and label each sample.","Method step 2 (pH): pour a small, equal volume of each sample into a separate test tube or dimple tile, add a few drops of universal indicator and match the colour against the chart, or measure the pH with a pH meter or probe calibrated against buffer solutions; record each pH.","Method step 3 (dissolved solids, weighing): weigh a clean, dry evaporating basin on a balance and record its mass.","Method step 4 (dissolved solids, evaporating): measure the same fixed volume of the first sample (for example 25.0 cm³ with a measuring cylinder) into the basin and evaporate the water off by standing the basin on a water bath, or by heating it gently over a Bunsen burner with a tripod and gauze, until the basin is dry.","Method step 5 (dissolved solids, reweighing): let the basin cool fully to room temperature, reweigh it, and take the increase in mass as the mass of dissolved solids in that volume; repeat steps 3 to 5 for each of the other samples, using the same volume every time.","Method step 6 (distillation): set up distillation apparatus — a flask of the impure sample with anti-bumping granules, a thermometer with its bulb level with the side arm, a Liebig condenser with cold water flowing through the outer jacket, and a measuring cylinder or conical flask to collect the distillate — and heat the flask with an electric heater or water bath.","Method step 7 (purity check): record the temperature at which the liquid distils over, collect the distillate, and test it by measuring its pH and by evaporating a measured volume of it to dryness in a weighed basin as before; pure water distils at 100 °C and leaves no residue.","Independent variable: the source of the water sample (and, in the last stage, whether the water has been distilled or not).","Dependent variables: the pH of each sample, the mass of dissolved solids left from a fixed volume of each sample, and the distillation temperature and residue left by the distillate.","Control variables: the volume of sample used for every evaporation, the same balance, basin and apparatus, the same indicator or calibrated meter, and cooling each basin to the same room temperature before it is weighed.","Apparatus (the specification lists the AT, WS and MS skills for this practical rather than a fixed equipment list; these are the items standard classroom practice uses): universal indicator solution and colour chart or a pH meter, test tubes or dimple tile, measuring cylinder, evaporating basin, balance reading to at least 0.01 g, water bath or electric heater, Bunsen burner with tripod, gauze and heatproof mat, tongs, distillation flask with anti-bumping granules, thermometer, Liebig condenser with tubing, collecting vessel and eye protection.","Safety: wear eye protection throughout; hot evaporating basins and distillation flasks cause burns, so move them with tongs and let them cool on a heatproof mat; never heat a sealed apparatus, and keep the condenser outlet clear so pressure cannot build up; untreated pond, river or waste water may carry microbes, so do not taste any sample and wash your hands afterwards.","Likely errors: the sample spits or splashes out of the basin if it is heated too strongly, losing dissolved solids; weighing the basin before it has cooled gives a mass that is too low because of convection currents and evaporation of the last traces of water; judging the universal indicator colour by eye is subjective and only gives the pH to the nearest unit; reading the measuring cylinder off the meniscus at an angle gives a parallax error in the volume; and the distillate is contaminated if the sample boils so fast that droplets are carried over into the condenser.","Expected result and how it is analysed: tap water has a pH near 7 and leaves a small residue; pond or river water leaves rather more; sea water or salt solution leaves a large white residue. Convert each result to a mass per dm³ — for example a residue of 0.48 g from 25.0 cm³ is 0.48 × (1000 ÷ 25.0) = 19.2 g/dm³ — and rank the sources. The distillate from any of them boils at 100 °C and leaves no measurable residue, showing that distillation removes the dissolved solids while filtering and sterilising would not.","Apparatus and techniques assessed: AT 2 — safe use of appropriate heating devices and techniques including use of a Bunsen burner and a water bath or electric heater; AT 3 — use of appropriate apparatus and techniques for the measurement of pH in different situations; AT 4 — safe use of a range of equipment to purify and/or separate chemical mixtures including evaporation, distillation.","Key opportunities for skills development: WS 2.3 — apply a knowledge of a range of techniques, instruments, apparatus and materials to select those appropriate to the experiment; WS 2.4 — carry out experiments appropriately having due regard for the correct manipulation of apparatus, the accuracy of measurements and health and safety considerations; WS 2.5 — recognise when to apply a knowledge of sampling techniques to ensure any samples collected are representative; WS 2.6 — make and record observations and measurements using a range of apparatus and methods; WS 2.7 — evaluate methods and suggest possible improvements and further investigations."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: comparing potable water production from fresh water and by desalination","items":["Question (6 marks): Compare the methods used to produce potable water from a fresh water source with the methods used to produce potable water by desalinating salty water or sea water.","Level 3 (5–6 marks) model answer: When fresh water is available, potable water is produced by choosing an appropriate source (such as a reservoir, river or ground water) that already has low levels of dissolved substances, then passing it through filter beds to remove solid particles, and finally sterilising it with chlorine, ozone or ultraviolet light to kill microbes; this sequence uses relatively little energy because the starting water is already close to potable quality. Where fresh water is limited, salty water or sea water must instead be desalinated, either by distillation (evaporating the water and condensing the vapour, leaving the dissolved salts behind) or by a membrane process such as reverse osmosis (forcing water through a membrane that dissolved salts cannot pass through); both desalination methods require large amounts of energy because far more dissolved salt has to be removed. Overall, treating fresh water is quicker, cheaper and less energy-intensive than desalination, so desalination is generally only used where fresh water supplies are insufficient to meet demand.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): describes the fresh water treatment steps or a desalination method correctly, and states that desalination uses more energy, but does not explain why (for example does not link the amount of dissolved salt removed to the energy needed), or omits one of the two routes.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): makes only one or two isolated correct points, for example 'sea water needs desalinating' or 'filter beds remove solids', without comparing the two routes or explaining the energy difference between them.","What a bottom-level answer misses: it does not give the fresh water route in the correct order with a reason for each step, does not name both desalination methods (distillation and reverse osmosis), and does not explain why desalination needs more energy than treating fresh water."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: comparing industrial and laboratory production of ammonium sulfate (chemistry only)","items":["Question (6 marks): Ammonium sulfate is made industrially in a continuous process, by neutralising ammonia from the Haber process with sulfuric acid, and the same salt can be prepared in the laboratory from ammonia solution and dilute sulfuric acid by titration and crystallisation. Compare the industrial production of ammonium sulfate with the laboratory preparation of it.","Level 3 (5–6 marks) model answer: Both routes are the same neutralisation, ammonia + sulfuric acid → ammonium sulfate, but they are organised quite differently. Industrially the reaction is one of several integrated processes on the same site: the Haber process supplies the ammonia and a sulfuric acid plant the acid, and the reactants are fed in and the product drawn off all the time, so it is a continuous process that runs day and night on a scale of tonnes. It is largely automated, so few workers are needed, and although the plant is very expensive to build, the cost per tonne of fertiliser is low; the ammonium sulfate is then blended with potassium and phosphorus salts into an NPK formulation, so it does not have to be laboratory-pure. In the laboratory the same salt is made a few grams at a time as a batch process: a measured volume of dilute sulfuric acid is titrated against ammonia solution with an indicator to find exactly the volume that neutralises it, the titration is repeated with those volumes and no indicator so the product is not contaminated, the solution is then evaporated to the point of crystallisation and left to crystallise, and the crystals are filtered off and dried. Each batch has to be set up, watched and cleared away by a person, so it is slow and the cost per gram is far higher, but it gives a small, pure sample rather than bulk fertiliser. The comparison is therefore continuous, automated, large-scale and cheap per tonne against batch, hand-worked, small-scale and expensive per gram.","Level 2 (3–4 marks): describes one route properly — for example the titration and crystallisation steps, or the continuous industrial process — and states that industry works on a much larger scale, but does not compare the two on more than one point, or names the laboratory steps without saying that it is a batch process.","Level 1 (1–2 marks): isolated correct points, such as 'industry makes much more of it' or 'in the laboratory you use a titration', with no description of either method in order and no comparison.","What a bottom-level answer misses: it does not say that the industrial process is continuous while the laboratory preparation is a batch process, does not give the laboratory steps in order (titration with an indicator, repeat without it, evaporate to the point of crystallisation, filter and dry), and does not weigh scale, automation, labour, purity and cost against each other."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:14:03.221055Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file after the fix pass: all nine fixes verified and every fix is correct — the LD/HD poly(ethene) text now gives conditions only (high temperature and very high pressure with a trace of oxygen as initiator against a catalyst at lower temperature and pressure), the phosphate-rock equations are gone with AQA's product names kept, the atom-economy illustration is CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ at 56.0 %, the orders-of-magnitude item now separates 'how long a reserve lasts' from 'finite or renewable', worked example 4 derives ammonium phosphate (Mr 149, 42 ÷ 149 × 100 = 28.2 %), the new six-mark extra covers continuous versus batch production of ammonium sulfate (which 4.10.4.2 requires and nothing else covered), the practical specimen figure is 0.48 g from 25.0 cm³ = 19.2 g/dm³, example 1 step 1 now makes a move, and cards 8 to 14 carry '(chemistry only)'. Re-checked in Python: 7 equations balance atom by atom (Haber, Fe + CuSO₄, 4Fe + 3O₂ + 2H₂O → 2Fe₂O₃·H₂O, the three ammonium salts and CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂) and 11 calculations re-solve (75 %, 4.5 g, 29.5 %, 70 %, 28.2 %, 18.0/6.0 g, 7.2 g/dm³, 19.2 g/dm³, 56.0 %, 35 %/82.5 %, 0.375 MJ per use); no worked example's data now appears in any bank question; the practical sheet still matches 8.2.8 word for word on AT 2, 3 and 4 and WS 2.3 to 2.7; the paper facts (Paper 2, 1 h 45, 100 marks, 50 %, 8462F/8462H, 4.10.3 and 4.10.4 chemistry only, 4.10.1.4 and the Haber equilibrium reasoning HT only) match the specification; no caret, no ASCII subscript, card backs all within 40 words. Publish it: only the practical sheet's two different example volumes are worth tidying."}}},"10.1":{"id":"AQA-8462","topic":"10.1","family":"science","lesson":{"why":"This room covers AT 1 to AT 8, the eight apparatus and techniques statements that sit behind the eight required practical activities for GCSE Chemistry. Questions drawing on the knowledge and understanding gained from those eight required practicals count for at least 15 % of the overall marks, and apparatus and techniques are assessed on every paper, so a practical question can appear in Paper 1 or Paper 2 whatever the topic. A question can ask you to choose apparatus, read a scale, calculate a result from practical data, name the variables, identify a hazard, or evaluate a method you have never carried out, so you need to understand why each technique works and not only remember doing it. AT 1 to AT 7 are common with GCSE Combined Science: Trilogy; AT 8, using qualitative reagents and techniques to identify unknown samples and to determine the concentrations of strong acids and strong alkalis, is chemistry only, and so are required practicals 2 and 7.","idea":[{"code":"AT 1","h":"AT 1 – Making and recording measurements accurately","t":"AT 1 is choosing apparatus that will make and record a measurement accurately, covering mass, time, temperature, and the volume of liquids and of gases. Mass is measured on a balance, usually reading to 0.01 g or 0.1 g; time on a stopwatch; temperature on a thermometer or temperature probe; the volume of a liquid in a measuring cylinder, a pipette or a burette, always read at eye level from the bottom of the meniscus; and the volume of a gas in a gas syringe or an inverted measuring cylinder over water. The specification ties AT 1 to four required practicals: the volume of liquids in required practical 2 (a 25.0 cm³ pipette for the alkali and a burette, read to the nearest 0.05 cm³, for the acid), mass, temperature and volume of liquids in required practical 4, mass, time, temperature and volume of liquids and gases in required practical 5, and accurate measurement of distances in required practical 6. Exam questions ask you to choose apparatus with the resolution the measurement needs, to read a scale correctly and to use the reading in a calculation."},{"code":"AT 2","h":"AT 2 – Heating safely with a Bunsen burner, a water bath or an electric heater","t":"AT 2 is the safe use of heating devices: a Bunsen burner, and a water bath or electric heater. A Bunsen burner is lit with the air hole closed, giving a yellow, easily seen safety flame, and the air hole is opened for the hotter, roaring blue flame only while something is actually being heated; use a heatproof mat, a tripod and gauze, and tongs where needed. A water bath or electric heater heats more gently and evenly with no naked flame, which matters when a liquid is flammable or when heating too strongly would decompose the product. The specification assigns AT 2 to three required practicals: required practical 1, where a Bunsen burner warms the dilute acid and a water bath or electric heater evaporates the salt solution; required practical 7, where a Bunsen burner provides the flame for flame tests; and required practical 8, where a Bunsen burner or water bath evaporates the water sample and heats it for distillation."},{"code":"AT 3","h":"AT 3 – Conducting and monitoring reactions, and measuring pH","t":"AT 3 is choosing apparatus and techniques to carry out a reaction and to follow it while it happens, including measuring pH in different situations. The pH scale runs from 0 to 14: below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral and above 7 is alkaline. Universal indicator, as paper or solution, is matched against a colour chart and gives an approximate whole-number pH, while a pH probe gives a precise numerical reading and can follow pH continuously as an alkali is added to an acid. Monitoring can also mean watching for a colour change, a temperature change or bubbles of gas as evidence that a reaction is happening. The specification assigns AT 3 to five required practicals: required practical 1 (conducting the reaction with appropriate reagents), required practical 3, required practical 4 and required practical 5 (conducting and monitoring reactions), and required practical 8 (measurement of pH in different situations)."},{"code":"AT 4","h":"AT 4 – Purifying and separating mixtures","t":"AT 4 is the safe use of equipment to purify or separate mixtures by evaporation, filtration, crystallisation, chromatography and distillation. Filtration separates an insoluble solid, the residue caught in the filter paper, from a liquid, the filtrate that runs through. Evaporation drives the solvent off a solution; crystallisation stops at the saturation point and then lets the solution cool slowly so crystals grow, which protects a salt that would decompose or spit if it were evaporated to dryness. Paper chromatography separates soluble coloured substances between a stationary phase and a mobile phase, and each spot is identified by its Rf value, the distance moved by the substance divided by the distance moved by the solvent. Simple distillation separates a solvent from a solution by boiling point. The specification assigns AT 4 to required practical 1 (evaporation, filtration, crystallisation), required practical 6 (chromatography) and required practical 8 (evaporation, distillation)."},{"code":"AT 5","h":"AT 5 – Observations during reactions, and measuring rates","t":"AT 5 is making and recording the right observations while a reaction happens, including temperature changes and the measurement of rate by a variety of methods such as production of gas and colour change. A temperature change is the final, highest or lowest, temperature minus the starting temperature: a rise means the reaction is exothermic, a fall means it is endothermic, and that is required practical 4. Rate is followed either by collecting the gas given off in a gas syringe, by weighing the flask as gas escapes, or by timing how long a mark takes to disappear behind a cloudy, turbid mixture, and required practical 5 asks for both a gas-volume method and a colour or turbidity method. Mean rate of reaction = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken, or quantity of product formed ÷ time taken, in g/s or cm³/s, and (Higher tier) the gradient of a tangent drawn to a curved graph gives the rate at one instant."},{"code":"AT 6","h":"AT 6 – Safe handling of gases, liquids and solids","t":"AT 6 is the safe use and careful handling of gases, liquids and solids, including mixing reagents under controlled conditions and using apparatus that lets you explore the chemical change or its products. In practice this means eye protection whenever acids, alkalis or hot liquids are used, adding a solid to an acid a spatula measure at a time rather than all at once, stirring with a glass rod, keeping the total volume of liquid the same when a solution is diluted so that only the intended variable changes, and working in a fume cupboard when a toxic gas such as chlorine can escape. The specification assigns AT 6 to required practical 1, where the insoluble oxide or carbonate is added to the warm acid in small measures until it is in excess, and to required practicals 4 and 5, where measured volumes of solution are mixed under controlled conditions. Exam questions expect you to name a hazard from a described method and give a specific control, not simply to write be careful."},{"code":"AT 7","h":"AT 7 – Drawing, setting up and using electrochemical cells","t":"AT 7 is using appropriate apparatus and techniques to draw, set up and use electrochemical cells for the separation and production of elements and compounds, and the specification assigns it to one required practical only, required practical 3, the electrolysis of aqueous solutions using inert electrodes. The cell is two inert electrodes of graphite or platinum, which do not react themselves, dipping into the electrolyte in a beaker and connected by wires and crocodile clips to a d.c. power supply, with an inverted test tube over each electrode to collect any gas. You should be able to draw a labelled diagram of that cell, link the product at each electrode to the ions present in the solution, and identify each gas collected with the standard test. Required practical 3 must be an investigation involving developing a hypothesis, so you predict the products from the ions present before you test them."},{"code":"AT 8","h":"AT 8 – Qualitative reagents and techniques (chemistry only)","t":"AT 8 is chemistry only: the use of qualitative reagents and techniques to analyse and identify unknown samples or products, including gas tests, flame tests, precipitation reactions, and the determination of the concentrations of strong acids and strong alkalis. It is assigned to three required practicals. In required practical 2 it is the titration itself, where a strong acid and a strong alkali are run together with an indicator to find the reacting volumes, and (Higher tier) the concentration of one solution is calculated from the other. In required practical 3 it is the gas tests for hydrogen, oxygen and chlorine on the products of electrolysis. In required practical 7 it is the full set of tests on an unknown ionic compound: flame colours for lithium, sodium, potassium, calcium and copper; white or coloured precipitates with sodium hydroxide solution; carbon dioxide from a carbonate; silver nitrate for the halides; and barium chloride for sulfates."}],"examples":[{"title":"Rf value from a chromatogram (required practical 6)","setup":"In required practical 6 a student runs a paper chromatogram of a blue food colouring. Measured from the pencil baseline, the solvent front has moved 12.0 cm and the centre of the single blue spot has moved 4.8 cm. Calculate the Rf value of the blue dye and state what the single spot shows about the dye.","steps":["Rf value = distance moved by the substance ÷ distance moved by the solvent, both taken from the baseline.","Rf = 4.8 ÷ 12.0","Rf = 0.40, with no unit.","One spot in the solvent means the colouring is a single pure compound, not a mixture."],"cues":["Which relationship turns two measured distances into an Rf value?","Which distance is divided by which?","What is the Rf value, and what unit does it carry?","What does the number of spots tell you about the colouring?"]},{"title":"Mean rate of reaction from a gas volume (required practical 5)","setup":"In required practical 5 a student reacts marble chips with dilute hydrochloric acid in a conical flask sealed with a bung and delivery tube leading to a gas syringe. The syringe collects 36 cm³ of carbon dioxide before the reaction stops, and the reaction takes 90 s. Calculate the mean rate of reaction and give the unit.","steps":["Mean rate of reaction = quantity of product formed ÷ time taken.","Mean rate = 36 ÷ 90","Mean rate = 0.40 cm³/s."],"cues":["Which relationship gives a mean rate from an amount and a time?","Which two numbers go into it, and in which order?","What is the mean rate, and what unit comes from dividing cm³ by s?"]},{"title":"Temperature change and type of reaction (required practical 4)","setup":"In required practical 4 a student adds solid sodium hydrogencarbonate to 25 cm³ of dilute citric acid in an insulated polystyrene cup with a lid. The temperature of the acid before mixing is 21.4 °C and the lowest temperature reached after mixing is 13.9 °C. Calculate the temperature change and state whether the reaction is exothermic or endothermic.","steps":["Temperature change = final temperature − starting temperature.","Temperature change = 13.9 − 21.4","Temperature change = −7.5 °C, that is a fall of 7.5 °C.","The temperature of the surroundings falls, so energy is taken in from them and the reaction is endothermic."],"cues":["Which relationship gives a temperature change from two readings?","Which reading is subtracted from which?","What is the change, and does the sign mean a rise or a fall?","What does a falling temperature of the surroundings tell you about the reaction?"]},{"title":"Mean titre from concordant results (required practical 2, chemistry only)","setup":"In required practical 2 a student runs dilute hydrochloric acid from a burette into 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution with a few drops of indicator. The titres recorded are a rough titration of 26.20 cm³, then 25.45 cm³ and 25.55 cm³. Calculate the mean titre the student should use.","steps":["Leave the rough titration out: it is run quickly only to find the approximate end point.","Check the remaining titres are concordant, that is within 0.10 cm³ of each other: 25.55 − 25.45 = 0.10 cm³, so they are.","Mean titre = (25.45 + 25.55) ÷ 2","Mean titre = 25.50 cm³."],"cues":["Which of the three readings should not be used, and why?","What test decides whether the remaining titres may be averaged?","Which numbers are added, and what are they divided by?","What is the mean titre, to two decimal places, with its unit?"]},{"title":"(Higher tier) Concentration of an alkali from a titration (required practical 2, chemistry only)","setup":"(Higher tier) In required practical 2 a student titrates 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution of unknown concentration against hydrochloric acid of concentration 0.100 mol/dm³, and finds a mean titre of 20.00 cm³ of acid. The equation is HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O. The relative formula mass of NaOH is 40. Calculate the concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution in mol/dm³ and in g/dm³.","steps":["Change the acid volume from cm³ into dm³: 20.00 ÷ 1000 = 0.02000 dm³.","Moles of hydrochloric acid = concentration × volume = 0.100 × 0.02000 = 0.00200 mol.","The equation shows 1 mol of HCl reacts with 1 mol of NaOH, so there are also 0.00200 mol of sodium hydroxide, in 25.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.0250 dm³ of solution.","Concentration of sodium hydroxide = 0.00200 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.0800 mol/dm³, and its relative formula mass is 40.","Concentration in g/dm³ = 0.0800 × 40 = 3.2 g/dm³."],"cues":["The concentration is in mol/dm³, so what must happen to a volume measured in cm³?","Which quantity of the acid can now be worked out, and from which two numbers?","What does the 1 : 1 ratio in the equation let you say about the alkali?","Which two numbers are divided to give the concentration in mol/dm³?","What turns a concentration in mol/dm³ into one in g/dm³?"]},{"title":"Dissolved solids in a water sample (required practical 8)","setup":"In required practical 8 a student measures 50.0 cm³ of a water sample into an evaporating basin of mass 48.62 g, evaporates the sample to dryness over a water bath and reweighs the basin, which now has a mass of 48.77 g. Calculate the mass of dissolved solids in the sample and the concentration of dissolved solids in g/dm³.","steps":["Mass of dissolved solids = mass of basin and residue − mass of empty basin = 48.77 − 48.62 = 0.15 g.","Change the volume of the sample from cm³ into dm³: 50.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.0500 dm³.","Concentration of dissolved solids = mass ÷ volume = 0.15 ÷ 0.0500","Concentration of dissolved solids = 3.0 g/dm³."],"cues":["Which two masses give the mass of the solid left behind, and in which order?","The answer is wanted per dm³, so what must be done with the volume first?","Which two numbers are divided to give a concentration?","What is the concentration, with its unit?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the equation for an Rf value?","a":"Rf = distance moved by the substance ÷ distance moved by the solvent, both measured from the baseline. Rf has no unit."},{"q":"Which Bunsen burner flame is used when the burner is not heating anything, and how is it made?","a":"The yellow safety flame, made by closing the air hole; it is easy to see, so people are less likely to be burnt."},{"q":"Why is a salt solution evaporated only to the point of saturation and then left to cool, rather than evaporated to dryness?","a":"Strong heating to dryness can decompose the salt, drive off water of crystallisation or make the solution spit; cooling a saturated solution slowly grows proper crystals."},{"q":"Name the five metal ions the specification requires you to identify by flame test, and give each colour.","a":"Lithium crimson, sodium yellow, potassium lilac, calcium orange-red, copper green."},{"q":"Why must the electrodes used in required practical 3 be inert?","a":"So the electrodes do not react or take part themselves, and the products formed come only from the ions in the solution being electrolysed."}]},"room":{"facts":["Chemistry is assessed by two written papers, Paper 1 and Paper 2. Each is a written exam of 1 hour 45 minutes, worth 100 marks and worth 50 % of the GCSE; use of apparatus and techniques is assessed on every paper.","The entry codes are 8462F for Foundation Tier and 8462H for Higher Tier.","Questions drawing on the knowledge and understanding gained from the eight required practical activities count for at least 15 % of the overall marks for the qualification.","AT 1 to AT 7 are common with GCSE Combined Science: Trilogy; AT 8 is chemistry only. Required practicals 1, 3, 4, 5, 6 and 8 are common with Combined Science: Trilogy; required practicals 2 and 7 are GCSE Chemistry only.","Mean rate of reaction = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken, or quantity of product formed ÷ time taken. Units g/s or cm³/s, and (Higher tier) mol/s.","Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent, both measured from the baseline. Rf has no unit and is always between 0 and 1.","Temperature change = final (highest or lowest) temperature − starting temperature. A rise means the reaction is exothermic; a fall means it is endothermic.","Mean titre = the mean of the concordant titres, those within 0.10 cm³ of each other, leaving out the rough titration.","(Higher tier) Concentration in mol/dm³ = amount of solute in mol ÷ volume in dm³; volume in cm³ ÷ 1000 = volume in dm³; concentration in g/dm³ = concentration in mol/dm³ × relative formula mass.","The pH scale runs from 0 to 14 and is measured with universal indicator or a pH probe: below 7 acidic, 7 neutral, above 7 alkaline.","Flame tests: lithium crimson, sodium yellow, potassium lilac, calcium orange-red, copper green. In a mixture of ions some flame colours can be masked.","Gas tests: hydrogen — a burning splint gives a pop; oxygen — a glowing splint relights; carbon dioxide — limewater turns milky (cloudy); chlorine — damp litmus paper is bleached white."],"questions":[{"q":"State the colour of the flame a Bunsen burner gives when the air hole is closed, and state when this flame should be used.","a":"Yellow (the safety flame); used whenever the burner is alight but not actively heating anything.","sol":"With the air hole closed the burner burns with a yellow, luminous safety flame (1). This flame is easy to see, so it is the flame the burner is left on whenever it is lit but not being used to heat something (1). The air hole is opened for the hotter, roaring blue flame only while something is actually being heated.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["AT 2 asks you to know both Bunsen flames and what each one is for.","Think about which of the two flames is easier to notice from across a laboratory bench.","Your answer needs a colour and a statement of when that flame is on."],"codes":["AT 2"]},{"q":"Give the flame colour produced by lithium compounds and the flame colour produced by calcium compounds in a flame test.","a":"Lithium: crimson. Calcium: orange-red.","sol":"Lithium compounds give a crimson flame (1). Calcium compounds give an orange-red flame (1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification names flame colours for five metal ions only; two of them are asked for here.","Both of these colours sit at the red end of the list, so keep them apart carefully.","Give one colour for each of the two metals named."],"codes":["AT 8"]},{"q":"Name the technique used to separate an insoluble solid from a liquid, and give the name used for the liquid that passes through the filter paper.","a":"Filtration; the liquid that passes through is the filtrate.","sol":"The technique is filtration, in which the mixture is poured through filter paper held in a filter funnel (1). The liquid that runs through the paper is called the filtrate, and the insoluble solid left in the paper is the residue (1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["AT 4 lists five separation techniques; only one of them removes an insoluble solid.","The two parts of the mixture end up with different names once they are separated.","Name the technique first, then the liquid product."],"codes":["AT 4"]},{"q":"A student needs the pH of a water sample. Describe how universal indicator and a pH probe each give a pH, and state which gives the more precise value.","a":"Universal indicator is matched to a colour chart giving an approximate whole-number pH; a pH probe gives a numerical reading directly, and is the more precise.","sol":"Universal indicator, as paper or solution, changes colour and the colour is matched against a printed colour chart, which gives an approximate, whole-number pH (1). A pH probe (pH meter) is dipped into the sample and shows a numerical pH directly on a display (1). The pH probe gives the more precise value, because it does not depend on judging a colour by eye (1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["AT 3 covers the reagents and the techniques used to measure pH in different situations.","One of these methods needs something printed to compare against; the other does not.","Say how each one is used, then say which you would trust for a precise value."],"codes":["AT 3"]},{"q":"In required practical 3 aqueous solutions are electrolysed using inert electrodes. Name a material the electrodes can be made from, and explain why the electrodes must be inert.","a":"Graphite (or platinum); so the electrodes do not react themselves, and the products come only from the ions in the solution.","sol":"The electrodes can be made of graphite (carbon) or of platinum (1). They must be inert so that the electrode material does not react or take part in the electrolysis (1), which means the products formed at each electrode come only from the ions in the solution being investigated (1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["AT 7 is about setting up and using an electrochemical cell so that it tests what you meant to test.","Ask what would happen to your results if the electrode itself joined in the reaction.","Name one material, then give the reason in terms of what is produced at the electrodes."],"codes":["AT 7"]},{"q":"In a titration a student records a rough titre of 22.90 cm³, then titres of 22.20 cm³ and 22.30 cm³. Calculate the mean titre.","a":"22.25 cm³","sol":"The rough titration is left out, because it is run quickly only to find the approximate end point (1). The two remaining titres differ by 22.30 − 22.20 = 0.10 cm³, so they are concordant and may be averaged. Mean titre = (22.20 + 22.30) ÷ 2 = 44.50 ÷ 2 = 22.25 cm³ (1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["AT 1 and AT 8 both rest on the burette readings from required practical 2; not every reading listed is used.","Decide which readings are concordant before you add anything up.","Give a volume to two decimal places, with its unit."],"codes":["AT 1","AT 8"]},{"q":"A flask of dilute hydrochloric acid and calcium carbonate stands open on a balance. The flask loses 1.08 g of mass over 180 s as carbon dioxide escapes. Calculate the mean rate of reaction and give the unit.","a":"0.0060 g/s","sol":"Mean rate of reaction = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken (1). Substituting the loss in mass and the time: mean rate = 1.08 ÷ 180 (1) = 0.0060 g/s (1). The unit is g/s because a mass in grams has been divided by a time in seconds.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["AT 5 lists more than one way of following a rate; here the gas is not collected but its loss is weighed.","Write down the rate relationship first, then decide which of the two quantities in it the 1.08 g is.","Your answer is a number with a unit made from a mass unit and a time unit."],"codes":["AT 5","AT 1"]},{"q":"On a chromatogram a green spot has moved 3.6 cm from the baseline while the solvent front has moved 8.0 cm. Calculate the Rf value of the green substance.","a":"0.45","sol":"Rf = distance moved by the substance ÷ distance moved by the solvent, both measured from the baseline (1). Rf = 3.6 ÷ 8.0 (1) = 0.45. Rf has no unit and is always between 0 and 1.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["AT 4 covers chromatography, and required practical 6 asks you to calculate this value.","Decide which of the two distances belongs on the bottom of the division.","Give a number between 0 and 1, and do not add a unit."],"codes":["AT 4","AT 1"]},{"q":"Concentrated sodium chloride solution is electrolysed using inert electrodes. Hydrogen is given off at the cathode and chlorine at the anode. Describe the test for each gas and give the result that confirms it.","a":"Hydrogen: a burning splint held at the mouth of the tube burns rapidly with a pop. Chlorine: damp litmus paper is bleached white.","sol":"For hydrogen, hold a burning (lit) splint at the open end of the test tube of gas collected at the cathode (1); hydrogen burns rapidly with a pop sound (1). For chlorine, put damp litmus paper into the test tube of gas collected at the anode (1); the litmus paper is bleached and turns white (1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Required practical 3 pairs the electrochemical cell of AT 7 with the gas tests of AT 8.","Each answer needs both the thing you hold in the gas and what happens to it.","Deal with the two electrodes one at a time, naming the apparatus then the observation."],"codes":["AT 7","AT 8"]},{"q":"In required practical 1 the copper sulfate solution is evaporated using a water bath or an electric heater rather than a Bunsen burner flame. Explain why.","a":"A water bath or electric heater heats gently and evenly with no naked flame, so the solution does not spit and the salt is not decomposed or dried out too far.","sol":"A water bath or electric heater cannot heat the solution above about 100 °C and heats it gently and evenly, with no naked flame (1). Heating strongly over a Bunsen flame would make the hot solution spit out of the basin and could decompose the salt or drive off its water of crystallisation, so the sample would be lost or impure (1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["AT 2 sets a Bunsen burner and a water bath side by side; the difference between them is how hot and how evenly each heats.","Think about what happens to the crystals, and to the solution in the basin, if the heat is too fierce.","Say what the gentler heating does, then say what the fierce heating would have done to the product."],"codes":["AT 2","AT 4"]},{"q":"In required practical 4 a student mixes dilute hydrochloric acid with sodium hydroxide solution in an insulated cup. The starting temperature is 19.8 °C and the highest temperature reached is 28.5 °C. Calculate the temperature change and state whether the reaction is exothermic or endothermic.","a":"A rise of 8.7 °C; the reaction is exothermic.","sol":"Temperature change = final temperature − starting temperature (1) = 28.5 − 19.8 = +8.7 °C, that is a rise of 8.7 °C (1). The temperature of the surroundings rises, so energy has been transferred to the surroundings and the neutralisation is exothermic (1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["AT 5 asks you to record temperature changes during a reaction, and AT 1 to read the thermometer correctly.","Work out the change from the two readings before you decide what type of reaction it is.","Give a temperature change with its unit, then one word describing the reaction."],"codes":["AT 1","AT 3","AT 5"]},{"q":"In required practical 1 solid copper(II) oxide is added to warm dilute sulfuric acid. Describe how the solid should be added and explain why it is added in this controlled way.","a":"Add it a spatula measure at a time, stirring with a glass rod, until no more dissolves and some solid is left; this makes sure all the acid has reacted, and the excess is then filtered off.","sol":"The copper(II) oxide is added a spatula measure at a time and the mixture is stirred with a glass rod after each addition (1), and additions continue until no more solid dissolves and some solid remains, showing the oxide is in excess (1). Adding it in excess makes sure that all the acid has reacted, so no corrosive acid is left to contaminate the product; the unreacted solid is then removed by filtration (1). Eye protection is worn because the acid is an irritant and the mixture is warm.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["AT 6 is about mixing reagents under controlled conditions rather than tipping them together.","Ask how the student can tell, by looking, when enough solid has been added.","Describe the adding and stirring first, then say what would be wrong with the product if some acid were left."],"codes":["AT 6","AT 3"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) 20.0 cm³ of potassium hydroxide solution is neutralised by 23.50 cm³ of hydrochloric acid of concentration 0.200 mol/dm³. The equation is HCl + KOH → KCl + H₂O and the relative formula mass of KOH is 56. Calculate the concentration of the potassium hydroxide solution in mol/dm³ and in g/dm³.","a":"0.235 mol/dm³, which is 13.16 g/dm³.","sol":"Volume of acid = 23.50 ÷ 1000 = 0.02350 dm³. Moles of HCl = concentration × volume = 0.200 × 0.02350 = 0.00470 mol (1). The equation has a 1 : 1 ratio, so moles of KOH = 0.00470 mol (1). Volume of alkali = 20.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.0200 dm³, so concentration of KOH = 0.00470 ÷ 0.0200 = 0.235 mol/dm³ (1). Concentration in g/dm³ = 0.235 × 56 = 13.16 g/dm³ (1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["AT 8 includes determining the concentration of a strong acid or a strong alkali from a titration.","A concentration in mol/dm³ needs a volume in dm³, so deal with both burette and pipette volumes before anything else.","Start from the solution whose concentration you already know, and use the equation to cross to the other one."],"codes":["AT 8","AT 1"]},{"q":"A student is given a sample of sea water. Describe how to obtain a sample of pure water from it, and describe one way of checking that the water obtained is pure.","a":"Simple distillation: heat the sea water, condense the vapour and collect the distillate. Check purity by its boiling point (100 °C) or by evaporating a sample and finding no residue.","sol":"Set up simple distillation: put the sea water in a flask with a thermometer at the neck, sealed with a bung and delivery tube leading to a condenser (1). Heat the flask gently so the water boils and evaporates, leaving the dissolved salts behind in the flask (1). The water vapour passes into the condenser, where it is cooled and condenses back to a liquid, and the distillate is collected in a clean beaker (1). To check the distillate is pure, measure its boiling point: at normal atmospheric pressure pure water boils at exactly 100 °C, while a solution boils above 100 °C and over a range; alternatively evaporate a small sample to dryness and check that no solid residue is left (1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Required practical 8 uses the AT 4 technique that separates a solvent from a solution by boiling point.","Follow the water through the apparatus: where it boils, where it cools, and where it is caught.","Describe the apparatus and the heating first, then add a separate test on the liquid you collected."],"codes":["AT 4","AT 2","AT 3"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) A curve of volume of gas collected against time is drawn for a reaction. A tangent drawn to the curve at 30 s passes through the points (10 s, 6 cm³) and (50 s, 38 cm³). Determine the rate of reaction at 30 s.","a":"0.80 cm³/s","sol":"The rate at one instant is the gradient of the tangent drawn to the curve at that time (1). Gradient = change in volume ÷ change in time = (38 − 6) ÷ (50 − 10) (1) = 32 ÷ 40 = 0.80 cm³/s (1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["AT 5 measures rate from a graph as well as from a single pair of readings; a curve has a different rate at every moment.","The two points given lie on the straight tangent, so use them the way you would use any two points on a straight line.","Your answer is a gradient, so its unit comes from the units on the two axes."],"codes":["AT 5"]},{"q":"A student investigates the temperature change when magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid, using an open glass beaker with a thermometer held in it. Evaluate this part of the method and suggest one improvement.","a":"Heat is lost to the surroundings through the glass and the open top, so the temperature rise recorded is too small; use an insulated polystyrene cup with a lid.","sol":"Glass conducts energy away and an open beaker lets hot air and vapour escape, so energy is transferred to the surroundings during the reaction (1). The highest temperature recorded is therefore lower than the true value, and the temperature change measured is too small (1). Replacing the beaker with an insulated polystyrene cup fitted with a lid, with the thermometer through a hole in the lid, reduces this energy loss, so the recorded temperature change is closer to the true value (1). Stirring gently with the thermometer also makes the reading represent the whole mixture rather than one spot.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["AT 1 and AT 5 together ask not just for a reading but for a reading you can trust.","Follow the energy released by the reaction and decide where some of it goes before the thermometer can read it.","Say what goes wrong and which way it shifts the result, then name a specific replacement piece of apparatus."],"codes":["AT 1","AT 5","AT 6"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What colour flame does a lithium compound give in a flame test?","a":"Crimson."},{"q":"Give the equation for an Rf value.","a":"Rf = distance moved by the substance ÷ distance moved by the solvent."},{"q":"Which gas relights a glowing splint?","a":"Oxygen."},{"q":"Which separation technique is used to obtain pure water from a salt solution?","a":"Simple distillation."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Apparatus for measuring in chemistry","back":"Mass: balance, often to 0.01 g. Time: stopwatch. Temperature: thermometer or probe. Volume of liquid: measuring cylinder, 25.0 cm³ pipette or burette read to the nearest 0.05 cm³, read at the meniscus. Volume of gas: gas syringe.","code":"AT 1","open":false},{"front":"Bunsen burner flames","back":"Air hole closed: yellow, luminous safety flame, easy to see, used when not heating. Air hole open: roaring blue flame, much hotter, used only while heating.","code":"AT 2","open":false},{"front":"Water bath or electric heater","back":"Gives gentle, even heating with no naked flame and no more than about 100 °C. Used for flammable liquids and for evaporating a salt solution without decomposing it.","code":"AT 2","open":false},{"front":"Measuring pH","back":"Universal indicator paper or solution: colour matched to a chart, approximate whole-number pH. pH probe: numerical reading, more precise, can follow pH continuously. Scale 0–14, 7 neutral.","code":"AT 3","open":false},{"front":"Filtration and crystallisation","back":"Filtration separates an insoluble solid (residue) from a liquid (filtrate) using filter paper in a funnel. Crystallisation: evaporate to saturation, cool slowly so crystals grow, filter and pat dry.","code":"AT 4","open":false},{"front":"Simple distillation","back":"Separates a solvent from a solution by boiling point: heat the solution, the solvent evaporates and the solute stays behind, the vapour is cooled in a condenser and collected as distillate.","code":"AT 4","open":false},{"front":"Rf value","back":"Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent, both from the pencil baseline. No unit, always between 0 and 1. A pure compound gives one spot in every solvent.","code":"AT 4","open":false},{"front":"Mean rate of reaction","back":"Mean rate = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken, or quantity of product formed ÷ time taken. Units g/s or cm³/s, and (Higher tier) mol/s.","code":"AT 5","open":false},{"front":"Name one method used to measure the rate of a reaction in required practical 5","back":"Collect the gas in a gas syringe and record its volume at set times; stand the open flask on a balance and record the loss in mass as gas escapes; time how long a black cross takes to disappear through the cloudy, turbid mixture.","code":"AT 5","open":true},{"front":"Handling substances safely","back":"Wear eye protection with acids, alkalis and hot liquids; add a solid to acid a spatula at a time, stirring; keep the total volume of liquid fixed when diluting; use a fume cupboard for toxic gases such as chlorine.","code":"AT 6","open":false},{"front":"Electrolysis cell with inert electrodes","back":"Two graphite or platinum electrodes dip into the electrolyte in a beaker, joined by wires and crocodile clips to a d.c. power supply, with an inverted test tube over each electrode to collect gas.","code":"AT 7","open":false},{"front":"Flame test colours","back":"Lithium crimson, sodium yellow, potassium lilac, calcium orange-red, copper green. In a mixture of ions some flame colours can be masked.","code":"AT 8","open":false},{"front":"Tests for the four gases","back":"Hydrogen: burning splint burns rapidly with a pop. Oxygen: glowing splint relights. Carbon dioxide: limewater turns milky. Chlorine: damp litmus paper is bleached white.","code":"AT 8","open":false},{"front":"Sodium hydroxide solution precipitate tests","back":"Aluminium, calcium and magnesium give white precipitates, but only aluminium hydroxide dissolves in excess sodium hydroxide. Copper(II) blue, iron(II) green, iron(III) brown.","code":"AT 8","open":false},{"front":"Tests for halide ions and sulfate ions","back":"Halides with silver nitrate solution and dilute nitric acid: chloride white, bromide cream, iodide yellow. Sulfates with barium chloride solution and dilute hydrochloric acid: white precipitate.","code":"AT 8","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Key equations, values and tests for practical chemistry","items":["Mean rate of reaction = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken, or = quantity of product formed ÷ time taken. The quantity is a mass in g or a volume in cm³, so the unit of rate is g/s or cm³/s; (Higher tier) quantities in moles give mol/s.","(Higher tier) Rate of reaction at one instant = gradient of the tangent drawn to the curve at that time = change in quantity ÷ change in time.","Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent, both measured from the pencil baseline. Rf has no unit and always lies between 0 and 1.","Temperature change = final (highest or lowest) temperature − starting temperature. A positive change (a rise) means the reaction is exothermic; a negative change (a fall) means it is endothermic.","Mean titre = mean of the concordant titres only, those within 0.10 cm³ of each other; the rough titration is never included.","(Higher tier) Concentration in mol/dm³ = amount of solute in mol ÷ volume of solution in dm³, rearranged to amount in mol = concentration × volume.","Volume in dm³ = volume in cm³ ÷ 1000.","(Higher tier) Concentration in g/dm³ = concentration in mol/dm³ × relative formula mass (Mr).","Concentration of dissolved solids in a water sample (required practical 8) = mass of residue left after evaporation ÷ volume of sample, in g/dm³.","The pH scale runs from 0 to 14: below 7 acidic, 7 neutral, above 7 alkaline. Measured with universal indicator (approximate) or a pH probe (numerical).","Gas tests: hydrogen — burning splint, burns rapidly with a pop; oxygen — glowing splint relights; carbon dioxide — limewater turns milky (cloudy); chlorine — damp litmus paper is bleached white.","Flame test colours: lithium crimson, sodium yellow, potassium lilac, calcium orange-red, copper green.","A periodic table is supplied in both papers, and it gives the relative atomic masses needed for a relative formula mass; no equation sheet is provided, so every relationship above must be recalled."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical 1: preparation of a pure, dry sample of a soluble salt from an insoluble oxide or carbonate using a Bunsen burner to heat dilute acid and a water bath or electric heater to evaporate the solution","items":["Method: 1) Measure a fixed volume of dilute acid (for example dilute sulfuric acid) into a beaker with a measuring cylinder.","2) Warm the acid gently with a Bunsen burner on a tripod and gauze over a heatproof mat, using the blue flame, and do not let it boil; turn the burner to the yellow safety flame as soon as heating stops.","3) Add the insoluble oxide or carbonate (for example copper(II) oxide) a spatula measure at a time, stirring with a glass rod, until no more dissolves and some solid remains, showing it is in excess.","4) Filter the mixture through filter paper in a filter funnel into a conical flask to remove the excess solid; the filtrate is the salt solution.","5) Pour the filtrate into an evaporating basin and heat it over a water bath or an electric heater until a drop taken on a cold glass rod forms crystals, showing the solution is saturated.","6) Leave the saturated solution to cool slowly so that crystals grow, then filter the crystals from the remaining solution.","7) Pat the crystals dry between sheets of filter paper; do not heat them, or water of crystallisation will be driven off.","This is a preparation, not an investigation, so there is no independent or dependent variable.","Quantities kept the same throughout: the volume and concentration of the dilute acid, the same acid and the same insoluble base used for the whole preparation, gentle non-boiling heat at both the reaction and the evaporation stage.","Apparatus: Bunsen burner, heatproof mat, tripod and gauze, beaker, measuring cylinder, spatula, glass rod, dilute acid, the insoluble metal oxide or carbonate, filter funnel and filter paper, conical flask, evaporating basin, water bath or electric heater, balance.","Safety: wear eye protection throughout; dilute acid is an irritant and the warm acid can spit, so heat gently and never point the beaker at anyone; use tongs to move hot apparatus and leave it on a heatproof mat to cool.","Expected result and analysis: the acid loses its acidity as the base is added and the excess solid confirms the acid has all reacted; the filtrate gives a crop of coloured or colourless crystals of the salt, which can be weighed to find the yield.","Likely errors: adding too little oxide or carbonate leaves unreacted acid in the filtrate, so the product is contaminated; evaporating too far or heating too strongly makes the solution spit or decomposes the salt; filtering before the solution is saturated gives few or no crystals; drying the crystals with heat drives off water of crystallisation.","Skills assessed: AT 2 – safe use of appropriate heating devices and techniques including use of a Bunsen burner and a water bath or electric heater; AT 3 – use of appropriate apparatus and techniques for conducting chemical reactions, including appropriate reagents; AT 4 – safe use of a range of equipment to purify and/or separate chemical mixtures including evaporation, filtration, crystallisation; AT 6 – safe use and careful handling of liquids and solids, including careful mixing of reagents under controlled conditions; WS 2.3, WS 2.4. No MS skills are listed for this practical."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical 2: (chemistry only) determination of the reacting volumes of solutions of a strong acid and a strong alkali by titration. (HT only) determination of the concentration of one of the solutions in mol/dm³ and g/dm³ from the reacting volumes and the known concentration of the other solution","items":["Method: 1) Rinse a pipette with the alkali, then use it with a pipette filler to transfer exactly 25.0 cm³ of the sodium hydroxide solution into a clean conical flask.","2) Add a few drops of a suitable indicator to the flask, for example phenolphthalein or methyl orange, and stand the flask on a white tile.","3) Rinse the burette with the acid, fill it with the dilute strong acid (hydrochloric, nitric or sulfuric only), run a little through the tap to fill the jet, and record the initial reading at eye level from the bottom of the meniscus.","4) Run the acid into the flask, swirling constantly, until the indicator changes colour permanently; record the final burette reading and subtract to find the titre. This first, rough titration only locates the approximate end point.","5) Repeat, adding the acid dropwise near the expected end point, until at least two titres are concordant, that is within 0.10 cm³ of each other.","6) Calculate the mean titre from the concordant titres only, leaving out the rough titration.","7) (Higher tier) Use the mean titre and the known concentration to find moles of the known solution, use the balanced equation to find moles of the unknown, then divide by the volume in dm³ to give a concentration in mol/dm³, and multiply by the relative formula mass for g/dm³.","Independent variable: none is changed — the volume of alkali is fixed and the titration is repeated to a constant result. Dependent variable: the titre, the volume of acid needed to reach the end point.","Control variables: the same volume and concentration of alkali in every run (25.0 cm³ by pipette), the same acid and the same indicator, the same number of drops of indicator, and the same person judging the end point.","Apparatus: burette and stand with a white tile, 25.0 cm³ pipette and pipette filler, conical flask, small funnel, beakers, the strong acid and the strong alkali, indicator (phenolphthalein or methyl orange), distilled water for rinsing.","Safety: wear eye protection; the acid and the alkali are irritant or corrosive, so use a pipette filler and never mouth-pipette, fill the burette below eye level using a funnel, and wash any splashes off the skin at once with plenty of water.","Expected result and analysis: the concordant titres agree within 0.10 cm³ and are averaged to give the mean titre, the reacting volume of acid for 25.0 cm³ of alkali; (Higher tier) that mean titre is then used to calculate the unknown concentration in mol/dm³ and g/dm³.","Likely errors: including the rough titre in the mean; reading the burette from above or below eye level instead of at the meniscus; an air bubble left in the burette jet, which adds to the titre; overshooting the end point by adding acid too quickly; rinsing the conical flask with the alkali, which adds extra alkali.","Skills assessed: AT 1 – use of appropriate apparatus to make and record a range of measurements accurately, including volume of liquids; AT 8 – the determination of concentrations of strong acids and strong alkalis; WS 2.4, WS 2.6; MS 1a, MS 1c, MS 2a."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical 3: investigate what happens when aqueous solutions are electrolysed using inert electrodes. This should be an investigation involving developing a hypothesis","items":["Method: 1) Half fill a beaker with the first aqueous solution, for example copper(II) sulfate solution, sodium chloride solution or dilute sulfuric acid.","2) Use the ions present in the solution to develop a hypothesis about which product will form at each electrode before switching on.","3) Clamp two inert electrodes of graphite or platinum in the solution without letting them touch, and connect them with wires and crocodile clips to a d.c. power supply.","4) Fill two small test tubes with the solution and invert one over each electrode to collect any gas produced.","5) Switch on at a fixed low voltage, start a stopwatch and record what is seen at each electrode: bubbles, a colour change, or a coating forming on an electrode.","6) After a fixed time, switch off and test each gas collected: burning splint for hydrogen, glowing splint for oxygen, damp litmus paper for chlorine.","7) Repeat with each of the other solutions, keeping the electrodes, the voltage and the time the same, and compare the products with the hypothesis.","Independent variable: the aqueous solution (electrolyte) being electrolysed.","Dependent variable: the product formed at each electrode, identified by observation and by the gas tests.","Control variables: the electrode material (inert graphite or platinum), the voltage or current from the d.c. supply, the depth, spacing and surface area of the electrodes, the volume of electrolyte and the time the current flows.","Apparatus: beaker or electrolysis cell, two inert graphite or platinum electrodes, stand and clamps, d.c. power supply, wires and crocodile clips, small test tubes, wooden splints, damp litmus paper, the aqueous solutions.","Safety: wear eye protection; chlorine is toxic, so electrolyse chloride solutions only in small quantities in a well-ventilated room or fume cupboard and do not breathe in the gas; switch off the power supply before touching the electrodes, and treat the solutions as irritants.","Expected result and analysis: hydrogen or a metal is produced at the cathode and oxygen or a halogen at the anode; compare each observed product with the hypothesis made from the ions present, and explain any difference.","Likely errors: using a reactive rather than an inert electrode, so the electrode itself reacts and the true products are hidden; collecting too little gas to give a clear test result; letting the electrodes touch, which short-circuits the cell; confusing the bleaching of damp litmus by chlorine with the colour change caused by an acidic gas.","Skills assessed: AT 3 – use of appropriate apparatus and techniques for conducting and monitoring chemical reactions; AT 7 – use of appropriate apparatus and techniques to draw, set up and use electrochemical cells for separation and production of elements and compounds; AT 8 – use of appropriate qualitative reagents and techniques to analyse and identify unknown samples or products including gas tests for hydrogen, oxygen and chlorine; WS 2.1, WS 2.2, WS 2.3, WS 2.4, WS 2.6. No MS skills are listed for this practical."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical 4: investigate the variables that affect temperature changes in reacting solutions such as, eg acid plus metals, acid plus carbonates, neutralisations, displacement of metals","items":["Method: 1) Measure a fixed volume of the solution, for example 25 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid, into a polystyrene cup with a lid, standing in a beaker for support.","2) Record the starting temperature of the solution with a thermometer through the hole in the lid.","3) Add the second reactant — a measured mass of metal or carbonate, or a measured volume of alkali — and replace the lid at once.","4) Stir gently with the thermometer and record the highest (or lowest) temperature reached.","5) Calculate the temperature change: final temperature − starting temperature.","6) Repeat with a different value of the variable being investigated, for example a different concentration of acid, keeping everything else the same.","7) Repeat each condition and calculate a mean temperature change, then plot temperature change against the variable tested.","Independent variable: the variable chosen for the investigation, for example the concentration of the acid, or the metal used in a displacement reaction.","Dependent variable: the temperature change of the reacting solution.","Control variables: the volume of solution, the mass or amount of the other reactant, the starting temperature, the same insulated cup and lid, and the same stirring.","Apparatus: polystyrene cup with lid, beaker to hold it, thermometer or temperature probe, measuring cylinder, balance, spatula, stopwatch, the reactants (dilute acid and a metal, a carbonate or an alkali).","Safety: wear eye protection; acids and alkalis are irritant or corrosive and some of these reactions get hot enough to spit, so keep the lid on and stand the cup in a beaker so it cannot be knocked over.","Expected result and analysis: an exothermic reaction gives a temperature rise, an endothermic reaction a fall; the mean temperature change is plotted against the variable tested and the pattern described, for example a larger rise as concentration increases.","Likely errors: energy lost to the surroundings from an uninsulated or lidless container makes the measured change too small; not stirring means the thermometer reads only one part of the mixture; a delay in replacing the lid or reading the thermometer misses the true peak or lowest temperature.","Skills assessed: AT 1 – use of appropriate apparatus to make and record a range of measurements accurately, including mass, temperature, and volume of liquids; AT 3 – use of appropriate apparatus and techniques for conducting and monitoring chemical reactions; AT 5 – making and recording of appropriate observations during chemical reactions including changes in temperature; AT 6 – safe use and careful handling of gases, liquids and solids, including careful mixing of reagents under controlled conditions, using appropriate apparatus to explore chemical changes; WS 2.1, WS 2.2, WS 2.3, WS 2.4, WS 2.6, WS 2.7; MS 1a, MS 2a, MS 2b, MS 4a, MS 4c."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical 5: investigate how changes in concentration affect the rates of reactions by a method involving measuring the volume of a gas produced and a method involving a change in colour or turbidity. This should be an investigation involving developing a hypothesis","items":["Method (gas volume): 1) Use collision theory to develop a hypothesis about how concentration will affect the rate.","2) Measure a fixed volume of dilute hydrochloric acid at the first concentration into a conical flask, diluting with distilled water so the total volume of liquid is the same for every concentration tested.","3) Add a fixed mass or length of magnesium ribbon, immediately fit the bung and delivery tube leading to a gas syringe, and start the stopwatch.","4) Record the volume of gas in the syringe at regular intervals, for example every 10 s, until no more gas is produced.","5) Repeat at a range of other concentrations, keeping everything else the same, and repeat each concentration to find a mean.","Method (colour or turbidity): 6) Stand a conical flask holding sodium thiosulfate solution at the chosen concentration over a paper marked with a black cross on a white tile, add dilute hydrochloric acid, and start the stopwatch on mixing.","7) Stop the stopwatch the moment the cross can no longer be seen through the cloudy mixture, record the time, and repeat at a range of concentrations.","8) Plot volume of gas against time for each concentration, and plot 1 ÷ time against concentration for the turbidity method.","Independent variable: the concentration of one reactant, with the total volume of liquid kept the same by dilution with water.","Dependent variable: the volume of gas collected at set times (gas method), or the time taken for the cross to disappear (turbidity method).","Control variables: the volume and concentration of the other reactant, the total volume of liquid, the temperature, the mass, length and surface area of the magnesium, the same cross and the same observer.","Apparatus: conical flask, bung and delivery tube, gas syringe, measuring cylinders, stopwatch, thermometer, balance, white tile and paper marked with a black cross, dilute hydrochloric acid, magnesium ribbon, sodium thiosulfate solution, distilled water.","Safety: wear eye protection; the reaction of an acid with sodium thiosulfate produces sulfur dioxide, which is toxic and can trigger asthma, so work in a well-ventilated room and keep the volumes small; dispose of the cloudy mixtures promptly.","Expected result and analysis: a higher concentration gives a steeper curve of gas volume against time and a shorter time for the cross to disappear; mean rate = volume of gas ÷ time, and 1 ÷ time plotted against concentration gives a straight line through the origin if rate is proportional to concentration.","Likely errors: gas escaping before the bung is fully in place lowers the volume recorded; judging by eye when the cross disappears is subjective and differs between observers; not keeping the total volume of liquid the same when diluting changes more than the intended variable; the temperature drifting between runs.","Skills assessed: AT 1 – use of appropriate apparatus to make and record a range of measurements accurately, including mass, time, temperature, and volume of liquids and gases; AT 3 – use of appropriate apparatus and techniques for conducting and monitoring chemical reactions; AT 5 – making and recording of appropriate observations during chemical reactions including the measurement of rates of reaction by a variety of methods such as production of gas and colour change; AT 6 – safe use and careful handling of gases, liquids and solids, including careful mixing of reagents under controlled conditions; WS 2.1, WS 2.2, WS 2.3, WS 2.4, WS 2.6, WS 2.7; MS 1a, MS 1c, MS 1d, MS 2a, MS 2b, MS 4a, MS 4b, MS 4c, MS 4d, MS 4e."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical 6: investigate how paper chromatography can be used to separate and tell the difference between coloured substances. Students should calculate Rf values","items":["Method: 1) Draw a baseline in pencil about 1 cm above the bottom edge of a piece of chromatography paper, and mark on it where each sample will go.","2) Use a capillary tube or fine dropper to put a small, concentrated spot of each sample, and of each known reference substance, on its mark, letting each spot dry before adding more.","3) Pour a shallow layer of solvent into a beaker so that the solvent level will be below the baseline.","4) Hang or stand the paper in the beaker with the baseline just above the solvent surface and the spots not touching the solvent, and cover the beaker with a lid or watch glass.","5) Leave the solvent to rise until it is near the top of the paper, then remove the paper and mark the solvent front in pencil at once, before it evaporates.","6) Let the chromatogram dry, then measure, from the baseline, the distance moved by the solvent front and the distance moved by the centre of each spot.","7) Calculate Rf = distance moved by the substance ÷ distance moved by the solvent for each spot, and compare the values with those of the reference substances.","This is an analysis technique rather than a variable-testing investigation, so there is no independent or dependent variable; different samples are compared side by side under identical conditions.","Quantities kept the same for a fair comparison: the same chromatography paper and the same solvent, the baseline above the solvent level, the same running time and the same distance travelled by the solvent for every sample on one chromatogram.","Apparatus: chromatography paper, pencil, ruler, capillary tube or fine dropper, beaker, lid or watch glass, solvent, the samples to be tested and known reference substances.","Safety: wear eye protection; if an organic solvent such as ethanol is used it is flammable and its vapour is harmful, so keep it away from naked flames and cover the beaker to reduce vapour.","Expected result and analysis: a pure substance stays as a single spot in every solvent, while a mixture separates into several spots; a sample is identified by matching its Rf values with those of the reference substances run on the same chromatogram.","Likely errors: drawing the baseline in ink, so the ink itself dissolves and separates; letting a spot dip below the solvent surface, so the sample dissolves into the solvent instead of travelling up the paper; leaving the beaker uncovered, so the solvent evaporates and its level changes; measuring to the edge of a spot rather than its centre.","Skills assessed: AT 1 – use of appropriate apparatus to make and record a range of measurements accurately; AT 4 – safe use of a range of equipment to purify and/or separate chemical mixtures including chromatography; WS 2.4, WS 2.6. No MS skills are listed for this practical."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical 7: use of chemical tests to identify the ions in unknown single ionic compounds covering the ions from sections Flame tests to Sulfates (chemistry only)","items":["Method: 1) Clean a nichrome wire loop by dipping it in dilute hydrochloric acid and holding it in a blue Bunsen flame until it gives no colour.","2) Dip the clean loop in the unknown solid and hold it in the edge of the blue flame; record the flame colour and read it against lithium crimson, sodium yellow, potassium lilac, calcium orange-red and copper green.","3) Dissolve a little of the unknown in distilled water, add sodium hydroxide solution drop by drop and then in excess, and record the precipitate: white for aluminium, calcium or magnesium, with only the aluminium precipitate redissolving in excess; blue for copper(II), green for iron(II), brown for iron(III).","4) In a fresh sample, add dilute acid and pass any gas given off through limewater; a carbonate fizzes and the limewater turns milky, showing carbon dioxide.","5) In a fresh sample, add dilute nitric acid and then silver nitrate solution: a white precipitate shows chloride, cream shows bromide, yellow shows iodide.","6) In a fresh sample, add dilute hydrochloric acid and then barium chloride solution: a white precipitate shows sulfate.","7) Combine the positive result for the cation with the positive result for the anion to name the unknown compound.","This is an identification exercise, not a variable-testing investigation, so there is no independent or dependent variable; the unknown sample is the thing being identified and the reagents are fixed.","Quantities kept the same for a fair comparison: a fresh portion of the unknown for each test, the same small volume of solution and the same reagents each time, and the wire loop cleaned between flame tests.","Apparatus: Bunsen burner and heatproof mat, nichrome wire loop, test tubes and rack, dropping pipettes, spatula, the unknown solid, distilled water, dilute hydrochloric acid, dilute nitric acid, sodium hydroxide solution, silver nitrate solution, barium chloride solution, limewater.","Safety: wear eye protection; sodium hydroxide solution is corrosive and silver nitrate stains skin and clothing, so use dropping pipettes and small volumes; tie hair back and keep the Bunsen on the yellow safety flame between flame tests.","Expected result and analysis: one flame colour and one precipitate identify the metal ion, and one precipitate or gas test identifies the negative ion, so the two together name the compound; if a mixture of ions were present some flame colours could be masked.","Likely errors: a loop not cleaned properly carries sodium over from an earlier sample and masks the true colour with yellow; adding the wrong acid before the precipitation test, for example hydrochloric acid before silver nitrate, gives a false white precipitate; using the same test tube twice without rinsing; not adding sodium hydroxide in excess, so an aluminium result is read as calcium or magnesium.","Skills assessed (as listed at 8.2.7): AT 2 – safe use of a Bunsen burner; AT 8 – use of appropriate qualitative reagents and techniques to analyse and identify unknown samples or products including gas tests, flame tests, precipitation reactions; WS 2.4, WS 2.6. The summary line beside the practical in section 4.8.3 lists the AT skills for this activity as 1 and 8. No MS skills are listed for this practical."]},{"kind":"practical","title":"Required practical 8: analysis and purification of water samples from different sources, including pH, dissolved solids and distillation","items":["Method: 1) Collect water samples from different sources, for example tap water, sea water, rainwater and pond water, taking a representative sample of each.","2) Measure the pH of each sample with universal indicator matched to a colour chart, or with a pH probe rinsed with distilled water between samples.","3) Weigh a clean, dry evaporating basin, then measure a fixed volume of the first sample into it with a measuring cylinder.","4) Evaporate the sample to dryness over a water bath or gently with a Bunsen burner, let the basin cool and reweigh it; the increase in mass is the mass of dissolved solids.","5) Repeat steps 3 and 4 with the same volume of each of the other samples.","6) Set up simple distillation for one sample: a flask with a thermometer at the neck, a bung and delivery tube to a condenser, and a beaker to collect the distillate.","7) Heat gently, note the temperature at which the vapour passes over, and collect the distillate.","8) Test the distillate: measure its pH, and evaporate a small volume of it to dryness to show that no dissolved solids are left.","Independent variable: the water sample, that is the source of the water.","Dependent variables: the pH of the sample, and the mass of dissolved solids left after evaporating a fixed volume.","Control variables: the same volume of every sample evaporated, the same evaporating basin or identical basins, the same balance and the same heating method, and the same indicator or probe used for every pH.","Apparatus: universal indicator and colour chart or pH probe, measuring cylinder, evaporating basin, balance, Bunsen burner, tripod, gauze and heatproof mat or water bath, tongs, distillation flask with bung and delivery tube, thermometer, condenser, beaker, the water samples.","Safety: wear eye protection; the evaporating basin and the distillation flask become very hot, so move them with tongs and let them cool on a heatproof mat, and do not seal the distillation apparatus, since trapped vapour can force the bung out.","Expected result and analysis: distilled or rain water leaves little or no residue and sea water leaves the most; the concentration of dissolved solids is the mass of residue ÷ volume of sample in dm³, in g/dm³, and the distillate leaves no residue, showing distillation removes the dissolved solids while filtration would not.","Likely errors: reweighing the basin before the residue is fully dry overestimates the dissolved solids; heating so strongly that the solution spits loses some residue; heating too fast during distillation carries droplets of the original sample over into the distillate, so it is not pure; judging a universal indicator colour by eye gives only an approximate pH.","Skills assessed: AT 2 – safe use of appropriate heating devices and techniques including use of a Bunsen burner and a water bath or electric heater; AT 3 – use of appropriate apparatus and techniques for the measurement of pH in different situations; AT 4 – safe use of a range of equipment to purify and/or separate chemical mixtures including evaporation, distillation; WS 2.3, WS 2.4, WS 2.5, WS 2.6, WS 2.7. No MS skills are listed for this practical."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: preparing a pure, dry sample of copper sulfate crystals (required practical 1)","items":["Question: A student is given dilute sulfuric acid and solid copper(II) oxide, which is insoluble in water. Describe how the student should prepare a pure, dry sample of copper sulfate crystals, and explain the reason for each stage. (6 marks)","Level 3 (5–6 marks) model answer: Measure a fixed volume of dilute sulfuric acid into a beaker and warm it gently with a Bunsen burner on a tripod and gauze, using the blue flame and not letting the acid boil, because the reaction is faster when the acid is warm.","Add copper(II) oxide a spatula measure at a time, stirring with a glass rod, and keep adding until some solid remains undissolved; this shows the oxide is in excess, so all of the acid has reacted and no corrosive acid is left to contaminate the product.","Filter the mixture through filter paper in a filter funnel to remove the excess copper(II) oxide; the blue filtrate that runs through is the copper sulfate solution and the black residue left in the paper is the unreacted oxide.","Pour the filtrate into an evaporating basin and heat it over a water bath or an electric heater until a drop on a cold glass rod forms crystals, showing the solution is saturated; a water bath is used rather than a naked flame because gentle, even heating stops the solution spitting and stops the salt decomposing.","Leave the saturated solution to cool slowly so that crystals grow, then filter the crystals from the remaining solution and pat them dry between sheets of filter paper rather than heating them, because heating would drive off the water of crystallisation.","What a Level 1 (1–2 mark) answer misses: it gives isolated fragments such as 'add copper oxide to acid and filter' with no order and no reasons — it does not say the oxide is added until it is in excess or why that matters, does not distinguish the filtrate from the residue, does not say that evaporation is stopped at the saturation point and the solution cooled slowly, and does not explain why gentle heating and patting dry protect the crystals, so there is no sustained line of reasoning from reagents to a pure, dry product."]},{"kind":"extended","title":"Six-mark model answer: finding the reacting volumes of a strong acid and a strong alkali by titration (required practical 2, chemistry only)","items":["Question: Describe how to carry out a titration to find accurately the volume of dilute hydrochloric acid that reacts with 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution, and explain how the results are used. (6 marks)","Level 3 (5–6 marks) model answer: Rinse a pipette with the sodium hydroxide solution and use it with a pipette filler to transfer exactly 25.0 cm³ of the alkali into a clean conical flask, then add a few drops of an indicator such as phenolphthalein or methyl orange and stand the flask on a white tile so the colour change is easy to see.","Rinse a burette with the dilute hydrochloric acid, fill it using a small funnel, run a little acid through the tap so no air bubble is left in the jet, and record the initial reading at eye level from the bottom of the meniscus.","Run the acid into the flask while swirling constantly, and stop as soon as the indicator changes colour permanently; record the final burette reading and subtract the initial reading to give the titre. This first titration is a rough one, used only to find the approximate end point.","Repeat the titration with a fresh 25.0 cm³ portion of alkali, adding the acid dropwise as the rough end point is approached, and keep repeating until at least two titres are concordant, that is within 0.10 cm³ of each other.","Calculate the mean titre from the concordant titres only, leaving the rough titration out, and quote it to two decimal places; this mean titre is the volume of acid that reacts with 25.0 cm³ of the alkali, and (Higher tier) it can be used with the known concentration of one solution and the balanced equation to calculate the concentration of the other in mol/dm³ and then in g/dm³.","What a Level 1 (1–2 mark) answer misses: it names a piece of apparatus or two, such as 'use a burette and an indicator', without an ordered procedure — it does not say how the fixed volume of alkali is measured, does not describe finding the end point by swirling and adding dropwise, does not mention repeating to concordant results within 0.10 cm³, and does not say that the rough titration is excluded from the mean, so the answer gives no method that would produce an accurate reacting volume."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:32:47.135631Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Every calculation, AT-to-practical assignment and chemical test in this kit checks out against sections 8.1, 8.2.1–8.2.8 and 4.4/4.6/4.8/4.10 of the specification; the only repairs needed are four wording points (the RP5 open card, the electric-heater temperature ceiling, the reactant/product label in the mass-loss rate solution, and the AT 1 / RP7 cross-reference)."}}}} };
